rs/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/a6304b754b10957f6572f9fc085b5cc5f2443010","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKGE2MzA0Yjc1NGIxMDk1N2Y2NTcyZjlmYzA4NWI1Y2M1ZjI0NDMwMTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/bdea1ed9a42ca74230bb7e3b41d0f8805973ed2e","sha":"bdea1ed9a42ca74230bb7e3b41d0f8805973ed2e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/bdea1ed9a42ca74230bb7e3b41d0f8805973ed2e"}],"sha":"a6304b754b10957f6572f9fc085b5cc5f2443010","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/a6304b754b10957f6572f9fc085b5cc5f2443010"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/130109357?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dylan-savage","id":130109357,"login":"dylan-savage","node_id":"U_kgDOB8FPrQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/bdea1ed9a42ca74230bb7e3b41d0f8805973ed2e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-08T03:54:02Z","email":"130109357+dylan-savage@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dylan Savage"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-08T03:54:02Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(ai, appdev): show whether an app is actually serving in App Builder (#2580)\n\n* fix(ai): report real serving state on app pins and clear the serving cache on deploy changes (#2461)\n\nApp owners could see deployed and published, but never whether a browser\nwould actually be served the app. The where-list reported the deployment''s\nreview state and kept disabled bindings, and the shell''s serving cache kept a\nsession''s escalated \"not found\" verdict after the version became servable\n(8 s observed, up to 300 s).\n\n- One serving gate (_serving_block_of) now feeds both the manifest scope walk\n  and the where-list, so what is served and what is shown cannot drift.\n- Where pins gain enabled, serving, reason and servesYou (additive; state\n  keeps its meaning).\n- App publish/disable/remove push apaevt_deploy like every other deployment\n  mutation, and broadcast_deploy_changed drops the app''s cached serving\n  verdicts (invalidate_app_serving), best-effort.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(appdev): show serving state and live updates in App Builder (#2461)\n\nApp Builder said \"live\" and \"serves vN\" for every pin and showed a green\n\"ready\" on a version whose build was still running, so owners deployed,\npublished, and still could not tell whether a browser would load the app.\n\n- servingStatus.ts phrases the server''s per-pin serving truth (serving /\n  reason / servesYou) once for both views, falling back to today''s wording\n  when an older server sends none of it.\n- Deploy tab: where-live rows read \"serving\" or why not (still building,\n  build failed, disabled, in review); version cards show the running build\n  word instead of the review badge; unserved audience chips are muted.\n- Dashboard: \"Right now @x serves vN\" only when it does, plus \"You''d get vN\n  through @x\"; the where card badge follows the same truth.\n- The VS Code host relays apaevt_deploy for an open app as a re-fetch\n  signal, so a landed build or anyone''s publish/disable updates every open\n  App Builder.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(apps): explain serving state on the App Builder Deploy tab (#2461)\n\nA deploy returns before the server build finishes, and a version serves\nonly once it does. Say so, and describe what the Deploy tab''s \"Where this\napp is live\" list and the dashboard now show: what a browser in each\naudience actually gets, or why not.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): resolve servesYou like the catalog and scope serving-cache drops to apps (#2461)\n\nReview fixes on the serving-state change:\n\n- servesYou now picks the scope-walk winner FIRST and only then applies\n  requiredPermissions, and only where serving enforces them (SaaS). OSS\n  never gates on requiredPermissions, so a gated app no longer reads \"You\n  wouldn''t get any published version\" while it serves; on SaaS a gated\n  winner hides the pin instead of falling back to a less specific one,\n  exactly as the catalog drops it.\n- Serving-cache invalidation moves out of broadcast_deploy_changed into a\n  new app-only broadcast_app_changed (build stamps, publish/disable/remove,\n  review transitions). A pipeline''s project id is user-chosen, so pipeline\n  deploy events must not drop another tenant''s app verdicts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c5e03a0462e3e678149672213de631f212a0a81c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/c5e03a0462e3e678149672213de631f212a0a81c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/bdea1ed9a42ca74230bb7e3b41d0f8805973ed2e","verification":{"payload":"tree c5e03a0462e3e678149672213de631f212a0a81c\nparent a7e2fec5af7414455ff8d0efdcaa1b5eb7416b02\nauthor Dylan Savage <130109357+dylan-savage@users.noreply.github.com> 1791431642 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791431642 -0700\n\nfeat(ai, appdev): show whether an app is actually serving in App Builder (#2580)\n\n* fix(ai): report real serving state on app pins and clear the serving cache on deploy changes (#2461)\n\nApp owners could see deployed and published, but never whether a browser\nwould actually be served the app. The where-list reported the deployment''s\nreview state and kept disabled bindings, and the shell''s serving cache kept a\nsession''s escalated \"not found\" verdict after the version became servable\n(8 s observed, up to 300 s).\n\n- One serving gate (_serving_block_of) now feeds both the manifest scope walk\n  and the where-list, so what is served and what is shown cannot drift.\n- Where pins gain enabled, serving, reason and servesYou (additive; state\n  keeps its meaning).\n- App publish/disable/remove push apaevt_deploy like every other deployment\n  mutation, and broadcast_deploy_changed drops the app''s cached serving\n  verdicts (invalidate_app_serving), best-effort.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(appdev): show serving state and live updates in App Builder (#2461)\n\nApp Builder said \"live\" and \"serves vN\" for every pin and showed a green\n\"ready\" on a version whose build was still running, so owners deployed,\npublished, and still could not tell whether a browser would load the app.\n\n- servingStatus.ts phrases the server''s per-pin serving truth (serving /\n  reason / servesYou) once for both views, falling back to today''s wording\n  when an older server sends none of it.\n- Deploy tab: where-live rows read \"serving\" or why not (still building,\n  build failed, disabled, in review); version cards show the running build\n  word instead of the review badge; unserved audience chips are muted.\n- Dashboard: \"Right now @x serves vN\" only when it does, plus \"You''d get vN\n  through @x\"; the where card badge follows the same truth.\n- The VS Code host relays apaevt_deploy for an open app as a re-fetch\n  signal, so a landed build or anyone''s publish/disable updates every open\n  App Builder.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(apps): explain serving state on the App Builder Deploy tab (#2461)\n\nA deploy returns before the server build finishes, and a version serves\nonly once it does. Say so, and describe what the Deploy tab''s \"Where this\napp is live\" list and the dashboard now show: what a browser in each\naudience actually gets, or why not.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): resolve servesYou like the catalog and scope serving-cache drops to apps (#2461)\n\nReview fixes on the serving-state change:\n\n- servesYou now picks the scope-walk winner FIRST and only then applies\n  requiredPermissions, and only where serving enforces them (SaaS). OSS\n  never gates on requiredPermissions, so a gated app no longer reads \"You\n  wouldn''t get any published version\" while it serves; on SaaS a gated\n  winner hides the pin instead of falling back to a less specific one,\n  exactly as the catalog drops it.\n- Serving-cache invalidation moves out of broadcast_deploy_changed into a\n  new app-only broadcast_app_changed (build stamps, publish/disable/remove,\n  review transitions). A pipeline''s project id is user-chosen, so pipeline\n  deploy events must not drop another tenant''s app verdicts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxxPaCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA1WIQAHYD2szYQD3JJBqOW26Q/Yvi\ncaF9Q47cUgLgvv5O6WgFqGamOgatfovR8p0QySXEgyKFFqATPgODtnrghwzmQ9aC\nUHCHmjuXR4+mmkKrdUkyfXAzAXv3FJNrAbqRh6RIEvbgf1hWvogn85oB+zIM/Lka\n7NzsQ1WgWlutzIZi9HkbNRFUx14gUz10pf+c/4Gq6jwRy17cr9mbHh6x4aXdwoAq\n0aaer/NLeHC1pfRlsGnv3uxHWtRXUtFxZi3Oj7PZXQXD9ltZrw4hAi6SpcxX+WrN\n4n5QdezaELvy9uTpyBfatoMN7792PYOvdOylT/xJMRzSbW3225cu12h9INUsMMkj\nM6W3Quu6xH9Aw/0VTR5hTuDfCSayLOdblgaMYKW/hrQ816lO/DrdfRaWDPB4vcwK\nTI7zlpRg6Onf7uccfaH6WNDNFQbfY8ga+0vDGb7YpYpp4l41TOseLkiaTuCJzTVf\ng00ovGYRuHwDvmpxKC0oaQhB9j90DSoTFKVRcf6ZKLeFftkZO1tlM/PKntYnCxFO\nhSE/yQKdRkmyGG7mDkGiUcaBbmAPSP5x9LIZ53uXc3fTR74MtT5UyU/m9/WgxoDK\nM/237xZqwQrD7zszUJj2WgtJa8BS0AcV67r54AC2cTqA54Hy6DIS7QkV8hL99Uzu\ngiRhvieZ3EYrNUEKQpMb\n=ekOr\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-08T03:54:03Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/bdea1ed9a42ca74230bb7e3b41d0f8805973ed2e","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKGJkZWExZWQ5YTQyY2E3NDIzMGJiN2UzYjQxZDBmODgwNTk3M2VkMmU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/a7e2fec5af7414455ff8d0efdcaa1b5eb7416b02","sha":"a7e2fec5af7414455ff8d0efdcaa1b5eb7416b02","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/a7e2fec5af7414455ff8d0efdcaa1b5eb7416b02"}],"sha":"bdea1ed9a42ca74230bb7e3b41d0f8805973ed2e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/bdea1ed9a42ca74230bb7e3b41d0f8805973ed2e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/181051779?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/krishhgg","id":181051779,"login":"krishhgg","node_id":"U_kgDOCsqhgw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/a7e2fec5af7414455ff8d0efdcaa1b5eb7416b02/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T15:40:02Z","email":"kgarg@chapman.edu","name":"Krish Garg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T15:40:02Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(agent_rocketride): enforce the tool time limit; keep run state per run (#2521)\n\nW8. executor.py declared _TOOL_TIMEOUT_S = 120 \"per individual tool call\",\nbut nothing read it: one tool that never answered held the wave, and the\nturn, forever. Each call now has a time limit from when it starts: a new\nnode setting, tool_timeout, 300 s by default, 0 for no limit. (Not the 120 s\nof the old constant: develop never enforced any limit, and some tools are\nmeant to run longer. A node whose tools run past five minutes, such as a\ntool_python script given a long timeout (it allows up to 1,200 s) or\nanother agent called as a tool, raises the setting.) A call that runs longer is\nreported to the model as timed out and the wave goes on without\nit. Resolving the arguments and storing the result count as part of the\ncall: a memory write that hangs cannot hold the wave either, a result that\narrives after the limit is a timeout, and a tool whose arguments took the\nwhole time to resolve is never started. Python cannot stop a running\nthread, so the call finishes in the background; whether its result is\npublished or dropped is decided under a lock that is never held across a\ntool call or a memory write, so a call the wave gave up on is cleared from\nmemory if it got there and never reaches the duplicate tracking. Calls\nstill queued are reported as not run only when every worker is held by a\ncall that timed out, since only then can they never start, and a worker\nthat frees up in the meantime skips a call that was reported that way.\n\nA call the wave gave up on is still inside the engine''s invoke, and\nreturns into the pipeline''s state (the calling and the called node''s). The\nengine tears a pipeline down, or hands it to the next request, only after\nevery request in it has ended, and a call returning into a torn-down or\nreused pipeline would read freed state. So a run in which a call timed out\nwaits for every such call before it ends, without a limit, and clearing\nthe run''s memory waits for the store the same way. The model is not held:\nit is told the call timed out and goes on, and it reaches its answer. But\nthe answer is delivered when the run ends, so a tool that never returns\nholds the run and its answer, as it held the turn before this change. Ending such a run sooner, and safely, needs the engine to track\ninvokes in flight (issue #2511); this change does not touch the engine.\n\nA call that timed out can still change the work after a check has passed\n(a write that lands late). With a check tool configured, done=true is\nrefused while such a call is still running, and one that returns counts\nas a change, so the answer stands only after a passing check that came\nafter it. If it never returns, the run reaches the wave limit and answers\nfrom what it gathered instead of claiming checked work. The guard reads\n\"still running\" and \"returned since\" in one locked snapshot, so a call\nreturning between two reads cannot slip past both.\n\nCalls a step gave up on keep their threads, and each step builds a new\npool, so they count against the same eight-worker limit: once every worker\nis held by a call that timed out earlier in the run, further calls are\nreported as not run instead of starting more threads. Since a run ends only\nafter its calls return, they cannot pile up across runs either.\n\nW11. One driver object serves every run of the node, and runs overlap (one\npipe per concurrent request, all calling the same driver), yet each run\nkept its duplicate-result table on the driver: a second run replaced the\nfirst run''s table mid-flight, hiding its repeats. Per-run state now lives\nin a RunState created for each run and passed to the executor (result\nfingerprints, call fingerprints, removed keys, and the calls the run gave\nup on that are still running). Memory had\nthe same problem one level down: a memory node can serve a parent agent and\nthe child it calls as a tool, and both wrote wave-0.r0. Each run now sees\nthe store through RunMemory, which keeps its keys under a private prefix\nand clears them when the run ends; the model still sees short keys. A key\nremoved during the run is hidden at once and deleted at the end, so a\nremoval never waits on a write in progress. The final cleanup does wait\nfor the store (a write that lands after it is cleared too), and a key that\nwill not clear is logged rather than raised, so cleanup cannot replace the\nrun''s answer.\n\nW15. The executor has always substituted a whole-argument\n{{memory.ref:key}} tag before a tool runs, but the prompt only documented\ntags in the final answer, so the model never used it and paged large\nresults through its own context instead. The Memory instructions now\ndocument the tool-argument form with two examples. A whole-argument tag for\na key that is not stored (removed, mistyped, or held back from a call that\ntimed out) used to become None, and the tool ran with it: a write created an\nempty file and the model saw a normal result. It now fails the call before\nthe tool runs, and the model is told why.\n\nAlso fixes a one-space indent slip on the memory.peek bullet in the Memory\nblock, which becomes visible once prompt instructions keep their indentation.\n\nBeing stored and being readable are kept apart in the run''s memory. A\nresult is hidden while the wave decides whether to keep it, so a result\nthe wave gave up on is never read, even if the store wrote it before it\nhung (a later {{memory.ref}} to it finds nothing). A key the model removes\nis hidden at once and deleted from the store when the run ends: deleting it\nduring the run could wait behind a write that hangs, in a store that takes\none call at a time, and stop the planning. Keys are trimmed the way the store trims\nthem, so \"wave-0.r0 \" cannot read what is hidden under \"wave-0.r0\". If a\nwave itself fails (a worker thread that cannot start, say), it cancels the\ncalls that have not started and waits for the rest before it raises, so no\ncall it started is still inside the engine when the run ends.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","tree":{"sha":"05323c32d8849e194a4c87c40a1caf06f2b97e5c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/05323c32d8849e194a4c87c40a1caf06f2b97e5c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/a7e2fec5af7414455