n, or hands it to the next request, only after\nevery request in it has ended, and a call returning into a torn-down or\nreused pipeline would read freed state. So a run in which a call timed out\nwaits for every such call before it ends, without a limit, and clearing\nthe run''s memory waits for the store the same way. The model is not held:\nit is told the call timed out and goes on, and it reaches its answer. But\nthe answer is delivered when the run ends, so a tool that never returns\nholds the run and its answer, as it held the turn before this change. Ending such a run sooner, and safely, needs the engine to track\ninvokes in flight (issue #2511); this change does not touch the engine.\n\nA call that timed out can still change the work after a check has passed\n(a write that lands late). With a check tool configured, done=true is\nrefused while such a call is still running, and one that returns counts\nas a change, so the answer stands only after a passing check that came\nafter it. If it never returns, the run reaches the wave limit and answers\nfrom what it gathered instead of claiming checked work. The guard reads\n\"still running\" and \"returned since\" in one locked snapshot, so a call\nreturning between two reads cannot slip past both.\n\nCalls a step gave up on keep their threads, and each step builds a new\npool, so they count against the same eight-worker limit: once every worker\nis held by a call that timed out earlier in the run, further calls are\nreported as not run instead of starting more threads. Since a run ends only\nafter its calls return, they cannot pile up across runs either.\n\nW11. One driver object serves every run of the node, and runs overlap (one\npipe per concurrent request, all calling the same driver), yet each run\nkept its duplicate-result table on the driver: a second run replaced the\nfirst run''s table mid-flight, hiding its repeats. Per-run state now lives\nin a RunState created for each run and passed to the executor (result\nfingerprints, call fingerprints, removed keys, and the calls the run gave\nup on that are still running). Memory had\nthe same problem one level down: a memory node can serve a parent agent and\nthe child it calls as a tool, and both wrote wave-0.r0. Each run now sees\nthe store through RunMemory, which keeps its keys under a private prefix\nand clears them when the run ends; the model still sees short keys. A key\nremoved during the run is hidden at once and deleted at the end, so a\nremoval never waits on a write in progress. The final cleanup does wait\nfor the store (a write that lands after it is cleared too), and a key that\nwill not clear is logged rather than raised, so cleanup cannot replace the\nrun''s answer.\n\nW15. The executor has always substituted a whole-argument\n{{memory.ref:key}} tag before a tool runs, but the prompt only documented\ntags in the final answer, so the model never used it and paged large\nresults through its own context instead. The Memory instructions now\ndocument the tool-argument form with two examples. A whole-argument tag for\na key that is not stored (removed, mistyped, or held back from a call that\ntimed out) used to become None, and the tool ran with it: a write created an\nempty file and the model saw a normal result. It now fails the call before\nthe tool runs, and the model is told why.\n\nAlso fixes a one-space indent slip on the memory.peek bullet in the Memory\nblock, which becomes visible once prompt instructions keep their indentation.\n\nBeing stored and being readable are kept apart in the run''s memory. A\nresult is hidden while the wave decides whether to keep it, so a result\nthe wave gave up on is never read, even if the store wrote it before it\nhung (a later {{memory.ref}} to it finds nothing). A key the model removes\nis hidden at once and deleted from the store when the run ends: deleting it\nduring the run could wait behind a write that hangs, in a store that takes\none call at a time, and stop the planning. Keys are trimmed the way the store trims\nthem, so \"wave-0.r0 \" cannot read what is hidden under \"wave-0.r0\". If a\nwave itself fails (a worker thread that cannot start, say), it cancels the\ncalls that have not started and waits for the rest before it raises, so no\ncall it started is still inside the engine when the run ends.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxmfSCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAlsEQAFe7uCrFmQ6PaBnelEIWjqMa\nVr8xFTSxKFRipq9OEglqXzxxGKVlztJBpIhpnCPUd8FnU6pFn9M0lCDlkmgGga1u\nrQBmCaxWXwvLKsi94lXoRTOlTZ18Jgz2ZUkqD2trcqNF68EqKZ7RDqp9A78B6kua\nEw9l6/+pBtfqeMPJBHBer1flx0k69OMJSu37akGjnFVNU6uFsvo8TCP2YhYowqyl\nVOW/cVbXBwBlMonrW/xWg3ZtkePzKQxXwqVCBzyqLWxLWN6h0vbIwDIU1B2Kj2ve\nSoyW50PwUt3ljBSHBPlApkBv/szVbSApD7E5u2clX/xjJxKhGGcGZFYd8S7uwRKu\n1E1tOOWsyA8+O3E4TUjCm14D789zEhavOtz9xvplCQtAF4u67D0B6V8WdF1nJFkB\ndEAled6yTJA8vQ2X3iEPds7XLXk5qtxT6UJt02kWnwc3Y6h9dR9EyoFPGuuKoAiy\ntsdy/+O94g3ruKYg17SJeIfE4mfM7bkjXGxOcyxahwt0ZeYUea6Q877HJjEw6TN9\naiLrH3mSdW5BfXZ2r6OSNFQk50shHQqGjTbLr/InXpjYDeGMMhz5lqAavnQf7o+R\nA9zs5ZaIQfSmDfIMsreoEbu5a3LxBc743OAXBs1ntSsjReY5Oq+Ike1KnSfH73C/\nRAXlyrUDhoGbeZO2/sCU\n=DEyw\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-07T15:40:19Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repo