9J78KCKAo\nvKII6Yp43bLz596XHybz\n=Hl/Q\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-07T13:51:17Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/a59aa16a91dc3cb376a6f6ecde35b6e9725e304e","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKGE1OWFhMTZhOTFkYzNjYjM3NmE2ZjZlY2RlMzViNmU5NzI1ZTMwNGU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/dd5618f16f193e9fd7943614f4eeb54a9ac16c56","sha":"dd5618f16f193e9fd7943614f4eeb54a9ac16c56","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/dd5618f16f193e9fd7943614f4eeb54a9ac16c56"}],"sha":"a59aa16a91dc3cb376a6f6ecde35b6e9725e304e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/a59aa16a91dc3cb376a6f6ecde35b6e9725e304e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/181051779?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/krishhgg","id":181051779,"login":"krishhgg","node_id":"U_kgDOCsqhgw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/dd5618f16f193e9fd7943614f4eeb54a9ac16c56/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T00:14:38Z","email":"kgarg@chapman.edu","name":"Krish Garg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T00:14:38Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(agent_rocketride): show the model its own calls; add an enforced check tool (#2520)\n\nW12. The planning prompt showed each result but not the call that produced\nit: not the code the model ran, not the content it wrote. It could not tell\na retry from a repeat, and a rule told it \"a plan unchanged from the\nprevious response means you are not making progress\" about a plan it was\nnever shown. Each result now carries a short form of the call''s arguments\n(as the model wrote them; their texts share a 600-character budget,\na judgment call rather than a measured figure, wherever they sit down to\nsix levels of nesting, records in a list included: texts that fit are shown whole, longer ones keep\ntheir start and end, at least 80 characters each, a plain list shows its\nfirst three items, and only a preview over 1,200 characters in all is cut), and a call identical to an earlier one\nin the run gets a note naming it. The note never blocks: a\nre-read after a write is legitimate. Failed calls store no result, so they\nwere invisible to the result-level duplicate check; the call-level note\ncovers them. Call tracking also outlives \"remove\": in live runs a model\nread two spec files, removed them, created directories, and read them\nagain, round after round, until the wave limit. Re-running a call whose\nresult was removed now says so, and suggests keeping needed data in\nscratch before removing a result. The call history is kept per run and\npassed to the executor: the driver serves every run of the node, and runs\noverlap.\n\nW13. Wave had no way to check its work, its rules said \"you do not need to\nverify\", and every prompt ended with \"Plan the next set of tool calls\",\nwhich pushes against finishing. A new optional node setting, verify_tool,\nnames a connected tool that checks the work (e.g. workspace.compile). When\nset, the trust rule is replaced by one that says to run the check after the\nlast change and fix what it reports, and the loop enforces it: done=true is\nsent back until the check has passed in a later step than the last call to\nany other tool, and a check that fails cancels any earlier pass. A check\npasses when it does not raise and its result does not report a failure in\nany of the ways the loop recognizes for every tool (see the shape check\nPR: ok or success false, isError, an error message, an HTTP error status,\na failed child agent (its stack, or a meta.stop_reason of error), a code runner''s non-zero exit_code or timed_out, a\nscript''s own failing result, or a list of errors). Any other call counts\nas a change even if storing its result failed, since the change may have\nlanded. The check is recognized by its tool name, so a second optional\nsetting, verify_args, fixes its exact arguments (a JSON object) for a check\ntool that can also change things, such as a command runner: then only that\nexact call checks the work, any other call to the tool counts as a change,\nand the prompt names the call. A verify_args that is not a JSON object\nstops the node at startup. A repeated check is never called a repeat: the\nrule requires it after every change. Only the check itself is exempt: with\nverify_args set, other calls to the same tool get repeat notes like any\ncall. A verify_tool that names no connected tool stops the run\nat its start with an error listing the connected tools, after the host has\nlooked the name up again in case its server has grown the tool since\ndiscovery: no check could\npass, so every run would otherwise use up its waves without saying why. Both settings are on the node''s settings panel (\"shape\"). The rule covers changes: a run that only calls the check\n(asked whether the project compiles, say) may report a failing result. A\ncheck sent in the same reply as done=true must be read first, and a reply\nthat asked to finish but did not (a call failed or could not be read, or\nthe rule sent it back) keeps its remove list unapplied, and the result\nfingerprints of those keys with it, so the model always gets to read what\nits calls returned; normalize_plan records the request as\nasked_done, since it clears done when it drops a call. Without verify_tool\nthe rule text is unchanged. The closing line now names both replies: \"Plan the next set of tool calls, or\nset done=true if the goal is already met.\" The README''s generated table\ngains the verify_tool and verify_args rows (regenerated with\nnodes/scripts/gen-node-tables.mjs).\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","tree":{"sha":"cb6fbe401f7c19c15f2c6c194cb52cc2b99b09fa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/cb6fbe401f7c19c15f2c6c194cb52cc2b99b09fa"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/dd5618f16f193e9fd7943614f4eeb54a9ac16c56","verification":{"payload":"tree cb6fbe401f7c19c15f2c6c194cb52cc2b99b09fa\nparent db789bd4d0eea3cd70eb4e8f51ed6efdbcce7eb0\nauthor Krish Garg <kgarg@chapman.edu> 1791332078 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791332078 +0000\n\nfix(agent_rocketride): show the model its own calls; add an enforced check tool (#2520)\n\nW12. The planning prompt showed each result but not the call that produced\nit: not the code the model ran, not the content it wrote. It could not tell\na retry from a repeat, and a rule told it \"a plan unchanged from the\nprevious response means you are not making progress\" about a plan it was\nnever shown. Each result now carries a short form of the call''s arguments\n(as the model wrote them; their texts share a 600-character budget,\na judgment call rather than a measured figure, wherever they sit down to\nsix levels of nesting, records in a list included: texts that fit are shown whole, longer ones keep\ntheir start and end, at least 80 characters each, a plain list shows its\nfirst three items, and only a preview over 1,200 characters in all is cut), and a call identical to an earlier one\nin the run gets a note naming it. The note never blocks: a\nre-read after a write is legitimate. Failed calls store no result, so they\nwere invisible to the result-level duplicate check; the call-level note\ncovers them. Call tracking also outlives \"remove\": in live runs a model\nread two spec files, removed them, created directories, and read them\nagain, round after round, until the wave limit. Re-running a call whose\nresult was removed now says so, and suggests keeping needed data in\nscratch before removing a result. The call history is kept per run and\npassed to the executor: the driver serves every run of the node, and runs\noverlap.\n\nW13. Wave had no way to check its work, its rules said \"you do not need to\nverify\", and every prompt ended with \"Plan the next set of tool calls\",\nwhich pushes against finishing. A new optional node setting, verify_tool,\nnames a connected tool that checks the work (e.g. workspace.compile). When\nset, the trust rule is replaced by one that says to run the check after the\nlast change and fix what it reports, and the loop enforces it: done=true is\nsent back until the check has passed in a later step than the last call to\nany other tool, and a check that fails cancels any earlier pass. A check\npasses when it does not raise and its result does not report a failure in\nany of the ways the loop recognizes for every tool (see the shape check\nPR: ok or success false, isError, an error message, an HTTP error status,\na failed child agent (its stack, or a meta.stop_reason of error), a code runner''s non-zero exit_code or timed_out, a\nscript''s own failing result, or a list of errors). Any other call counts\nas a change even if storing its result failed, since the change may have\nlanded. The check is recognized by its tool name, so a second optional\nsetting, verify_args, fixes its exact arguments (a JSON object) for a check\ntool that can also change things, such as a command runner: then only that\nexact call checks the work, any other call to the tool counts as a change,\nand the prompt names the call. A verify_args that is not a JSON object\nstops the node at startup. A repeated check is never called a repeat: the\nrule requires it after every change. Only the check itself is exempt: with\nverify_args set, other calls to the same tool get repeat notes like any\ncall. A verify_tool that names no connected tool stops the run\nat its start with an error listing the connected tools, after the host has\nlooked the name up again in case its server has grown the tool since\ndiscovery: no check could\npass, so every run would otherwise use up its waves without saying why. Both settings are on the node''s settings panel (\"shape\"). The rule covers changes: a run that only calls the check\n(asked whether the project compiles, say) may report a failing result. A\ncheck sent in the same reply as done=true must be read first, and a reply\nthat asked to finish but did not (a call failed or could not be read, or\nthe rule sent it back) keeps its remove list unapplied, and the result\nfingerprints of those keys with it, so the model always gets to read what\nits calls returned; normalize_plan records the request as\nasked_done, since it clears done when it drops a call. Without verify_tool\nthe rule text is unchanged. The closing line now names both replies: \"Plan the next set of tool calls, or\nset done=true if the goal is already met.\" The README''s generated table\ngains the verify_tool and verify_args rows (regenerated with\nnodes/scripts/gen-node-tables.mjs).\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxY7uCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAaxsQAJQ734QHIvTVJMl7gugfSi9J\nrA8mC+nXO3mAz/CJYVCR0o8MIzBPCZa3x4+BrPIas2C3JHIvKIMW/R0IeHRN+Qh4\nMC0pF9dhETD7nEThSCYhkWv5Ea0qUJtqY+759qCRAP5Dey/dQsEEAgHt1bgFYTg7\nHtH+GhMHsPlv4yLYDdoQaxB+UgOENue/x1JcZg3uaGJUoYIPdoeGlalZFvTwXxPQ\n6XS4FA/CD5vaRUkV5SD2I3qAGwMnxFA1LmlcJKGaRV8O8J2k2TlVkbNKl/uBaClu\n+IfkBv89JR4lKdSElF2p81AwgX8dUUdJ92VaNWp32rHjGFK9mK2dsRLtY5ytsjkQ\nxUtCuHWQxkM5inasEMHa8WeGdXMl/a4OS7oWFIQi81CArJLid8aDuaYxKzC+fZ8R\nKzpHnaKz6UPp/tfIVffr/BkidOeAMgAGYeS/EsYrIkFZiYwmV/roHyQWgtEp/rlX\nEO3AR3R66jEN1n00Au7QQaNFyb1Ebiwlgj1UKeBmdCCbbwbTeaboEMhIXRL7uQ2M\n5PiLL1ka8NnJXp63RovK4a/49fkPa4zVwgavLyh/47X3AyV09vqzKvussTe46ovo\neF0Mxh09ojXZYNtVQ5uEYBWMpy6w0AJZdHRRhQPNpZjhdbyZmHr9KLWMPziIsLnp\ns1uwNagnnxF6zncO35dD\n=Ic5G\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-07T00:14:38Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/dd5618f16f193e9fd7943614f4eeb54a9ac16c56","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKGRkNTYxOGYxNmYxOTNlOWZkNzk0MzYxNGY0ZWViNTRhOWFjMTZjNTY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/db789bd4d0eea3cd70eb4e8f51ed6efdbcce7eb0","sha":"db789bd4d0eea3cd70eb4e8f51ed6efdbcce7eb0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/db789bd4d0eea3cd70eb4e8f51ed6efdbcce7eb0"}],"sha":"dd5618f16f193e9fd7943614f4eeb54a9ac16c56","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/dd5618f16f193e9fd7943614f4eeb54a9ac16c56"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/181051779?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/krishhgg","id":181051779,"login":"krishhgg","node_id":"U_kgDOCsqhgw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/db789bd4d0eea3cd70eb4e8f51ed6efdbcce7eb0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T23:24:20Z","email":"kgarg@chapman.edu","name":"Krish Garg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T23:24:20Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(agent_rocketride): never lose gathered work; record why a run stopped (#2519)\n\nW3. When planning failed (unreadable replies, a provider error), the run\nreturned \"LLM error: ...\" and dropped everything it had gathered. If any\nwork was gathered, the run now answers from it through the synthesis\nfallback. If the fallback cannot reach the model either (a persistent\nprovider error) or gets an empty reply, the answer is a plain report: the\ncause (a failed call, or an empty reply), the scratch notes and each gathered result, whole (each is already\nbounded by the summary budget or the peek cap). With nothing gathered, the\nerror is still the answer, since its cause (a bad key, a rate limit) is the\nuseful part.\n\nW5. Keys in a reply''s \"remove\" list were cleared before the same reply''s\nanswer was built, so an answer that removed and referenced a key rendered\na blank. A reply''s keys are now cleared after its answer is built (when\nit finishes) or after its calls run (when the loop goes on), so neither\nthe answer nor a call in the same reply loses a key it uses. Their\nduplicate-result fingerprints are dropped before the calls run, so no\nnote can point at a key that is about to disappear. A blank key is never\nsent to memory: the memory node reads a clear with no key as \"clear\neverything\", which would now also wipe the results the reply just fetched. Only\nresults from earlier waves can be removed, so a reply cannot clear the\nresult its own call is about to get. A tag for a key that\ndoes not exist renders as \"[missing data: <key>]\" instead of nothing; a key\nthat holds null renders as null; an answer that is a single tag is always\nreturned as text (JSON, or plain text for data JSON cannot hold).\n\nW6. The fallback answer at the wave limit saw only result summaries, so a\nconclusion the model had written in its scratch notes was lost, and\nmemory.peek results showed up empty. The fallback prompt now includes the\nscratch notes and each peek with its path and completeness (\"showing 50 of\n80 items\", a character range, or where a long preview was cut), and asks\nthe model to say what is missing rather than guess. Notes from a reply that\ncould not be used are kept too: the planner''s retry is asked only to fix\nits shape, so the first reply''s notes stand unless the retry writes new\nones, and if the retry call raises, the notes go with the error so the\nrun can still answer from them.\n\nW7. A forced fallback answer looked exactly like a finished one. The trace\nnow records stop_reason (done, max_waves, empty_plan or error) and the\nlast status event carries it on every exit, so a caller can warn the user.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","tree":{"sha":"320d884ffd38b8ad4cdb91eec38f84672dd1acbc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/320d884ffd38b8ad4cdb91eec38f84672dd1acbc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/db789bd4d0eea3cd70eb4e8f51ed6efdbcce7eb0","verification":{"payload":"tree 320d884ffd38b8ad4cdb91eec38f84672dd1acbc\nparent 9024586e44dc27e0fa08ff56267a62ff47d30bd3\nauthor Krish Garg <kgarg@chapman.edu> 1791329060 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791329060 -0700\n\nfix(agent_rocketride): never lose gathered work; record why a run stopped (#2519)\n\nW3. When planning failed (unreadable replies, a provider error), the run\nreturned \"LLM error: ...\" and dropped everything it had gathered. If any\nwork was gathered, the run now answers from it through the synthesis\nfallback. If the fallback cannot reach the model either (a persistent\nprovider error) or gets an empty reply, the answer is a plain report: the\ncause (a failed call, or an empty reply), the scratch notes and each gathered result, whole (each is already\nbounded by the summary budget or the peek cap). With nothing gathered, the\nerror is still the answer, since its cause (a bad key, a rate limit) is the\nuseful part.\n\nW5. Keys in a reply''s \"remove\" list were cleared before the same reply''s\nanswer was built, so an answer that removed and referenced a key rendered\na blank. A reply''s keys are now cleared after its answer is built (when\nit finishes) or after its calls run (when the loop goes on), so neither\nthe answer nor a call in the same reply loses a key it uses. Their\nduplicate-result fingerprints are dropped before the calls run, so no\nnote can point at a key that is about to disappear. A blank key is never\nsent to memory: the memory node reads a clear with no key as \"clear\neverything\", which would now also wipe the results the reply just fetched. Only\nresults from earlier waves can be removed, so a reply cannot clear the\nresult its own call is about to get. A tag for a key that\ndoes not exist renders as \"[missing data: <key>]\" instead of nothing; a key\nthat holds null renders as null; an answer that is a single tag is always\nreturned as text (JSON, or plain text for data JSON cannot hold).\n\nW6. The fallback answer at the wave limit saw only result summaries, so a\nconclusion the model had written in its scratch notes was lost, and\nmemory.peek results showed up empty. The fallback prompt now includes the\nscratch notes and each peek with its path and completeness (\"showing 50 of\n80 items\", a character range, or where a long preview was cut), and asks\nthe model to say what is missing rather than guess. Notes from a reply that\ncould not be used are kept too: the planner''s retry is asked only to fix\nits shape, so the first reply''s notes stand unless the retry writes new\nones, and if the retry call raises, the notes go with the error so the\nrun can still answer from them.\n\nW7. A forced fallback answer looked exactly like a finished one. The trace\nnow records stop_reason (done, max_waves, empty_plan or error) and the\nlast status event carries it on every exit, so a caller can warn the user.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxYMkCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAyC0QAH5p8QzSUxEwtWDYfCop4FrF\nwWO7Mt59lmfG7Rk/hie51PFK/Sc5+/9JRrOM8KuA6c5+XizzG8QKLJd/eKtgP8yH\nQ43xI9+NegsGC5Me8nyptmEwzjLVixcfMmvXPmi+FDtr5R3vVNc8l533V47AsGlp\n+Kl+ErAcM1UtEW7IedL722AZKTxoGmpKm5zrxACWtqZfXd/zpySyLsCcyQFzB1tz\nCFFcZHlgFA1jsfFEc1YGvIHpVr5ahUBY33DcM8TMLkqYdKKzWqGRiBmgPSB7+ohD\naYedpkjChCuGxFAraHZNoRkCZSLaTz6QCjC1mSn6z+ou+9x2O5vgZqszR16Yk067\nYzvlsElYuhO3kotbB72lxzy+AmWhSv2AOag0k9Rsi37NL1cfQWk9a79oR34qzZ7t\nKmGpJHY9taDeXR6oYUW3OIya0JGeu3u0nOF6TuPrmf/MR6Y+5SsZFl5/g/PzTvHB\nek14x9IPo7VCgirVYKhrHWY3BwFs9mtCELuEsqP0p6bvScnve38odYiy0bhgE5lU\nui1hPzrebruFvZh3ImIe3jCmqDCeP4dop2czWETrxrzmTtpZyD4kAyaNVFuScXlf\nez3uj9botsTCKmN30JY6zWTrqmG1rwGFgv0zen6Q0gLaNvvjdPmYHbOOqMvJaQ5s\n/TZ4GBgPCjXc5TQZnDn7\n=0BRN\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T23:24:21Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/db789bd4d0eea3cd70eb4e8f51ed6efdbcce7eb0","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKGRiNzg5YmQ0ZDBlZWEzY2Q3MGViNGU4ZjUxZWQ2ZWZkYmNjZTdlYjA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/9024586e44dc27e0fa08ff56267a62ff47d30bd3","sha":"9024586e44dc27e0fa08ff56267a62ff47d30bd3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/9024586e44dc27e0fa08ff56267a62ff47d30bd3"}],"sha":"db789bd4d0eea3cd70eb4e8f51ed6efdbcce7eb0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/db789bd4d0eea3cd70eb4e8f51ed6efdbcce7eb0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55141824?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mithileshgau","id":55141824,"login":"mithileshgau","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MTQxODI0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mithileshgau","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/9024586e44dc27e0fa08ff56267a62ff47d30bd3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T22:39:14Z","email":"mithileshgaurihar@gmail.com","name":"mithileshgau"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T22:39:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(nodes): add Discord bot source node (discord) (#1503)\n\n* feat(nodes): add Discord bot source node (discord)\n\nAdd a Discord bot source node (discord://, classType [\"source\"]) that\nconnects a Discord bot to a pipeline over the Discord Gateway — the Discord\ncounterpart to the telegram node. Routes incoming text and image/audio/\nvideo/document attachments to the text/image/audio/video/tags lanes, and\nposts the pipeline''s first answer back to the originating channel, message,\nor thread.\n\nMirrors the Telegram endpoint architecture: scanObjects() -> WebServer\n(kept alive via self._server.run()) with the discord.py Gateway client\nlaunched as a background task from the async on_startup hook.\n\nConfig: botToken (secure), guildIds, channelIds, ignoreBots,\nrequireMention, replyMode (channel|reply|thread), showTyping,\nmaxAttachmentBytes, sendResponses. Includes 2000-char reply chunking,\nrate-limit backoff, bot-loop prevention, and mention gating.\n\nTests: 21 unit tests plus an env-gated live-gateway integration suite\n(skips unless DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN is set).\n\nCloses #1502\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): drop Discord live-gateway integration test, fix CI encoding\n\n- Remove nodes/test/discord/test_integration.py: it imports the discord.py\n  library at module top-level, which requires the (uninstalled-in-CI)\n  dependency and a live DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN, and the package name collides\n  with the node''s own `discord` package during collection. It cannot run in\n  CI. The pure-logic unit tests in test_discord.py remain.\n- Open services.json with encoding=''utf-8'' in the schema tests so they pass\n  under CI''s ASCII locale.\n- Replace the non-ASCII em dash in services.json with a hyphen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): address Discord node review feedback\n\n- Extract pure helpers (chunk_message, guess_media_type) into text_utils.py\n  with no discord.py dependency, so unit tests exercise the real production\n  code instead of reimplementing it, and can run in CI without discord.py.\n- chunk_message now hard-splits any single token/sentence longer than the\n  2000-char limit, so every emitted chunk is guaranteed to fit (previously an\n  unbroken long token could produce an oversized chunk Discord would reject).\n- Stop processing further attachments once a non-empty answer is found.\n- Handle discord.RateLimited (which carries retry_after) instead of reading a\n  nonexistent retry_after off discord.HTTPException.\n- Declare _guild_ids/_channel_ids as annotations only to avoid mutable list\n  defaults shared at class scope; they are set per-instance in _run().\n- requirements.txt: keep only discord.py (requests/fastapi/aiohttp are not\n  imported directly and flagged unpinned/vulnerable by OSV).\n- README: use the UI-facing lane name \"Data\" for the document lane.\n- Rewrite test_discord.py to test the real helpers and services.json schema;\n  drop tautological config/filtering tests.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): address second Discord review round\n\n- Process attachments independently of the text pipeline so captioned uploads\n  are ingested; the first non-empty answer (text first, then attachments in\n  order, stopping at the first that answers) becomes the reply.\n- _send_response now abandons the remaining chunks on an unrecoverable send\n  failure instead of silently continuing and dropping part of the reply.\n- Extract the message gating (self/bot/guild/channel/mention) into a pure\n  should_process_message() predicate in text_utils.py and unit-test it;\n  _on_message just wires primitives and schedules the task.\n- README: correct the attachment note to describe in-order processing that\n  stops at the first attachment answer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): correct Discord MIME-detection precedence in README\n\nguess_media_type() prefers Discord''s reported content_type (parameters\nstripped) and only falls back to the file extension; the README had the\norder reversed. Fix the description to match, keeping the octet-stream default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): address Discord review — ingest all attachments, correct docs\n\n- Remove the early `break` in the attachment loop so a source node ingests\n  every attachment in a multi-attachment message (up to 10); still reply with\n  only the first non-empty answer. The break silently dropped later\n  attachments (no download, routing, or monitorCompleted).\n- Revert README lane name `Data` -> `tags` to match services.json and the\n  telegram/webhook/core convention (no `Data` lane exists).\n- Clarify that discord.py handles 429s internally; the RateLimited branch is\n  defensive (only fires with max_ratelimit_timeout, which is unset).\n- Regenerate stale GENERATED:PARAMS em-dash back to the services.json hyphen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): Discord node — use shared WebServer + correctness fixes from deep review\n\nCritical:\n- _run no longer constructs its own WebServer bound to --data_port. node.py\n  already binds a shared WebServer on that port for every subprocess, so the\n  second bind hit EADDRINUSE (and wired the target onto the wrong server) — the\n  node could not start under EaaS. Now mirrors the Telegram node: register on\n  the shared server and drive _startup/_shutdown on the shared server_loop,\n  blocking scanObjects() on a shutdown event.\n\nCorrectness:\n- _run_with_optional_typing now runs the pipeline exactly once; a typing-\n  indicator enter/exit failure no longer re-executes it (double ingestion).\n- require_mention no longer satisfied by @everyone/@here (use direct\n  message.mentions, not mentioned_in).\n- guildIds/channelIds coerced via _as_str_list so a scalar string no longer\n  becomes a per-character allowlist; non-dict ''discord'' config block no longer\n  crashes _run.\n- guess_media_type lowercases the reported content type (mixed-case IMAGE/PNG\n  routed correctly) and falls back to the extension when it normalizes empty.\n\nDocs:\n- Attachments download via discord.py''s Attachment.read(), not aiohttp directly\n  (aiohttp is transitive); drop the misleading \"concurrently\" wording.\n\nTests:\n- New test_process_message.py (import-guarded, py3.10+/discord.py): attachment\n  ingest-all + first-answer, exactly-once typing, allowlist coercion, and the\n  @everyone gating fix. New MIME normalization cases in test_discord.py.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): list Discord node in nodes catalog and CHANGELOG\n\n- Add a `discord` row to the Sources table in docs/README-nodes.md.\n- Add a Discord node line under [Unreleased] / Added in CHANGELOG.md.\n\nAddresses review feedback that both shared surfaces were missing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): Discord lifecycle failure propagation, mention safety, and develop conformance\n\nAddresses kgarg2468''s review:\n\nLifecycle: terminal Gateway failures now fail the source promptly instead of\nidling forever with a dead bot. A missing token raises from _startup; login\nfailure, missing Message Content Intent, an unexpected disconnect, or a generic\ngateway error record a fatal error and unblock _run, which re-raises so the\nengine marks the source failed. Successful startup still blocks until controlled\nshutdown; an intentional close during _shutdown is not treated as a failure.\n\nSecurity: all outbound sends (reply, thread, channel) pass\ndiscord.AllowedMentions.none() so model-generated text can never ping users,\nroles, @here or @everyone.\n\nTests: the behavioral suite now stubs discord in sys.modules and loads the node\nas a synthetic package, so it runs deterministically in clean CI without\ndiscord.py installed and without a version skip (node annotations switched to\nOptional[...] so it imports on 3.10-). Added lifecycle tests (missing token,\nlogin failure, privileged-intent failure, unexpected close, cancellation,\nshutdown-marks-closing), allowed-mentions tests for all three reply modes plus\nthread reuse, and an oversized-attachment regression. Retained the\nmulti-attachment ingest-all regression.\n\nDevelop conformance:\n- services.json: add \"documentation\" URL.\n- README.md: restructure to the current node README schema (top-level Lanes\n  table, drop the hand-written field table, fold sections under Notes); passes\n  scripts/validate-node-readme.py.\n- credentials.json: register the discord credential (nodes:credentials-generate)\n  so nodes:credentials-check passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): Discord thread reply mode handles DMs and existing threads\n\nAddresses CodeRabbit re-review:\n\n- _send_chunk thread mode no longer calls message.create_thread()\n  unconditionally: it posts into message.channel when the message is already\n  inside a thread, creates a thread only for TextChannel messages, and falls\n  back to a plain reply (mentions suppressed) in DMs and other non-threadable\n  channels — previously these raised and dropped the response.\n- README: document that thread reply mode also needs Create Public Threads and\n  Send Messages in Threads, and that it falls back to a reply where a thread\n  cannot be created.\n- Tests: add thread-in-TextChannel, reuse-existing-thread, post-into-own-thread,\n  and DM-fallback cases (58 tests pass on 3.9 and 3.11).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(nodes): discord node support-bot parity features\n\nBrings the discord source node to parity with the discord.js support bot it\nreplaces. Every new setting is opt-in; with defaults the node behaves as it\ndid before.\n\n- Replies: thread name template, length cap and archive duration; optional\n  (i/n) chunk labels; allowlisted user/role mentions (never @everyone/@here);\n  a failed thread creation falls back to a reply instead of losing it.\n- Context: earlier thread messages as context (threadHistoryLimit).\n- Escalation: pause a thread after an escalating reply, rebuild the pause from\n  thread history after a restart, resume on an @mention; a team-name alias is\n  turned into the team role mention; per-thread serialization while the pause\n  is on.\n- Aimed at someone else: optional ack emoji, and the thread pauses.\n- Feedback: optional reactions on the last chunk of an answer.\n- Reply hygiene: strip leaked agent scratchpad and final-answer envelopes,\n  never post engine or model errors, retry empty or scratchpad answers.\n- Attachments: optional text-file decoding and one merged answer per message.\n- Gating: allowed bot ids, mention-only channels, system and empty messages\n  dropped.\n- Events: reaction, no_reply and outbound events with the message metadata,\n  broadcast as apaevt_sse; reactions are scoped by the same guild, channel and\n  bot gates as messages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(nodes): durable event capture for the discord node\n\nWith captureEvents on, the node writes every event it broadcasts (message,\nplus outbound, no_reply and reaction when their emit options are on) into a\ndatabase tool node wired to the source by a control edge, so a support\ndashboard has a complete log even when nobody is watching the live stream.\n\n- One row per event in a fixed INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING through the\n  database node''s execute tool, keyed (message_id, event_type, event_key), so\n  a redelivered Gateway event is ignored.\n- Off the answering path: a bounded queue and one writer thread; a slow or\n  dead database costs a logged, rate-limited warning, never a Discord reply.\n- captureNodeId picks the database component, captureTable the table\n  (validated, created if missing), captureSource the label written to each\n  row''s source column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): cover the discord node parity features and capture\n\nUnit tests for every new behaviour and its default: thread replies and\nfallbacks, thread-history context, escalation pause and history rebuild,\nper-thread serialization, aimed-elsewhere acks, feedback reactions, reply\nhygiene and retries, error suppression, team mention alias, chunk labels,\nattachment merge, reaction scoping, event payloads, config coercion, and the\ncapture writer (row shape, SQL, failures, queue, thread lifecycle). The\nservices.json schema tests assert every field, its UI registration and its\ndefault.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): document the discord node''s new settings\n\nDescribes each parity feature and the capture log in the node README, and\nregenerates the PARAMS schema table with nodes:docs-generate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): add the gated live Discord harness and test notes\n\nA live suite for the discord node against a real Discord server and engine,\nskipped unless DISCORD_LIVE=1 (ids and the token are read from files outside\nthe repo). The README records each live round: what was run, what passed and\nthe bugs it found.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): read discord live-harness files as UTF-8\n\nCollection of the live suite opened its seed and config files with the\nlocale''s encoding, which fails under an ASCII locale before the DISCORD_LIVE\ngate can skip anything. Every read and write now names UTF-8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: note the discord node''s support-bot features in the changelog\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): never post an engine LLM error as a discord reply\n\nWith sanitizeReplies on, a provider failure still reached Discord: the\nengine''s LLM layer turns the exception into the answer text\n\"**LLM error** - ValueError: An error occurred with the API.\", and no\nerror signature matched it (the API pattern needs a word before \"API\").\n\nTwo signatures now catch it: the \"**LLM error**\" prefix at the start of\nthe answer, and the bare \"An error occurred with the API.\" sentence\nwhen it is the whole answer. Prose that only mentions API errors is\nnot matched. The existing path then emits no_reply model_error.\n\nFound by the live engine suite (F40); verified live after the fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): split fenced discord replies between lines\n\nInside a code fence the chunker hard-split at a character count, so\nevery boundary cut a code line in two and a copied block was broken\n(live F12 and F44: 2 of 2 and 10 of 10 boundaries).\n\nIt now breaks at the last newline in the second half of the window.\nThe synthetic close/reopen fence pair stands in for that newline, so\nthe reply rebuilds exactly by replacing each boundary pair with \"\\n\".\nA newline right before a fence is passed over, so a boundary never\nproduces an empty code block, and a single line longer than a chunk is\nstill hard-split.\n\nA numbered reply also stripped no trailing whitespace before its\nlabel, leaving blank lines between the closing fence and \"*(n/n)*\";\neach piece is now stripped first.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(nodes): add discord pipelineTimeoutSeconds and name config failures\n\npipelineTimeoutSeconds (default 0, off) gives up on a pipeline run\nthat has not answered within the limit and emits no_reply with reason\ntimeout. Each run (the question, each attachment, each retry) gets the\nfull limit; the run itself cannot be cancelled, so it finishes in the\nbackground, returns its pipe, and its late answer is dropped. Before\nthis a hung pipeline left the question with no outcome at all.\n\nConfiguration and start failures now say what to fix:\n- a list setting that starts with \"[\" but is not valid JSON is reported\n  in the task''s warnings with the setting''s name, instead of silently\n  becoming a literal id that matches nothing; in guildIds or channelIds\n  it fails the start, since the bot would otherwise answer nothing\n- a botToken that is still an unresolved ${NAME} fails the start naming\n  the variable, instead of \"login failed (invalid token)\"\n- with includeMemberMetadata on, a refused privileged intent leads with\n  the Server Members Intent (Message Content is usually already on)\n\nFound by the live engine suite (F36, F38, F39, F41); all verified live.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): add the full live engine suite for the discord node\n\ntest_engine_full.py (F01..F46) walks every discord node feature,\nsuccess and failure, through real pipelines on a running engine, driven\nby the second bot in the support channel only. Node behaviour runs on\ndeterministic pipes: an echo pipe (discord -> response_text keyed\nanswers) and a fake-model pipe pointed at fake_llm.py, a scripted\nOpenAI-compatible endpoint for scratchpad, final-envelope, error-text,\nempty, retry, HTTP 429 and slow answers. One case runs the production\npipe without tool_slack and capture.\n\nThe suite is opt-in (DISCORD_LIVE=1 and DISCORD_E2E_FULL=1) and skips\noffline. The driver bot gains attachments, embeds, replies, reactions,\ndeletes, thread creation and a typing recorder; its posts suppress\nmentions by default. The live README records the 44-row result matrix,\nthe findings fixed in this branch, and the residue a run leaves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): report capture rows left unwritten when the discord writer stops\n\nstop() waits a bounded time for the capture worker. When a write is\nstuck on the database the worker is still busy after the join, and the\nrows still queued are lost when the daemon thread dies with the\nprocess, with nothing logged and nothing counted. stop() now warns\nwith the approximate number of unwritten rows in that case.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): label the discord tile by token presence, not connection\n\nThe tile only tests whether botToken is set, yet read \"connected\"\neven when login failed, an intent was missing or the ${NAME} never\nresolved. It now reads \"Token: configured\" or \"Token: missing\", and the\nREADME says the task status line is what reports the connection.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): list the reply patterns the discord node treats as errors\n\nThe error-suppression sentence chained several clauses and did not\nparse. It is now a list that matches _ERROR_SIGNATURES in text_utils,\nwith the posting, retry and sanitizeReplies-off behaviour unchanged.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): parse live discord harness config through the real _run\n\nmake_endpoint kept a hand-written copy of IEndpoint._run''s config\nblock that had drifted: escalation markers and feedback emojis were\nsplit on whitespace, several newer settings (mergeAttachments,\nnumberChunks, teamMentionAlias, nonAnswerRetries, pipelineTimeoutSeconds,\ncapture) were never applied, and numbers skipped the production\ncoercion. It now runs the real _run(), stopped where it would reach the\nengine''s shared web server, as the unit tests already do.\n\nThe live README also marks B1 and G6 as fixed, pointing to N7 and F03.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): look up a discord reply''s target with the real discord.py API\n\nThe aimed-at-others check called Message.fetch_reference, which\ndiscord.py does not have. The AttributeError was swallowed, so the node\nnever learned who a reply was for, and with ignoreAimedAtOthers on every\nreply without a mention was treated as aimed at someone else, including\na reply to the bot''s own answer: it got the ack emoji and no answer.\n\nThe target now comes from reference.resolved (sent with the reply by\nthe Gateway), then reference.cached_message, then\nchannel.fetch_message(reference.message_id). The unit tests delete\nfetch_reference from the fake message, so a call to a method discord.py\ndoes not have fails the test instead of passing; the old test mocked it.\n\nReported in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): add a live case for a reply to the discord bot without a mention\n\nF23b replies to the bot''s own answer with the ping off, so only the\nreply-target lookup can tell the node the message is for it. The live\nREADME records the finding as N8; the case has not been run live yet.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): move discord support behaviours to a follow-up PR\n\nPR #1503 is being split into three stacked PRs. This removes the support\nbehaviours from the base Discord node: thread history as context, the\nescalation pause and per-thread serialization, skipping messages aimed at\nothers, feedback reactions, the team mention alias, reply sanitizing and\nerror detection, non-answer retries, the pipeline timeout and backfill.\n\nTheir services.json fields, config parsing, helpers, text utilities, README\nsections and unit tests go with them; the follow-up PR re-adds them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): move discord event capture to a follow-up PR\n\nThe durable Postgres capture of the node''s events moves to the third PR of\nthe split. This deletes capture.py and its tests, the captureEvents,\ncaptureNodeId, captureTable and captureSource fields, and the capture\nwriter lifecycle and call sites in IEndpoint.\n\nThe engine-warning helper _config_warning relied on now lives in IEndpoint,\nso the node no longer imports the capture module.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): fail the discord start on an unset variable in guildIds or channelIds\n\nThe engine passes an unknown ${NAME} through as literal text. In guildIds or\nchannelIds it became an allowlist id that matches nothing, so the bot\nconnected and answered nobody. The start now fails, on the same path as the\nbroken-JSON check, naming the field and the variable.\n\nIn allowedMentionRoleIds and allowedMentionUserIds such an entry was only\ndropped with a debug line; it now also produces a task warning naming the\nfield and the variable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): keep discord thread settings within what Discord accepts\n\nDiscord rejects a thread name longer than 100 characters and any\nauto-archive duration other than 60, 1440, 4320 or 10080 minutes, either of\nwhich cost the whole answer with a 400.\n\nthreadNameMaxLength now declares minimum 1 and maximum 100 and is clamped\nto that range in code. threadAutoArchiveMinutes is an enum of the accepted\ndurations plus 0 for the channel default; the code still falls back to the\nchannel default for any other value that reaches it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): say which discord text is broadcast and kept in the run log\n\nThe README called the apaevt_sse broadcast live-only. It is not: the engine\nwrites every SSE body into the task''s run log, so it is visible to every\nclient monitoring the task and kept afterwards.\n\nThe README now says so and lists exactly which message text the node puts\nin those bodies (the question on message events, the answer on outbound\nevents), so operators can cover it in their privacy notice.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(nodes): add comment blocks to the discord services.json\n\nEvery top-level key of the Discord services.json now has a // comment block\nexplaining its purpose, in the same form as the Telegram node''s.\n\nThe file is JSONC from here on, so the unit tests and the live suite drop\nits whole-line comments before parsing it, and a schema test checks that\neach top-level key keeps its comment block.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): remove internal data from the discord live suite\n\nThe repo is public and the live folder carried internal data. The replay\nseeds are now ten made-up questions about a generic pipeline tool, the\nREADME is cut down to the layers, how to run them, the variables they need,\nthe residue policy and what they do not cover, and the test pipe''s prompt no\nlonger names an internal bot.\n\nThe token and id files are read from DISCORD_LIVE_TOKEN_FILE and\nDISCORD_LIVE_IDS_FILE instead of fixed home-directory paths, and the layers\nskip with a reason when they are unset. Result files go to a temp directory\nor a *_RESULTS_DIR override instead of a repo-local folder.\n\nThe live cases for behaviours that moved to the follow-up PRs (D11, D16..D19\nand F14, F20..F26, F32..F35, F40, F41b, F45, F46) and the helpers only they\nused are removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): regenerate the discord README settings table\n\nGenerated by nodes:docs-generate after the support behaviours and\ncapture moved to follow-up PRs and the thread settings gained their\nlimits.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): list decoded text attachments among the broadcast discord text\n\nWith mergeAttachments off, a text-like attachment is sent through the\ntext lane as \"[attachment <name>]\" plus its decoded contents, and that\ntext is broadcast on its message event (clipped at 2000 characters),\nso it lands in the run log too. The privacy list now names it.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): handle blank discord text, partial delivery and late messages\n\nThree edge cases in the base node:\n\n- The intake gate strips a message''s text to decide whether it has\n  anything to ask, but processing read the raw text, so whitespace next\n  to a file ran a text pass of its own (and skipped the file-only\n  framing when merging). Processing now uses the same stripped answer.\n- When a later chunk failed after earlier ones were posted, the\n  outbound event looked like a full delivery. _send_response now\n  reports whether every chunk went out, and the outbound event carries\n  it as \"complete\".\n- A message arriving after shutdown began could start a task the\n  shutdown never awaited. _on_message now ignores messages once\n  closing.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): say which discord events need emitOutbound or emitNoReply\n\nThe README implied an outbound or send_failed no_reply event always\nfollows an answer. Both need their opt-in flag, which is off by default.\nIt also documents the new \"complete\" field on outbound events.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: keep the discord changelog line to what the base node ships\n\nThe entry still listed thread history, escalation pause, ack and\nfeedback reactions, reply hygiene and retries, and event capture, which\nmoved to follow-up PRs. List only what this node ships.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): catch every unset variable in the discord settings\n\nThe engine resolves only ROCKETRIDE_* variables and replaces any other\n${NAME} with the literal <REDACTED>, which the node did not recognise:\na token failed as \"invalid token\" and list entries matched nothing.\nBoth forms are now named, and the message says only ROCKETRIDE_* server\nvariables are resolved. requireMentionChannelIds now fails the start\nlike guildIds and channelIds (an unset entry made the mention gate fail\nopen), allowedBotIds warns like the mention lists, and JSON list items\nget the same strip and comma/whitespace split as a bare string.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): say how to keep strangers from adding the discord bot\n\nWith guildIds and channelIds empty (the default) the bot answers in\nevery server it is added to and in DMs, and a new Discord application is\na Public Bot. The setup steps now say to turn off Public Bot, the README\nrecommends setting guildIds in production and says DMs are answered only\nwhile both lists are empty, and the node warns once at start when\nguildIds is empty.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(nodes): bound how many discord messages are processed at once\n\nEvery message that passed the gates started its own task, and each one\nholds its downloaded attachments (up to maxAttachmentBytes, up to 10\nfiles) in memory until the pipeline answers, so nothing limited memory.\nA new maxConcurrentMessages setting (default 4, 1 to 32) sizes a\nsemaphore created in _startup; further messages wait their turn rather\nthan being dropped. maxAttachmentBytes gets a 100 MB maximum, enforced\nin code as well.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): never let a blank discord answer win over a real one\n\nAnswers were chosen by truthiness, so a whitespace-only answer such as\na newline beat a real attachment answer, and with merging on a blank\ntext pass hid the first attachment answer. Every pipeline answer is now\nnormalised the way the question text is before it is chosen, a blank\nattachment answer is no longer folded into the merged question, and\n_send_response treats an answer with no non-blank chunks as nothing to\nsend.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): decode discord text attachments the same way on both paths\n\nWith mergeAttachments off a text file was sliced to\ntextAttachmentMaxChars (0 sent an empty body, a negative value dropped\nthe end) and never checked for binary content; with it on, 0 or less\nkept everything and a NUL byte meant binary. Both paths now share one\ndecode helper and one cap rule: 0 means no limit, and a file with a NUL\nbyte (such as UTF-16) is not text. textAttachmentMaxChars gets a\nminimum of 0 in the schema.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(nodes): drop the unexplained discord.py version floor\n\nEvery discord.py API the node uses exists in 2.0 and later, and node\ndependencies are resolved together into one constraints lock, so an\narbitrary floor could move other packages for no reason.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): document what the discord send helpers return\n\n_send_response said it returned None but returns messageIds,\ndestination and complete; the docstring now describes each key.\n_emit_outbound_event and _record_sent had no docstring.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): route discord files of unlisted types by their extension\n\nThe extension table knows 15 extensions; any other file without a\ncontent type became application/octet-stream and went to the tags lane,\nso .m4a or .flac audio reached the document lane. After the content type\nand the table, the extension is now looked up with Python''s mimetypes\nbefore falling back to application/octet-stream.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): regenerate the discord README settings table\n\nGenerated by nodes:docs-generate for maxConcurrentMessages, the new\nbounds on maxAttachmentBytes and textAttachmentMaxChars, and the\ndependency line without a version floor.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): skip queued discord messages once shutdown has begun\n\nA message waiting for a maxConcurrentMessages slot is already tracked as\nin flight, so shutdown waits for it; once it got its slot nothing checked\nthat shutdown had begun, and it downloaded, ran the pipeline and replied\nanyway. It now returns as soon as it gets the slot (with a no_reply\n\"shutdown\" event when emitNoReply is on). The README notes that the slot\nlimit is shared across servers and what it does not buy.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): warn about an open discord bot only when no allowlist limits it\n\nWith channelIds set, only those channels and their threads are answered\nand DMs are refused, so the start-up warning that the bot answers in any\nserver it is added to was wrong. It now fires only when guildIds and\nchannelIds are both empty, and the README says the same.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): fail the discord start when an allowlist resolves to nothing\n\nThe engine replaces a set ${ROCKETRIDE_X} with its value, so an empty\nvalue reaches the node as \"\". Blank items are dropped, the list became\nempty, and an empty guildIds, channelIds or requireMentionChannelIds\nmeans \"everywhere\". A list that was given items but resolves to no ids\nnow fails the start with the field''s name; a genuinely empty list\n(missing, None, [], '''', ''[]'') still means all.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): let discord list settings keep phrases whole\n\n_as_str_list always split JSON items and bare strings on commas and\nwhitespace, which suits ids but breaks a list of phrases. It now takes\nsplit=True; with split=False items are kept whole (still stripped, blank\nones still dropped). Every current caller keeps the default.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): read UTF-16 and BOM-prefixed discord text files as text\n\nAny NUL byte meant \"binary\", so UTF-16 files (Windows Notepad \"Unicode\",\nPowerShell 5.1 redirects) were never read as text: with merging off they\nwent to the binary lanes, with merging on they were dropped with only a\ndebug line. A UTF-8 BOM also survived into the text. The decode now\nchecks for a UTF-16 BOM first, otherwise decodes as utf-8-sig, and only\nthen treats a NUL as binary content. In merge mode a text-like file that\nis still binary becomes its own binary lane object (reusing the bytes\nalready downloaded) instead of being dropped.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): guess discord file types from Python''s built-in table only\n\nmimetypes.guess_type also reads the Windows registry and /etc/mime.types,\nso the extension fallback routed the same file differently per host: on\nWindows .csv became application/vnd.ms-excel and .ts a video type (it is\nvideo/mp2t on macOS too). The fallback now asks a module-level\nmimetypes.MimeTypes(), which CPython fills from its built-in defaults\nonly; the node''s own extension table still answers first.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): accept only numeric ids in the discord allowlists\n\nAn entry in guildIds, channelIds or requireMentionChannelIds that is not\nplain ASCII digits (a pasted <#123>, a channel or server name) matched\nnothing, so the mention gate failed open or the bot answered nothing. The\nstart now fails naming the field and the entry, and suggests the id inside\na pasted mention. The mention allowlists and allowedBotIds use the same\ndigit rule, so ''123²'' no longer reaches int() in every send.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): ignore discord reactions once shutdown has begun\n\nThe Gateway keeps delivering reactions while _shutdown waits for in-flight\nmessages, and each one still opened a pipe. _on_raw_reaction now returns\nwhen _closing is set, and checks again just before the emit. The reaction\nauthorIsBot now uses the same _reactor_is_bot answer as the gate, so a\nremoval by an allowlisted bot is reported as a bot.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): split prose in fenced discord replies at a word boundary\n\nA reply with a code fence anywhere goes through the fenced chunker, which\nonly broke at a newline in the second half of the window and otherwise cut\nby character count, even in prose outside the code block. Outside an open\nfence it now falls back to the last sentence end, then the last whitespace,\nin the second half before cutting by count.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): bound and normalise the discord attachment settings\n\nmaxAttachmentBytes of zero or below skipped every attachment with only a\ndebug line; it is now clamped to at least 1 and the schema declares\nminimum 1. textAttachmentExtensions entries typed without a dot (md, json)\nnever matched os.path.splitext; each entry is now normalised to a\nlowercase extension with one leading dot, and blank entries are dropped.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): coerce discord booleans and overflowing numbers from the engine\n\n_as_int caught TypeError and ValueError but not OverflowError, so a number\nsetting of inf or 1e400 failed _run with an unrelated traceback; it now\nfalls back to the default. The ten boolean settings were used raw, so a\nstring-like ''false'' read as True; a new _as_bool reads real bools as-is and\ntrue/false, 1/0, yes/no, on/off strings in any case, else the default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): document discord events, reasons and setup accurately\n\nThe tags lane also carries the reaction, no_reply and outbound event\nobjects, and events have their own entry URL and name; the README now says\nso. The no_reply reasons are listed (no_answer, send_failed, shutdown, or\nan error message clipped to 200 characters), and so is what happens when\nthe question is deleted before the answer is posted. Configuration\nheadings use the field titles, each paragraph sits under the field it is\nabout, and the broadcast section moves under Notes. The Developer Portal\nsteps use Reset Token, the reactions intent is described as one the node\nturns on itself, and the MIME fallback examples are .avi and .bmp, which\nthe node''s own table does not list. In services.json, guildIds and\nchannelIds default to [] and the text-attachment and emitNoReply\ndescriptions match the behaviour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): complete the discord docstrings and drop references to a private bot\n\nSeveral Args sections left out parameters the functions take (_on_message\nwait, _process_attachment meta and attachment_index, _run_text_pipeline\nmeta, object_name and attachment_id, _run_binary_pipeline message_id,\nattachment_index and meta, should_process_message parent_channel_id and\nallowed_bot_ids). Comments and docstrings no longer point at functions of\na bot that is not in this repo; the sentences explaining the behaviour\nstay.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): make the discord live suite neutral and self-describing\n\nThe live README now lists the variables L3 and L4 need on the engine and\nsays ROCKETRIDE_DISCORD_E2E_NO_SUCH_TOKEN must stay unset, and asks for\nPython 3.10+. engine_min.pipe gets a fresh project_id (the SDK''s use()\ncontract carries it at the top level) and a short generic assistant\nprompt with no escalation line. E05 tested that escalation prompt, which\nbelongs to a follow-up change, and E06 was a skipping placeholder; both\nare removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): regenerate the discord README settings table\n\nGenerated by nodes:docs-generate for the new bounds and list defaults\nand the updated textAttachmentExtensions and emitNoReply descriptions.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): wait for discord reaction emits at shutdown and hint ids by kind\n\nA reaction emit already under way was not tracked, so shutdown did not\nwait for it and tore the endpoint down beneath it. The emit is now a\ntracked task, like a message handler. The second closing check before\nthe emit could never fire (nothing can change the flag between the two\nchecks) and its test only passed by setting the flag from a mock; both\nare gone.\n\nA pasted mention in an id list suggested the digits inside it even when\nthey were the id of something else (a role in a channel list, a channel\nin the server list). The hint now gives the digits only for the right\nkind of mention and otherwise says what was pasted.\n\nAlso documents the arguments of _emit_event_pipeline and the return of\n_emit_no_reply_event.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): never cut a discord reply inside a code-fence opener or a word\n\nWhen a chunk window ended inside or just after a code-fence opener line,\nchunk 1 ended with a split language name or an empty code block. A block\nwhose opener line and first code line do not both fit now starts the next\nchunk, and the newline search never cuts right after an opener line.\n\nA reply with no fence also hard-split a line longer than the limit in the\nmiddle of a word. It is now cut at the last sentence end or whitespace in\nthe second half of the window, by character count only when there is none.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): read discord booleans with the shared parse_bool\n\nThe node had its own _as_bool, which duplicated parse_bool in\nai.common.utils. Every boolean setting now goes through the shared\nhelper. The engine passes JSON booleans as real bool values, so the\ntests that fed string-like proxies to a boolean are replaced with tests\nfor real bools and plain strings.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): keep secrets out of discord id errors and bound id length\n\nA bot token or secret ${ROCKETRIDE_*} value pasted into an id list was\nechoed in full by the start error, the task warnings and the debug log.\nEvery message about a list entry now goes through one helper that shows\na mention whole and anything else longer than 12 characters as its first\n12 characters followed by an ellipsis.\n\nAn id is also bounded to a 64-bit snowflake (at most 20 digits, below\n2**64), so two ids pasted together are rejected as non-numeric on the\nsame fatal or warning path as any other bad entry.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): treat a non-positive discord attachment cap as unset\n\nmaxAttachmentBytes was floored at 1, but a 1-byte cap skips every real\nattachment just as 0 did. A value of 0 or below now means \"not set\" and\nfalls back to the 25 MB default, matching config_int in ai.common.utils;\nthe value is still clamped to at most 100 MB. The schema keeps its\nminimum of 1.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Mithilesh Gaurihar <mithileshgaurihar@Mithileshs-MacBook-Pro.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Mithilesh Gaurihar <mithileshgaurihar@Mithileshs-MBP.attlocal.net>\nCo-authored-by: mithileshgau <mithilesh.gaurihar@rocketride.ai>","tree":{"sha":"580fb18d00053386b186f10d8af55270f6e8b5b0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/580fb18d00053386b186f10d8af55270f6e8b5b0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/9024586e44dc27e0fa08ff56267a62ff47d30bd3","verification":{"payload":"tree 580fb18d00053386b186f10d8af55270f6e8b5b0\nparent d6329ba538f0626058af4d154f3871edc5ba534a\nauthor mithileshgau <mithileshgaurihar@gmail.com> 1791326354 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791326354 -0700\n\nfeat(nodes): add Discord bot source node (discord) (#1503)\n\n* feat(nodes): add Discord bot source node (discord)\n\nAdd a Discord bot source node (discord://, classType [\"source\"]) that\nconnects a Discord bot to a pipeline over the Discord Gateway — the Discord\ncounterpart to the telegram node. Routes incoming text and image/audio/\nvideo/document attachments to the text/image/audio/video/tags lanes, and\nposts the pipeline''s first answer back to the originating channel, message,\nor thread.\n\nMirrors the Telegram endpoint architecture: scanObjects() -> WebServer\n(kept alive via self._server.run()) with the discord.py Gateway client\nlaunched as a background task from the async on_startup hook.\n\nConfig: botToken (secure), guildIds, channelIds, ignoreBots,\nrequireMention, replyMode (channel|reply|thread), showTyping,\nmaxAttachmentBytes, sendResponses. Includes 2000-char reply chunking,\nrate-limit backoff, bot-loop prevention, and mention gating.\n\nTests: 21 unit tests plus an env-gated live-gateway integration suite\n(skips unless DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN is set).\n\nCloses #1502\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): drop Discord live-gateway integration test, fix CI encoding\n\n- Remove nodes/test/discord/test_integration.py: it imports the discord.py\n  library at module top-level, which requires the (uninstalled-in-CI)\n  dependency and a live DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN, and the package name collides\n  with the node''s own `discord` package during collection. It cannot run in\n  CI. The pure-logic unit tests in test_discord.py remain.\n- Open services.json with encoding=''utf-8'' in the schema tests so they pass\n  under CI''s ASCII locale.\n- Replace the non-ASCII em dash in services.json with a hyphen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): address Discord node review feedback\n\n- Extract pure helpers (chunk_message, guess_media_type) into text_utils.py\n  with no discord.py dependency, so unit tests exercise the real production\n  code instead of reimplementing it, and can run in CI without discord.py.\n- chunk_message now hard-splits any single token/sentence longer than the\n  2000-char limit, so every emitted chunk is guaranteed to fit (previously an\n  unbroken long token could produce an oversized chunk Discord would reject).\n- Stop processing further attachments once a non-empty answer is found.\n- Handle discord.RateLimited (which carries retry_after) instead of reading a\n  nonexistent retry_after off discord.HTTPException.\n- Declare _guild_ids/_channel_ids as annotations only to avoid mutable list\n  defaults shared at class scope; they are set per-instance in _run().\n- requirements.txt: keep only discord.py (requests/fastapi/aiohttp are not\n  imported directly and flagged unpinned/vulnerable by OSV).\n- README: use the UI-facing lane name \"Data\" for the document lane.\n- Rewrite test_discord.py to test the real helpers and services.json schema;\n  drop tautological config/filtering tests.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): address second Discord review round\n\n- Process attachments independently of the text pipeline so captioned uploads\n  are ingested; the first non-empty answer (text first, then attachments in\n  order, stopping at the first that answers) becomes the reply.\n- _send_response now abandons the remaining chunks on an unrecoverable send\n  failure instead of silently continuing and dropping part of the reply.\n- Extract the message gating (self/bot/guild/channel/mention) into a pure\n  should_process_message() predicate in text_utils.py and unit-test it;\n  _on_message just wires primitives and schedules the task.\n- README: correct the attachment note to describe in-order processing that\n  stops at the first attachment answer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): correct Discord MIME-detection precedence in README\n\nguess_media_type() prefers Discord''s reported content_type (parameters\nstripped) and only falls back to the file extension; the README had the\norder reversed. Fix the description to match, keeping the octet-stream default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): address Discord review — ingest all attachments, correct docs\n\n- Remove the early `break` in the attachment loop so a source node ingests\n  every attachment in a multi-attachment message (up to 10); still reply with\n  only the first non-empty answer. The break silently dropped later\n  attachments (no download, routing, or monitorCompleted).\n- Revert README lane name `Data` -> `tags` to match services.json and the\n  telegram/webhook/core convention (no `Data` lane exists).\n- Clarify that discord.py handles 429s internally; the RateLimited branch is\n  defensive (only fires with max_ratelimit_timeout, which is unset).\n- Regenerate stale GENERATED:PARAMS em-dash back to the services.json hyphen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): Discord node — use shared WebServer + correctness fixes from deep review\n\nCritical:\n- _run no longer constructs its own WebServer bound to --data_port. node.py\n  already binds a shared WebServer on that port for every subprocess, so the\n  second bind hit EADDRINUSE (and wired the target onto the wrong server) — the\n  node could not start under EaaS. Now mirrors the Telegram node: register on\n  the shared server and drive _startup/_shutdown on the shared server_loop,\n  blocking scanObjects() on a shutdown event.\n\nCorrectness:\n- _run_with_optional_typing now runs the pipeline exactly once; a typing-\n  indicator enter/exit failure no longer re-executes it (double ingestion).\n- require_mention no longer satisfied by @everyone/@here (use direct\n  message.mentions, not mentioned_in).\n- guildIds/channelIds coerced via _as_str_list so a scalar string no longer\n  becomes a per-character allowlist; non-dict ''discord'' config block no longer\n  crashes _run.\n- guess_media_type lowercases the reported content type (mixed-case IMAGE/PNG\n  routed correctly) and falls back to the extension when it normalizes empty.\n\nDocs:\n- Attachments download via discord.py''s Attachment.read(), not aiohttp directly\n  (aiohttp is transitive); drop the misleading \"concurrently\" wording.\n\nTests:\n- New test_process_message.py (import-guarded, py3.10+/discord.py): attachment\n  ingest-all + first-answer, exactly-once typing, allowlist coercion, and the\n  @everyone gating fix. New MIME normalization cases in test_discord.py.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): list Discord node in nodes catalog and CHANGELOG\n\n- Add a `discord` row to the Sources table in docs/README-nodes.md.\n- Add a Discord node line under [Unreleased] / Added in CHANGELOG.md.\n\nAddresses review feedback that both shared surfaces were missing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): Discord lifecycle failure propagation, mention safety, and develop conformance\n\nAddresses kgarg2468''s review:\n\nLifecycle: terminal Gateway failures now fail the source promptly instead of\nidling forever with a dead bot. A missing token raises from _startup; login\nfailure, missing Message Content Intent, an unexpected disconnect, or a generic\ngateway error record a fatal error and unblock _run, which re-raises so the\nengine marks the source failed. Successful startup still blocks until controlled\nshutdown; an intentional close during _shutdown is not treated as a failure.\n\nSecurity: all outbound sends (reply, thread, channel) pass\ndiscord.AllowedMentions.none() so model-generated text can never ping users,\nroles, @here or @everyone.\n\nTests: the behavioral suite now stubs discord in sys.modules and loads the node\nas a synthetic package, so it runs deterministically in clean CI without\ndiscord.py installed and without a version skip (node annotations switched to\nOptional[...] so it imports on 3.10-). Added lifecycle tests (missing token,\nlogin failure, privileged-intent failure, unexpected close, cancellation,\nshutdown-marks-closing), allowed-mentions tests for all three reply modes plus\nthread reuse, and an oversized-attachment regression. Retained the\nmulti-attachment ingest-all regression.\n\nDevelop conformance:\n- services.json: add \"documentation\" URL.\n- README.md: restructure to the current node README schema (top-level Lanes\n  table, drop the hand-written field table, fold sections under Notes); passes\n  scripts/validate-node-readme.py.\n- credentials.json: register the discord credential (nodes:credentials-generate)\n  so nodes:credentials-check passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): Discord thread reply mode handles DMs and existing threads\n\nAddresses CodeRabbit re-review:\n\n- _send_chunk thread mode no longer calls message.create_thread()\n  unconditionally: it posts into message.channel when the message is already\n  inside a thread, creates a thread only for TextChannel messages, and falls\n  back to a plain reply (mentions suppressed) in DMs and other non-threadable\n  channels — previously these raised and dropped the response.\n- README: document that thread reply mode also needs Create Public Threads and\n  Send Messages in Threads, and that it falls back to a reply where a thread\n  cannot be created.\n- Tests: add thread-in-TextChannel, reuse-existing-thread, post-into-own-thread,\n  and DM-fallback cases (58 tests pass on 3.9 and 3.11).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(nodes): discord node support-bot parity features\n\nBrings the discord source node to parity with the discord.js support bot it\nreplaces. Every new setting is opt-in; with defaults the node behaves as it\ndid before.\n\n- Replies: thread name template, length cap and archive duration; optional\n  (i/n) chunk labels; allowlisted user/role mentions (never @everyone/@here);\n  a failed thread creation falls back to a reply instead of losing it.\n- Context: earlier thread messages as context (threadHistoryLimit).\n- Escalation: pause a thread after an escalating reply, rebuild the pause from\n  thread history after a restart, resume on an @mention; a team-name alias is\n  turned into the team role mention; per-thread serialization while the pause\n  is on.\n- Aimed at someone else: optional ack emoji, and the thread pauses.\n- Feedback: optional reactions on the last chunk of an answer.\n- Reply hygiene: strip leaked agent scratchpad and final-answer envelopes,\n  never post engine or model errors, retry empty or scratchpad answers.\n- Attachments: optional text-file decoding and one merged answer per message.\n- Gating: allowed bot ids, mention-only channels, system and empty messages\n  dropped.\n- Events: reaction, no_reply and outbound events with the message metadata,\n  broadcast as apaevt_sse; reactions are scoped by the same guild, channel and\n  bot gates as messages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(nodes): durable event capture for the discord node\n\nWith captureEvents on, the node writes every event it broadcasts (message,\nplus outbound, no_reply and reaction when their emit options are on) into a\ndatabase tool node wired to the source by a control edge, so a support\ndashboard has a complete log even when nobody is watching the live stream.\n\n- One row per event in a fixed INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING through the\n  database node''s execute tool, keyed (message_id, event_type, event_key), so\n  a redelivered Gateway event is ignored.\n- Off the answering path: a bounded queue and one writer thread; a slow or\n  dead database costs a logged, rate-limited warning, never a Discord reply.\n- captureNodeId picks the database component, captureTable the table\n  (validated, created if missing), captureSource the label written to each\n  row''s source column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): cover the discord node parity features and capture\n\nUnit tests for every new behaviour and its default: thread replies and\nfallbacks, thread-history context, escalation pause and history rebuild,\nper-thread serialization, aimed-elsewhere acks, feedback reactions, reply\nhygiene and retries, error suppression, team mention alias, chunk labels,\nattachment merge, reaction scoping, event payloads, config coercion, and the\ncapture writer (row shape, SQL, failures, queue, thread lifecycle). The\nservices.json schema tests assert every field, its UI registration and its\ndefault.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): document the discord node''s new settings\n\nDescribes each parity feature and the capture log in the node README, and\nregenerates the PARAMS schema table with nodes:docs-generate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): add the gated live Discord harness and test notes\n\nA live suite for the discord node against a real Discord server and engine,\nskipped unless DISCORD_LIVE=1 (ids and the token are read from files outside\nthe repo). The README records each live round: what was run, what passed and\nthe bugs it found.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): read discord live-harness files as UTF-8\n\nCollection of the live suite opened its seed and config files with the\nlocale''s encoding, which fails under an ASCII locale before the DISCORD_LIVE\ngate can skip anything. Every read and write now names UTF-8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: note the discord node''s support-bot features in the changelog\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): never post an engine LLM error as a discord reply\n\nWith sanitizeReplies on, a provider failure still reached Discord: the\nengine''s LLM layer turns the exception into the answer text\n\"**LLM error** - ValueError: An error occurred with the API.\", and no\nerror signature matched it (the API pattern needs a word before \"API\").\n\nTwo signatures now catch it: the \"**LLM error**\" prefix at the start of\nthe answer, and the bare \"An error occurred with the API.\" sentence\nwhen it is the whole answer. Prose that only mentions API errors is\nnot matched. The existing path then emits no_reply model_error.\n\nFound by the live engine suite (F40); verified live after the fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): split fenced discord replies between lines\n\nInside a code fence the chunker hard-split at a character count, so\nevery boundary cut a code line in two and a copied block was broken\n(live F12 and F44: 2 of 2 and 10 of 10 boundaries).\n\nIt now breaks at the last newline in the second half of the window.\nThe synthetic close/reopen fence pair stands in for that newline, so\nthe reply rebuilds exactly by replacing each boundary pair with \"\\n\".\nA newline right before a fence is passed over, so a boundary never\nproduces an empty code block, and a single line longer than a chunk is\nstill hard-split.\n\nA numbered reply also stripped no trailing whitespace before its\nlabel, leaving blank lines between the closing fence and \"*(n/n)*\";\neach piece is now stripped first.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(nodes): add discord pipelineTimeoutSeconds and name config failures\n\npipelineTimeoutSeconds (default 0, off) gives up on a pipeline run\nthat has not answered within the limit and emits no_reply with reason\ntimeout. Each run (the question, each attachment, each retry) gets the\nfull limit; the run itself cannot be cancelled, so it finishes in the\nbackground, returns its pipe, and its late answer is dropped. Before\nthis a hung pipeline left the question with no outcome at all.\n\nConfiguration and start failures now say what to fix:\n- a list setting that starts with \"[\" but is not valid JSON is reported\n  in the task''s warnings with the setting''s name, instead of silently\n  becoming a literal id that matches nothing; in guildIds or channelIds\n  it fails the start, since the bot would otherwise answer nothing\n- a botToken that is still an unresolved ${NAME} fails the start naming\n  the variable, instead of \"login failed (invalid token)\"\n- with includeMemberMetadata on, a refused privileged intent leads with\n  the Server Members Intent (Message Content is usually already on)\n\nFound by the live engine suite (F36, F38, F39, F41); all verified live.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): add the full live engine suite for the discord node\n\ntest_engine_full.py (F01..F46) walks every discord node feature,\nsuccess and failure, through real pipelines on a running engine, driven\nby the second bot in the support channel only. Node behaviour runs on\ndeterministic pipes: an echo pipe (discord -> response_text keyed\nanswers) and a fake-model pipe pointed at fake_llm.py, a scripted\nOpenAI-compatible endpoint for scratchpad, final-envelope, error-text,\nempty, retry, HTTP 429 and slow answers. One case runs the production\npipe without tool_slack and capture.\n\nThe suite is opt-in (DISCORD_LIVE=1 and DISCORD_E2E_FULL=1) and skips\noffline. The driver bot gains attachments, embeds, replies, reactions,\ndeletes, thread creation and a typing recorder; its posts suppress\nmentions by default. The live README records the 44-row result matrix,\nthe findings fixed in this branch, and the residue a run leaves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): report capture rows left unwritten when the discord writer stops\n\nstop() waits a bounded time for the capture worker. When a write is\nstuck on the database the worker is still busy after the join, and the\nrows still queued are lost when the daemon thread dies with the\nprocess, with nothing logged and nothing counted. stop() now warns\nwith the approximate number of unwritten rows in that case.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): label the discord tile by token presence, not connection\n\nThe tile only tests whether botToken is set, yet read \"connected\"\neven when login failed, an intent was missing or the ${NAME} never\nresolved. It now reads \"Token: configured\" or \"Token: missing\", and the\nREADME says the task status line is what reports the connection.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): list the reply patterns the discord node treats as errors\n\nThe error-suppression sentence chained several clauses and did not\nparse. It is now a list that matches _ERROR_SIGNATURES in text_utils,\nwith the posting, retry and sanitizeReplies-off behaviour unchanged.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): parse live discord harness config through the real _run\n\nmake_endpoint kept a hand-written copy of IEndpoint._run''s config\nblock that had drifted: escalation markers and feedback emojis were\nsplit on whitespace, several newer settings (mergeAttachments,\nnumberChunks, teamMentionAlias, nonAnswerRetries, pipelineTimeoutSeconds,\ncapture) were never applied, and numbers skipped the production\ncoercion. It now runs the real _run(), stopped where it would reach the\nengine''s shared web server, as the unit tests already do.\n\nThe live README also marks B1 and G6 as fixed, pointing to N7 and F03.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): look up a discord reply''s target with the real discord.py API\n\nThe aimed-at-others check called Message.fetch_reference, which\ndiscord.py does not have. The AttributeError was swallowed, so the node\nnever learned who a reply was for, and with ignoreAimedAtOthers on every\nreply without a mention was treated as aimed at someone else, including\na reply to the bot''s own answer: it got the ack emoji and no answer.\n\nThe target now comes from reference.resolved (sent with the reply by\nthe Gateway), then reference.cached_message, then\nchannel.fetch_message(reference.message_id). The unit tests delete\nfetch_reference from the fake message, so a call to a method discord.py\ndoes not have fails the test instead of passing; the old test mocked it.\n\nReported in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): add a live case for a reply to the discord bot without a mention\n\nF23b replies to the bot''s own answer with the ping off, so only the\nreply-target lookup can tell the node the message is for it. The live\nREADME records the finding as N8; the case has not been run live yet.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): move discord support behaviours to a follow-up PR\n\nPR #1503 is being split into three stacked PRs. This removes the support\nbehaviours from the base Discord node: thread history as context, the\nescalation pause and per-thread serialization, skipping messages aimed at\nothers, feedback reactions, the team mention alias, reply sanitizing and\nerror detection, non-answer retries, the pipeline timeout and backfill.\n\nTheir services.json fields, config parsing, helpers, text utilities, README\nsections and unit tests go with them; the follow-up PR re-adds them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): move discord event capture to a follow-up PR\n\nThe durable Postgres capture of the node''s events moves to the third PR of\nthe split. This deletes capture.py and its tests, the captureEvents,\ncaptureNodeId, captureTable and captureSource fields, and the capture\nwriter lifecycle and call sites in IEndpoint.\n\nThe engine-warning helper _config_warning relied on now lives in IEndpoint,\nso the node no longer imports the capture module.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): fail the discord start on an unset variable in guildIds or channelIds\n\nThe engine passes an unknown ${NAME} through as literal text. In guildIds or\nchannelIds it became an allowlist id that matches nothing, so the bot\nconnected and answered nobody. The start now fails, on the same path as the\nbroken-JSON check, naming the field and the variable.\n\nIn allowedMentionRoleIds and allowedMentionUserIds such an entry was only\ndropped with a debug line; it now also produces a task warning naming the\nfield and the variable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): keep discord thread settings within what Discord accepts\n\nDiscord rejects a thread name longer than 100 characters and any\nauto-archive duration other than 60, 1440, 4320 or 10080 minutes, either of\nwhich cost the whole answer with a 400.\n\nthreadNameMaxLength now declares minimum 1 and maximum 100 and is clamped\nto that range in code. threadAutoArchiveMinutes is an enum of the accepted\ndurations plus 0 for the channel default; the code still falls back to the\nchannel default for any other value that reaches it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): say which discord text is broadcast and kept in the run log\n\nThe README called the apaevt_sse broadcast live-only. It is not: the engine\nwrites every SSE body into the task''s run log, so it is visible to every\nclient monitoring the task and kept afterwards.\n\nThe README now says so and lists exactly which message text the node puts\nin those bodies (the question on message events, the answer on outbound\nevents), so operators can cover it in their privacy notice.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(nodes): add comment blocks to the discord services.json\n\nEvery top-level key of the Discord services.json now has a // comment block\nexplaining its purpose, in the same form as the Telegram node''s.\n\nThe file is JSONC from here on, so the unit tests and the live suite drop\nits whole-line comments before parsing it, and a schema test checks that\neach top-level key keeps its comment block.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): remove internal data from the discord live suite\n\nThe repo is public and the live folder carried internal data. The replay\nseeds are now ten made-up questions about a generic pipeline tool, the\nREADME is cut down to the layers, how to run them, the variables they need,\nthe residue policy and what they do not cover, and the test pipe''s prompt no\nlonger names an internal bot.\n\nThe token and id files are read from DISCORD_LIVE_TOKEN_FILE and\nDISCORD_LIVE_IDS_FILE instead of fixed home-directory paths, and the layers\nskip with a reason when they are unset. Result files go to a temp directory\nor a *_RESULTS_DIR override instead of a repo-local folder.\n\nThe live cases for behaviours that moved to the follow-up PRs (D11, D16..D19\nand F14, F20..F26, F32..F35, F40, F41b, F45, F46) and the helpers only they\nused are removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): regenerate the discord README settings table\n\nGenerated by nodes:docs-generate after the support behaviours and\ncapture moved to follow-up PRs and the thread settings gained their\nlimits.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): list decoded text attachments among the broadcast discord text\n\nWith mergeAttachments off, a text-like attachment is sent through the\ntext lane as \"[attachment <name>]\" plus its decoded contents, and that\ntext is broadcast on its message event (clipped at 2000 characters),\nso it lands in the run log too. The privacy list now names it.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): handle blank discord text, partial delivery and late messages\n\nThree edge cases in the base node:\n\n- The intake gate strips a message''s text to decide whether it has\n  anything to ask, but processing read the raw text, so whitespace next\n  to a file ran a text pass of its own (and skipped the file-only\n  framing when merging). Processing now uses the same stripped answer.\n- When a later chunk failed after earlier ones were posted, the\n  outbound event looked like a full delivery. _send_response now\n  reports whether every chunk went out, and the outbound event carries\n  it as \"complete\".\n- A message arriving after shutdown began could start a task the\n  shutdown never awaited. _on_message now ignores messages once\n  closing.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): say which discord events need emitOutbound or emitNoReply\n\nThe README implied an outbound or send_failed no_reply event always\nfollows an answer. Both need their opt-in flag, which is off by default.\nIt also documents the new \"complete\" field on outbound events.\n\nRaised by CodeRabbit on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: keep the discord changelog line to what the base node ships\n\nThe entry still listed thread history, escalation pause, ack and\nfeedback reactions, reply hygiene and retries, and event capture, which\nmoved to follow-up PRs. List only what this node ships.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): catch every unset variable in the discord settings\n\nThe engine resolves only ROCKETRIDE_* variables and replaces any other\n${NAME} with the literal <REDACTED>, which the node did not recognise:\na token failed as \"invalid token\" and list entries matched nothing.\nBoth forms are now named, and the message says only ROCKETRIDE_* server\nvariables are resolved. requireMentionChannelIds now fails the start\nlike guildIds and channelIds (an unset entry made the mention gate fail\nopen), allowedBotIds warns like the mention lists, and JSON list items\nget the same strip and comma/whitespace split as a bare string.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): say how to keep strangers from adding the discord bot\n\nWith guildIds and channelIds empty (the default) the bot answers in\nevery server it is added to and in DMs, and a new Discord application is\na Public Bot. The setup steps now say to turn off Public Bot, the README\nrecommends setting guildIds in production and says DMs are answered only\nwhile both lists are empty, and the node warns once at start when\nguildIds is empty.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(nodes): bound how many discord messages are processed at once\n\nEvery message that passed the gates started its own task, and each one\nholds its downloaded attachments (up to maxAttachmentBytes, up to 10\nfiles) in memory until the pipeline answers, so nothing limited memory.\nA new maxConcurrentMessages setting (default 4, 1 to 32) sizes a\nsemaphore created in _startup; further messages wait their turn rather\nthan being dropped. maxAttachmentBytes gets a 100 MB maximum, enforced\nin code as well.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): never let a blank discord answer win over a real one\n\nAnswers were chosen by truthiness, so a whitespace-only answer such as\na newline beat a real attachment answer, and with merging on a blank\ntext pass hid the first attachment answer. Every pipeline answer is now\nnormalised the way the question text is before it is chosen, a blank\nattachment answer is no longer folded into the merged question, and\n_send_response treats an answer with no non-blank chunks as nothing to\nsend.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): decode discord text attachments the same way on both paths\n\nWith mergeAttachments off a text file was sliced to\ntextAttachmentMaxChars (0 sent an empty body, a negative value dropped\nthe end) and never checked for binary content; with it on, 0 or less\nkept everything and a NUL byte meant binary. Both paths now share one\ndecode helper and one cap rule: 0 means no limit, and a file with a NUL\nbyte (such as UTF-16) is not text. textAttachmentMaxChars gets a\nminimum of 0 in the schema.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(nodes): drop the unexplained discord.py version floor\n\nEvery discord.py API the node uses exists in 2.0 and later, and node\ndependencies are resolved together into one constraints lock, so an\narbitrary floor could move other packages for no reason.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): document what the discord send helpers return\n\n_send_response said it returned None but returns messageIds,\ndestination and complete; the docstring now describes each key.\n_emit_outbound_event and _record_sent had no docstring.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): route discord files of unlisted types by their extension\n\nThe extension table knows 15 extensions; any other file without a\ncontent type became application/octet-stream and went to the tags lane,\nso .m4a or .flac audio reached the document lane. After the content type\nand the table, the extension is now looked up with Python''s mimetypes\nbefore falling back to application/octet-stream.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): regenerate the discord README settings table\n\nGenerated by nodes:docs-generate for maxConcurrentMessages, the new\nbounds on maxAttachmentBytes and textAttachmentMaxChars, and the\ndependency line without a version floor.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): skip queued discord messages once shutdown has begun\n\nA message waiting for a maxConcurrentMessages slot is already tracked as\nin flight, so shutdown waits for it; once it got its slot nothing checked\nthat shutdown had begun, and it downloaded, ran the pipeline and replied\nanyway. It now returns as soon as it gets the slot (with a no_reply\n\"shutdown\" event when emitNoReply is on). The README notes that the slot\nlimit is shared across servers and what it does not buy.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): warn about an open discord bot only when no allowlist limits it\n\nWith channelIds set, only those channels and their threads are answered\nand DMs are refused, so the start-up warning that the bot answers in any\nserver it is added to was wrong. It now fires only when guildIds and\nchannelIds are both empty, and the README says the same.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): fail the discord start when an allowlist resolves to nothing\n\nThe engine replaces a set ${ROCKETRIDE_X} with its value, so an empty\nvalue reaches the node as \"\". Blank items are dropped, the list became\nempty, and an empty guildIds, channelIds or requireMentionChannelIds\nmeans \"everywhere\". A list that was given items but resolves to no ids\nnow fails the start with the field''s name; a genuinely empty list\n(missing, None, [], '''', ''[]'') still means all.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): let discord list settings keep phrases whole\n\n_as_str_list always split JSON items and bare strings on commas and\nwhitespace, which suits ids but breaks a list of phrases. It now takes\nsplit=True; with split=False items are kept whole (still stripped, blank\nones still dropped). Every current caller keeps the default.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): read UTF-16 and BOM-prefixed discord text files as text\n\nAny NUL byte meant \"binary\", so UTF-16 files (Windows Notepad \"Unicode\",\nPowerShell 5.1 redirects) were never read as text: with merging off they\nwent to the binary lanes, with merging on they were dropped with only a\ndebug line. A UTF-8 BOM also survived into the text. The decode now\nchecks for a UTF-16 BOM first, otherwise decodes as utf-8-sig, and only\nthen treats a NUL as binary content. In merge mode a text-like file that\nis still binary becomes its own binary lane object (reusing the bytes\nalready downloaded) instead of being dropped.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): guess discord file types from Python''s built-in table only\n\nmimetypes.guess_type also reads the Windows registry and /etc/mime.types,\nso the extension fallback routed the same file differently per host: on\nWindows .csv became application/vnd.ms-excel and .ts a video type (it is\nvideo/mp2t on macOS too). The fallback now asks a module-level\nmimetypes.MimeTypes(), which CPython fills from its built-in defaults\nonly; the node''s own extension table still answers first.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): accept only numeric ids in the discord allowlists\n\nAn entry in guildIds, channelIds or requireMentionChannelIds that is not\nplain ASCII digits (a pasted <#123>, a channel or server name) matched\nnothing, so the mention gate failed open or the bot answered nothing. The\nstart now fails naming the field and the entry, and suggests the id inside\na pasted mention. The mention allowlists and allowedBotIds use the same\ndigit rule, so ''123²'' no longer reaches int() in every send.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): ignore discord reactions once shutdown has begun\n\nThe Gateway keeps delivering reactions while _shutdown waits for in-flight\nmessages, and each one still opened a pipe. _on_raw_reaction now returns\nwhen _closing is set, and checks again just before the emit. The reaction\nauthorIsBot now uses the same _reactor_is_bot answer as the gate, so a\nremoval by an allowlisted bot is reported as a bot.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): split prose in fenced discord replies at a word boundary\n\nA reply with a code fence anywhere goes through the fenced chunker, which\nonly broke at a newline in the second half of the window and otherwise cut\nby character count, even in prose outside the code block. Outside an open\nfence it now falls back to the last sentence end, then the last whitespace,\nin the second half before cutting by count.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): bound and normalise the discord attachment settings\n\nmaxAttachmentBytes of zero or below skipped every attachment with only a\ndebug line; it is now clamped to at least 1 and the schema declares\nminimum 1. textAttachmentExtensions entries typed without a dot (md, json)\nnever matched os.path.splitext; each entry is now normalised to a\nlowercase extension with one leading dot, and blank entries are dropped.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): coerce discord booleans and overflowing numbers from the engine\n\n_as_int caught TypeError and ValueError but not OverflowError, so a number\nsetting of inf or 1e400 failed _run with an unrelated traceback; it now\nfalls back to the default. The ten boolean settings were used raw, so a\nstring-like ''false'' read as True; a new _as_bool reads real bools as-is and\ntrue/false, 1/0, yes/no, on/off strings in any case, else the default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): document discord events, reasons and setup accurately\n\nThe tags lane also carries the reaction, no_reply and outbound event\nobjects, and events have their own entry URL and name; the README now says\nso. The no_reply reasons are listed (no_answer, send_failed, shutdown, or\nan error message clipped to 200 characters), and so is what happens when\nthe question is deleted before the answer is posted. Configuration\nheadings use the field titles, each paragraph sits under the field it is\nabout, and the broadcast section moves under Notes. The Developer Portal\nsteps use Reset Token, the reactions intent is described as one the node\nturns on itself, and the MIME fallback examples are .avi and .bmp, which\nthe node''s own table does not list. In services.json, guildIds and\nchannelIds default to [] and the text-attachment and emitNoReply\ndescriptions match the behaviour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): complete the discord docstrings and drop references to a private bot\n\nSeveral Args sections left out parameters the functions take (_on_message\nwait, _process_attachment meta and attachment_index, _run_text_pipeline\nmeta, object_name and attachment_id, _run_binary_pipeline message_id,\nattachment_index and meta, should_process_message parent_channel_id and\nallowed_bot_ids). Comments and docstrings no longer point at functions of\na bot that is not in this repo; the sentences explaining the behaviour\nstay.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(nodes): make the discord live suite neutral and self-describing\n\nThe live README now lists the variables L3 and L4 need on the engine and\nsays ROCKETRIDE_DISCORD_E2E_NO_SUCH_TOKEN must stay unset, and asks for\nPython 3.10+. engine_min.pipe gets a fresh project_id (the SDK''s use()\ncontract carries it at the top level) and a short generic assistant\nprompt with no escalation line. E05 tested that escalation prompt, which\nbelongs to a follow-up change, and E06 was a skipping placeholder; both\nare removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): regenerate the discord README settings table\n\nGenerated by nodes:docs-generate for the new bounds and list defaults\nand the updated textAttachmentExtensions and emitNoReply descriptions.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): wait for discord reaction emits at shutdown and hint ids by kind\n\nA reaction emit already under way was not tracked, so shutdown did not\nwait for it and tore the endpoint down beneath it. The emit is now a\ntracked task, like a message handler. The second closing check before\nthe emit could never fire (nothing can change the flag between the two\nchecks) and its test only passed by setting the flag from a mock; both\nare gone.\n\nA pasted mention in an id list suggested the digits inside it even when\nthey were the id of something else (a role in a channel list, a channel\nin the server list). The hint now gives the digits only for the right\nkind of mention and otherwise says what was pasted.\n\nAlso documents the arguments of _emit_event_pipeline and the return of\n_emit_no_reply_event.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): never cut a discord reply inside a code-fence opener or a word\n\nWhen a chunk window ended inside or just after a code-fence opener line,\nchunk 1 ended with a split language name or an empty code block. A block\nwhose opener line and first code line do not both fit now starts the next\nchunk, and the newline search never cuts right after an opener line.\n\nA reply with no fence also hard-split a line longer than the limit in the\nmiddle of a word. It is now cut at the last sentence end or whitespace in\nthe second half of the window, by character count only when there is none.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(nodes): read discord booleans with the shared parse_bool\n\nThe node had its own _as_bool, which duplicated parse_bool in\nai.common.utils. Every boolean setting now goes through the shared\nhelper. The engine passes JSON booleans as real bool values, so the\ntests that fed string-like proxies to a boolean are replaced with tests\nfor real bools and plain strings.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): keep secrets out of discord id errors and bound id length\n\nA bot token or secret ${ROCKETRIDE_*} value pasted into an id list was\nechoed in full by the start error, the task warnings and the debug log.\nEvery message about a list entry now goes through one helper that shows\na mention whole and anything else longer than 12 characters as its first\n12 characters followed by an ellipsis.\n\nAn id is also bounded to a 64-bit snowflake (at most 20 digits, below\n2**64), so two ids pasted together are rejected as non-numeric on the\nsame fatal or warning path as any other bad entry.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(nodes): treat a non-positive discord attachment cap as unset\n\nmaxAttachmentBytes was floored at 1, but a 1-byte cap skips every real\nattachment just as 0 did. A value of 0 or below now means \"not set\" and\nfalls back to the 25 MB default, matching config_int in ai.common.utils;\nthe value is still clamped to at most 100 MB. The schema keeps its\nminimum of 1.\n\nRaised in review on #1503.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Mithilesh Gaurihar <mithileshgaurihar@Mithileshs-MacBook-Pro.local>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Mithilesh Gaurihar <mithileshgaurihar@Mithileshs-MBP.attlocal.net>\nCo-authored-by: mithileshgau <mithilesh.gaurihar@rocketride.ai>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxXiSCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAotMQAJgVCzLAtA8Qlgi5lQ4KNAHe\ni7UxvlOt2ZAOi85ElhtKb74t9WSyb5k4YEJgYy0Y9i9EGJHMG/qX1WnlLUpJ5Q0B\nt6nnPLkb/gmAzKOASH89cZrvpqDJC9WFNwbRI3BrEFgk0jF6hybx6Pfq8X1j1BfC\nQOj4FziGo8l1hNBUhG2vtYs+Udhi5w49dYvN96FkVGkcGme4Ys0vtdnxNx7nGydL\nZ8NQRewBViqPbtdbmgK0kv7S6elDkzgQ1UfL97VtiAFywiNDe7Llgj8aVCdrDLTC\nX1PpqV980dMCdQowPBcww/wpDV+4Fnw4DJkdIB66bT4ubuf5uGHP2Xsi0rJeo9bY\nKmL53R3usbf7zL50vAfa+LMNRnwJ5KjfbiDZHtSCEtXZZxvN5jblfPOkJDJId/RY\nw2I8nDYCT8Z48LvwFbcRPbIl9lgJCaM+kDct5a6hbQrXiNZ8Wdf0P8tjYNuhfcAg\nt3YfTQmvGhBGnAehtsCgTIYwA9UE8ZEQMXNxvkw5UdZG8ikqrkWj8UEnNI/77xly\nE+fjx8mbodkagmAQAEu02sbLBmdWkkTy70DlmO2+3TDgLOZiYd4ODkBywZOYliA6\nAjkpdS7YKNcBzutG9d1IKLPkgLMzUXO+5ZuQUajo4V58wmBpJt3IFPgzkbiWyH7b\npayxGuDjM6f8/KkV9Vtu\n=0T1g\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T22:39:14Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/9024586e44dc27e0fa08ff56267a62ff47d30bd3","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDkwMjQ1ODZlNDRkYzI3ZTBmYTA4ZmY1NjI2N2E2MmZmNDdkMzBiZDM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/d6329ba538f0626058af4d154f3871edc5ba534a","sha":"d6329ba538f0626058af4d154f3871edc5ba534a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/d6329ba538f0626058af4d154f3871edc5ba534a"}],"sha":"9024586e44dc27e0fa08ff56267a62ff47d30bd3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/9024586e44dc27e0fa08ff56267a62ff47d30bd3"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/181051779?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/krishhgg","id":181051779,"login":"krishhgg","node_id":"U_kgDOCsqhgw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/d6329ba538f0626058af4d154f3871edc5ba534a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T21:51:42Z","email":"kgarg@chapman.edu","name":"Krish Garg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T21:51:42Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(nodes): let Claude on Bedrock cache the unchanging start of an agent''s prompt (#2569)\n\nBuilds on the Anthropic prompt-cache change: ChatBase publishes the start of a\nquestion''s prompt that stays the same between calls (Question.cachePrefix, set\nby the Wave planner), and a driver that supports caching marks it. The\nAnthropic node did; the Bedrock node, which runs the same Claude models, sent\none plain block. Bedrock''s own caching of Claude prompts is best effort, so a\nWave run on Claude through Bedrock could pay for the full prompt every round.\n\n- The Bedrock driver''s request builder splits the last user message at that\n  prefix into two text blocks, the first marked cache_control ephemeral, the\n  same as the Anthropic node. ChatBedrock builds both the invoke and the stream\n  request through _prepare_input_and_invoke(_stream), so both are covered.\n- Only Claude models that accept the marker get it: every Claude from 4 on,\n  Claude 3.7 Sonnet and Claude 3.5 Haiku. Bedrock rejects the request with a\n  ValidationException for older Claude models, and 3.5 Sonnet v2 has caching\n  only as a preview. The gate lists the old models, so a Claude released later\n  is covered without a change. Other providers and Nova get the plain prompt;\n  Nova caches repeated prompt starts on its own.\n- Metering: Bedrock''s input count leaves out cached tokens (AWS: total input\n  = inputTokens + cacheReadInputTokens + cacheWriteInputTokens). langchain-aws\n  adds them back for the Converse API but not for InvokeModel, which\n  ChatBedrock uses for every model but Nova, and the shared meter subtracts\n  the cache detail from input_tokens. A call with 200 fresh and 1,800 cached\n  tokens was metered as 0 fresh input. Claude on Bedrock already caches on its\n  own at times, so this was wrong before this change too. The driver now adds\n  the cached tokens to input_tokens on the InvokeModel paths only.\n\nTests run the real driver and the real langchain-aws client against a local\nserver that plays bedrock-runtime (InvokeModel, the event stream, Converse), so\nthey check the request on the wire and what gets metered. 18 of the 21 fail\nwithout the change. langchain-aws is already in the node test requirements\n(from the token-count fix, #2527), so they run in CI instead of skipping;\nits line now names this test too.\n\nThe node''s token count no longer needs the transformers package since\n#2527; these tests still stub the count, so they test only the cache.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","tree":{"sha":"a16ee7bb0dd3c7b92ab4a97e7457c752d86a8114","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/a16ee7bb0dd3c7b92ab4a97e7457c752d86a8114"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/d6329ba538f0626058af4d154f3871edc5ba534a","verification":{"payload":"tree a16ee7bb0dd3c7b92ab4a97e7457c752d86a8114\nparent 98652da7ee9a2e14dc979309a2fd9d3c1697e711\nauthor Krish Garg <kgarg@chapman.edu> 1791323502 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791323502 +0000\n\nfeat(nodes): let Claude on Bedrock cache the unchanging start of an agent''s prompt (#2569)\n\nBuilds on the Anthropic prompt-cache change: ChatBase publishes the start of a\nquestion''s prompt that stays the same between calls (Question.cachePrefix, set\nby the Wave planner), and a driver that supports caching marks it. The\nAnthropic node did; the Bedrock node, which runs the same Claude models, sent\none plain block. Bedrock''s own caching of Claude prompts is best effort, so a\nWave run on Claude through Bedrock could pay for the full prompt every round.\n\n- The Bedrock driver''s request builder splits the last user message at that\n  prefix into two text blocks, the first marked cache_control ephemeral, the\n  same as the Anthropic node. ChatBedrock builds both the invoke and the stream\n  request through _prepare_input_and_invoke(_stream), so both are covered.\n- Only Claude models that accept the marker get it: every Claude from 4 on,\n  Claude 3.7 Sonnet and Claude 3.5 Haiku. Bedrock rejects the request with a\n  ValidationException for older Claude models, and 3.5 Sonnet v2 has caching\n  only as a preview. The gate lists the old models, so a Claude released later\n  is covered without a change. Other providers and Nova get the plain prompt;\n  Nova caches repeated prompt starts on its own.\n- Metering: Bedrock''s input count leaves out cached tokens (AWS: total input\n  = inputTokens + cacheReadInputTokens + cacheWriteInputTokens). langchain-aws\n  adds them back for the Converse API but not for InvokeModel, which\n  ChatBedrock uses for every model but Nova, and the shared meter subtracts\n  the cache detail from input_tokens. A call with 200 fresh and 1,800 cached\n  tokens was metered as 0 fresh input. Claude on Bedrock already caches on its\n  own at times, so this was wrong before this change too. The driver now adds\n  the cached tokens to input_tokens on the InvokeModel paths only.\n\nTests run the real driver and the real langchain-aws client against a local\nserver that plays bedrock-runtime (InvokeModel, the event stream, Converse), so\nthey check the request on the wire and what gets metered. 18 of the 21 fail\nwithout the change. langchain-aws is already in the node test requirements\n(from the token-count fix, #2527), so they run in CI instead of skipping;\nits line now names this test too.\n\nThe node''s token count no longer needs the transformers package since\n#2527; these tests still stub the count, so they test only the cache.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxW1uCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAATX8QAEsfrgffBeETXY5iH1rzXelR\nGx3ndJtx2PJe5MAF9q33zRxF1EWt5n7Ff0h3v7mIkAqWx5JWUPbUchH0LEHjw7KQ\nuMGxb44B/J6qNtckSNhZGDXiQTGXaxAvrqlsC1V/WoF9Jmn4KBHSZ/947SdUVwXr\noNkEbOMFH80kFuCEl3yMpjHQ0I4k6QSdTdK3kd7E1oj9bzfR+UtuJa4OWoMiO+C0\nq9TtERO2dhz8Uq3QWoVuhfNrYnN2L71CGFUBJKKCpQ2VmfCji1qXFSgLXCXwFFJ4\nAEuatusalAlogcyU4Z/8GsBpdjkdbgP5kHKrvqNEHHWttyXqtFhHznfwwHb73GTE\nXIuQVmYsgRhvVvgRgTvfaroyy9KxwzhlZROwPzzVKmZVcHfx9If89vi3/fU6DjeN\nO7VxHk+hg+bieC9Vpg53RIlCM/RfGUwfg8wiJO0oVbLqE9co+Q5Hfua9SyTYV4da\n6s7sLG+bGH+T19reUgrMKDFbm9jawH8iWLf1Ex7sAihJ/e1yZ/tA9EvfDAF/muPP\n4nACOV0KoRRQZg2iOUaZabNn8Q6lMgoI5BKTqSVLtTZ5zELhm4sbG13q1tHzsI1Y\nhBFVMy3LprYzVDAHfgaMqjqGktL5+L5yt1zVzR9BKiqA16Kek8NXw/0kMxM9XXJr\nBYNm7MVXmte3H85eJchk\n=bCTr\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T21:51:42Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/d6329ba538f0626058af4d154f3871edc5ba534a","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKGQ2MzI5YmE1MzhmMDYyNjA1OGFmNGQxNTRmMzg3MWVkYzViYTUzNGE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/98652da7ee9a2e14dc979309a2fd9d3c1697e711","sha":"98652da7ee9a2e14dc979309a2fd9d3c1697e711","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/98652da7ee9a2e14dc979309a2fd9d3c1697e711"}],"sha":"d6329ba538f0626058af4d154f3871edc5ba534a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/d6329ba538f0626058af4d154f3871edc5ba534a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/181051779?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/krishhgg","id":181051779,"login":"krishhgg","node_id":"U_kgDOCsqhgw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/98652da7ee9a2e14dc979309a2fd9d3c1697e711/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T21:34:03Z","email":"kgarg@chapman.edu","name":"Krish Garg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T21:34:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(agent_rocketride): check the shape of every plan before acting on it (#2568)\n\nW4. The loop trusted each planning reply as sent, so ordinary format slips\nchanged what happened:\n- \"done\": \"false\" ended the run, because a non-empty string is truthy;\n- a reply that set done=true and also asked for calls returned its answer\n  without running the calls, so \"renamed the button\" came back while the\n  file was unchanged;\n- one call written as plain text crashed the run with\n  \"''str'' object has no attribute ''get''\";\n- a call in OpenAI''s function-call shape ({\"name\", \"arguments\": \"<json>\"}),\n  which a model trained on function calling can send even when asked for\n  {\"tool\", \"args\"}, failed as \"Tool not found\";\n- a reply with neither done nor calls went straight to the fallback.\n\nnormalize_plan now checks every reply once: done counts by meaning, both\nOpenAI call shapes are read, a single call or remove key is wrapped in a\nlist, and anything it must drop is reported. done is not done when the\nanswer is blank or when any of the reply''s calls had to be dropped, since\nthe work it describes did not all happen. When a reply has nothing to run\nand is not done, the planner asks once more and says exactly what was\nwrong. Calls dropped from a usable reply appear in the next prompt as\nerrors; ignored remove entries appear as notes (in the trace, when the\nreply ends the run), and when even the retry is unusable its problems are\nrecorded before the fallback answer (in the trace and in that answer''s\nprompt), so nothing disappears silently.\n\nA reply that sets done=true and asks for calls now runs the calls first.\nIf all of them succeed, its answer stands with no extra round; if any\nfails, the model sees why and continues. A call fails when it raises, or\nwhen it returns a failure such as {\"ok\": false}, {\"isError\": true} (MCP),\nan HTTP status of 400 or more (tool_http_request returns error responses\nas normal results), an agent called as a tool whose run raised or whose\ntool-call guard tripped (its answer''s stack holds RocketRide.agent.error.v1\nor .guard.v1), a non-zero \"exit_code\" (true included: tool_python keeps\nSystemExit(True) as is) or \"timed_out\": true (tool_python, tool_daytona), a tool_python script whose own \"result\" (a dict or a list)\nreports a failure, a list of errors (tool_vertex_search), a list in which\nany item says outright that it failed (ok or success false, isError), or\nan \"error\" that holds text, a structure or true (a number there is a\nmeasurement, not a failure): such results are now marked \"failed\" in the\nprompt, and in the fallback answer''s list of results. Each tool''s\nfields count only on that tool''s result shape (the runner fields on one\nwith stdout, stderr or output; the status on one with status_code,\nstatus_text and headers), and a list of plain error fields only when it\nholds nothing but error entries, so data such as log rows with an error column, a CI job''s exit\ncode or a record''s status_code is not taken for a failure. A 404 the model\nexpected still counts: that costs one more round, while missing a real\nfailure would report work that never happened. A script result that holds\nitself is followed once, not forever. A\ntool_calls value that is not a list of calls, even an empty object, is\nchecked like a call, so it can never make a done reply''s work vanish; a\nremove value that is not a key or a list of keys, even an empty object or\nfalse, is reported the same way. A blank remove key is dropped and\nreported too: the memory node reads a clear with no key as \"clear\neverything\".\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","tree":{"sha":"b4da0206b8197c12fb812c58d322a1a87611aada","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/b4da0206b8197c12fb812c58d322a1a87611aada"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/98652da7ee9a2e14dc979309a2fd9d3c1697e711","verification":{"payload":"tree b4da0206b8197c12fb812c58d322a1a87611aada\nparent 81ecaaf079cccc9bf7b16b557529713e80012549\nauthor Krish Garg <kgarg@chapman.edu> 1791322443 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791322443 +0000\n\nfix(agent_rocketride): check the shape of every plan before acting on it (#2568)\n\nW4. The loop trusted each planning reply as sent, so ordinary format slips\nchanged what happened:\n- \"done\": \"false\" ended the run, because a non-empty string is truthy;\n- a reply that set done=true and also asked for calls returned its answer\n  without running the calls, so \"renamed the button\" came back while the\n  file was unchanged;\n- one call written as plain text crashed the run with\n  \"''str'' object has no attribute ''get''\";\n- a call in OpenAI''s function-call shape ({\"name\", \"arguments\": \"<json>\"}),\n  which a model trained on function calling can send even when asked for\n  {\"tool\", \"args\"}, failed as \"Tool not found\";\n- a reply with neither done nor calls went straight to the fallback.\n\nnormalize_plan now checks every reply once: done counts by meaning, both\nOpenAI call shapes are read, a single call or remove key is wrapped in a\nlist, and anything it must drop is reported. done is not done when the\nanswer is blank or when any of the reply''s calls had to be dropped, since\nthe work it describes did not all happen. When a reply has nothing to run\nand is not done, the planner asks once more and says exactly what was\nwrong. Calls dropped from a usable reply appear in the next prompt as\nerrors; ignored remove entries appear as notes (in the trace, when the\nreply ends the run), and when even the retry is unusable its problems are\nrecorded before the fallback answer (in the trace and in that answer''s\nprompt), so nothing disappears silently.\n\nA reply that sets done=true and asks for calls now runs the calls first.\nIf all of them succeed, its answer stands with no extra round; if any\nfails, the model sees why and continues. A call fails when it raises, or\nwhen it returns a failure such as {\"ok\": false}, {\"isError\": true} (MCP),\nan HTTP status of 400 or more (tool_http_request returns error responses\nas normal results), an agent called as a tool whose run raised or whose\ntool-call guard tripped (its answer''s stack holds RocketRide.agent.error.v1\nor .guard.v1), a non-zero \"exit_code\" (true included: tool_python keeps\nSystemExit(True) as is) or \"timed_out\": true (tool_python, tool_daytona), a tool_python script whose own \"result\" (a dict or a list)\nreports a failure, a list of errors (tool_vertex_search), a list in which\nany item says outright that it failed (ok or success false, isError), or\nan \"error\" that holds text, a structure or true (a number there is a\nmeasurement, not a failure): such results are now marked \"failed\" in the\nprompt, and in the fallback answer''s list of results. Each tool''s\nfields count only on that tool''s result shape (the runner fields on one\nwith stdout, stderr or output; the status on one with status_code,\nstatus_text and headers), and a list of plain error fields only when it\nholds nothing but error entries, so data such as log rows with an error column, a CI job''s exit\ncode or a record''s status_code is not taken for a failure. A 404 the model\nexpected still counts: that costs one more round, while missing a real\nfailure would report work that never happened. A script result that holds\nitself is followed once, not forever. A\ntool_calls value that is not a list of calls, even an empty object, is\nchecked like a call, so it can never make a done reply''s work vanish; a\nremove value that is not a key or a list of keys, even an empty object or\nfalse, is reported the same way. A blank remove key is dropped and\nreported too: the memory node reads a clear with no key as \"clear\neverything\".\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxWlLCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA6Q4QAKU9juiZdGrqyac694pnEoUL\nCqDxF6VslF8trNae3L2jFv4SeFQoeZmZlzWOU8rxkKWt5avWrHIJP91eue33EYk/\nNXL8OCskVt1TonlWTWwg3OW9h3ILCT2RX1hhyR0oYPWm0OQfynp4xPN9C80MdCZ2\numltEJpzSdLDLNHnhNZsgSw3rJMWtCKxzpLY0XVPXMA59nMnCtLuV7bta/jfIb28\nuOb2QVeRaU7oqhyabeueuqAaYVYIbI8KhcweNNJWXfdy2YlUGZQ17RTboaGvVXvT\nlIosiwcyPj0EQ0wbfTs/5GlH0YPslOzRFHn94eSzT/K7eASapdfhAX7txPGHxaWS\nZ89L3/Ru/3ZKbr6OZMrd7E5xHumdoEV5CVo8chNL7xGr2F9W7hzAZrOFWi41nvPu\nzP+CkOxWd8mBaQre6QavSavba5BEjwyiXVauhWnHI27GuXd/jJUTcSE/Xv3OJAcs\nVBXkr3DVShAcIcRkwefMurLxNSb4EKbkLrdyiwfWmoc9bXjw73FLhjdn3gERxgI/\nCgQcXPWcY8E0a1+JGjl6+EOraHMGoCnkLJBpoUSxbgVhbNDDlbjdXJTaTJiKg5dz\npSYgN7Vxwtn2uulVSalFgEkg2F+TOZ7YT8ypdVkGWunuOovR/uUt8DilgE94i7yp\nGrH1tyEW+6/ATP9pEOqW\n=59Fs\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T21:34:03Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/98652da7ee9a2e14dc979309a2fd9d3c1697e711","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDk4NjUyZGE3ZWU5YTJlMTRkYzk3OTMwOWEyZmQ5ZDNjMTY5N2U3MTE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/81ecaaf079cccc9bf7b16b557529713e80012549","sha":"81ecaaf079cccc9bf7b16b557529713e80012549","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/81ecaaf079cccc9bf7b16b557529713e80012549"}],"sha":"98652da7ee9a2e14dc979309a2fd9d3c1697e711","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/98652da7ee9a2e14dc979309a2fd9d3c1697e711"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/181051779?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/krishhgg","id":181051779,"login":"krishhgg","node_id":"U_kgDOCsqhgw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/81ecaaf079cccc9bf7b16b557529713e80012549/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T20:58:18Z","email":"kgarg@chapman.edu","name":"Krish Garg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T20:58:18Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(ai): let Anthropic cache the unchanging start of an agent''s prompt (#2525)\n\nAn agent loop resends its whole prompt every round, and most of it (role,\ntool list, memory and format instructions, rules) never changes within a\nrun. OpenAI caches a repeated prefix on its own (62-89% of Wave''s input\ntokens were cache hits with GPT-5.5). Anthropic caches only up to an\nexplicit cache_control marker, and ChatBase sends the prompt as one plain\nstring, so every Wave round with Claude paid for the full prompt: 0 cached\ntokens on every run, with about 10 KB of a 12.8 KB prompt unchanged.\n\n- Question.cachePrefix (default False) lets a caller say the prompt will be\n  resent with only its context, documents, goals or questions changed. The\n  Wave planner sets it.\n- ChatBase.chat renders the question without those parts, which getPrompt\n  always places after role, instructions, examples and history, so the\n  result is a prefix of the full prompt. It publishes it on a contextvar\n  (like STOP_SEQUENCES_VAR) for its first model call only: a JSON repair\n  prompt inserts an instruction near the top, so a cache write there would\n  never be read back.\n- The Anthropic driver''s request builder splits the user message there\n  into two text blocks, the first marked ephemeral. Invoke, the LangChain\n  stream and the native thinking stream all build requests through it.\n- Usage metering counts cache writes that langchain-anthropic reports per\n  lifetime (ephemeral_5m/1h_input_tokens, with cache_creation 0), which\n  were otherwise billed as fresh input.\n\nEvery other provider receives the same prompt string as before. A model\nwhose minimum cacheable length is longer than the prefix simply does not\ncache it, at no extra cost.\nA cache write costs 1.25x, so a run must reach a second round to come out\nahead; Wave runs nearly always do.\n\nThe TypeScript Question gains the same field, so the two clients keep the\nsame fields: a cachePrefix constructor option, carried through toDict and\nfromDict. Both clients document it in reference.md and chat.md, and the\ndocs parity check covers it.\n\nlangchain-anthropic is added to the node test requirements: the wire test\nimports it, and without the entry it would skip in CI. Installing it also\ninstalls langchain_core; test_agent_langchain_parser.py already compares\nagainst the AIMessage class the parser uses (fixed with the Bedrock\ntoken-count change, #2527), so collecting this test first is safe.\n\nMerge note: the JSON-reply PR also wraps the first chat_string call in\nChatBase.chat with a context variable. Whichever lands second keeps both\nwrappers around that call.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","tree":{"sha":"7d17e678979b2656bc91f37beef7600c0721771f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/7d17e678979b2656bc91f37beef7600c0721771f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/81ecaaf079cccc9bf7b16b557529713e80012549","verification":{"payload":"tree 7d17e678979b2656bc91f37beef7600c0721771f\nparent ade5dbccf8346421a6417b190138371bb46588af\nauthor Krish Garg <kgarg@chapman.edu> 1791320298 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791320298 -0700\n\nfeat(ai): let Anthropic cache the unchanging start of an agent''s prompt (#2525)\n\nAn agent loop resends its whole prompt every round, and most of it (role,\ntool list, memory and format instructions, rules) never changes within a\nrun. OpenAI caches a repeated prefix on its own (62-89% of Wave''s input\ntokens were cache hits with GPT-5.5). Anthropic caches only up to an\nexplicit cache_control marker, and ChatBase sends the prompt as one plain\nstring, so every Wave round with Claude paid for the full prompt: 0 cached\ntokens on every run, with about 10 KB of a 12.8 KB prompt unchanged.\n\n- Question.cachePrefix (default False) lets a caller say the prompt will be\n  resent with only its context, documents, goals or questions changed. The\n  Wave planner sets it.\n- ChatBase.chat renders the question without those parts, which getPrompt\n  always places after role, instructions, examples and history, so the\n  result is a prefix of the full prompt. It publishes it on a contextvar\n  (like STOP_SEQUENCES_VAR) for its first model call only: a JSON repair\n  prompt inserts an instruction near the top, so a cache write there would\n  never be read back.\n- The Anthropic driver''s request builder splits the user message there\n  into two text blocks, the first marked ephemeral. Invoke, the LangChain\n  stream and the native thinking stream all build requests through it.\n- Usage metering counts cache writes that langchain-anthropic reports per\n  lifetime (ephemeral_5m/1h_input_tokens, with cache_creation 0), which\n  were otherwise billed as fresh input.\n\nEvery other provider receives the same prompt string as before. A model\nwhose minimum cacheable length is longer than the prefix simply does not\ncache it, at no extra cost.\nA cache write costs 1.25x, so a run must reach a second round to come out\nahead; Wave runs nearly always do.\n\nThe TypeScript Question gains the same field, so the two clients keep the\nsame fields: a cachePrefix constructor option, carried through toDict and\nfromDict. Both clients document it in reference.md and chat.md, and the\ndocs parity check covers it.\n\nlangchain-anthropic is added to the node test requirements: the wire test\nimports it, and without the entry it would skip in CI. Installing it also\ninstalls langchain_core; test_agent_langchain_parser.py already compares\nagainst the AIMessage class the parser uses (fixed with the Bedrock\ntoken-count change, #2527), so collecting this test first is safe.\n\nMerge note: the JSON-reply PR also wraps the first chat_string call in\nChatBase.chat with a context variable. Whichever lands second keeps both\nwrappers around that call.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxWDqCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAV6MQAIHQD0CZa1q6qLI/1lqe9R3j\nlFQRC+Lr+gqBMAKNBtUzjZ7L7yesl49u8I+bRnhzuqqvDvwOMIQEKvrPRlkZXQv1\nEC72UKLp6QZthdbhbbmCbhaVVy6cSI0o831TLGBVujXcfz+cy14hiNA5NyYBOoBA\nM5gKDaR3PMeY2t4o2NwA6iTTk8T/FPnEZ619pJoQtSglzOVhaGoTuSHHJuYDgxNx\nuubuslfYeFi9SeixUUZScTFdKcJpbkFww75csK0K1o2Q3hUydkBqM/swvm+9Nd2+\nmLng2bdl/P8DGbGzQfdMIKizYJR4GvFtjfOwH9Pmg14uZsSv9ZWMWjQACMK8DLZx\nnPqXAU94cczmvs/LgHdHAcuqosS8oIRN4cC3lOz3cYn6QamZQExoytQlRJutAbQp\nbn6A0wAD6Xnt0QvVsM3c0uqxyBF6Z53ZNy+6jFMvfvZBGFisNYjmOoc9EBLCWnRk\nUfnHmRGx7ee6APrB0zRNJG3QmjQ7sE9yq5ArrGld8xyoJTxqr58YcWmcIFgD6WjF\ntpGNHH2ZVoVp4g3zDLlZF7B7U6N4ayogROs48Beg78mG+uW7sYbHlDkTvBaBWr6C\ng0PzqOK8AhWrwfjLReNI0Ih8simmzUdGCEnSLH4wJBYIMFnazrqncGnWiPWEpqVL\nmde0aIO3MISh14x7lpnJ\n=0cEf\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T20:58:19Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/81ecaaf079cccc9bf7b16b557529713e80012549","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDgxZWNhYWYwNzljY2NjOWJmN2IxNmI1NTc1Mjk3MTNlODAwMTI1NDk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/ade5dbccf8346421a6417b190138371bb46588af","sha":"ade5dbccf8346421a6417b190138371bb46588af","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/ade5dbccf8346421a6417b190138371bb46588af"}],"sha":"81ecaaf079cccc9bf7b16b557529713e80012549","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/81ecaaf079cccc9bf7b16b557529713e80012549"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/181051779?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/krishhgg","id":181051779,"login":"krishhgg","node_id":"U_kgDOCsqhgw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/krishhgg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/ade5dbccf8346421a6417b190138371bb46588af/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T20:57:52Z","email":"kgarg@chapman.edu","name":"Krish Garg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T20:57:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(agent_rocketride): show whole tool results and send instructions once (#2517)\n\nW1. Every text field in a tool result was cut to its first 80 characters before\nthe planner saw it. A file read is one long string, so the model saw a line\nor two of each file and either guessed the rest or spent a round peeking.\nText now gets its share of the 4,000-character summary budget; past that,\nits start and end are kept and the middle is dropped, with the full length\nreported so the model can page through the rest. Text inside sampled list\nrows is still cut at 80 characters, so the rows of the window tuned in #2072\n(for #2032) stay narrow.\n\nThe summary is built in two passes over each container. The first sizes\nevery field exactly (its key, its separator, its length note or list header,\nand the short fields of whatever it nests) without rendering it. The second\ngives each field a room and renders it inside that room. A container keeps\nto its room by leaving out whole fields or whole rows, never by cutting a\nrendered string, so no parent cuts a child again, a list''s header counts the\nrows under it, and a short field such as an exit code is shown whole or not\nat all.\n\nA dict allocates its room once. Its keys and short fields (scalars, strings\nof up to 80 characters, empty and small containers) are paid for first,\nfields named like a status (exit code, status, ok, error, stderr) ahead of\nthe rest; then each long text''s length note and each container''s header or\nnotice; then the short fields of nested dicts and of short lists'' rows. What\nis left is split between the long texts and the containers, and a field\nthat needs less than an equal share takes only what it needs, so a page\ntoken beside a file list costs the list only the token''s length and the row\nwindow stays as tuned. When the shares get too small for a text to show\nanything, the text shows its length instead. A list of one or two dicts,\nsuch as an MCP tool''s content blocks, is shown whole: its rows split its\nroom the same way, each row''s own short fields paid first. A longer list\nshows its field names and as many whole rows as fit, in order, and its\nheader counts them; no row is shown partly. A plain list''s first three items\nare rendered the same way, so a huge item is cut like any text, a datetime\nor a cycle is summarised instead of raising, and inside a sampled row its\nstrings stay at 80 characters. A list inside that sample, such as a row of\na Google Sheets values_get result, shows as many of its items as fit its\nroom, so a header row is shown whole. The summary stops six levels down.\n\nThe budget is what texts and lists share. Short fields, keys and notes can\ntake a summary past it, up to the 6,000-character cap; past the cap a dict\nkeeps the fields that fit and says how many it left out. Two things no room\nbounds are cut at the cap as a backstop, with a note to peek: a list''s\nfield names, which are written in full, and a value that is not JSON,\nwhich is written with str() (bytes, a tuple). The cut keeps the last line\ndescribed below whole. Whatever is left\nout is recorded by path, and the short fields among them (an exit code, an\nok flag, a short error, an empty container, the fields of nested dicts and\nof short lists'' rows, six levels down at most) are listed on one last line\nwithin 400 characters kept under the cap: status-like names first, then the\nshortest entries, an entry too long for the line skipped rather than ending\nit, keys quoted when they hold a dot or a colon, and a count of what the\nline could not hold. The line remembers at most 5,000 left-out fields.\nNothing is found by searching the rendered text, so a status that looks the\nsame elsewhere, a line in a log or a row label inside a message cannot be\nmistaken for the real thing, and a 5,000-field result costs milliseconds\nrather than most of a second.\n\nA seeded test checks the room invariant over 300 random results, another\nchecks that a status survives 300 random mixes of long texts, wide rows, MCP\nblocks and nested dicts, and tests for each finding of the final review\ncover the cases earlier versions got wrong.\n\nW2. AgentBase.run_agent already adds the node''s configured instructions to\nthe question; the planner added them a second time, so every planning\nprompt carried two copies. The planner''s copy and its now-unused\n`instructions` parameter are removed.\n\nBoth fixes were first proposed and measured live by Dylan Savage (Sonnet\n4.6: 22/26 to 26/26 task passes). This version differs from his in keeping\nlist rows at 80 characters and in splitting the budget between long fields.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","tree":{"sha":"7b2da8725fcee12df751c476510b32386bf5b4fe","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/7b2da8725fcee12df751c476510b32386bf5b4fe"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/ade5dbccf8346421a6417b190138371bb46588af","verification":{"payload":"tree 7b2da8725fcee12df751c476510b32386bf5b4fe\nparent cf74d1711bded5d6775725ea6705d5e5aafe80ad\nauthor Krish Garg <kgarg@chapman.edu> 1791320272 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791320272 -0700\n\nfix(agent_rocketride): show whole tool results and send instructions once (#2517)\n\nW1. Every text field in a tool result was cut to its first 80 characters before\nthe planner saw it. A file read is one long string, so the model saw a line\nor two of each file and either guessed the rest or spent a round peeking.\nText now gets its share of the 4,000-character summary budget; past that,\nits start and end are kept and the middle is dropped, with the full length\nreported so the model can page through the rest. Text inside sampled list\nrows is still cut at 80 characters, so the rows of the window tuned in #2072\n(for #2032) stay narrow.\n\nThe summary is built in two passes over each container. The first sizes\nevery field exactly (its key, its separator, its length note or list header,\nand the short fields of whatever it nests) without rendering it. The second\ngives each field a room and renders it inside that room. A container keeps\nto its room by leaving out whole fields or whole rows, never by cutting a\nrendered string, so no parent cuts a child again, a list''s header counts the\nrows under it, and a short field such as an exit code is shown whole or not\nat all.\n\nA dict allocates its room once. Its keys and short fields (scalars, strings\nof up to 80 characters, empty and small containers) are paid for first,\nfields named like a status (exit code, status, ok, error, stderr) ahead of\nthe rest; then each long text''s length note and each container''s header or\nnotice; then the short fields of nested dicts and of short lists'' rows. What\nis left is split between the long texts and the containers, and a field\nthat needs less than an equal share takes only what it needs, so a page\ntoken beside a file list costs the list only the token''s length and the row\nwindow stays as tuned. When the shares get too small for a text to show\nanything, the text shows its length instead. A list of one or two dicts,\nsuch as an MCP tool''s content blocks, is shown whole: its rows split its\nroom the same way, each row''s own short fields paid first. A longer list\nshows its field names and as many whole rows as fit, in order, and its\nheader counts them; no row is shown partly. A plain list''s first three items\nare rendered the same way, so a huge item is cut like any text, a datetime\nor a cycle is summarised instead of raising, and inside a sampled row its\nstrings stay at 80 characters. A list inside that sample, such as a row of\na Google Sheets values_get result, shows as many of its items as fit its\nroom, so a header row is shown whole. The summary stops six levels down.\n\nThe budget is what texts and lists share. Short fields, keys and notes can\ntake a summary past it, up to the 6,000-character cap; past the cap a dict\nkeeps the fields that fit and says how many it left out. Two things no room\nbounds are cut at the cap as a backstop, with a note to peek: a list''s\nfield names, which are written in full, and a value that is not JSON,\nwhich is written with str() (bytes, a tuple). The cut keeps the last line\ndescribed below whole. Whatever is left\nout is recorded by path, and the short fields among them (an exit code, an\nok flag, a short error, an empty container, the fields of nested dicts and\nof short lists'' rows, six levels down at most) are listed on one last line\nwithin 400 characters kept under the cap: status-like names first, then the\nshortest entries, an entry too long for the line skipped rather than ending\nit, keys quoted when they hold a dot or a colon, and a count of what the\nline could not hold. The line remembers at most 5,000 left-out fields.\nNothing is found by searching the rendered text, so a status that looks the\nsame elsewhere, a line in a log or a row label inside a message cannot be\nmistaken for the real thing, and a 5,000-field result costs milliseconds\nrather than most of a second.\n\nA seeded test checks the room invariant over 300 random results, another\nchecks that a status survives 300 random mixes of long texts, wide rows, MCP\nblocks and nested dicts, and tests for each finding of the final review\ncover the cases earlier versions got wrong.\n\nW2. AgentBase.run_agent already adds the node''s configured instructions to\nthe question; the planner added them a second time, so every planning\nprompt carried two copies. The planner''s copy and its now-unused\n`instructions` parameter are removed.\n\nBoth fixes were first proposed and measured live by Dylan Savage (Sonnet\n4.6: 22/26 to 26/26 task passes). This version differs from his in keeping\nlist rows at 80 characters and in splitting the budget between long fields.\n\nCo-authored-by: Krish Garg <kg@g14-node1.tailb99b1b.ts.net>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxWDQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA8cgQAGwkaoNiC