regression tests, which fail on the\nprevious code with ''city_1'' twice and [1, 3] respectively.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(db_hotdata): retry on the statement-error contract, not a bare 400\n\nReview catch. _run_sql drives four endpoints - create_database, query,\nget_query_run, get_result - and `status_code == 400` treated a failure\nfrom any of them as SQL the model could rewrite. A bad ttl, a malformed\nlimit or a missing scoping header would each burn every LLM attempt and\nthen be replaced by \"could not answer after N attempts\", which is the\nexact masking this branch set out to remove.\n\nThe server distinguishes the two itself: it answers with a query_run_id\nonly once it has created a run and executed the statement. Verified\nagainst the live API - missing table, parse error and unknown function\nall return 400 with a run id; bad ttl and out-of-range limit return 400\nwithout one.\n\nHotdataError now carries query_run_id off the error body, and\n_is_sql_fixable requires it. SqlStatementError still covers the deferred\npath, where a terminal run failure is reported in the poll body with no\nHTTP status to read.\n\nFour tests: the client lifts the run id when present and leaves it empty\nwhen absent, and get_data retries the first while raising the second\nas-is without spending a second LLM call.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(db_hotdata): close out the review - signature, schema, docs, rendering\n\nReview catch, plus a full pass over the branch for the same class of\nproblem so they land together rather than one per round.\n\nget_result kept `offset` and `limit` in their original positions.\nDatabase scoping had been inserted ahead of them, so a positional\nget_result(result_id, offset, limit) written against the old signature\nwould have sent its offset as the X-Database-Id header. `database_id` is\nnow keyword-only and last.\n\nservices.json advertised `minimum: 0` for async_after_ms while the node\nfloors it at 1000, so the form accepted a value the node then silently\noverrode. Both now read 1000 - the smallest the API accepts - and a test\nties the schema to the constant so they cannot drift apart.\n\nA cancelled run is no longer classified as a statement failure. A\nrewritten query cannot un-cancel a run, so it must not earn an LLM turn.\n\nTwo rendering bugs that only became reachable once rows were objects:\nheader cells were not escaped, so an alias such as \"a|b\" split its\ncolumn in two, and SQL NULL rendered as the Python word \"None\". Headers\ngo through _cell like body cells, and NULL is an empty cell.\n\nREADME: get_data retries a rejected statement, not any failure, and the\ntroubleshooting table says why a 401/404 surfaces on the first attempt.\n\nCorrects a claim made earlier on this branch. The docstrings cited\n`SELECT city, city` as the source of repeated column names; the planner\nrejects that outright. Verified against the live API, the real source is\na join - `SELECT a.city, b.city` answers [''city'', ''city''] and `SELECT *`\nover a self-join answers [''city'', ''units'', ''city'', ''units'']. The naming\nhelper is needed for exactly that, and a test now pins the live shape.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(db_hotdata): feed the real failure reason back for a deferred run\n\nFound by driving the deferred path against the live API, which no test\nhad done: every earlier live query was small enough to return inline.\n\nA run that fails after deferring reports its cause as `error_message`:\n\n    {\"status\": \"failed\",\n     \"error_message\": \"query execution failed: Arrow error: Divide by zero error\"}\n\n_await_run read `error` or `message`, found neither, and fell back to the\nstatus, so the exception read \"query failed: failed\". That text is what\nthe model is handed for its corrective attempt, so every deferred\nfailure was being retried blind. The jobs poller in the same file\nalready reads `error_message`; this was the one place with guessed keys,\ncovered by a mock that encoded the guess.\n\n_run_failure_text reads `error_message` first, then `error` as text or\nas the {\"message\": ...} object the query endpoint uses, then `message`.\n\nVerified live with a 20M-row window query that divides by zero on its\nlast row: the run deferred, polled four times, failed, and the caller\nnow receives the Arrow error. The same session confirmed the deferred\nsuccess path (query -> 4x get_query_run -> get_result) and the truncated\npath (query truncated=true -> get_result) end to end for the first time.\n\nAlso adds docstrings to the functions this branch touches; coverage on\ntouched functions goes from 66% to 90%.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(db_hotdata): wait for the run before reading a truncated result\n\nAddresses the review on #2372, read against the published contract at\nhttps://www.hotdata.dev/openapi.yaml.\n\nA result that was still being saved came back as an empty answer. A\ntruncated /v1/query response always carries a result_id, so the old\n`result_id is None` test never waited for it - and per QueryResponse\nthat id \"is issued while the save is still in flight, so on its own it\ndoes not mean the result can be retrieved\". Reading it early answers 202\n{status, result_id} with no rows; _request only raises from 400 up, so\n_run_sql returned zero rows for a result large enough to be truncated.\nReproduced live with an 8M-row query: 200 truncated, then 202\n\"processing\", then 200 once the run had succeeded. _run_sql now waits on\nthe run whenever the server deferred or truncated, and get_result raises\non a pending/processing body instead of letting it read as empty.\n\n`interrupted` is a documented terminal run status the poll loop did not\nknow, so it was polled until job_timeout_secs ran out and reported as a\ntimeout that never happened. It is now terminal and raised as a plain\nRuntimeError, not SqlStatementError: a rewritten statement cannot\nun-interrupt a run. `cancelled`/`canceled` were never seen live; the\ncomment on the set says so.\n\n_query_run_id reads inside the `error` object as well as beside it, the\nway _error_code does, since the documented error body is\n{\"error\": {code, message}}.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Update nodes/src/nodes/db_hotdata/IInstance.py\n\nCo-authored-by: Alexandru Sclearuc <asclearuc@gmail.com>\n\n* fix(db_hotdata): run an interrupted query once more, as the contract says\n\nAddresses the three notes left with the approval on #2372.\n\n`interrupted` was classified correctly - terminal, not the statement''s\nfault, never SqlStatementError - but nothing acted on the other half of\nthe contract, which calls it \"terminal, and safe to retry\". The caller\nlost a whole tool call because Hotdata replaced a server, and so did\n`execute` and `get_schema`, which share the path.\n\n_run_sql now runs the same statement once more when the run comes back\ninterrupted. It is bounded at one re-run, so a run interrupted every\ntime still ends; it sends the same SQL, so it costs no LLM turn; and it\nis safe to send twice because only read-only statements reach it.\n_await_run signals the case with its own RunInterrupted type, a\nRuntimeError, so `cancelled` and failed statements are never re-run and\neverything that already treats it as \"not the SQL''s fault\" still does.\n\nPins the suggestion applied in e5fa0d8f: a run that reaches a\nterminal-OK status without a result_id still reads the id the query\nanswered with, instead of falling through to an empty result. The test\nfails on c6282753 and passes from e5fa0d8f.\n\nSettles the result_id handed out on the inline fast path. It is issued\nwhile the save is still in flight, and it is safe to hand out anyway:\nthe loads endpoint documents 409 \"the result is still being computed\"\nrather than loading part of a result, and three loads issued right\nbehind a 9,500-row query each returned all 9,500 rows. The code says so\nwhere the id is returned.\n\nThe re-run is covered by mocks only - a server replacement cannot be\nprovoked from outside - so it rests on the spec''s wording.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(db_hotdata): a run is finished when its status says so\n\nAddresses the review on #2372 at 1c67cd9e.\n\nBlocking: _await_run returned as soon as a run named a result_id, even\nwhile it was still `running`. The spec''s `running` covers \"still\nexecuting, or still being saved\", so that id names a result that is not\nready; a read against it answers 202, which raises and fails the whole\nquestion. Truncated results have come through this path since c6282753,\nwhich is what made it reachable. It now returns on the status alone.\n\nA run that ends succeeded with no result_id \"saved nothing\", so the id on\nthe query body names a result that was never saved. e5fa0d8f fell back\nto reading that id, which answers 404 or 202. The statement did succeed,\nand a truncated response carries a preview of its rows, so that preview\nis returned instead, cut to the limit, and no result_id is offered. A\ndeferred query has nothing inline, so it raises with the run''s\nwarning_message rather than report an empty result.\n\nNot changed, deliberately: a `failed` run behind a truncated preview is\nstill handed to the model as a statement failure. The preview is\nstreamed before the statement finishes, so it can fail on a later row.\nChecked live - an 8M-row query whose last row divides by zero answered\n200 truncated with 10,000 rows in 0.3s, and its run then ended failed\nwith \"query execution failed: Arrow error: Divide by zero error\". A\nrewrite fixes that. `failed` also covers a failed save, but only\nerror_message tells the two apart, and that is prose, not contract. The\ncode and a test now record this.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Alexandru Sclearuc <asclearuc@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsWQsCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0X0QAAHBGz6kpbIQDsSW/0E3IJph\ni4tWeqDnfUj7RLQRUHaYTDIX3/cfMaugfe9wJaeQFQxO6kH+4YQCHKq/ikruk/fD\ncL+R9DtBODZNwVVYzNyh0rSZa4bQE9kjCcLcF+cG6CgVAIgycKZrc6g4U44HxFjB\nNn9LgFWl/RBVjNvWgoWeUczixkZNEzYSwudy5BH7eJpOQCiDXuigGUK5DD79JH2G\n5fdq8OBG33+hb0NncBaDetVlTzEKsb5zBu1ZFi/jqRwcEPSMevlMCzIlYAizOW52\n099uAZOWX1uy9tF+s7hdZLZ56wC6s+9eS8PWB6WReiEdMevpmxEdAM1/HT08mU2a\nnSqUK8K5XF3gkwJkGo1UJTX9HVmHRiJjqLFlzdiCUw4PyEc+joidqNzjnHYMfGaE\nN1iCOF1Y2vWs++294o3xe62SrQ+O6WYDgxpSRfDgAJAf5eFnht0qThJ9EtWvU/6F\n7mj4tQ6DC5+sSdmy3UDZ3ychDNUZIIx2W4WL8HcmsRh3Os5mE9FP0aAUyxi/9+4F\nujw2IFm3QvRalOy2l91OFq0HJOq+JtFKVd5yPBUSw5lEIY2BeCfE0WXJ5UnX7Hw+\n7HxEgTYpCxk94JVwitzt8+EpKJ4fxLKQfAY0CAPzV4kE8IzyR7T//Ne/NLcIp6zt\n/OcOIrLrTZ/SpSqmTEo5\n=7Igz\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-21T17:06:52Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/51345baa5f8226f93a8ce77340a7ae0e3143d554","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDUxMzQ1YmFhNWY4MjI2ZjkzYThjZTc3MzQwYTdhZTBlMzE0M2Q1NTQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/9e902c1c5fccdcdeb3be969f3e80cd6b2002a991","sha":"9e902c1c5fccdcdeb3be969f3e80cd6b2002a991","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/9e902c1c5fccdcdeb3be969f3e80cd6b2002a991"}],"sha":"51345baa5f8226f93a8ce77340a7ae0e3143d554","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/51345baa5f8226f93a8ce77340a7ae0e3143d554"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/198140699?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kiet08hogit","id":198140699,"login":"kiet08hogit","node_id":"U_kgDOC89jGw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kiet08hogit","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/9e902c1c5fccdcdeb3be969f3e80cd6b2002a991/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T16:53:34Z","email":"hokiet.nguyen08@gmail.com","name":"Nguyen Tuan Kiet Ho"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T16:53:34Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(ai): pass single-component validate payloads through unwrapped (#2376)\n\n* fix(ai): pass single-component validate payloads through unwrapped\n\n* style(ai): drop extra blank line so ruff format passes","tree":{"sha":"2b1109a4042adca31f8af85788c9c0466fe7909f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/2b1109a4042adca31f8af85788c9c0466fe7909f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/9e902c1c5fccdcdeb3be969f3e80cd6b2002a991","verification":{"payload":"tree 2b1109a4042adca31f8af85788c9c0466fe7909f\nparent d069ce7fd9ebfda1fb638a9b84fd08734603cc17\nauthor Nguyen Tuan Kiet Ho <hokiet.nguyen08@gmail.com> 1790009614 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790009614 -0700\n\nfix(ai): pass single-component validate payloads through unwrapped (#2376)\n\n* fix(ai): pass single-component validate payloads through unwrapped\n\n* style(ai): drop extra blank line so ruff format passes","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsWEOCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAU5AQADFtngwWHFco8Ey0ynycwmKJ\nPAhRgmiRt3rOzCEi2rJM8UuaiKjZ5ozS2FaYooWXgxIVsDIWFE0kIAIrKttlc3lJ\nqrFQ+ZsJSjgPCPcRnfbETgn/SGuJlCYzO7c/6mByimuX4w0P/aQz2xXrdCTApmY6\nhKceb4uHQFTLzFBzxXcuzUUuuEfjGc0ou43XzKZLe1ufcaTPqGK1xdEoPKkasq4z\nYZyBiR3vGQ7Tnqq/LydDswvDhXCwx/Owdg2Iy8VOHeANogP0fmQsGKh2NnFToYWX\nAnD8vXOPTnRb44K6x30+Frx1fv9gffTezug++bQNt+ZoIrdjhB27hblMN8AsmcRS\nEk2Qi8nvsdIXRSxHLqhjIVABBeGUWk9F0+V1hGPrqDyxFT1P9bBzWYGGt1hCN8Rd\nLw1z+H9gCHCsjynu3wIBGnOPZzp9Z8Dbzcb8C1CEecNe3CW5yMMFfJE5v1p7QhXq\nihii4y1DEs3YVPp3Sjx8jxC8/XGa9VkQWJMwThu62tVLi52zCywTaUq+l3l+oE1Y\ntrCORRuUfMXIC/uOhXSCni89Ry8OrLTTwWzNsfxIZN8MtDsjIFqIm1bB0ZMorx/Z\njP9y0FRmIzcsKswVM31a9QiL7U7iM/MnJcF0qiNZDsi2ud2PEkPmXTD9l+SScfcH\n0riwpSXyk1nmPl0zsJpi\n=d+Bd\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-21T16:53:35Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/9e902c1c5fccdcdeb3be969f3e80cd6b2002a991","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDllOTAyYzFjNWZjY2RjZGViM2JlOTY5ZjNlODBjZDZiMjAwMmE5OTE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/d069ce7fd9ebfda1fb638a9b84fd08734603cc17","sha":"d069ce7fd9ebfda1fb638a9b84fd08734603cc17","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/d069ce7fd9ebfda1fb638a9b84fd08734603cc17"}],"sha":"9e902c1c5fccdcdeb3be969f3e80cd6b2002a991","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/9e902c1c5fccdcdeb3be969f3e80cd6b2002a991"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24360630?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/nihalnihalani","id":24360630,"login":"nihalnihalani","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzYwNjMw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/d069ce7fd9ebfda1fb638a9b84fd08734603cc17/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T16:21:08Z","email":"nihal.nihalani@gmail.com","name":"NIHAL NIHALANI"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T16:21:08Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(sql-ui): statement runner, real error text, schema-aware completion, history, relationships, plain EXPLAIN, honest designer (#2282)\n\n* test(sql-ui): add node:test harness and pure-logic suites\n\nSQL Explorer had no test setup. Add the explorer-ui harness (tsx +\nnode:test, a `sql-ui:test` builder action over tests/) and six pure-logic\nsuites covering the modules where the audit found bugs.\n\nUnlike explorer-ui, docs.ts imports Documents / NOOP_VFS from ''shell'' as\nvalues, so the runner preloads a local stub-shell.cjs (apps/shared''s\npattern, copied rather than shared: extending apps/shared''s stub would\nchange a file its own suite depends on). Every other tested module reaches\nthe platform through `import type` alone, which tsx erases.\n\n15 of the 71 tests fail on purpose. They encode the fixed behaviour for two\naudited correctness bugs, ahead of the fix in the next commit:\n\n- sql/paging.ts inlines filter and search values, passing numeric-looking\n  strings through unquoted, so `code >= ''0100''` on a VARCHAR column becomes\n  a number comparison (MySQL coerces the column, Postgres raises).\n- docs.ts leaves the table name unencoded in `table:` / `design:` URIs, so\n  a table whose name contains a colon splits one segment too late and\n  yields an endpoint key that does not exist.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 8f788cf75fd14235ed07d3bb5ba0acac24f8041b)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): parse plain EXPLAIN output into a plan tree\n\nAdds the pure half of the query-plan feature: buildExplain() prefixes the\ndialect''s plain EXPLAIN form (mysql FORMAT=JSON, postgres (FORMAT JSON),\nclickhouse text) to the statement exactly as it would run, and returns null\nfor dialects with no support here. parseExplain() normalises the three\ndocumented result shapes into one IPlanNode tree, flagging planner numbers so\nthe UI can suffix them with `est.`.\n\nPlain EXPLAIN only — nothing here emits ANALYZE, so the explained statement is\nnever executed. Postgres arrives either already parsed or as a JSON string\ndepending on the driver; both are handled.\n\nA shape the parser does not recognise reports `ok: false` with a reason rather\nthan throwing, so the caller keeps showing the database''s raw output.\n\nEvery fixture is hand-built from vendor documentation, not captured from a\nlive server: no MySQL, PostgreSQL or ClickHouse instance was reachable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit a1c0678b2531f0dfe049c7c41b07841b4c6c30a9)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): bind filter values, remote sort, page errors, URI table encoding\n\nFive audited correctness bugs in the data browser and its URI scheme, plus\nthe two session capabilities the rest of the upgrade needs.\n\npaging: filter, range-bound, and search values were rendered into the\nstatement by quoteValue, which passed numeric-looking strings through\nunquoted — `code >= ''0100''` on a VARCHAR column became a number\ncomparison, matching the wrong rows on MySQL and raising a type error on\nPostgres. buildPageStatements now returns `params` alongside the two\nstatements and binds every user value as `$1..$n`; quoteValue is gone and\nintrospect''s always-quoting quoteLiteral moves to sql/quote.ts as the app''s\nsingle literal quoter. LIKE patterns are assembled as bind VALUES, because\nthe node rewrites `$n` textually before binding.\n\nTableDataView: SELECT and COUNT now run in parallel; header sorts reach\n`req.sort` (remoteSort); a failed page shows an error Banner instead of\nfailing silently behind the grid''s transient toast; and a monotonic request\nsequence keeps a slow, superseded page from overwriting a newer one. A\ntable with no primary key and no user sort pages without any ORDER BY, so\nthe view says the order is not guaranteed.\n\ndocs: `table:` / `design:` URIs percent-encode the table segment. The\nendpoint key contains colons, so these schemes split on the last one — a\ntable named `odd:name` split one segment too late and produced an endpoint\nkey that does not exist.\n\nconnect: `execute` takes optional bind params, and `ISqlSession` gains\n`refreshSchema()` over the node''s `refresh_schema` tool, falling back to\n`get_schema` with `stale: true` when the node does not have it. The\nengine''s exact wording for an unowned tool is not readable from this repo\n(it comes from the compiled engine), so `isUnsupportedToolError` classifies\nconservatively: false only for errors that clearly came from a tool that\nran, true otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 98f7303c6b8cef104a80ddce0c43d327ffa200ad)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): map declared foreign keys into a relation graph and join paths\n\nThe record drawer and the join-path finder need to answer three questions\nfrom a schema snapshot alone: what points at this table, how do I get from\nhere to there, and what does that join look like as SQL.\n\nEverything here comes from keys the engine DECLARED. Nothing is inferred\nfrom a column or table name, anywhere — a name that looks like a key is a\nguess, and a guess presented as a relationship is worse than no answer. A\nschema that models its relationships only by convention (and ClickHouse,\nwhich declares none at all) therefore produces an empty graph, which is the\nhonest result rather than a bug.\n\nfindJoinPaths returns EVERY shortest path instead of picking one. Two\ntables are frequently joinable in several equally short ways — through\nparallel keys, or through different intermediate tables — and which one is\nmeant depends on the question being asked, which this module cannot know.\nSelf-referential keys are shown in the drawer but never walked, so a path\ncannot loop; composite keys carry all their column pairs into a multi-column\nON clause; and keys pointing outside the snapshot are kept as edges for the\nquality rules but are never walkable.\n\nThe generated SELECT says what it is on its first line: generated from\ndeclared foreign keys, review before running.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit e2c893e669d798fc6d60e9125283ecbfeac04189)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): record finished statements and bound them before they are kept\n\nQuery history rides the app''s workspace preferences file, which the shell\nreads and writes on every app switch. An unbounded list would make that\nswitch slower for everyone, so the bounds are enforced in pure functions\nbefore every write rather than left as good intentions: 100 entries per\nconnection, 8 KB per statement, 1 KB per error, 256 KB across the whole bag.\n\nPins buy priority, not immunity. They count against the same 100 and are\ndropped last, because an unbounded pinned list is the same problem wearing a\nnicer name. When a repeat of the same statement collapses onto the entry\nbefore it, the OLDER entry survives with its id, pin and note intact — a\nperson who annotated a statement meant that statement, and re-running it\nmust not quietly discard what they wrote.\n\nrunEvents is a one-way bus so the runner never imports the store: a run is\nnever blocked by, or aware of, whether anything is recording it, and a\nlistener that throws cannot fail the query that just succeeded.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit e1fbc2e8a48ee9b60953dd9d074992f7e933f7f4)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): tell the truth about the schema after a designer Apply\n\nThe node reflects its schema once, at task start. The designer used to re-read\nthat same snapshot after Apply and repaint it, so a freshly added column\nappeared to vanish. Apply now asks for a fresh reflection and says which of the\ntwo things actually happened:\n\n- re-read succeeded: `Applied HH:MM · schema re-read from the database.`\n- the node has no refresh_schema tool, or the re-read failed: a warning saying\n  the tree and diagram will not show the change until the pipeline restarts.\n\nBecause the answer depends on the snapshot that comes back, the banner is\ncomposed in an effect against the post-refresh snapshot rather than inside the\nApply closure, which cannot see it.\n\nA batch that stops part-way now reports what committed, which statement failed\nwith the database''s own first line, what never ran, and how many statements the\nplan still holds — instead of a bare `Apply failed:`.\n\nThe confirmation no longer says schema changes \"cannot be undone from here\",\nwhich reads as if a rollback existed elsewhere. It states the statement count\nand the engine''s actual behaviour for a half-finished plan.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 167ea69d4c081f8e85746c6f82000c95f403e3b5)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add dialect-aware statement splitter and classifier\n\nThe database node executes exactly ONE statement per `execute` call, so a\nbuffer holding several statements has to be split in the app before anything\nis sent. `sql/split.ts` is a hand-written scanner rather than a regex because\na `;` separates statements only in code: inside a string literal, a quoted\nidentifier, a line or block comment, or a PostgreSQL dollar-quoted body it is\nordinary text. Every dialect difference the scanner claims is asserted in the\ntests instead of assumed — MySQL''s `#` line comments (which ClickHouse does\nnot have), PostgreSQL''s nested block comments, dollar-quoted bodies and\n`E''...''` backslash escapes, and ClickHouse''s plain backslash escapes.\n\nMySQL''s `DELIMITER` directive is NOT supported and says so in the JSDoc and in\na test that pins the wrong-but-documented split, because a half-working\nimplementation of a client-side directive is worse than an honest gap.\n\n`sql/classify.ts` decides whether the runner may pass `idempotent: true` (which\npermits one silent retry with a fresh token), so anything not clearly read-only\nis not a read: `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` is classified by the statement it runs, and a\n`WITH` chain containing a data-modifying keyword is a write. Its `patternCheck`\nis the text heuristic behind the confirmation dialog; the false positives it is\nallowed to have (a WHERE that exists only inside a subquery) are pinned by test\nso they are not \"fixed\" into false negatives.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 1942a0b4f4487f06bc8972e4f99cb1c9e512f7d3)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add pattern notes and raw-text rendering to the plan parser\n\nplanNotes() turns a handful of documented planner markers into plain-language\nobservations, each citing the exact field it read: MySQL access_type = ALL,\nfilesort and temporary tables (in either the tabular Extra column or the\nFORMAT=JSON booleans), and PostgreSQL sequential scans. Row estimates carried\ninto a note keep the `est.` suffix.\n\nThese are text rules over planner output, not a cost model. A node no rule\nmatches gets no note — silence is not a verdict — and the UI labels the column\n`detected by pattern` so nobody mistakes them for measurements.\n\nformatRawPlan() renders the result as the text a reader would have seen at a\nSQL prompt, which is what the panel shows first and by default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 3de518dd4d52aec8ab06d5b5214632192f4dd2fd)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): keep query history in the workspace, never in the browser\n\nHistory lives in the shell''s workspace preferences — server-side and per\nuser — so it follows the person to another browser or machine, which is the\nwhole reason it is worth keeping at all.\n\nThat store has one sharp edge, and it decides the design of this file.\n`setPref` patches whichever app is active AT WRITE TIME, and the shell\nre-reads each app''s preferences from disk on every app switch. A write\nissued while SQL Explorer sits in the background would be filed under the\napp in the foreground and then lost. So the store writes only while a\nHistoryPrefsBridge is mounted, and the shell unmounts the app''s whole\nsubtree when another app takes over, which makes \"mounted\" mean \"SQL\nExplorer is on screen\". On unmount the pending write is dropped rather than\nflushed: up to 300 ms of history can be lost if someone switches apps\nimmediately after a run, and that is the honest cost of never writing into\nanother app''s preferences.\n\nEverything read back from the workspace file is narrowed field by field.\nThe file outlives this version of the app and a person can edit it by hand,\nso it is treated as untrusted input rather than as our own types.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit f2a265442a71369598e73a94d232422de4c8fd20)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add a plain EXPLAIN plan panel\n\nExplainPanel is self-contained: given an endpoint, a dialect and the statement\nexactly as it would run, it performs the EXPLAIN itself through the session and\nshows what came back. Integration into the query runner is a separate change.\n\nThe database''s RAW OUTPUT is the default view. The interpreted tree sits behind\na second toggle, and a plan shape the parser does not recognise is an info note\nbeside the raw text — not an error, because nothing failed on the database''s\nside. A node that refuses execute gets the allow_execute sentence instead of\ndriver text, since the statement never reached the database.\n\nPlanTree renders the plan as an ARIA tree: arrow keys walk and expand rows,\nevery planner number carries `est.`, and the notes column is labelled\n`detected by pattern` with each note citing the field it read. No cost bars and\nno colour as meaning — every state is text.\n\nPlain EXPLAIN only. Nothing here runs the statement being explained.\n\nThe parsers are fixture-tested against documented output shapes; no live MySQL,\nPostgreSQL or ClickHouse server was reachable to validate them against.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 98c005937c34c7b67caa5db6a244e4813cbbad2b)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add the query history drawer\n\nA modeless right drawer, so the editor behind it stays usable — the point of\nhistory is to work alongside the statement being written, not instead of it.\nSearch, filter by reads/writes/errors/pinned, expand a row for the full text,\nthen load it into the editor, rerun it, pin it, annotate it, delete it, or\nopen it in a new query document.\n\nThe privacy notice is permanent, not a first-run tip. These statements are\nwritten verbatim to the user''s workspace file on the RocketRide server,\nliteral values and all. Someone who assumed browser-local storage would be\nwrong in a way that matters, so the drawer says where the text goes for as\nlong as it is open.\n\nRerunning anything that changed data asks first, and the warning quotes what\nthe statement did LAST time rather than pretending to know what it will do\nnow — nothing is counted or checked against the database beforehand. The\nkind badge comes from a text pattern, which the wording reflects.\n\n\"Load into editor\" does not ask before replacing the editor''s text: the\ndrawer cannot tell an untouched editor from an edited one, so the query view\nowns that confirmation. \"Open in new query\" deliberately does not mark the\ndocument as generated — the statement is the user''s own text and must not\nclaim to be something this app wrote.\n\nannounce() lands here because the shell has no live region: useAnnouncements\nfetches GitHub product announcements and only Banner sets aria-live, so\npinning or clearing an entry would otherwise be silent to a screen reader.\nThe visible live region that reads this channel is mounted by the app shell.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 7c88324f53c787838395a86a2829213a10dbe82f)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): retry a failed tool call only when re-running it is safe\n\nA session caches the owning task''s token, and that token goes stale\nwhenever the task restarts. The transport reports a stale token exactly\nlike a statement the database refused, so the blanket one-retry meant a\nwrite could be sent twice: the first INSERT may well have committed before\nthe failure surfaced.\n\nRetrying is now a per-call decision. `execute` takes `opts.idempotent` and\nretries only when the caller sets it; reflection and dialect reads keep the\nretry, since repeating them is free. TableDataView''s SELECT and COUNT pass\nit; the query runner will pass it only for statements its classifier marks\nas reads. An unmarked statement is assumed to write and its first failure\nis the answer.\n\nrefreshSchema no longer inspects error text to decide whether the node has\nthe refresh tool. The engine offers no dependable signal for \"no such\ntool\", and matching its wording would rot the first time it changed, so ANY\nfailure falls back to get_schema with `stale: true` — and schemaStore\nremembers that per endpoint, so later refreshes on a node without the tool\nread the snapshot directly instead of paying for a doomed call each time.\nisUnsupportedToolError and its marker lists are gone.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 9c2f7826d52c0d2ab4ce7eaca010b2de82d6647f)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): show inbound keys, preview queries and join paths in the record drawer\n\nThe drawer already said what a table declares. It could not say what points\nAT the table, which is the half of a relationship a person usually arrives\nlooking for, and it left the obvious next step — actually querying the thing\n— as an exercise.\n\n\"Referenced by\" lists the inbound foreign keys and opens the referencing\ntable in a STACKED drawer, so following a relationship keeps the way back\nunder the shell''s own back arrow instead of quietly replacing what was on\nscreen. The join-path finder walks declared keys undirectedly to four joins\nand lists EVERY shortest path rather than picking one, because which join is\nmeant depends on a question the schema cannot answer. Parallel keys and\ncomposite keys each come out as their own path, with all their column pairs\nin the ON clause.\n\nBoth sections carry \"from schema snapshot HH:MM\". A snapshot read at task\nstart can be older than the database it describes, and a relationship\npresented without its age invites a decision based on a stale reading.\n\nWhere there are no declared keys there is no guessing: the section says the\nschema declares none, and ClickHouse says so in its own terms. Nothing here\ninfers a relationship from a column name.\n\nSelect top 100 and Count rows open a NEW query document whose first line\nmarks it as a generated preview. Neither runs anything.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 27c89777443c87ca3b4e217b812e27546a5f5d18)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): show inbound foreign keys before applying a designer plan\n\nThe Apply confirmation now has an Impact section naming every table that\ndeclares a foreign key on a column the plan drops, renames or retypes, and\nstamps where those facts came from: `from schema snapshot HH:MM`. The wording\nsays the database MAY reject or cascade the change, because the tool asks the\nsnapshot, not the database.\n\nRenames are traced back to the name the snapshot knows. A plan that renames a\ncolumn and then retypes it under its new name still finds the keys pointing at\nthe original, which a match on the operation''s own name would miss. Composite\nkeys pair their columns positionally.\n\nDropping a column adds a line saying its data is not recoverable by this tool.\n\nColumn rows already acted as buttons on click; they now carry role, a tab stop,\nan accessible name and Enter/Space, so the designer is reachable without a\nmouse. Dropped rows and create-mode drafts stay inert, as they were.\n\nExplainPanel announces a finished plan through the app''s live region: unlike a\nBanner, a tree and a `pre` do not announce themselves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit c7dc728d9fbab75f4bbe3f0dc701bed40eec2732)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): type result columns and suggest from the schema snapshot\n\n`sql/resultTypes.ts` replaces the blanket `rrType: ''string''` on query results,\nwhich disabled numeric sorting and the grid''s number and date range filters.\nTypes come from the schema snapshot only when the source table is CERTAIN —\none FROM, no join, and every returned column belongs to that table — and from\nthe returned values otherwise; which of the two was used is reported so the\ncolumn tooltip can say `BIGINT (schema)` or `number (inferred from 200 rows)`\nrather than presenting a guess as a fact.\n\nType-name matching is whole-token, fixing the substring test that claimed\nPOINT as a number and missed MONEY, REAL and SERIAL. Value inference accounts\nfor the node''s sanitisation (Decimal arrives as a float, datetime as an ISO\nstring) and keeps `0123` a string, since a leading zero is the difference\nbetween a product code and a number.\n\n`sql/completion.ts` builds the suggestion model: alias-scoped columns for an\n`alias.` prefix, table names after FROM/JOIN/UPDATE/INTO, columns of the\ntables the buffer mentions, and keyword snippets — ordered entirely through\n`sortText` because Monaco merges these with its own keyword list. JOIN clauses\nare prefilled from DECLARED foreign keys, in both directions, with composite\nkeys producing a multi-column ON; nothing is inferred from a column''s name.\nIdentifiers that need quoting get it in the inserted text only.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit c4af7d8cad2d922b71a6413be6d7ee67486f5ee4)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): mark the EXPLAIN round trip idempotent\n\nPlain EXPLAIN plans the statement without running it, so the session may retry\nit once with a re-resolved token after a task restart. Without the flag the\nEXPLAIN failed outright whenever the cached token had gone stale.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 24ad6e1e361a7a1d6e0ebc01bb4ffe5b74e557ef)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): keep history recorded before preferences were reachable\n\nRuns can finish before any prefs bridge is mounted — a query document open\nwhile its connection workbench is closed records straight into memory. The\nstore then replaced that bag wholesale on hydration, throwing those entries\naway at the exact moment they first became saveable.\n\nHydration now merges instead: the in-memory entry wins on a shared id, the\ntwo lists are combined by age and re-trimmed, and a write is scheduled for\nwhatever was waiting. A recording switch flipped before the bridge mounted\noutranks the stored one for the same reason.\n\nThe connection workbench also mounts a bridge, so an open workbench keeps\nthe write channel alive for every query document on that connection rather\nthan only while the History drawer happens to be open.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 0a70a2429089b4087e6d63b91aa65c5cb2ed0340)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add the Insights page — schema shape and snapshot-only review\n\nA second page on the connection workbench that says how the schema is shaped\nand what is demonstrably wrong with it, without touching the database: hubs,\nleaves and isolated tables from declared keys, then three rules over the same\nsnapshot. It works with execute disabled, because it never executes anything.\n\nEach rule stops where the evidence stops. R1, a table with no primary key, is\ncertain. R3, a key naming a table or column the snapshot does not have, is\ncertain within the snapshot — and a snapshot read before a migration can\nraise it against a perfectly consistent database, which is why the page\nstates when it was read. R2, the two sides of a key spelling their type\ndifferently, is a TEXT comparison after normalisation rather than a\ncompatibility check, so it is information and never a verdict.\n\nNormalisation drops parenthesised arguments (INT(11) is INT) and resolves a\nshort, exact alias list — every entry is a spelling the engines themselves\ntreat as identical, not a judgement that two types are close enough.\n\nThere is no naming rule. A column called customer_id that happens to match\nanother table''s primary key is a guess, and a guess sitting in a list of\nfindings reads as a fact.\n\nThe limitations banner is not boilerplate. It is the scope of every claim\nthe page makes, and it stays on screen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit ef3359f00dd523ca249c4bfcf848e6f54d565baa)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): give the editor a run handle, schema completion and decorations\n\n`SqlEditor` grows the surface the runner needs: a ref handle for the live\nselection, caret offset and statement decorations, `Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+Enter` for\nrun-all, and `Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+E` behind an OPTIONAL `onExplain` prop so the\nkeybinding exists before the panel that answers it does.\n\nTwo things are module-level on purpose. The completion provider is registered\nonce per language id, not per editor: `registerCompletionItemProvider` is\nglobal and every query document stays mounted, so per-instance registration\nwould stack providers and repeat every suggestion once per open tab. What\nvaries per editor is looked up by model URI in a map each instance maintains,\nand the providers are deliberately never disposed — they belong to the module,\nand tearing them down when one tab closes would break the others. The\ndecoration stylesheet is injected once behind an id guard, the pattern\n`InputField` uses for its placeholder colour, because inline styles cannot\nreach a Monaco decoration class.\n\n`sql/batch.ts` holds the per-statement run model and its wording, including\nthe batch summary that has to say a failed batch left earlier statements\ncommitted, and `applyRowLimit`, which appends the header limit only to\nstatements that return rows and leaves a user''s own LIMIT alone.\n\n`sql/failure.ts` reads a failed execute against the four messages the node\nactually produces, matching the machine-stable fragment (`allow_execute`,\n`max_execute_rows`) rather than a whole sentence. It recognises the generic\nplaceholder that node versions predating the error-text fix return, so the app\ncan say the driver''s message was not returned instead of presenting the\nplaceholder as what the database said.\n\n`LiveRegion` renders the existing `a11y/announce` store into one polite live\nregion, keyed by the store''s revision so announcing the same sentence twice\nstill speaks.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 6a429e2790cd938a54b277bb06be6e5f58a367b7)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): run the selection or statement at the cursor, honestly\n\nThe query runner previously sent the WHOLE editor buffer as one `execute`,\nwhich the driver rejects for anything multi-statement, and reported failures\nthrough a bare error string. It now splits the buffer and runs one statement\nper call, because that is what the node supports.\n\nEverything the runner says is tied to how the node actually executes:\n\n- Each statement runs in its OWN transaction, so a batch that fails partway\n  leaves the earlier statements committed. The status strip shows every\n  statement''s outcome and the summary line says which ones committed, which\n  failed and which never ran. The error banner repeats it for the failure.\n- There is no cancel anywhere in the tool protocol. \"Stop waiting\" appears\n  after a second, bumps a run sequence so the late answer is discarded, and\n  says the database may still be running the statement. It never claims to\n  have stopped anything.\n- Transaction-control statements would each land in a separate autocommit\n  call, so they are refused before anything is sent rather than run into a\n  false sense of safety.\n- `idempotent` is passed only for statements the classifier reads as READ, so\n  the session''s retry-with-a-fresh-token can never re-send a write.\n- The confirmation dialog for an unbounded UPDATE/DELETE, TRUNCATE, DROP or\n  ALTER calls itself a text check and says it is not a database safeguard. It\n  is not called a safe mode, because it prevents nothing.\n- When the node returns its generic placeholder instead of the driver''s\n  message, the banner says the message was not returned rather than showing\n  the placeholder as the database''s words. When the driver''s text IS there, it\n  is shown verbatim in a collapsible block.\n\nWhat a run would send is named in TEXT under the editor before the key is\npressed, not only by a decoration, and the same applies to the result meta:\nthe applied limit is stated, and a result that exactly fills the limit is\nbadged as possibly incomplete rather than presented as the whole answer.\n\nExport is a text hint pointing at the grid''s gear menu. `IDataGridHandle`\nexposes the Tabulator instance but the shell registers 13 Tabulator modules\nand DownloadModule is not among them, so `table.download()` does not exist;\nadding a second CSV writer to the app was explicitly out of scope.\n\nResult columns are typed from the schema when the source table is certain and\nfrom the values otherwise, which restores numeric sorting and the range\nfilters, and the column tooltip says which of the two it was.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 863dc06a7f6ae2a9f2b9d994e9e560db21803fed)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): read the live selection on the run path, settle a pending confirm\n\nTwo narrow correctness fixes in the runner.\n\nThe run path took the selected TEXT from the editor handle but its OFFSETS\nfrom the debounced cursor state, so a run fired within the debounce window of\na drag attributed the run to the previous selection''s lines — the statement\nsent was right, the lines reported for it were not. The handle now exposes\n`getSelection()`, returning text and offsets together from the live editor.\n\nA pattern-check confirmation open when the document unmounts left the run loop\nawaiting a promise nothing could settle. The unmount now answers it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit f753c373e15dbad037b4e43e6729864385214cec)\n\n* docs(apps): add the SQL Explorer guide\n\nSQL Explorer had no product page: the only mention of the app anywhere in\ndocs/ was a builder task reference. This adds the spine page and its Apps\nsidebar entry, describing the app as it actually behaves today.\n\nThe page leads with the constraints rather than hiding them, because they\nare what surprises people: every statement travels through a pipeline\ndatabase node, so `allow_execute` gates execution, each statement commits\non its own, transaction control is refused, nothing can be cancelled, and\nthe schema is a snapshot taken when the task started. The verbatim UI\nmicrocopy is quoted so the docs and the app say the same words.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 1601764e6db8eabbf2de7e20b7dc77c0cd605271)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): finish the runner''s drawers — focus return, explain gating, prefs bridge\n\nCompletes the integration points QueryView owns, now that the history and\nexplain panels are on the branch.\n\nFocus return. Closing either drawer puts focus back on the button that opened\nit. The shell''s Button forwards no ref, so each opener sits in a\n`display: contents` wrapper the view can query — no layout change, and the\nalternative (leaving focus on the document body) strands keyboard users at the\ntop of the page after every close.\n\nExplain gating. The button is disabled with a reason rather than silently\ndoing nothing: `buildExplain` returns null for engines with no supported\nEXPLAIN form and for an empty statement, and the title says which it was. The\npanel is now mounted unconditionally and driven by `open`, as its own contract\nasks, and is handed the statement exactly as Run would send it — applied LIMIT\nincluded — so the plan describes the real statement.\n\nHistory prefs bridge. The history store only writes while a bridge is mounted.\nOne at app level covers a query document whose connection tab has been closed;\nthe bridge is reference-counted, so the copies in ConnectionView and in the\ndrawer stay harmless.\n\nAnnouncements. `Banner` is itself a live region for every variant — assertive\nfor error, polite otherwise — so announcing an event that is also rendered in\na Banner reads it out twice. The transaction refusal, the stop-waiting notice\nand the failure announcement are dropped for that reason; what remains\nannounced is only what has no live region of its own: the batch summary, the\nrow and affected counts, the pattern-check switch, and loading a statement\ninto the editor.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 902f2cd76c9b0d0361225a091ba6e1560919726f)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): ask for a fresh read when the user presses Refresh\n\nEvery user-facing Refresh called `refreshSchema` without `{ fresh: true }`,\nso it re-read the node''s task-start snapshot and returned the same answer\nit already had — even on a node that has the `refresh_schema` tool and could\nhave gone to the database. The one place that asked for a fresh read was the\ndesigner''s post-Apply path. Pressing Refresh after a CREATE TABLE therefore\ndid nothing visible, which is the worst possible result: the control looks\nbroken when the capability was there all along.\n\nThe workbench header, the Overview retry and the sidebar tree''s refresh now\nall ask for a fresh read. Automatic first loads still do not, because a page\nopening does not need two round trips to say what it already serves.\n\nThe header stamp stops blurring the two things a \"read\" can mean. A node\nthat re-read the database says `snapshot re-read HH:MM`; a node serving the\nreflection taken when its task started says `task-start snapshot, read\nHH:MM`, however recently we asked. The old wording let the second pass for\nthe first, which is what made the missing table look like our bug rather\nthan a node capability that is not there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 17353eb86de6cabda7ec205f70b834c65b67afce)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): stop quoting the node''s placeholder as the database''s words\n\nA node without the execute error-text fix returns `SQL execution failed (check\nserver logs for details)`. The Explain panel and the designer both printed it\nafter `Database reported: `, presenting the node''s placeholder as if the\ndatabase had said it. Both now route through describeFailure and show the\nfallback sentence when the message is generic, with no \"Database said\" block\nto open — there is nothing in it.\n\nWhere the driver''s text IS real, both surfaces now match the query runner: the\nfirst line as the headline, the full text behind a collapsible block. The\ndesigner had no such block before, so multi-line driver text was lost.\n\nThe Explain subtitle said \"Validated by EXPLAIN\". Nothing is validated — the\nplanner returned an estimate, which the same line goes on to say. It now reads\n`EXPLAIN ran HH:MM`.\n\nThe ClickHouse note claimed ALTER runs as an asynchronous mutation. That holds\nfor ALTER ... UPDATE/DELETE, not for metadata operations like ADD COLUMN, so it\nnow says some ALTER forms do.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 724d70696fcaa8dbde790ce66bd9d7f620738685)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): let the review rules know the dialect, and say what they mean\n\nR1 had no dialect input, so \"table has no primary key\" fired on every\nClickHouse table — the engine reflects no primary-key constraint for any of\nthem, a MergeTree ORDER BY not being one. The Insights tab then showed a\nnumber equal to the table count and the grid called each row \"definitely\nwrong in this snapshot\". Every part of that was false, and the loudest part\nwas the verdict.\n\nR1 now takes the dialect and drops to information on ClickHouse with wording\nthat names the engine rather than accusing the schema. Severities read\n`warning` and `info` throughout: a missing primary key is worth fixing on\nMySQL or PostgreSQL, but a log table without one is a choice, not an error,\nand \"definitely wrong\" was never ours to say.\n\nThe tab badge counts warnings only. Information is something to know, not\nsomething to do, and a badge lit on every healthy schema stops being read.\n\nThe Rule column shows the rule''s name instead of R1/R2/R3. The ids were for\ncross-referencing a document the reader does not have, and there was no\nlegend anywhere on the page. Hub chips say what their number counts —\n`orders · 5 referencing tables` rather than `orders 5`, which could have\nbeen rows, columns or anything else.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 8698af8c30b79c04b840d69f79447f027fb79472)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): give history rows their limit, and stop announcing a load that may not happen\n\nA history row said `1000 rows`. That number cannot tell you whether it is\nthe whole answer or the ceiling cutting it off, and those mean opposite\nthings — every other count in this app carries its suffix for exactly that\nreason. The entry gains an optional `limit`, the badge reads `12 rows (limit\n1000)` when one was applied and `12 rows returned` when none was, and the\nrunner fills the field in.\n\n\"Load into editor\" no longer announces. The query view may raise a \"Replace\neditor text?\" confirm that the user cancels, in which case the announcement\nwas a lie, and on the path where the load does happen the query view already\nannounces it — so the drawer was either wrong or repeating.\n\nThe recording toggle now says `Recording: on` / `Recording: off`. It was a\npressed ghost button whose text never changed, which left the state carried\nby colour alone for anyone reading it by eye.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 3aadbae8ebc612b1a5a6bcec68a672b9a7c8808b)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): say when the join-path list hit its cap\n\nThe finder stops enumerating at 25 equally short paths, and the drawer\nreported that as \"25 paths found — choose one\", which claims there are\nexactly 25 and that one of them is the one you want. At the cap the list is\na sample, so it now reads \"25 paths shown (cap reached — more may exist)\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit dfef8bab31035f140aa045a598c56f8c1e71f89b)\n\n* refactor(sql-ui): type the query-document payload and hide module internals\n\n`IQueryDocPayload` existed but every producer built the payload as an\nuntyped object literal, so a misspelled field would have shipped silently\nand shown up as a query document that opened blank. The type moves to\ndocs.ts, beside the URI builders every producer already imports — nobody\nshould have to import a view to describe what they are handing it — and the\ntwo call sites that build a payload now declare it.\n\nOpus B: SqlApp''s local copy can become a re-export from docs.ts; the shapes\nare identical, so nothing breaks in the meantime.\n\n`DEFAULT_MAX_DEPTH`, `JOIN_SQL_HEADER` and the history store''s preference\nkeys, `attachPrefs` and `recordRun` were exported with no caller outside\ntheir own module. An export is a promise to keep something stable, and these\nwere promises nobody had asked for. `MAX_JOIN_PATHS` stays exported with a\nnote saying why: the drawer compares against it to admit the path list is a\nsample rather than the answer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit d6a927e390f93aa18892d5a94af2009d264c73ce)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): split a selection instead of sending it as one statement\n\nA dragged selection was sent as a single `execute`, classified by whatever\nkeyword happened to come first. Selecting `SELECT …; DELETE FROM t` therefore\nread as a SELECT: the statement was marked read-only and so became eligible\nfor the session''s retry-with-a-fresh-token, which could run the DELETE twice;\nthe pattern check only ever saw the first verb; the transaction refusal only\nfired when the FIRST statement was transaction control, so selecting\n`UPDATE …; ROLLBACK;` sent both and the ROLLBACK silently did nothing. psycopg2\nexecutes multi-statement strings, so this was reachable, not theoretical.\n\n`splitStatementsIn` splits a slice of the buffer and rebases every offset and\nline number onto the whole buffer, so each statement in a selection gets its\nown classification, pattern check, limit, history entry and strip position\nwhile decorations still point at the real text. A selection holding part of\none statement still yields that one fragment, which is what a partial\nselection wants.\n\nThe pre-run line now says how many statements a selection holds, so a\nmulti-statement selection cannot be mistaken for one before it runs, and a\nbuffer holding only comments reads `no statement found` rather than claiming\nthe editor is empty.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit e7c2adbd935f159e1cc207132c1c0f9fe5f115f2)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): publish the completion model once the editor model exists\n\nSchema autocomplete was silently dead on the ordinary path. The effect that\npublishes this editor''s completion model into the shared provider''s map read\nthe model URI from a ref and returned early when it was null, keyed only on\n`[completion, dialect]`. @monaco-editor/react mounts asynchronously — loader\npromise, then editor, then onMount — so the URI is null on the first pass, and\nnothing re-ran the effect unless the snapshot happened to arrive afterwards.\n\nBrowsing a connection and then opening a query document, which is the normal\norder, therefore produced a completion model that was never published: the\nprovider found no entry for the model and returned nothing, leaving only\nMonaco''s built-in keyword list. It appeared to work only when the schema\nfinished loading after the editor had mounted.\n\nThe URI is now state as well as a ref, so the effect runs again once it is\nknown, and `handleMount` also publishes immediately from prop mirrors rather\nthan waiting a render. The JSDoc records why.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 09f046243fcc62ae6b84836d12fd5119d10b6c41)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): say \"ran\" for reads, and stop claiming no limit was applied\n\nThree wording and modelling corrections. The QueryView side of each landed in\ne7c2adbd; this is the model and the tests.\n\nA read that finished now says `ran`; only a write or DDL says `committed`. The\nstrip could previously report `1–3 committed` for three SELECTs, which dilutes\nthe one word the app uses to mean the change is durable and cannot be undone\nfrom here. Anything not clearly read-only is still called committed, which is\nthe safe direction. `formatPriorStatements` builds the failure banner''s second\nsentence the same way, so a batch that failed after two reads says they ran\nrather than implying data changed.\n\n`applyRowLimit` now reports which of three states applies. A statement\ncarrying its own LIMIT used to render `100 rows returned (no limit applied)` —\ntrue of the app and badly misleading about the result, since the generated\n\"Select top 100\" reads exactly that way and a reader concludes the table holds\n100 rows. It now reads `(limit in statement)`, and the check runs before the\nheader selection so `All` reports it too.\n\nA statement rerun from the history drawer has no position in the editor\nbuffer, so `startLine`/`endLine` are optional rather than faked as line 1 — the\nstatus strip omits the line reference instead of pointing at unrelated text.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 91fb9fe13ed64c2aaf3f415b1fa72720af8e0153)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): stop telling users Refresh will find a missing table\n\nThe suggestion hint read \"Missing a table? Refresh schema on the connection\npage.\" On a node without the `refresh_schema` tool that advice does nothing at\nall: the node reflects once at task start and serves that reflection however\noften Refresh is pressed, so a table created since will not appear no matter\nwhat the user does from here.\n\nIt now reads \"Missing a table? The snapshot dates from HH:MM; Refresh re-reads\nit only on nodes with refresh_schema.\" — which dates the data and says what\nthe button can and cannot do, rather than sending the user round a loop that\ncannot succeed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit a1c7ca002cf2a1f6c20bf55bc9762989235b3d35)\n\n* chore(sql-ui): drop the unused table-name decoder\n\n`tableFromTableDataUri` was added as the symmetric counterpart to the URI\nencoder and has never had a caller — the views read their table from the\ndocument''s content payload. An export with no caller is a maintenance claim\nnobody is checking, so it and its tests go.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 497540e230285405ac222b1104419400a7e9343e)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): make Explain give the same answer however it is invoked\n\nThe button''s disabled state was computed from the debounced caret while the\nclick handler read the live editor handle, so the two could briefly disagree:\nthe button could look available and then do nothing. Worse, Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+E\nreturned silently whenever the statement could not be explained — a keybinding\nthat does nothing at all reads as a broken app, and unlike the button it has\nno tooltip to explain itself.\n\nBoth paths now go through one `explainRefusal` function, so they can never\noffer different reasons. The live handle still decides WHAT is explained, so a\ncaret move that has not settled cannot send the previous statement, and when\nthe answer is that nothing can be explained the reason is announced — the same\nsentence the button''s title carries.\n\nAlso drops SqlApp''s own copy of the query-document payload type in favour of\nthe one in docs.ts, which is where the panels that build the payload already\nread it from. The shapes were identical; two declarations of the same contract\nis one too many.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit d83e48224c0ab1d840ba86411f8a6861af2f724d)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): ask for a fresh schema read from the diagram''s Retry and Reverse Engineer\n\nThe two user-initiated refreshes in DiagramView still served the task-start\nsnapshot even on nodes that provide refresh_schema. Also drops the unused\nIQueryDocPayload re-export from SqlApp (producers import it from docs.ts).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit c9331827e435ab0f1bf3a8baf5be92b36c39d3ef)\n\n* docs(apps): sync the SQL Explorer guide with the reviewed UI wording\n\nReads say \"ran\" and writes \"committed\" in batch outcomes; the results line\nhas three limit states; the ClickHouse ALTER note covers only mutation forms.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit f9c7664ab4a6fbd27752db8fd59449874511e7d8)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): make the appended row limit reach the statement it bounds\n\n`applyRowLimit` ran its checks on comment-stripped text but built the\nstatement from the raw one and joined the clause with a space, so\n`SELECT * FROM orders -- daily` was sent as `... -- daily LIMIT 200`:\nthe database ran an unbounded SELECT while the meta line reported\n\"limit 200\". The clause is now joined with a newline, which terminates\na trailing line comment before LIMIT.\n\nThe `in-statement` check had the mirror-image problem: `/\\blimit\\b/i`\nmatched anywhere in the statement, so a LIMIT inside a subquery (or\ninside a string literal) made the app skip its own limit and label the\nresult \"limit in statement\" while the outer query streamed every row.\nIt now uses the splitter''s `hasTopLevelKeyword`, which already masks\nstrings and comments and only accepts a match at parenthesis depth 0.\n\nThe existing comment test asserted `.limit` alone and so never saw the\nbroken SQL; the regression tests assert `.sql` and re-strip comments\nfrom the emitted statement to prove LIMIT survives as code.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): enforce each dialect''s delimiter preconditions in the splitter\n\nTwo delimiters were recognised more eagerly than the engines recognise\nthem, and in both cases the scanner swallowed a statement separator.\n\nMySQL only starts a line comment when whitespace (or a control\ncharacter) follows `--`: it parses `SELECT 1--2; SELECT 3` as\narithmetic across two statements, while the scanner hid the semicolon\nand returned one. `opensDashComment` applies that precondition for\nMySQL alone — Postgres and ClickHouse keep taking a bare `--`.\n\nPostgreSQL allows `$` inside an unquoted identifier, so `foo$tag$` is\none identifier and opens no dollar-quoted body. `continuesIdentifier`\nrefuses a dollar quote whose `$` follows an identifier character, so\n`SELECT foo$tag$; SELECT 2` splits again.\n\nNear-miss cases for both are in the splitter''s table, including the\ncontrasting Postgres reading of the same `--` text.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): stop one bad refresh from disabling fresh reflection\n\nTwo ways a refresh could quietly stop happening, both leaving the app\ndescribing the database against a snapshot it knows is out of date.\n\nA single fallback latched `refreshTool: ''unavailable''` for the rest of\nthe app session, and nothing ever cleared it. But the session cannot\ntell a missing tool from a restarted task or a timed-out call — it says\nso itself in `connect/session.ts` — so one fallback during a `fresh`\nrefresh made every later Refresh Schema, Reverse Engineer and post-DDL\nread serve the task-start snapshot. The state now carries a `suspect`\nstep: one fallback suspects, a SECOND CONSECUTIVE one latches, and a\nfresh read that answers clears the suspicion. A node that really has no\n`refresh_schema` still stops paying two round trips after two tries.\n\n`refreshSchema` also returned immediately whenever a refresh was already\nloading, so Apply in TableDesignView could neither start nor await its\npost-DDL reflection — and the outcome effect composes its banner once,\nfrom the snapshot present when Apply resolves, so the change was\nreported against pre-DDL structure. Refreshes are now serialised per\nconnection: an ordinary read joins whatever is running, while a `fresh`\nrequest queues behind it and runs its own, because a plain re-read\ncannot answer a caller that just ran DDL.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): do not record history before the recording switch is read\n\nA persisted `sql.historyRecording[key] === false` is unreadable until a\nbridge mounts and `hydrate` runs. In that window `recording[key]` is\nmerely absent, which the guard read as the default — on — so a run that\nfinished first stored the user''s SQL, and `hydrate` then merged that\nentry and scheduled it to the workspace preferences file. History the\nuser had explicitly turned off was persisted, statement text and\nliteral values included.\n\nRuns that arrive before hydration now wait in a bounded queue and are\ndecided once the stored flags are in hand: replayed oldest-first when\nrecording is on for that connection, dropped when it is off. The queue\nis capped at 100 entries for the same reason the history itself is\nbounded — it holds the user''s SQL.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): keep a history entry''s row limit and its full statement\n\n`IHistoryEntry.limit` exists because a row count without its limit is\nambiguous — 1,000 rows is either the whole answer or the ceiling cutting\nit off. Two transformations lost it anyway: `collapse` kept the OLDER\nrun''s limit beside the NEWER run''s row count, and `readEntry` dropped\nthe field entirely on hydration, so every limit vanished the first time\nthe workspace file was re-read. The limit is now replaced with the rest\nof the run facts on collapse, and validated as a number on hydration.\n\n`trimHistory` also deduplicated AFTER truncating, so two different\nstatements sharing their first 8 KiB collapsed into one entry and one of\nthem was lost. Repeats are now collapsed on the untruncated text and the\nstorage bound is applied to what survives.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): make \"Run and stop asking\" hold for the rest of the batch\n\n`setPatternChecks` only updated state, and the ref the run loop reads\nwas mirrored by a passive effect. The loop resumes from its awaited\nconfirmation in a microtask and reaches the next statement''s check\nbefore React runs that effect, so iteration i + 1 still saw `true` and\nopened the dialog again — for every further matching statement in the\nsame batch, however many times the user chose \"Run and stop asking\".\n\nThe ref is now written next to the state update, which is the only\nwrite ordering the run loop can rely on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): restore the mounted flag when StrictMode replays the effect\n\nThe cleanup set `mountedRef.current = false` and only `useRef(true)`\never set it back, which a re-run of the effect on the SAME instance does\nnot re-execute. Under the shell''s `React.StrictMode` root that leaves\nthe flag false after the replayed setup, so `record` returns early and\nno completed statement reaches history for as long as that instance\nlives. The setup now asserts the flag it owns.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): bind the table name in both foreign-key catalog queries\n\n`fetchForeignKeyNames` interpolated the table name through\n`quoteLiteral`, which doubles `''` and leaves `\\` alone. On a MySQL\nconnection that honours backslash escapes — the default outside\nNO_BACKSLASH_ESCAPES — a name containing `\\''` ends the literal early\nand the remainder of the name becomes part of the WHERE predicate. The\nname is a VALUE compared against INFORMATION_SCHEMA columns, so both\ndialects now take it as a `$1` bind, the way every value in\n`sql/paging.ts` already travels; the node rewrites `$n` per driver.\n\nThat left `sql/quote.ts` with no callers. Its own header says it exists\nfor the statements that cannot bind, and there are none left, so the\nmodule and its suite go with the last use rather than standing by as a\nready-made way to reintroduce the same class of defect.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): do not render an error headline with nothing after it\n\n`describeFailure('''')` returned `generic: false`, and both the query view\nand the Explain drawer show the headline whenever `generic` is false —\nso an Error carrying an empty message produced the banner \"Database\nreported:\" followed by nothing. An empty (or whitespace-only) message is\nthe same situation as the node''s own placeholder: there is nothing real\nto quote, which is exactly what the generic wording says.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): make the drawers report what actually happened\n\nFour places where the UI said nothing, or said the wrong thing.\n\nCopy in the row inspector took an optional chain through\n`navigator.clipboard`, so an absent Clipboard API skipped both callbacks\nand the button neither copied nor announced anything. A missing API now\nannounces the same failure a rejection does.\n\nThe Explain drawer showed \"Explaining…\" for as long as the shell\nconnection was down, because `run` returns before sending anything when\nthere is no client — so `running` never became true and no request was\never in flight. It now says it is not connected.\n\nThe history row''s statement and note were inline spans, where\n`overflow`, `text-overflow` and vertical margins have no effect: a long\none-line statement overflowed the drawer instead of ending in an\nellipsis, and the note ran on beside the statement instead of under it.\nBoth are block boxes now.\n\nThe live region replaced its child by key, which performs the removal\nand the insertion in ONE commit; assistive technology can coalesce that\ninto no change and miss a repeated sentence. It now empties and restores\nthe text in separate tasks, cancelling a pending restore when a newer\nannouncement arrives.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): keep a control''s outcome to the control the user pressed\n\nThe data grid''s error banner survived until the NEXT request succeeded,\nso after one failed page a fresh search, sort or page left the previous\nrequest''s failure on screen while the grid loaded different parameters —\nthe banner described a question the user had already moved on from. The\nerror is now cleared where the new sequence is taken.\n\nIn the designer, the column row carries button semantics and contains\nthe Drop button, so a click (or Enter/Space) on Drop bubbled into\n`selectColumn`: the column was dropped and its editor opened at the same\ntime, leaving the user staging an edit for a column that is on its way\nout. The cell around the button stops propagation — the shell''s Button\npasses no event to its handler, so it cannot be stopped at the source.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): open the table record drawer where it can be seen\n\n`requestTableRecord` is raised by the Insights panel and by the sidebar\ntree, but the drawer it opens is rendered by OverviewPanel — and\nTabPanel hides the inactive panel with `display: none`, so a table\nopened while Insights was in front raised a drawer inside a hidden\nsubtree. The connection view now brings Overview forward when a request\nfor its own connection arrives, which covers the sidebar route as well.\nRequests raised before the document mounted are ignored, so opening a\nconnection never overrides the page the user left it on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(apps): state where a statement''s own LIMIT has to be\n\nTwo sentences in the SQL Explorer guide now match what the code does:\n`limit in statement` reports a LIMIT on the outermost query only, and\nthe splitter paragraph names the two dialect preconditions a reader\nwould otherwise have to discover — MySQL''s whitespace after `--`, and a\n`$tag$` that continues an identifier opening no dollar quote.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(sql-ui): test MySQL''s comment precondition without a control regex\n\nThe dash-comment check spelled its \"whitespace or control character\"\nrule as a regex class covering U+0000-U+001F, which the repo''s\n`no-control-regex` lint rejects. A `\\s` test plus a code-point\ncomparison says the same thing and keeps `eslint apps/sql-ui` clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): never collapse a history entry that is already truncated\n\ntrimHistory compares statements on their text, and the previous fix moved\nthe collapse ahead of the per-entry bound so a FRESH pair is compared on\nfull text. That protects an entry only up to its first truncation.\n\nhistoryStore.hydrate reads bounded entries back out of the workspace file\nand passes them through trimHistory again on every reload. By then `sql`\nis a prefix, so two different statements that agree for their first\nmaxSqlChars characters satisfy sameStatement and one real entry is\ncollapsed away — silently, and once per app open.\n\nA prefix cannot prove two statements are the same, so a truncated entry is\nno longer merged at all. The alternative, persisting a full-text\nfingerprint, was rejected: it widens the persisted schema with another\nfield read back from an untrusted, hand-editable file, and every entry\nalready on disk lacks it, so the conservative rule would still be needed\nas the fallback. What it buys is collapsing consecutive re-runs of an\n8 KB+ statement, which costs one extra row when it does not happen.\n\nTests: a reload-shaped regression (trim, then trim the output again) and\nthe fresh-repeat-of-a-truncated-entry case. Both fail without the guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): write the history recording switch through, not on the batch\n\nsetRecording only called schedulePersist, a 300 ms debounce, and the last\nbridge detach CANCELS a pending write. Switching apps within that window\ndropped the write: the workspace file kept saying recording was on, and\nthe next page load resumed recording SQL on a connection the user had\nswitched off. The queue added for pre-hydration runs narrowed the exposure\nbut never closed it, because it only governs runs that arrive before the\nflag is readable, not a flag that never reached disk.\n\nThe switch is now written through immediately. Runs still batch — they\narrive in bursts and one lost run costs a list row — but a switch is\nflipped once, deliberately, and losing it costs the user a decision about\ntheir own data that they will never be told was discarded.\n\nA second defect sat behind the same line. hydrate merged the whole\nin-memory `recording` map over the stored flags, so any flag from an\nearlier attach outranked the file for the rest of the session — a stale\n`true` surviving a detach turned recording back on for a connection the\nfile says is off. Only overrides that have NOT yet reached disk may\noutrank the file, so they are tracked separately in `unsavedRecording`,\ncleared on a successful write, and hydrate now schedules a write when one\nis still outstanding (a switch flipped while no bridge was mounted).\n\nattachPrefs is exported: it is the store''s real seam onto the shell, and\nthe rules worth testing live there rather than in the null-rendering\nbridge component.\n\nThe module had no test coverage at all. It now has seven tests driving it\nthe way the app does — attach, act, detach — asserting on what reached a\nfake workspace file, because that file is the privacy boundary. Three of\nthem fail against the previous code.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(apps): state what \"no limit applied\" means with All and an inner LIMIT\n\napplyRowLimit recognises only a LIMIT on the outermost query. With the\nheader set to All, a statement whose only LIMIT sits in a subquery\ntherefore reports state `none`: QueryView prints `no limit applied` and\nnothing is appended. The guide described `no limit applied` as the case\nfor All \"with no LIMIT in the text\", which reads as a contradiction the\nfirst time someone sees the line on a statement that plainly contains one.\n\nThe guide now names that combination and shows a join against a limited\nsubquery, where the inner bound genuinely does not bound the result, so\nthe line is read as a statement about the result rather than the text.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(sql-ui): fail the test target when discovery finds no tests\n\n`builder test` runs sql-ui:test in CI, and the action returned cleanly —\ntask.output set, exit 0 — both when tests/ was absent and when the\n*.test.ts(x) filter matched nothing. Either state reported the whole\ntarget green while not a single assertion had run, over a suite of 521\ntests that is the only cover this app has.\n\nBoth cases are now errors. tests/ is tracked source: a directory that has\ngone missing, or a…","tree":{"sha":"d3fd6b8504a787a946161df9414006a3b325a742","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/d3fd6b8504a787a946161df9414006a3b325a742"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/d069ce7fd9ebfda1fb638a9b84fd08734603cc17","verification":{"payload":"tree d3fd6b8504a787a946161df9414006a3b325a742\nparent 6a99fb3c00249869c337671a384786f1fc52d588\nauthor NIHAL NIHALANI <nihal.nihalani@gmail.com> 1790007668 +0530\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790007668 +0530\n\nfeat(sql-ui): statement runner, real error text, schema-aware completion, history, relationships, plain EXPLAIN, honest designer (#2282)\n\n* test(sql-ui): add node:test harness and pure-logic suites\n\nSQL Explorer had no test setup. Add the explorer-ui harness (tsx +\nnode:test, a `sql-ui:test` builder action over tests/) and six pure-logic\nsuites covering the modules where the audit found bugs.\n\nUnlike explorer-ui, docs.ts imports Documents / NOOP_VFS from ''shell'' as\nvalues, so the runner preloads a local stub-shell.cjs (apps/shared''s\npattern, copied rather than shared: extending apps/shared''s stub would\nchange a file its own suite depends on). Every other tested module reaches\nthe platform through `import type` alone, which tsx erases.\n\n15 of the 71 tests fail on purpose. They encode the fixed behaviour for two\naudited correctness bugs, ahead of the fix in the next commit:\n\n- sql/paging.ts inlines filter and search values, passing numeric-looking\n  strings through unquoted, so `code >= ''0100''` on a VARCHAR column becomes\n  a number comparison (MySQL coerces the column, Postgres raises).\n- docs.ts leaves the table name unencoded in `table:` / `design:` URIs, so\n  a table whose name contains a colon splits one segment too late and\n  yields an endpoint key that does not exist.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 8f788cf75fd14235ed07d3bb5ba0acac24f8041b)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): parse plain EXPLAIN output into a plan tree\n\nAdds the pure half of the query-plan feature: buildExplain() prefixes the\ndialect''s plain EXPLAIN form (mysql FORMAT=JSON, postgres (FORMAT JSON),\nclickhouse text) to the statement exactly as it would run, and returns null\nfor dialects with no support here. parseExplain() normalises the three\ndocumented result shapes into one IPlanNode tree, flagging planner numbers so\nthe UI can suffix them with `est.`.\n\nPlain EXPLAIN only — nothing here emits ANALYZE, so the explained statement is\nnever executed. Postgres arrives either already parsed or as a JSON string\ndepending on the driver; both are handled.\n\nA shape the parser does not recognise reports `ok: false` with a reason rather\nthan throwing, so the caller keeps showing the database''s raw output.\n\nEvery fixture is hand-built from vendor documentation, not captured from a\nlive server: no MySQL, PostgreSQL or ClickHouse instance was reachable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit a1c0678b2531f0dfe049c7c41b07841b4c6c30a9)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): bind filter values, remote sort, page errors, URI table encoding\n\nFive audited correctness bugs in the data browser and its URI scheme, plus\nthe two session capabilities the rest of the upgrade needs.\n\npaging: filter, range-bound, and search values were rendered into the\nstatement by quoteValue, which passed numeric-looking strings through\nunquoted — `code >= ''0100''` on a VARCHAR column became a number\ncomparison, matching the wrong rows on MySQL and raising a type error on\nPostgres. buildPageStatements now returns `params` alongside the two\nstatements and binds every user value as `$1..$n`; quoteValue is gone and\nintrospect''s always-quoting quoteLiteral moves to sql/quote.ts as the app''s\nsingle literal quoter. LIKE patterns are assembled as bind VALUES, because\nthe node rewrites `$n` textually before binding.\n\nTableDataView: SELECT and COUNT now run in parallel; header sorts reach\n`req.sort` (remoteSort); a failed page shows an error Banner instead of\nfailing silently behind the grid''s transient toast; and a monotonic request\nsequence keeps a slow, superseded page from overwriting a newer one. A\ntable with no primary key and no user sort pages without any ORDER BY, so\nthe view says the order is not guaranteed.\n\ndocs: `table:` / `design:` URIs percent-encode the table segment. The\nendpoint key contains colons, so these schemes split on the last one — a\ntable named `odd:name` split one segment too late and produced an endpoint\nkey that does not exist.\n\nconnect: `execute` takes optional bind params, and `ISqlSession` gains\n`refreshSchema()` over the node''s `refresh_schema` tool, falling back to\n`get_schema` with `stale: true` when the node does not have it. The\nengine''s exact wording for an unowned tool is not readable from this repo\n(it comes from the compiled engine), so `isUnsupportedToolError` classifies\nconservatively: false only for errors that clearly came from a tool that\nran, true otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 98f7303c6b8cef104a80ddce0c43d327ffa200ad)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): map declared foreign keys into a relation graph and join paths\n\nThe record drawer and the join-path finder need to answer three questions\nfrom a schema snapshot alone: what points at this table, how do I get from\nhere to there, and what does that join look like as SQL.\n\nEverything here comes from keys the engine DECLARED. Nothing is inferred\nfrom a column or table name, anywhere — a name that looks like a key is a\nguess, and a guess presented as a relationship is worse than no answer. A\nschema that models its relationships only by convention (and ClickHouse,\nwhich declares none at all) therefore produces an empty graph, which is the\nhonest result rather than a bug.\n\nfindJoinPaths returns EVERY shortest path instead of picking one. Two\ntables are frequently joinable in several equally short ways — through\nparallel keys, or through different intermediate tables — and which one is\nmeant depends on the question being asked, which this module cannot know.\nSelf-referential keys are shown in the drawer but never walked, so a path\ncannot loop; composite keys carry all their column pairs into a multi-column\nON clause; and keys pointing outside the snapshot are kept as edges for the\nquality rules but are never walkable.\n\nThe generated SELECT says what it is on its first line: generated from\ndeclared foreign keys, review before running.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit e2c893e669d798fc6d60e9125283ecbfeac04189)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): record finished statements and bound them before they are kept\n\nQuery history rides the app''s workspace preferences file, which the shell\nreads and writes on every app switch. An unbounded list would make that\nswitch slower for everyone, so the bounds are enforced in pure functions\nbefore every write rather than left as good intentions: 100 entries per\nconnection, 8 KB per statement, 1 KB per error, 256 KB across the whole bag.\n\nPins buy priority, not immunity. They count against the same 100 and are\ndropped last, because an unbounded pinned list is the same problem wearing a\nnicer name. When a repeat of the same statement collapses onto the entry\nbefore it, the OLDER entry survives with its id, pin and note intact — a\nperson who annotated a statement meant that statement, and re-running it\nmust not quietly discard what they wrote.\n\nrunEvents is a one-way bus so the runner never imports the store: a run is\nnever blocked by, or aware of, whether anything is recording it, and a\nlistener that throws cannot fail the query that just succeeded.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit e1fbc2e8a48ee9b60953dd9d074992f7e933f7f4)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): tell the truth about the schema after a designer Apply\n\nThe node reflects its schema once, at task start. The designer used to re-read\nthat same snapshot after Apply and repaint it, so a freshly added column\nappeared to vanish. Apply now asks for a fresh reflection and says which of the\ntwo things actually happened:\n\n- re-read succeeded: `Applied HH:MM · schema re-read from the database.`\n- the node has no refresh_schema tool, or the re-read failed: a warning saying\n  the tree and diagram will not show the change until the pipeline restarts.\n\nBecause the answer depends on the snapshot that comes back, the banner is\ncomposed in an effect against the post-refresh snapshot rather than inside the\nApply closure, which cannot see it.\n\nA batch that stops part-way now reports what committed, which statement failed\nwith the database''s own first line, what never ran, and how many statements the\nplan still holds — instead of a bare `Apply failed:`.\n\nThe confirmation no longer says schema changes \"cannot be undone from here\",\nwhich reads as if a rollback existed elsewhere. It states the statement count\nand the engine''s actual behaviour for a half-finished plan.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 167ea69d4c081f8e85746c6f82000c95f403e3b5)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add dialect-aware statement splitter and classifier\n\nThe database node executes exactly ONE statement per `execute` call, so a\nbuffer holding several statements has to be split in the app before anything\nis sent. `sql/split.ts` is a hand-written scanner rather than a regex because\na `;` separates statements only in code: inside a string literal, a quoted\nidentifier, a line or block comment, or a PostgreSQL dollar-quoted body it is\nordinary text. Every dialect difference the scanner claims is asserted in the\ntests instead of assumed — MySQL''s `#` line comments (which ClickHouse does\nnot have), PostgreSQL''s nested block comments, dollar-quoted bodies and\n`E''...''` backslash escapes, and ClickHouse''s plain backslash escapes.\n\nMySQL''s `DELIMITER` directive is NOT supported and says so in the JSDoc and in\na test that pins the wrong-but-documented split, because a half-working\nimplementation of a client-side directive is worse than an honest gap.\n\n`sql/classify.ts` decides whether the runner may pass `idempotent: true` (which\npermits one silent retry with a fresh token), so anything not clearly read-only\nis not a read: `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` is classified by the statement it runs, and a\n`WITH` chain containing a data-modifying keyword is a write. Its `patternCheck`\nis the text heuristic behind the confirmation dialog; the false positives it is\nallowed to have (a WHERE that exists only inside a subquery) are pinned by test\nso they are not \"fixed\" into false negatives.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 1942a0b4f4487f06bc8972e4f99cb1c9e512f7d3)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add pattern notes and raw-text rendering to the plan parser\n\nplanNotes() turns a handful of documented planner markers into plain-language\nobservations, each citing the exact field it read: MySQL access_type = ALL,\nfilesort and temporary tables (in either the tabular Extra column or the\nFORMAT=JSON booleans), and PostgreSQL sequential scans. Row estimates carried\ninto a note keep the `est.` suffix.\n\nThese are text rules over planner output, not a cost model. A node no rule\nmatches gets no note — silence is not a verdict — and the UI labels the column\n`detected by pattern` so nobody mistakes them for measurements.\n\nformatRawPlan() renders the result as the text a reader would have seen at a\nSQL prompt, which is what the panel shows first and by default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 3de518dd4d52aec8ab06d5b5214632192f4dd2fd)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): keep query history in the workspace, never in the browser\n\nHistory lives in the shell''s workspace preferences — server-side and per\nuser — so it follows the person to another browser or machine, which is the\nwhole reason it is worth keeping at all.\n\nThat store has one sharp edge, and it decides the design of this file.\n`setPref` patches whichever app is active AT WRITE TIME, and the shell\nre-reads each app''s preferences from disk on every app switch. A write\nissued while SQL Explorer sits in the background would be filed under the\napp in the foreground and then lost. So the store writes only while a\nHistoryPrefsBridge is mounted, and the shell unmounts the app''s whole\nsubtree when another app takes over, which makes \"mounted\" mean \"SQL\nExplorer is on screen\". On unmount the pending write is dropped rather than\nflushed: up to 300 ms of history can be lost if someone switches apps\nimmediately after a run, and that is the honest cost of never writing into\nanother app''s preferences.\n\nEverything read back from the workspace file is narrowed field by field.\nThe file outlives this version of the app and a person can edit it by hand,\nso it is treated as untrusted input rather than as our own types.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit f2a265442a71369598e73a94d232422de4c8fd20)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add a plain EXPLAIN plan panel\n\nExplainPanel is self-contained: given an endpoint, a dialect and the statement\nexactly as it would run, it performs the EXPLAIN itself through the session and\nshows what came back. Integration into the query runner is a separate change.\n\nThe database''s RAW OUTPUT is the default view. The interpreted tree sits behind\na second toggle, and a plan shape the parser does not recognise is an info note\nbeside the raw text — not an error, because nothing failed on the database''s\nside. A node that refuses execute gets the allow_execute sentence instead of\ndriver text, since the statement never reached the database.\n\nPlanTree renders the plan as an ARIA tree: arrow keys walk and expand rows,\nevery planner number carries `est.`, and the notes column is labelled\n`detected by pattern` with each note citing the field it read. No cost bars and\nno colour as meaning — every state is text.\n\nPlain EXPLAIN only. Nothing here runs the statement being explained.\n\nThe parsers are fixture-tested against documented output shapes; no live MySQL,\nPostgreSQL or ClickHouse server was reachable to validate them against.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 98c005937c34c7b67caa5db6a244e4813cbbad2b)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add the query history drawer\n\nA modeless right drawer, so the editor behind it stays usable — the point of\nhistory is to work alongside the statement being written, not instead of it.\nSearch, filter by reads/writes/errors/pinned, expand a row for the full text,\nthen load it into the editor, rerun it, pin it, annotate it, delete it, or\nopen it in a new query document.\n\nThe privacy notice is permanent, not a first-run tip. These statements are\nwritten verbatim to the user''s workspace file on the RocketRide server,\nliteral values and all. Someone who assumed browser-local storage would be\nwrong in a way that matters, so the drawer says where the text goes for as\nlong as it is open.\n\nRerunning anything that changed data asks first, and the warning quotes what\nthe statement did LAST time rather than pretending to know what it will do\nnow — nothing is counted or checked against the database beforehand. The\nkind badge comes from a text pattern, which the wording reflects.\n\n\"Load into editor\" does not ask before replacing the editor''s text: the\ndrawer cannot tell an untouched editor from an edited one, so the query view\nowns that confirmation. \"Open in new query\" deliberately does not mark the\ndocument as generated — the statement is the user''s own text and must not\nclaim to be something this app wrote.\n\nannounce() lands here because the shell has no live region: useAnnouncements\nfetches GitHub product announcements and only Banner sets aria-live, so\npinning or clearing an entry would otherwise be silent to a screen reader.\nThe visible live region that reads this channel is mounted by the app shell.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 7c88324f53c787838395a86a2829213a10dbe82f)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): retry a failed tool call only when re-running it is safe\n\nA session caches the owning task''s token, and that token goes stale\nwhenever the task restarts. The transport reports a stale token exactly\nlike a statement the database refused, so the blanket one-retry meant a\nwrite could be sent twice: the first INSERT may well have committed before\nthe failure surfaced.\n\nRetrying is now a per-call decision. `execute` takes `opts.idempotent` and\nretries only when the caller sets it; reflection and dialect reads keep the\nretry, since repeating them is free. TableDataView''s SELECT and COUNT pass\nit; the query runner will pass it only for statements its classifier marks\nas reads. An unmarked statement is assumed to write and its first failure\nis the answer.\n\nrefreshSchema no longer inspects error text to decide whether the node has\nthe refresh tool. The engine offers no dependable signal for \"no such\ntool\", and matching its wording would rot the first time it changed, so ANY\nfailure falls back to get_schema with `stale: true` — and schemaStore\nremembers that per endpoint, so later refreshes on a node without the tool\nread the snapshot directly instead of paying for a doomed call each time.\nisUnsupportedToolError and its marker lists are gone.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 9c2f7826d52c0d2ab4ce7eaca010b2de82d6647f)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): show inbound keys, preview queries and join paths in the record drawer\n\nThe drawer already said what a table declares. It could not say what points\nAT the table, which is the half of a relationship a person usually arrives\nlooking for, and it left the obvious next step — actually querying the thing\n— as an exercise.\n\n\"Referenced by\" lists the inbound foreign keys and opens the referencing\ntable in a STACKED drawer, so following a relationship keeps the way back\nunder the shell''s own back arrow instead of quietly replacing what was on\nscreen. The join-path finder walks declared keys undirectedly to four joins\nand lists EVERY shortest path rather than picking one, because which join is\nmeant depends on a question the schema cannot answer. Parallel keys and\ncomposite keys each come out as their own path, with all their column pairs\nin the ON clause.\n\nBoth sections carry \"from schema snapshot HH:MM\". A snapshot read at task\nstart can be older than the database it describes, and a relationship\npresented without its age invites a decision based on a stale reading.\n\nWhere there are no declared keys there is no guessing: the section says the\nschema declares none, and ClickHouse says so in its own terms. Nothing here\ninfers a relationship from a column name.\n\nSelect top 100 and Count rows open a NEW query document whose first line\nmarks it as a generated preview. Neither runs anything.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 27c89777443c87ca3b4e217b812e27546a5f5d18)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): show inbound foreign keys before applying a designer plan\n\nThe Apply confirmation now has an Impact section naming every table that\ndeclares a foreign key on a column the plan drops, renames or retypes, and\nstamps where those facts came from: `from schema snapshot HH:MM`. The wording\nsays the database MAY reject or cascade the change, because the tool asks the\nsnapshot, not the database.\n\nRenames are traced back to the name the snapshot knows. A plan that renames a\ncolumn and then retypes it under its new name still finds the keys pointing at\nthe original, which a match on the operation''s own name would miss. Composite\nkeys pair their columns positionally.\n\nDropping a column adds a line saying its data is not recoverable by this tool.\n\nColumn rows already acted as buttons on click; they now carry role, a tab stop,\nan accessible name and Enter/Space, so the designer is reachable without a\nmouse. Dropped rows and create-mode drafts stay inert, as they were.\n\nExplainPanel announces a finished plan through the app''s live region: unlike a\nBanner, a tree and a `pre` do not announce themselves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit c7dc728d9fbab75f4bbe3f0dc701bed40eec2732)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): type result columns and suggest from the schema snapshot\n\n`sql/resultTypes.ts` replaces the blanket `rrType: ''string''` on query results,\nwhich disabled numeric sorting and the grid''s number and date range filters.\nTypes come from the schema snapshot only when the source table is CERTAIN —\none FROM, no join, and every returned column belongs to that table — and from\nthe returned values otherwise; which of the two was used is reported so the\ncolumn tooltip can say `BIGINT (schema)` or `number (inferred from 200 rows)`\nrather than presenting a guess as a fact.\n\nType-name matching is whole-token, fixing the substring test that claimed\nPOINT as a number and missed MONEY, REAL and SERIAL. Value inference accounts\nfor the node''s sanitisation (Decimal arrives as a float, datetime as an ISO\nstring) and keeps `0123` a string, since a leading zero is the difference\nbetween a product code and a number.\n\n`sql/completion.ts` builds the suggestion model: alias-scoped columns for an\n`alias.` prefix, table names after FROM/JOIN/UPDATE/INTO, columns of the\ntables the buffer mentions, and keyword snippets — ordered entirely through\n`sortText` because Monaco merges these with its own keyword list. JOIN clauses\nare prefilled from DECLARED foreign keys, in both directions, with composite\nkeys producing a multi-column ON; nothing is inferred from a column''s name.\nIdentifiers that need quoting get it in the inserted text only.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit c4af7d8cad2d922b71a6413be6d7ee67486f5ee4)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): mark the EXPLAIN round trip idempotent\n\nPlain EXPLAIN plans the statement without running it, so the session may retry\nit once with a re-resolved token after a task restart. Without the flag the\nEXPLAIN failed outright whenever the cached token had gone stale.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 24ad6e1e361a7a1d6e0ebc01bb4ffe5b74e557ef)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): keep history recorded before preferences were reachable\n\nRuns can finish before any prefs bridge is mounted — a query document open\nwhile its connection workbench is closed records straight into memory. The\nstore then replaced that bag wholesale on hydration, throwing those entries\naway at the exact moment they first became saveable.\n\nHydration now merges instead: the in-memory entry wins on a shared id, the\ntwo lists are combined by age and re-trimmed, and a write is scheduled for\nwhatever was waiting. A recording switch flipped before the bridge mounted\noutranks the stored one for the same reason.\n\nThe connection workbench also mounts a bridge, so an open workbench keeps\nthe write channel alive for every query document on that connection rather\nthan only while the History drawer happens to be open.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 0a70a2429089b4087e6d63b91aa65c5cb2ed0340)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): add the Insights page — schema shape and snapshot-only review\n\nA second page on the connection workbench that says how the schema is shaped\nand what is demonstrably wrong with it, without touching the database: hubs,\nleaves and isolated tables from declared keys, then three rules over the same\nsnapshot. It works with execute disabled, because it never executes anything.\n\nEach rule stops where the evidence stops. R1, a table with no primary key, is\ncertain. R3, a key naming a table or column the snapshot does not have, is\ncertain within the snapshot — and a snapshot read before a migration can\nraise it against a perfectly consistent database, which is why the page\nstates when it was read. R2, the two sides of a key spelling their type\ndifferently, is a TEXT comparison after normalisation rather than a\ncompatibility check, so it is information and never a verdict.\n\nNormalisation drops parenthesised arguments (INT(11) is INT) and resolves a\nshort, exact alias list — every entry is a spelling the engines themselves\ntreat as identical, not a judgement that two types are close enough.\n\nThere is no naming rule. A column called customer_id that happens to match\nanother table''s primary key is a guess, and a guess sitting in a list of\nfindings reads as a fact.\n\nThe limitations banner is not boilerplate. It is the scope of every claim\nthe page makes, and it stays on screen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit ef3359f00dd523ca249c4bfcf848e6f54d565baa)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): give the editor a run handle, schema completion and decorations\n\n`SqlEditor` grows the surface the runner needs: a ref handle for the live\nselection, caret offset and statement decorations, `Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+Enter` for\nrun-all, and `Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+E` behind an OPTIONAL `onExplain` prop so the\nkeybinding exists before the panel that answers it does.\n\nTwo things are module-level on purpose. The completion provider is registered\nonce per language id, not per editor: `registerCompletionItemProvider` is\nglobal and every query document stays mounted, so per-instance registration\nwould stack providers and repeat every suggestion once per open tab. What\nvaries per editor is looked up by model URI in a map each instance maintains,\nand the providers are deliberately never disposed — they belong to the module,\nand tearing them down when one tab closes would break the others. The\ndecoration stylesheet is injected once behind an id guard, the pattern\n`InputField` uses for its placeholder colour, because inline styles cannot\nreach a Monaco decoration class.\n\n`sql/batch.ts` holds the per-statement run model and its wording, including\nthe batch summary that has to say a failed batch left earlier statements\ncommitted, and `applyRowLimit`, which appends the header limit only to\nstatements that return rows and leaves a user''s own LIMIT alone.\n\n`sql/failure.ts` reads a failed execute against the four messages the node\nactually produces, matching the machine-stable fragment (`allow_execute`,\n`max_execute_rows`) rather than a whole sentence. It recognises the generic\nplaceholder that node versions predating the error-text fix return, so the app\ncan say the driver''s message was not returned instead of presenting the\nplaceholder as what the database said.\n\n`LiveRegion` renders the existing `a11y/announce` store into one polite live\nregion, keyed by the store''s revision so announcing the same sentence twice\nstill speaks.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 6a429e2790cd938a54b277bb06be6e5f58a367b7)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): run the selection or statement at the cursor, honestly\n\nThe query runner previously sent the WHOLE editor buffer as one `execute`,\nwhich the driver rejects for anything multi-statement, and reported failures\nthrough a bare error string. It now splits the buffer and runs one statement\nper call, because that is what the node supports.\n\nEverything the runner says is tied to how the node actually executes:\n\n- Each statement runs in its OWN transaction, so a batch that fails partway\n  leaves the earlier statements committed. The status strip shows every\n  statement''s outcome and the summary line says which ones committed, which\n  failed and which never ran. The error banner repeats it for the failure.\n- There is no cancel anywhere in the tool protocol. \"Stop waiting\" appears\n  after a second, bumps a run sequence so the late answer is discarded, and\n  says the database may still be running the statement. It never claims to\n  have stopped anything.\n- Transaction-control statements would each land in a separate autocommit\n  call, so they are refused before anything is sent rather than run into a\n  false sense of safety.\n- `idempotent` is passed only for statements the classifier reads as READ, so\n  the session''s retry-with-a-fresh-token can never re-send a write.\n- The confirmation dialog for an unbounded UPDATE/DELETE, TRUNCATE, DROP or\n  ALTER calls itself a text check and says it is not a database safeguard. It\n  is not called a safe mode, because it prevents nothing.\n- When the node returns its generic placeholder instead of the driver''s\n  message, the banner says the message was not returned rather than showing\n  the placeholder as the database''s words. When the driver''s text IS there, it\n  is shown verbatim in a collapsible block.\n\nWhat a run would send is named in TEXT under the editor before the key is\npressed, not only by a decoration, and the same applies to the result meta:\nthe applied limit is stated, and a result that exactly fills the limit is\nbadged as possibly incomplete rather than presented as the whole answer.\n\nExport is a text hint pointing at the grid''s gear menu. `IDataGridHandle`\nexposes the Tabulator instance but the shell registers 13 Tabulator modules\nand DownloadModule is not among them, so `table.download()` does not exist;\nadding a second CSV writer to the app was explicitly out of scope.\n\nResult columns are typed from the schema when the source table is certain and\nfrom the values otherwise, which restores numeric sorting and the range\nfilters, and the column tooltip says which of the two it was.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 863dc06a7f6ae2a9f2b9d994e9e560db21803fed)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): read the live selection on the run path, settle a pending confirm\n\nTwo narrow correctness fixes in the runner.\n\nThe run path took the selected TEXT from the editor handle but its OFFSETS\nfrom the debounced cursor state, so a run fired within the debounce window of\na drag attributed the run to the previous selection''s lines — the statement\nsent was right, the lines reported for it were not. The handle now exposes\n`getSelection()`, returning text and offsets together from the live editor.\n\nA pattern-check confirmation open when the document unmounts left the run loop\nawaiting a promise nothing could settle. The unmount now answers it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit f753c373e15dbad037b4e43e6729864385214cec)\n\n* docs(apps): add the SQL Explorer guide\n\nSQL Explorer had no product page: the only mention of the app anywhere in\ndocs/ was a builder task reference. This adds the spine page and its Apps\nsidebar entry, describing the app as it actually behaves today.\n\nThe page leads with the constraints rather than hiding them, because they\nare what surprises people: every statement travels through a pipeline\ndatabase node, so `allow_execute` gates execution, each statement commits\non its own, transaction control is refused, nothing can be cancelled, and\nthe schema is a snapshot taken when the task started. The verbatim UI\nmicrocopy is quoted so the docs and the app say the same words.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 1601764e6db8eabbf2de7e20b7dc77c0cd605271)\n\n* feat(sql-ui): finish the runner''s drawers — focus return, explain gating, prefs bridge\n\nCompletes the integration points QueryView owns, now that the history and\nexplain panels are on the branch.\n\nFocus return. Closing either drawer puts focus back on the button that opened\nit. The shell''s Button forwards no ref, so each opener sits in a\n`display: contents` wrapper the view can query — no layout change, and the\nalternative (leaving focus on the document body) strands keyboard users at the\ntop of the page after every close.\n\nExplain gating. The button is disabled with a reason rather than silently\ndoing nothing: `buildExplain` returns null for engines with no supported\nEXPLAIN form and for an empty statement, and the title says which it was. The\npanel is now mounted unconditionally and driven by `open`, as its own contract\nasks, and is handed the statement exactly as Run would send it — applied LIMIT\nincluded — so the plan describes the real statement.\n\nHistory prefs bridge. The history store only writes while a bridge is mounted.\nOne at app level covers a query document whose connection tab has been closed;\nthe bridge is reference-counted, so the copies in ConnectionView and in the\ndrawer stay harmless.\n\nAnnouncements. `Banner` is itself a live region for every variant — assertive\nfor error, polite otherwise — so announcing an event that is also rendered in\na Banner reads it out twice. The transaction refusal, the stop-waiting notice\nand the failure announcement are dropped for that reason; what remains\nannounced is only what has no live region of its own: the batch summary, the\nrow and affected counts, the pattern-check switch, and loading a statement\ninto the editor.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 902f2cd76c9b0d0361225a091ba6e1560919726f)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): ask for a fresh read when the user presses Refresh\n\nEvery user-facing Refresh called `refreshSchema` without `{ fresh: true }`,\nso it re-read the node''s task-start snapshot and returned the same answer\nit already had — even on a node that has the `refresh_schema` tool and could\nhave gone to the database. The one place that asked for a fresh read was the\ndesigner''s post-Apply path. Pressing Refresh after a CREATE TABLE therefore\ndid nothing visible, which is the worst possible result: the control looks\nbroken when the capability was there all along.\n\nThe workbench header, the Overview retry and the sidebar tree''s refresh now\nall ask for a fresh read. Automatic first loads still do not, because a page\nopening does not need two round trips to say what it already serves.\n\nThe header stamp stops blurring the two things a \"read\" can mean. A node\nthat re-read the database says `snapshot re-read HH:MM`; a node serving the\nreflection taken when its task started says `task-start snapshot, read\nHH:MM`, however recently we asked. The old wording let the second pass for\nthe first, which is what made the missing table look like our bug rather\nthan a node capability that is not there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 17353eb86de6cabda7ec205f70b834c65b67afce)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): stop quoting the node''s placeholder as the database''s words\n\nA node without the execute error-text fix returns `SQL execution failed (check\nserver logs for details)`. The Explain panel and the designer both printed it\nafter `Database reported: `, presenting the node''s placeholder as if the\ndatabase had said it. Both now route through describeFailure and show the\nfallback sentence when the message is generic, with no \"Database said\" block\nto open — there is nothing in it.\n\nWhere the driver''s text IS real, both surfaces now match the query runner: the\nfirst line as the headline, the full text behind a collapsible block. The\ndesigner had no such block before, so multi-line driver text was lost.\n\nThe Explain subtitle said \"Validated by EXPLAIN\". Nothing is validated — the\nplanner returned an estimate, which the same line goes on to say. It now reads\n`EXPLAIN ran HH:MM`.\n\nThe ClickHouse note claimed ALTER runs as an asynchronous mutation. That holds\nfor ALTER ... UPDATE/DELETE, not for metadata operations like ADD COLUMN, so it\nnow says some ALTER forms do.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 724d70696fcaa8dbde790ce66bd9d7f620738685)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): let the review rules know the dialect, and say what they mean\n\nR1 had no dialect input, so \"table has no primary key\" fired on every\nClickHouse table — the engine reflects no primary-key constraint for any of\nthem, a MergeTree ORDER BY not being one. The Insights tab then showed a\nnumber equal to the table count and the grid called each row \"definitely\nwrong in this snapshot\". Every part of that was false, and the loudest part\nwas the verdict.\n\nR1 now takes the dialect and drops to information on ClickHouse with wording\nthat names the engine rather than accusing the schema. Severities read\n`warning` and `info` throughout: a missing primary key is worth fixing on\nMySQL or PostgreSQL, but a log table without one is a choice, not an error,\nand \"definitely wrong\" was never ours to say.\n\nThe tab badge counts warnings only. Information is something to know, not\nsomething to do, and a badge lit on every healthy schema stops being read.\n\nThe Rule column shows the rule''s name instead of R1/R2/R3. The ids were for\ncross-referencing a document the reader does not have, and there was no\nlegend anywhere on the page. Hub chips say what their number counts —\n`orders · 5 referencing tables` rather than `orders 5`, which could have\nbeen rows, columns or anything else.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 8698af8c30b79c04b840d69f79447f027fb79472)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): give history rows their limit, and stop announcing a load that may not happen\n\nA history row said `1000 rows`. That number cannot tell you whether it is\nthe whole answer or the ceiling cutting it off, and those mean opposite\nthings — every other count in this app carries its suffix for exactly that\nreason. The entry gains an optional `limit`, the badge reads `12 rows (limit\n1000)` when one was applied and `12 rows returned` when none was, and the\nrunner fills the field in.\n\n\"Load into editor\" no longer announces. The query view may raise a \"Replace\neditor text?\" confirm that the user cancels, in which case the announcement\nwas a lie, and on the path where the load does happen the query view already\nannounces it — so the drawer was either wrong or repeating.\n\nThe recording toggle now says `Recording: on` / `Recording: off`. It was a\npressed ghost button whose text never changed, which left the state carried\nby colour alone for anyone reading it by eye.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 3aadbae8ebc612b1a5a6bcec68a672b9a7c8808b)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): say when the join-path list hit its cap\n\nThe finder stops enumerating at 25 equally short paths, and the drawer\nreported that as \"25 paths found — choose one\", which claims there are\nexactly 25 and that one of them is the one you want. At the cap the list is\na sample, so it now reads \"25 paths shown (cap reached — more may exist)\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit dfef8bab31035f140aa045a598c56f8c1e71f89b)\n\n* refactor(sql-ui): type the query-document payload and hide module internals\n\n`IQueryDocPayload` existed but every producer built the payload as an\nuntyped object literal, so a misspelled field would have shipped silently\nand shown up as a query document that opened blank. The type moves to\ndocs.ts, beside the URI builders every producer already imports — nobody\nshould have to import a view to describe what they are handing it — and the\ntwo call sites that build a payload now declare it.\n\nOpus B: SqlApp''s local copy can become a re-export from docs.ts; the shapes\nare identical, so nothing breaks in the meantime.\n\n`DEFAULT_MAX_DEPTH`, `JOIN_SQL_HEADER` and the history store''s preference\nkeys, `attachPrefs` and `recordRun` were exported with no caller outside\ntheir own module. An export is a promise to keep something stable, and these\nwere promises nobody had asked for. `MAX_JOIN_PATHS` stays exported with a\nnote saying why: the drawer compares against it to admit the path list is a\nsample rather than the answer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit d6a927e390f93aa18892d5a94af2009d264c73ce)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): split a selection instead of sending it as one statement\n\nA dragged selection was sent as a single `execute`, classified by whatever\nkeyword happened to come first. Selecting `SELECT …; DELETE FROM t` therefore\nread as a SELECT: the statement was marked read-only and so became eligible\nfor the session''s retry-with-a-fresh-token, which could run the DELETE twice;\nthe pattern check only ever saw the first verb; the transaction refusal only\nfired when the FIRST statement was transaction control, so selecting\n`UPDATE …; ROLLBACK;` sent both and the ROLLBACK silently did nothing. psycopg2\nexecutes multi-statement strings, so this was reachable, not theoretical.\n\n`splitStatementsIn` splits a slice of the buffer and rebases every offset and\nline number onto the whole buffer, so each statement in a selection gets its\nown classification, pattern check, limit, history entry and strip position\nwhile decorations still point at the real text. A selection holding part of\none statement still yields that one fragment, which is what a partial\nselection wants.\n\nThe pre-run line now says how many statements a selection holds, so a\nmulti-statement selection cannot be mistaken for one before it runs, and a\nbuffer holding only comments reads `no statement found` rather than claiming\nthe editor is empty.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit e7c2adbd935f159e1cc207132c1c0f9fe5f115f2)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): publish the completion model once the editor model exists\n\nSchema autocomplete was silently dead on the ordinary path. The effect that\npublishes this editor''s completion model into the shared provider''s map read\nthe model URI from a ref and returned early when it was null, keyed only on\n`[completion, dialect]`. @monaco-editor/react mounts asynchronously — loader\npromise, then editor, then onMount — so the URI is null on the first pass, and\nnothing re-ran the effect unless the snapshot happened to arrive afterwards.\n\nBrowsing a connection and then opening a query document, which is the normal\norder, therefore produced a completion model that was never published: the\nprovider found no entry for the model and returned nothing, leaving only\nMonaco''s built-in keyword list. It appeared to work only when the schema\nfinished loading after the editor had mounted.\n\nThe URI is now state as well as a ref, so the effect runs again once it is\nknown, and `handleMount` also publishes immediately from prop mirrors rather\nthan waiting a render. The JSDoc records why.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 09f046243fcc62ae6b84836d12fd5119d10b6c41)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): say \"ran\" for reads, and stop claiming no limit was applied\n\nThree wording and modelling corrections. The QueryView side of each landed in\ne7c2adbd; this is the model and the tests.\n\nA read that finished now says `ran`; only a write or DDL says `committed`. The\nstrip could previously report `1–3 committed` for three SELECTs, which dilutes\nthe one word the app uses to mean the change is durable and cannot be undone\nfrom here. Anything not clearly read-only is still called committed, which is\nthe safe direction. `formatPriorStatements` builds the failure banner''s second\nsentence the same way, so a batch that failed after two reads says they ran\nrather than implying data changed.\n\n`applyRowLimit` now reports which of three states applies. A statement\ncarrying its own LIMIT used to render `100 rows returned (no limit applied)` —\ntrue of the app and badly misleading about the result, since the generated\n\"Select top 100\" reads exactly that way and a reader concludes the table holds\n100 rows. It now reads `(limit in statement)`, and the check runs before the\nheader selection so `All` reports it too.\n\nA statement rerun from the history drawer has no position in the editor\nbuffer, so `startLine`/`endLine` are optional rather than faked as line 1 — the\nstatus strip omits the line reference instead of pointing at unrelated text.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 91fb9fe13ed64c2aaf3f415b1fa72720af8e0153)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): stop telling users Refresh will find a missing table\n\nThe suggestion hint read \"Missing a table? Refresh schema on the connection\npage.\" On a node without the `refresh_schema` tool that advice does nothing at\nall: the node reflects once at task start and serves that reflection however\noften Refresh is pressed, so a table created since will not appear no matter\nwhat the user does from here.\n\nIt now reads \"Missing a table? The snapshot dates from HH:MM; Refresh re-reads\nit only on nodes with refresh_schema.\" — which dates the data and says what\nthe button can and cannot do, rather than sending the user round a loop that\ncannot succeed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit a1c7ca002cf2a1f6c20bf55bc9762989235b3d35)\n\n* chore(sql-ui): drop the unused table-name decoder\n\n`tableFromTableDataUri` was added as the symmetric counterpart to the URI\nencoder and has never had a caller — the views read their table from the\ndocument''s content payload. An export with no caller is a maintenance claim\nnobody is checking, so it and its tests go.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit 497540e230285405ac222b1104419400a7e9343e)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): make Explain give the same answer however it is invoked\n\nThe button''s disabled state was computed from the debounced caret while the\nclick handler read the live editor handle, so the two could briefly disagree:\nthe button could look available and then do nothing. Worse, Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+E\nreturned silently whenever the statement could not be explained — a keybinding\nthat does nothing at all reads as a broken app, and unlike the button it has\nno tooltip to explain itself.\n\nBoth paths now go through one `explainRefusal` function, so they can never\noffer different reasons. The live handle still decides WHAT is explained, so a\ncaret move that has not settled cannot send the previous statement, and when\nthe answer is that nothing can be explained the reason is announced — the same\nsentence the button''s title carries.\n\nAlso drops SqlApp''s own copy of the query-document payload type in favour of\nthe one in docs.ts, which is where the panels that build the payload already\nread it from. The shapes were identical; two declarations of the same contract\nis one too many.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit d83e48224c0ab1d840ba86411f8a6861af2f724d)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): ask for a fresh schema read from the diagram''s Retry and Reverse Engineer\n\nThe two user-initiated refreshes in DiagramView still served the task-start\nsnapshot even on nodes that provide refresh_schema. Also drops the unused\nIQueryDocPayload re-export from SqlApp (producers import it from docs.ts).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit c9331827e435ab0f1bf3a8baf5be92b36c39d3ef)\n\n* docs(apps): sync the SQL Explorer guide with the reviewed UI wording\n\nReads say \"ran\" and writes \"committed\" in batch outcomes; the results line\nhas three limit states; the ClickHouse ALTER note covers only mutation forms.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n(cherry picked from commit f9c7664ab4a6fbd27752db8fd59449874511e7d8)\n\n* fix(sql-ui): make the appended row limit reach the statement it bounds\n\n`applyRowLimit` ran its checks on comment-stripped text but built the\nstatement from the raw one and joined the clause with a space, so\n`SELECT * FROM orders -- daily` was sent as `... -- daily LIMIT 200`:\nthe database ran an unbounded SELECT while the meta line reported\n\"limit 200\". The clause is now joined with a newline, which terminates\na trailing line comment before LIMIT.\n\nThe `in-statement` check had the mirror-image problem: `/\\blimit\\b/i`\nmatched anywhere in the statement, so a LIMIT inside a subquery (or\ninside a string literal) made the app skip its own limit and label the\nresult \"limit in statement\" while the outer query streamed every row.\nIt now uses the splitter''s `hasTopLevelKeyword`, which already masks\nstrings and comments and only accepts a match at parenthesis depth 0.\n\nThe existing comment test asserted `.limit` alone and so never saw the\nbroken SQL; the regression tests assert `.sql` and re-strip comments\nfrom the emitted statement to prove LIMIT survives as code.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): enforce each dialect''s delimiter preconditions in the splitter\n\nTwo delimiters were recognised more eagerly than the engines recognise\nthem, and in both cases the scanner swallowed a statement separator.\n\nMySQL only starts a line comment when whitespace (or a control\ncharacter) follows `--`: it parses `SELECT 1--2; SELECT 3` as\narithmetic across two statements, while the scanner hid the semicolon\nand returned one. `opensDashComment` applies that precondition for\nMySQL alone — Postgres and ClickHouse keep taking a bare `--`.\n\nPostgreSQL allows `$` inside an unquoted identifier, so `foo$tag$` is\none identifier and opens no dollar-quoted body. `continuesIdentifier`\nrefuses a dollar quote whose `$` follows an identifier character, so\n`SELECT foo$tag$; SELECT 2` splits again.\n\nNear-miss cases for both are in the splitter''s table, including the\ncontrasting Postgres reading of the same `--` text.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): stop one bad refresh from disabling fresh reflection\n\nTwo ways a refresh could quietly stop happening, both leaving the app\ndescribing the database against a snapshot it knows is out of date.\n\nA single fallback latched `refreshTool: ''unavailable''` for the rest of\nthe app session, and nothing ever cleared it. But the session cannot\ntell a missing tool from a restarted task or a timed-out call — it says\nso itself in `connect/session.ts` — so one fallback during a `fresh`\nrefresh made every later Refresh Schema, Reverse Engineer and post-DDL\nread serve the task-start snapshot. The state now carries a `suspect`\nstep: one fallback suspects, a SECOND CONSECUTIVE one latches, and a\nfresh read that answers clears the suspicion. A node that really has no\n`refresh_schema` still stops paying two round trips after two tries.\n\n`refreshSchema` also returned immediately whenever a refresh was already\nloading, so Apply in TableDesignView could neither start nor await its\npost-DDL reflection — and the outcome effect composes its banner once,\nfrom the snapshot present when Apply resolves, so the change was\nreported against pre-DDL structure. Refreshes are now serialised per\nconnection: an ordinary read joins whatever is running, while a `fresh`\nrequest queues behind it and runs its own, because a plain re-read\ncannot answer a caller that just ran DDL.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): do not record history before the recording switch is read\n\nA persisted `sql.historyRecording[key] === false` is unreadable until a\nbridge mounts and `hydrate` runs. In that window `recording[key]` is\nmerely absent, which the guard read as the default — on — so a run that\nfinished first stored the user''s SQL, and `hydrate` then merged that\nentry and scheduled it to the workspace preferences file. History the\nuser had explicitly turned off was persisted, statement text and\nliteral values included.\n\nRuns that arrive before hydration now wait in a bounded queue and are\ndecided once the stored flags are in hand: replayed oldest-first when\nrecording is on for that connection, dropped when it is off. The queue\nis capped at 100 entries for the same reason the history itself is\nbounded — it holds the user''s SQL.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): keep a history entry''s row limit and its full statement\n\n`IHistoryEntry.limit` exists because a row count without its limit is\nambiguous — 1,000 rows is either the whole answer or the ceiling cutting\nit off. Two transformations lost it anyway: `collapse` kept the OLDER\nrun''s limit beside the NEWER run''s row count, and `readEntry` dropped\nthe field entirely on hydration, so every limit vanished the first time\nthe workspace file was re-read. The limit is now replaced with the rest\nof the run facts on collapse, and validated as a number on hydration.\n\n`trimHistory` also deduplicated AFTER truncating, so two different\nstatements sharing their first 8 KiB collapsed into one entry and one of\nthem was lost. Repeats are now collapsed on the untruncated text and the\nstorage bound is applied to what survives.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): make \"Run and stop asking\" hold for the rest of the batch\n\n`setPatternChecks` only updated state, and the ref the run loop reads\nwas mirrored by a passive effect. The loop resumes from its awaited\nconfirmation in a microtask and reaches the next statement''s check\nbefore React runs that effect, so iteration i + 1 still saw `true` and\nopened the dialog again — for every further matching statement in the\nsame batch, however many times the user chose \"Run and stop asking\".\n\nThe ref is now written next to the state update, which is the only\nwrite ordering the run loop can rely on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): restore the mounted flag when StrictMode replays the effect\n\nThe cleanup set `mountedRef.current = false` and only `useRef(true)`\never set it back, which a re-run of the effect on the SAME instance does\nnot re-execute. Under the shell''s `React.StrictMode` root that leaves\nthe flag false after the replayed setup, so `record` returns early and\nno completed statement reaches history for as long as that instance\nlives. The setup now asserts the flag it owns.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): bind the table name in both foreign-key catalog queries\n\n`fetchForeignKeyNames` interpolated the table name through\n`quoteLiteral`, which doubles `''` and leaves `\\` alone. On a MySQL\nconnection that honours backslash escapes — the default outside\nNO_BACKSLASH_ESCAPES — a name containing `\\''` ends the literal early\nand the remainder of the name becomes part of the WHERE predicate. The\nname is a VALUE compared against INFORMATION_SCHEMA columns, so both\ndialects now take it as a `$1` bind, the way every value in\n`sql/paging.ts` already travels; the node rewrites `$n` per driver.\n\nThat left `sql/quote.ts` with no callers. Its own header says it exists\nfor the statements that cannot bind, and there are none left, so the\nmodule and its suite go with the last use rather than standing by as a\nready-made way to reintroduce the same class of defect.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): do not render an error headline with nothing after it\n\n`describeFailure('''')` returned `generic: false`, and both the query view\nand the Explain drawer show the headline whenever `generic` is false —\nso an Error carrying an empty message produced the banner \"Database\nreported:\" followed by nothing. An empty (or whitespace-only) message is\nthe same situation as the node''s own placeholder: there is nothing real\nto quote, which is exactly what the generic wording says.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): make the drawers report what actually happened\n\nFour places where the UI said nothing, or said the wrong thing.\n\nCopy in the row inspector took an optional chain through\n`navigator.clipboard`, so an absent Clipboard API skipped both callbacks\nand the button neither copied nor announced anything. A missing API now\nannounces the same failure a rejection does.\n\nThe Explain drawer showed \"Explaining…\" for as long as the shell\nconnection was down, because `run` returns before sending anything when\nthere is no client — so `running` never became true and no request was\never in flight. It now says it is not connected.\n\nThe history row''s statement and note were inline spans, where\n`overflow`, `text-overflow` and vertical margins have no effect: a long\none-line statement overflowed the drawer instead of ending in an\nellipsis, and the note ran on beside the statement instead of under it.\nBoth are block boxes now.\n\nThe live region replaced its child by key, which performs the removal\nand the insertion in ONE commit; assistive technology can coalesce that\ninto no change and miss a repeated sentence. It now empties and restores\nthe text in separate tasks, cancelling a pending restore when a newer\nannouncement arrives.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): keep a control''s outcome to the control the user pressed\n\nThe data grid''s error banner survived until the NEXT request succeeded,\nso after one failed page a fresh search, sort or page left the previous\nrequest''s failure on screen while the grid loaded different parameters —\nthe banner described a question the user had already moved on from. The\nerror is now cleared where the new sequence is taken.\n\nIn the designer, the column row carries button semantics and contains\nthe Drop button, so a click (or Enter/Space) on Drop bubbled into\n`selectColumn`: the column was dropped and its editor opened at the same\ntime, leaving the user staging an edit for a column that is on its way\nout. The cell around the button stops propagation — the shell''s Button\npasses no event to its handler, so it cannot be stopped at the source.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): open the table record drawer where it can be seen\n\n`requestTableRecord` is raised by the Insights panel and by the sidebar\ntree, but the drawer it opens is rendered by OverviewPanel — and\nTabPanel hides the inactive panel with `display: none`, so a table\nopened while Insights was in front raised a drawer inside a hidden\nsubtree. The connection view now brings Overview forward when a request\nfor its own connection arrives, which covers the sidebar route as well.\nRequests raised before the document mounted are ignored, so opening a\nconnection never overrides the page the user left it on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(apps): state where a statement''s own LIMIT has to be\n\nTwo sentences in the SQL Explorer guide now match what the code does:\n`limit in statement` reports a LIMIT on the outermost query only, and\nthe splitter paragraph names the two dialect preconditions a reader\nwould otherwise have to discover — MySQL''s whitespace after `--`, and a\n`$tag$` that continues an identifier opening no dollar quote.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(sql-ui): test MySQL''s comment precondition without a control regex\n\nThe dash-comment check spelled its \"whitespace or control character\"\nrule as a regex class covering U+0000-U+001F, which the repo''s\n`no-control-regex` lint rejects. A `\\s` test plus a code-point\ncomparison says the same thing and keeps `eslint apps/sql-ui` clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): never collapse a history entry that is already truncated\n\ntrimHistory compares statements on their text, and the previous fix moved\nthe collapse ahead of the per-entry bound so a FRESH pair is compared on\nfull text. That protects an entry only up to its first truncation.\n\nhistoryStore.hydrate reads bounded entries back out of the workspace file\nand passes them through trimHistory again on every reload. By then `sql`\nis a prefix, so two different statements that agree for their first\nmaxSqlChars characters satisfy sameStatement and one real entry is\ncollapsed away — silently, and once per app open.\n\nA prefix cannot prove two statements are the same, so a truncated entry is\nno longer merged at all. The alternative, persisting a full-text\nfingerprint, was rejected: it widens the persisted schema with another\nfield read back from an untrusted, hand-editable file, and every entry\nalready on disk lacks it, so the conservative rule would still be needed\nas the fallback. What it buys is collapsing consecutive re-runs of an\n8 KB+ statement, which costs one extra row when it does not happen.\n\nTests: a reload-shaped regression (trim, then trim the output again) and\nthe fresh-repeat-of-a-truncated-entry case. Both fail without the guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): write the history recording switch through, not on the batch\n\nsetRecording only called schedulePersist, a 300 ms debounce, and the last\nbridge detach CANCELS a pending write. Switching apps within that window\ndropped the write: the workspace file kept saying recording was on, and\nthe next page load resumed recording SQL on a connection the user had\nswitched off. The queue added for pre-hydration runs narrowed the exposure\nbut never closed it, because it only governs runs that arrive before the\nflag is readable, not a flag that never reached disk.\n\nThe switch is now written through immediately. Runs still batch — they\narrive in bursts and one lost run costs a list row — but a switch is\nflipped once, deliberately, and losing it costs the user a decision about\ntheir own data that they will never be told was discarded.\n\nA second defect sat behind the same line. hydrate merged the whole\nin-memory `recording` map over the stored flags, so any flag from an\nearlier attach outranked the file for the rest of the session — a stale\n`true` surviving a detach turned recording back on for a connection the\nfile says is off. Only overrides that have NOT yet reached disk may\noutrank the file, so they are tracked separately in `unsavedRecording`,\ncleared on a successful write, and hydrate now schedules a write when one\nis still outstanding (a switch flipped while no bridge was mounted).\n\nattachPrefs is exported: it is the store''s real seam onto the shell, and\nthe rules worth testing live there rather than in the null-rendering\nbridge component.\n\nThe module had no test coverage at all. It now has seven tests driving it\nthe way the app does — attach, act, detach — asserting on what reached a\nfake workspace file, because that file is the privacy boundary. Three of\nthem fail against the previous code.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(apps): state what \"no limit applied\" means with All and an inner LIMIT\n\napplyRowLimit recognises only a LIMIT on the outermost query. With the\nheader set to All, a statement whose only LIMIT sits in a subquery\ntherefore reports state `none`: QueryView prints `no limit applied` and\nnothing is appended. The guide described `no limit applied` as the case\nfor All \"with no LIMIT in the text\", which reads as a contradiction the\nfirst time someone sees the line on a statement that plainly contains one.\n\nThe guide now names that combination and shows a join against a limited\nsubquery, where the inner bound genuinely does not bound the result, so\nthe line is read as a statement about the result rather than the text.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(sql-ui): fail the test target when discovery finds no tests\n\n`builder test` runs sql-ui:test in CI, and the action returned cleanly —\ntask.output set, exit 0 — both when tests/ was absent and when the\n*.test.ts(x) filter matched nothing. Either state reported the whole\ntarget green while not a single assertion had run, over a suite of 521\ntests that is the only cover this app has.\n\nBoth cases are now errors. tests/ is tracked source: a directory that has\ngone missing, or a rename that quietly stops matching the filter, is a\nbroken checkout and must be reported as one rather than absorbed.\n\nThis follows apps/vscode/scripts/tasks.js, which already throws on an\nempty glob for the same reason (\"a glob that matches nothing must not\nread as green\"). It diverges from apps/explorer-ui, whose copy of this\naction still returns cleanly; that one predates this PR and is left for a\nseparate change rather than widened into unrelated files here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(sql-ui): typecheck the tests under their own program\n\nThe app''s tsconfig includes src/** and rsbuild.config.mts, so the ~4.9k\nlines under tests/ were checked by nothing: tsx erases types rather than\nchecking them, and no other program reached the directory. A test could\nassert against a signature that no longer existed and still be counted.\n\nThe tests get a second program, tsconfig.test.json, which extends the app\nconfig and widens `include` to tests/. The reason is file scoping, not\ntype scoping: `typecheck` runs the default program, and that script name\nis the one scripts/lib/uiAudit.js requires of every app, so keeping it\nover src/** alone leaves it describing what this remote actually ships,\nwith the tests checked beside it by `typecheck:tests`. apps/shared\nexcludes its tests from typechecking outright and packages/chat-widget\nalready carries a tsconfig.test.json; this follows the latter.\n\nTo be explicit about what this does NOT do: it is not a type boundary.\ntsconfig.json declares no `types`, so tsc''s automatic @types walk already\nresolves the root @types/node into the src program — appending tests/**\nto the app''s own `include` typechecks clean too. `types: [\"node\"]` is\nnamed in the new file only to state that dependency outright, since\n@types/node is a root devDependency this app does not declare.\n\nWired into sql-ui:test rather than left as config nobody runs, so CI\nactually enforces it, and exposed as `typecheck:tests` alongside the\nexisting `typecheck` script for local use. Because the new step also\nre-checks src/**, sql-ui:test now assumes a built shell; CI already\norders build before test, and tasks.js notes it for local runs.\n\nThe pair is clean today: tsc exits 0 over src and over src + tests, and\nthe suite still reports 521 passing across 20 files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): pattern check reads the verb a WITH chain carries\n\n`patternCheck` anchored both verb tests on `^`, and `normalize` strips only\ncomments, whitespace and a leading `(`. A statement beginning with `WITH`\ntherefore produced no verb and returned null before the WHERE check ran, so\n`WITH audit AS (SELECT id FROM orders) DELETE FROM orders` — ordinary\nPostgreSQL that empties the table — ran with no confirmation, while\n`classifyStatement` 30 lines above already called it a write. The two\njudgements disagreed about whether `WITH` exists, and the disagreement was a\nfalse negative on the only guard the app offers.\n\nThe outer verb is now read at parenthesis depth 0. Every CTE body is\nparenthesised, so a WHERE in code at depth 0 belongs to the main statement\nand the existing rule applies unchanged.\n\nA mutation INSIDE a CTE body (depth >= 1) is reported with the new kind\n`<VERB> inside a WITH clause`. It never claims \"without WHERE\": a WHERE at\nthat depth cannot be attributed to that verb by a text check. The outer\nfinding is more specific and wins when both apply, so an outer WHERE that\ndoes not constrain the CTE still surfaces the inner mutation.\nINSERT/MERGE/REPLACE inside a clause stay unflagged, for parity with a\ntop-level INSERT.\n\nsplit.ts grows `hasNestedKeyword` and `firstNestedKeyword` over one shared\ndepth scanner (`keywordHits`), which `hasTopLevelKeyword` now reads too; its\nbehaviour is unchanged and its tests are untouched.\n\nThe one call site is the runner''s per-statement loop (QueryView.tsx:621-632),\nwhich every entry point — Run, Run all, and a history rerun — funnels\nthrough, so single and batch paths are both covered.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): accept Unicode letters in PostgreSQL dollar-quote tags\n\nDOLLAR_TAG took ASCII letters only, so `DO $café$ BEGIN PERFORM 1; END\n$café$` opened no quoted body: the scanner read the body''s `;` as a\nseparator and sent two invalid fragments to the database.\n\nPostgreSQL''s \"Lexical Structure\" says the tag \"follows the same rules as an\nunquoted identifier, except that it cannot contain a dollar sign\", and an\nidentifier may begin with \"letters with diacritical marks and non-Latin\nletters\". Which characters those are depends on encoding and locale, and the\nbackend treats any byte >= 0x80 as an identifier character, so the class is\n\\u0080-\\uffff - no `u` flag, which leaves astral tags matching through their\nsurrogate pairs. A digit still cannot start a tag, so `$1abc$` remains a\npositional parameter followed by text.\n\ncontinuesIdentifier and isEscapeStringPrefix now read that same class from\none constant. That closes a second, quieter hole: `Straße''a...''` was read as\nthe E-prefixed escape form because ß did not count as an identifier\ncharacter, and the backslash then swallowed the closing quote and the\nseparator behind it.\n\nTag matching is exact text, so case sensitivity (`$Tag$` is not closed by\n`$tag$`) needed no change; it is pinned by a test, as are the ASCII forms\nand the mysql behaviour, which are unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): offer \"Stop waiting\" only once a statement is in flight\n\nThe 1 s timer keyed on the batch-level `running` state, which is true from\nthe moment Run is pressed - including while a pattern-check confirmation sits\nopen with nothing sent. A dialog left open for a second put \"Stop waiting\" on\nscreen, and clicking it announced that the database might still be running a\nstatement it had never received.\n\n`hasStatementInFlight` answers the narrower question the button is really\nabout: is a run''s outcome `running`, which the loop sets immediately before\n`session.execute` and replaces immediately after it resolves or rejects. The\ntimer and the button now key on that. `stopWaiting`, `runningRef`, `canRun`\nand the `Running...` label keep reading `running`, because the batch IS\nrunning while a dialog is open.\n\nThe timer restarts per statement, since `inFlight` drops between two\nstatements of a batch - correct, because nothing is at the database while the\nnext confirmation is open. A late answer cannot leave a stale `running`\nbehind: the run-sequence guard drops it, and `stopWaiting` maps `running` to\n`abandoned` before bumping that sequence.\n\nThere is no React renderer in this harness, so the helper is unit-tested and\nthe wiring is covered by the typechecks and by reading the run loop.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(sql-ui): prefix the unused task context parameter\n\nThe build action''s callback never reads its first argument; the repo''s\nJavaScript rule is that an unused parameter is prefixed with an underscore.\nThe position is kept, since `task` is the second argument the action gets.\nVerified by running the task: it still discovers the tests/ directory and\nruns all 20 files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): write the identifier range as escapes, not raw bytes\n\nThe character class carried literal U+0080 and U+FFFF code points: a C1\ncontrol character and a noncharacter, invisible in an editor, easy for a\ncopy or a diff tool to mangle, and impossible to review by eye. Both bounds\nare now escape sequences, the prose says U+0080 through U+FFFF, and the\ncomment says why they are written that way. Same regexes, same behaviour:\nthe splitter tests are unchanged and still pass.\n\nThe two test comments that spelled the same two characters as escape TEXT\nare prose, so they become the real characters; the test DATA keeps its\nescapes, which is what makes the Cyrillic, CJK and astral tags readable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): read the WITH verb by position, not by presence\n\nThe outer rule took ANY whole-word update/delete at depth 0 as the verb, so\na WITH-led `SELECT * FROM a FOR UPDATE` (a locking clause that writes\nnothing) and the common upsert `... INSERT INTO t ... ON CONFLICT (id) DO\nUPDATE SET ...` were both announced as `UPDATE without WHERE`. A\nconfirmation whose kind is wrong spends the credibility the dialog needs for\nthe statement that really does empty a table.\n\nThe verb is now the FIRST statement leader at depth 0 - select, insert,\nupdate, delete, merge, values or table - and the top-level WHERE rule\napplies only when that leader is update or delete. A leader followed by\n`AS` is a CTE NAME, not a verb: `merge` is not reserved, so\n`WITH merge AS (SELECT 1) DELETE FROM t` must still be read as the DELETE\nit is. Without that guard the position rule would have reopened exactly the\nfalse negative this check exists to close.\n\nThe nested rule reads position too: a CTE mutation must OPEN the\nparenthesised body (the nearest non-whitespace character before it is\n`(`), so `(DELETE FROM t RETURNING *)` is the body''s own verb while the\nUPDATE of `(SELECT * FROM t FOR UPDATE)` or of an in-CTE `DO UPDATE`\nbelongs to whatever opened the body. Line breaks and comments between the\nparenthesis and the verb do not hide it, and a MATERIALIZED hint does not\neither. The first mutation in text order is still the one named.\n\nsplit.ts trades `hasNestedKeyword` and `firstNestedKeyword` - the first of\nwhich had no caller - for one `keywordSites`, which reports where each\nkeyword sits (index, depth, and whether it opens its parenthesis) and leaves\nthe meaning to the caller. `hasTopLevelKeyword` is untouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): read past a CTE named with a column list\n\nPostgreSQL lists INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and MERGE as non-reserved, so any of\nthe four can name a CTE - and a name may carry a column list, where the next\ntoken is `(` rather than `AS`. `WITH merge (x) AS (SELECT 1) DELETE FROM\norders` therefore still read its first depth-0 leader as the verb and asked\nnothing about the full-table DELETE behind it.\n\nA leader is now a name when `AS` follows it, OR when it is one of those four\nand `(` follows it. That second form is unambiguous for exactly those words,\nbecause a real statement never puts `(` straight after the verb: INSERT INTO,\nUPDATE t, DELETE FROM, MERGE INTO. The reserved leaders keep the old rule, so\n`SELECT (1 + 2)` stays a SELECT - reading it as a name would step past it and\ntake a trailing FOR UPDATE for the verb.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): read a CTE body by its verb, not by what touches its bracket\n\n`WITH a AS (WITH b AS (SELECT 1) DELETE FROM t RETURNING *) SELECT * FROM a`\nis valid PostgreSQL - a DELETE may carry its own read-only WITH clause - and\nthe nested rule stopped seeing it: the DELETE is preceded by the `)` that\ncloses `b`, not by the `(` that opens the body, so the opens-the-body test\nreturned false and a full-table delete inside a CTE went unasked. It was\nflagged at 68a23b8d, so the position rule had regressed it.\n\nEvery keyword site now carries the identity of its enclosing group - the\noffset of the `(` that opens it, or -1 for the statement itself - kept on a\nstack of open parentheses during the one scan. Depth alone could not do this:\n`WITH a AS (SELECT 1), b AS (DELETE FROM t)` holds two different bodies at\ndepth 1.\n\nA group is then judged exactly like the statement: its verb is its first\nleader that is not a CTE name. `(SELECT ... FOR UPDATE)` leads with SELECT,\n`(INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE)` leads with INSERT, `(id)` has no leader\nat all, and a body that opens with its own WITH chain leads with the verb\nbehind that chain. Every revision-2 result is unchanged; the first mutating\nbody in text order is still the one named.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): check the statement EXPLAIN ANALYZE would run\n\nPostgreSQL: \"the statement is actually executed when the ANALYZE option is\nused\"; MySQL 8.0.19+ the same. This app commits every call on its own, so\n`EXPLAIN ANALYZE DELETE FROM orders` empties the table - and `patternCheck`\nreturned null for it while `classifyStatement`, thirty lines above, already\ncalled it a write. That is the same disagreement the maintainer raised about\nWITH: the only guard the app offers, silent on a statement that deletes\neverything.\n\n`patternCheck` now peels `EXPLAIN_PREFIX` exactly as `classifyStatement`\ndoes. No `analy[sz]e` in the prefix means the statement only plans and is\nnever a finding; otherwise the check runs on the inner statement, and it\nrecurses, so `EXPLAIN ANALYZE WITH a AS (...) DELETE ...` is read like any\nother WITH-led delete.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): end a keyword where an identifier ends, not where ASCII does\n\nThe keyword search still used JavaScript''s \\b after the identifier class was\nwidened to U+0080-U+FFFF, and \\b is ASCII-only: it sees a word boundary\nbetween `where` and the `é` of `whereé`, a perfectly legal PostgreSQL\nalias. `DELETE FROM t whereé` therefore satisfied the top-level WHERE test\nand ran with no dialog, as did `DELETE FROM t AS whereé` and\n`UPDATE t whereé SET x = 1`. F3a''s own argument - the identifier class\ndecides where a word ends, so everything that asks must ask the same way -\napplies here too.\n\nThe needle is built from that class. The leading boundary is CONSUMED rather\nthan looked behind, because lookbehind reached Safari only in 16.4 and this\napp targets safari >= 13 (apps/sql-ui/package.json); group 1 is that consumed\ncharacter, so the keyword offset is `match.index + match[1].length`. Two\nkeywords sharing one separator are still found, since the separator is left\nfor the next match to consume.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): say what the stop-waiting timer really measures\n\nThe comment and the guide both claimed the timer restarts per statement. It\ndoes not: the publish that resolves statement i and the publish that\ndispatches i + 1 run in the same microtask continuation, and React 18 batches\nthem into one render (the app mounts with createRoot,\npackages/shell/src/bootstrap.tsx:94), so `inFlight` never observes false\nbetween uninterrupted statements and the effect does not re-run. The timer\nmeasures an uninterrupted STRETCH, counted from the first statement\ndispatched. A confirmation dialog does reset it, because nothing is at the\ndatabase while one is open.\n\nThe behaviour is unchanged and matches the base branch for that case; only\nthe words were wrong, and an untrue comment is worse than none.\n\nAlso: the pattern-checks-off tooltip and the guide now name the inside-a-WITH\nkind alongside the others, the Stop waiting section is rewrapped to the\nfile''s width, and the patternCheck LIMITS list gains the MERGE case - a MERGE\nis never a finding at either level, though its actions can update or delete.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): quote the database, not the node wrapper, in the headline\n\nWith the companion node change (PR #2283) a failed execute reaches the app as\n`SQL execution failed: <driver message>`, on both the session-bound and the\nplain path. `describeFailure` put that whole first line into the headline, so\nthe banner read \"Database reported: SQL execution failed: no such table:\norders\" - the node''s own prefix attributed to the database, under a label\nthat names the database, while the guide promises the database''s own first\nline.\n\nThe HEADLINE now quotes what follows the prefix. Nothing else moves:\n`verbatim` keeps every character (it is what gets pasted into a bug report,\nand the prefix is part of what the node sent), and `allowExecuteOff` and\n`maxExecuteRows` read the whole message, so they keep working through the\nwrapper. The older placeholder carries a parenthesis rather than a colon, so\nit does not match and stays generic - both node generations read correctly,\nwhich matters because this app runs against whatever node a workspace has.\n\nA wrapper with nothing behind it is treated as nothing to quote: that is the\ngeneric case, not an empty \"Database reported:\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): judge a WITH chain on code, not on raw text\n\nasclearuc, r4016783849: `classifyStatement` made its WITH decision with a\nregex over `head`, and `normalize` strips comments only - literals,\ndollar-quoted bodies and quoted identifiers survive. So every one of these\ncame back `write`:\n\n    WITH d AS (SELECT ''delete me'' AS t) SELECT * FROM d\n    WITH d AS (SELECT $$delete from orders$$ AS t) SELECT 1\n    WITH d AS (SELECT 1) SELECT * FROM \"delete\"\n\nThe third is the one that turns up: the word has to be quoted to be a table.\nEverything it cost lands in the feature this PR exists to make honest - the\nfailure banner said \"Statements 1-N already committed\" about SELECTs, the\nstrip labelled them `committed` instead of `ran`, and `idempotent` was false,\nso a plain read lost its one retry with a fresh token. `patternCheck` already\nread masked text and already had a test for this exact input; the two\njudgements simply disagreed.\n\nHis change, taken as written: the regex becomes the word list it was built\nfrom and the decision goes through `keywordSites`. The conservative cases stay\nconservative - `WITH gone AS (DELETE ... RETURNING *)` is still a write, and\nso is `... SELECT * FROM a FOR UPDATE`, where `update` is real code at depth\n0. The docstring parenthetical claimed a literal was not visible here; it was,\nand now it is not.\n\n`applyRowLimit` asked the same question with the same raw regex (DAF-1''s\nsibling), so those chains were treated as data-modifying and got no header\nLIMIT: a read returning every row under a meta line that says no limit was\napplied. It now shares `CTE_WRITE_WORDS` and the masked scan, which is why the\nlist is exported rather than copied.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): a CTE name is followed by the CTE grammar, not merely by (\n\nThe column-list rule read any of insert/update/delete/merge followed by `(` as\na CTE name, on the premise that no real statement puts a parenthesis straight\nafter the verb. MySQL does: the multi-table form whose first table factor is a\nderived table, `UPDATE (SELECT 1 AS id) AS d JOIN t ON t.id = d.id SET t.x =\n1`. Behind a WITH chain that verb was read as a name, no depth-0 leader\nremained, and the statement passed unconfirmed - while the identical statement\nwithout the chain was confirmed. The premise even held an `AS` behind the\nlist, so an AS-after-the-list rule would have misread it too.\n\nA name is now recognised by the grammar it actually has: an optional column\nlist, then `AS`, an optional materialisation hint, then the `(` that opens the\nbody. A verb has no body behind it, so the two cannot be confused, and\n`NAMEABLE_LEADERS` is no longer needed to tell them apart - the grammar\nrefuses the reserved words on its own.\n\nThe column list is walked as a balanced run over MASKED text, so a parenthesis\ninside a literal neither opens nor closes it; that is what `codeOnly` is\nexported from split.ts for - the offsets it preserves are the ones the keyword\nsites already carry.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): say what history keeps and when a read may be re-sent\n\nTwo things the guide left out, both pre-existing behaviour rather than\nanything this PR changed.\n\nHistory: an entry keeps the failed statement''s error text as well as the\nstatement, and under the companion node change that text is the database''s\nown sentence, which can quote a value from the statement. Both live in the\nworkspace preferences file until the entry is deleted or the list cleared -\nworth knowing next to the sentence that already warns this is not a place for\nsecrets.\n\nRetries: the session re-sends a statement once after a task restart only when\nthe app classifies it as a read, and that classification is by TEXT. `SELECT\n... INTO`, `nextval` and other volatile-function reads are reads by that\nrule, so they are the ones that can arrive twice. The guide had no mention of\nre-sending at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): put the retry note where its antecedent is\n\nThe retry paragraph landed between \"each statement commits on its own\" and\n\"For the same reason `BEGIN` ... are refused\", so \"the same reason\" read as\npointing at the retry rather than at autocommit. It now follows the refusal\nand opens with its own subject.\n\nAlso in that section and in Query history: the file''s em dashes instead of\nspaced hyphens; the history sentence names the third way an entry goes away\n(the bounds below trim it - 100 entries including pins, 8 KB per statement,\n256 KB overall); and it says what is actually stored, which is the failure\nmessage as it reached the app, node wrapper included, not the sentence the\nbanner quotes.\n\nOne factual correction in `classify.ts` and its matching test comment: they\nsaid `\"delete\"` has to be quoted \"because it is reserved\". That is MySQL.\nPostgreSQL does not reserve DELETE - which is the very fact `namesCte` relies\non to read `WITH delete AS (...)` as a name - so the comment contradicted the\none two hundred lines below it. Quoted either way, the identifier is text\nrather than code, which is all the decision needs.\n\nProse only: no behaviour change, 630/76 unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): move the SQL Explorer guide into the app package\n\ndevelop 1339a081 (#2289) makes per-app documentation live only inside the\napp''s own package: AGENTS.md (\"Document a shipped app\") now reads\n`apps/<app>/` only (`README.md`, `docs/`, `assets/`); never a site page or\nspine entry, with the VS Code extension and App Builder grandfathered as the\nonly exceptions. See also docs/README.md. dylan-savage''s review of this PR\nasks for exactly that alignment, so the guide moves into the app.\n\n- Move docs/public/product/guides/apps/sql-explorer.md to\n  apps/sql-ui/README.md (the rule lists README.md first and the app had\n  none) and drop the Docusaurus frontmatter. The `# SQL Explorer` H1 stays\n  and is now the README''s title.\n- Remove the `guides/apps/sql-explorer` spine entry from\n  docs/docusaurus/scripts/lib/spine.js, which restores that file to\n  develop''s content byte for byte; this PR now touches nothing under\n  `docs/`. The move and the spine removal are one commit on purpose: a\n  spine id with no page becomes a placeholder that `docs:gather` fails on.\n- Rewrite the links that assumed Docusaurus site routes as repo-relative\n  paths, since the page renders on GitHub from here: `/concepts/apps`,\n  `/nodes/db_mysql`, `/nodes/db_postgres`, `/nodes/db_clickhouse` and\n  `/guides/apps`. Every target was checked to exist; the in-page anchors\n  (`#limits`, `#insights`) are unchanged. The page has no images, and no\n  other prose is touched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): don''t append LIMIT after a trailing locking clause\n\nasclearuc''s MUST fix: `applyRowLimit` appended the header''s LIMIT to any\nrow-returning statement, so `SELECT * FROM orders FOR UPDATE` was sent as\n\n    SELECT * FROM orders FOR UPDATE\n    LIMIT 200\n\nwhich PostgreSQL rejects with `syntax error at or near \"LIMIT\"` and MySQL\nwith 1064. The locking clause is the LAST clause of a SELECT in both\ndialects, and the user never typed the LIMIT the error names — the editor''s\n\"Will run:\" line is built from the raw statement — so the error pointed at a\nclause that was nowhere on their screen. `targetSqlAsSent()` runs the same\nfunction, so the EXPLAIN drawer was explaining the same invalid SQL.\n\nBounding such a statement would mean reordering the user''s clauses, so this\ndoes what a data-modifying WITH chain already does: send the statement\nuntouched and report `none`, which keeps the meta line honest.\n\nThe guard detects the clause rather than the keyword. `LOCK` is NON-RESERVED\nin PostgreSQL, so `SELECT * FROM lock` is a valid unbounded read and testing\na bare `lock` would silently turn it from limited into unlimited; a bare\n`for` would also catch clauses that are not last. `endsWithLockingClause`\ntherefore matches the real syntax — FOR UPDATE / FOR NO KEY UPDATE /\nFOR SHARE / FOR KEY SHARE with an optional `OF ...`, `NOWAIT` or\n`SKIP LOCKED`, plus MySQL''s `LOCK IN SHARE MODE` — on the masked text the\nrest of the file already reads, at parenthesis depth 0, and only when\nnothing but that clause follows. A clause inside a string literal, a comment\nor a subquery is not the statement''s last clause and still gets the limit.\n\nTests in tests/failure.test.ts cover the six PostgreSQL spellings, the two\nMySQL ones, the `unknown` dialect the app defaults to, a trailing comment and\na trailing `;` after the clause, and the four cases that must KEEP the limit\n(`SELECT * FROM lock`, the clause inside a literal, inside a comment, inside\na subquery, and a statement whose own LIMIT precedes the clause).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): say what happens to a statement ending in a locking clause\n\nThe row-limit section documented the three limit states and the subquery\n`LIMIT` case but not the locking clause, which the previous commit made a\nfourth way to reach `no limit applied`. Under the repo''s same-change doc\nrule, the behaviour change and the guide move together.\n\nAdds a paragraph to \"The row limit\" saying that `FOR UPDATE`, `FOR NO KEY\nUPDATE`, `FOR SHARE`, `FOR KEY SHARE` and MySQL''s `LOCK IN SHARE MODE` are\nsent exactly as typed, that the line reads `no limit applied` whatever the\ntoggle says, and why: the clause must stay last, so the app will not reorder\nthe user''s SQL to make room for a limit.\n\nAlso adds two bullets under Limits. One repeats the locking-clause case in\nthe list a reader scans before filing a bug. The other documents a\nlimitation this PR does not change: an object-row projection with two\nsame-named columns shows one of them, because the node hands back each row\nas an object keyed by column name and the grid derives its headers from the\nfirst row — aliasing one of the two shows both.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(sql-ui): note the Node 20.0.0 floor next to the recursive test scan\n\nasclearuc asked for a note in the task: `apps/sql-ui/tests` growing one\nnested directory would silently stop running those tests on a Node 20.0.0\nruntime, because `readdir(..., { recursive: true })` ignores the option on\nexactly that version and the root package.json still declares\n`node: \">=20.0.0\"`.\n\nComment only, no behaviour change. It records why nothing is missed today\n(tests/ is flat, and CI''s `node-version: 20` resolves to a current 20.x) and\npoints at the open thread where the repo-wide engines floor is being decided\n— the same recursive scan is on develop in apps/explorer-ui, apps/shared and\npackages/shell, so the floor is not this app''s call to make.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(sql-ui): pin both halves of the refresh_schema fallback sequence\n\nThe fallback test asserted only the LAST tool call (`get_schema`), so it\ncould not tell the two behaviours apart that the assertion is there to\nseparate: a `refresh_schema` failure under an UNCHANGED token must not be\nretried before falling back, and one under a CHANGED token must be.\nEither sequence ends in `get_schema`, so a lost retry — or a retry that\nstarted firing where it should not — would have left the suite green.\n\nThe existing case now asserts the whole sequence\n(`[''refresh_schema'', ''get_schema'']`), which is what makes \"no retry here\"\nvisible. A second case covers the other half: a stale token makes the\nretry worth spending, so the call log reads\n`[''refresh_schema'', ''refresh_schema'', ''get_schema'']` on tokens\n`[''stale'', ''fresh'', ''fresh'']` and the fallback is only reached after the\nretry has also failed — a fallback is never taken on a failure the retry\nwould have fixed.\n\nTests only; `refreshSchema` and the retry policy are unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(sql-ui): drop the empty TABLE EXTRACTION banner from docs.test.ts\n\nThe file ended with a section banner that heads nothing: no table-name\nextraction helper exists in src/docs.ts (nothing in the app decodes the\npercent-encoded table segment back out of a URI), so the banner promises\na section that was never written and reads as a missing-tests marker to\nthe next person in the file.\n\nRemoves the banner and the trailing blank line after it; the file now\nends on the last `describe`. No test was added, removed or changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(sql-ui): pin the two edges the locking-clause guard narrows on\n\nThe guard deliberately matches the CLAUSE rather than the keywords\n`for` and `lock`, and only two cases show why. Neither was pinned, so a\nlater simplification back to the bare-keyword check the review suggested\nwould have kept the suite green.\n\n- `FOR UPDATE OF \"my orders\" NOWAIT`: the `OF` list can name a QUOTED\n  identifier, which masking blanks to spaces. The clause is still the\n  statement''s last one, so the statement is still sent unchanged.\n- `SELECT * FROM t FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF NOW() WHERE id = 1`: MariaDB''s\n  system-versioning clause is not a lock and is not last. It must keep\n  getting the header''s limit.\n\nEvidence that these bite: with `endsWithLockingClause` replaced by the\nbare `hasTopLevelKeyword(sql, ''for''|''lock'')` pair, the suite fails\n(\"still bounds a statement that only MENTIONS a locking clause\",\n''none'' !== ''applied''; tests 41 / pass 40 / fail 1) and the versioned\nread comes back {\"limit\":null,\"state\":\"none\"} — an unbounded read the\napp used to bound. With the shipped guard: tests 41 / pass 41 / fail 0.\n\nTests only; `applyRowLimit` is unchanged by this commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): insert the row limit before a trailing locking clause\n\nbafb1acb1 stopped appending the header''s LIMIT to a statement whose last\nclause is a locking clause and reported `no limit applied` instead. The\npremise behind it — that the clause has to stay last in both engines —\nholds for MySQL only. PostgreSQL''s grammar carries both orders\n(`for_locking_clause opt_select_limit` and `select_limit\nopt_for_locking_clause`), so on a postgres connection the statement the\napp sent at ae6a1e79 was valid AND bounded; skipping the limit turned it\ninto an unbounded read, every row of the table streamed into the browser.\n\n`endsWithLockingClause` becomes `trailingLockingClauseAt`, returning the\nindex the trailing clause starts at (or -1), and `applyRowLimit` INSERTS\nthe limit in front of that clause instead of returning early:\n\n    SELECT * FROM orders FOR UPDATE\n    ->  SELECT * FROM orders\n        LIMIT 200\n        FOR UPDATE\n\nThat is the order MySQL documents (`[LIMIT ...] [FOR UPDATE | LOCK IN\nSHARE MODE]`) and one PostgreSQL accepts, so the read stays bounded and\nthe statement stays valid on both engines, and the meta line''s `limit\n200` is true again. The masking is unchanged: the clause is still found\non `codeOnly()` text at parenthesis depth 0, so one inside a literal, a\ncomment, a quoted identifier or a subquery is not the statement''s\ntrailing clause, and `SELECT * FROM lock` keeps its limit appended at the\nend. `state: ''none''` is now reached only where it was before this round —\na statement that carries its own LIMIT, or one that returns no rows.\n\nOne carve-out: PostgreSQL also accepts `... FOR UPDATE OFFSET 5`, where\nthe user''s own limit clause is last. Inserting there would produce\n`LIMIT 200 FOR UPDATE OFFSET 5`, which does not parse, so a locking\nclause trailed by `OFFSET`/`FETCH` keeps the appended form it had before.\n\nVerification: against the previous guard the rewritten tests fail 3 of 42\n(`SELECT * FROM orders FOR UPDATE` came back {limit: null, state: ''none''}\nwhere the test expects the inserted form); at this commit failure.test.ts\nis tests 42 / pass 42 / fail 0 and the app suite tests 635 / pass 635 /\nfail 0. Every statement the function now produces for the PostgreSQL rows\nwas parsed with libpg-query 17.7.4 (the real PostgreSQL 17 grammar): all\nOK, limitCount set, locking clause intact. MySQL was not exercised live —\nno server on this machine; the MySQL half rests on the documented 8.0\nSELECT synopsis.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): the row limit goes in front of a locking clause\n\n19b30076a told the reader that a statement ending in `FOR UPDATE` runs\nunbounded because the clause has to stay last in both engines. That is\ntrue of MySQL and not of PostgreSQL, and the app no longer behaves that\nway: the limit is inserted before the clause.\n\nThe ''The row limit'' paragraph now says where the limit lands and why\nthat placement is the one valid on both engines — MySQL requires the\nlocking clause after `LIMIT`, PostgreSQL accepts either order — that the\nclause, its `OF` list, `NOWAIT` and `SKIP LOCKED` are sent as typed, and\nthat a statement already ending in its own `OFFSET` keeps the appended\nform. The `## Limits` bullet carries the same sentence in one line.\n\nDocs only.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): name the WITH-chain exception to the locking-clause rule\n\n`WITH x AS (SELECT 1) SELECT * FROM x FOR UPDATE` is classified as a\nWRITE: `classifyStatement`''s WITH branch asks whether the chain contains\nany of CTE_WRITE_WORDS at depth 0, and the `UPDATE` of the trailing\nlocking clause answers yes. A write gets no row limit, so this one read\ndoes not take the limit the paragraph above it now promises — and it is\nalso offered no idempotent retry and fails with write wording.\n\nThe behaviour predates this round (it is the same at ae6a1e79 and on\ndevelop) and fixing it means teaching the classification about the\nlocking clause, which reaches past this PR''s scope into code the review\nalready cleared. So it is documented rather than changed: the README\n''The row limit'' section names the exception and says the same chain\nending in `FOR SHARE` or `FOR KEY SHARE` is bounded normally, and\nfailure.test.ts pins both halves so the pair cannot drift unnoticed.\n\nfailure.test.ts: tests 43 / pass 43 / fail 0.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): never split a line that carries an unmasked # comment before a locking clause\n\n`#` starts a line comment in MySQL only, so `codeOnly()` masks it for that\ndialect alone. On every other dialect — `unknown` included, which is where the\napp lands when the dialect probe fails — `SELECT * FROM t # for update` reads\nas a statement whose last clause is a lock, and inserting the row limit in\nfront of that clause moved `for update` onto a line of its own, OUT of the\ncomment the user wrote it in. Sent to the MySQL server that `#` implies, the\nstatement then takes row locks the user had commented out: the one way this\nrewrite can change what a statement DOES.\n\nThe insertion branch now sends such a statement untouched with `state: ''none''`\n— the path a statement the app cannot bound already takes — when an unmasked\n`#` stands between the start of the clause''s line and the clause. The test is\nblunt on purpose: a PostgreSQL statement using `#` as an operator on that line\nloses its limit and reports `no limit applied`, which is an honest unbounded\nread rather than a rewritten statement.\n\nAlso updates the `LimitState` and `applyRowLimit` docstrings, which still said\nthe limit is only ever APPENDED.\n\nTests: the new block pins the `unknown` and `mysql` readings of `# for update`\n(masked on mysql, so it stays an ordinary read with the limit appended) and a\n`#` on an earlier line, which must not stop the insertion. It fails 1 of 44\nagainst the previous batch.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): correct three claims in the row-limit section\n\nThe guide overstated what the locking-clause rule covers:\n\n- `FETCH FIRST … ROWS ONLY` after a locking clause was covered by the same\n  sentence as `OFFSET`, which reads as \"both keep the appended form and stay\n  valid\". Only `OFFSET` does. `FETCH FIRST` is not recognised as a limit the\n  statement carries — a gap older than this rule — so that form gets a `LIMIT`\n  appended after the clause and PostgreSQL rejects it as a second limit clause.\n  Now stated.\n- The `WITH`-chain exception was described as a chain ENDING in `FOR UPDATE`.\n  The classifier takes any `FOR UPDATE` or `FOR NO KEY UPDATE` site in the\n  chain for the chain''s verb, a CTE body included, so\n  `WITH x AS (SELECT 1 FROM t FOR UPDATE) SELECT * FROM x` is a write with no\n  limit too. The paragraph now says \"anywhere\".\n- The `#` guard added in the previous commit is described, including its cost\n  on a PostgreSQL statement that uses `#` as an operator.\n\nThe `## Limits` bullet names the three shapes that keep no limit or the old\nplacement.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): treat FETCH FIRST/NEXT ... as an in-statement row limit\n\napplyRowLimit only recognised LIMIT as a statement''s own row bound. The\nSQL standard''s FETCH { FIRST | NEXT } n { ROW | ROWS } { ONLY | WITH TIES }\nclause shares LIMIT''s grammar slot (PostgreSQL''s select_limit admits at\nmost one), but was invisible to the guard, so a statement already bounded\nby FETCH got the header''s LIMIT appended too:\n\n  SELECT * FROM orders ORDER BY id FETCH FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY\n  -> ... FETCH FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY\\nLIMIT 200   (rejected by PostgreSQL)\n\nThe same gap hit the locking-clause path from the other side: a FETCH\nclause placed before a trailing locking clause (e.g. `FETCH FIRST 10 ROWS\nONLY FOR UPDATE`; PostgreSQL accepts the lock on either side of the limit\nclause, and both orders are pinned in the tests) reached\ntrailingLockingClauseAt and got a LIMIT spliced in between the two.\n\nAdd hasFetchLimit(sql, dialect) with an anchored FETCH_LIMIT_HEAD regex\n(/^fetch\\s+(?:first|next)(?![\\w$])/i), mirroring trailingLockingClauseAt''s\nown idiom: keywordSites() finds every `fetch` in masked code, keeps only\ndepth-0 sites (so a FETCH inside a subquery, CTE body, or parenthesised\nset member is never read as the outer bound), and tests the anchored\nregex against codeOnly() text sliced at each site. The in-statement guard\nnow checks `hasTopLevelKeyword(sql, ''limit'', ...) || hasFetchLimit(...)`\nbefore both the `All` short-circuit and the locking-clause insertion, so\nboth FETCH-then-lock and lock-then-FETCH orderings short-circuit to\n''in-statement'' before trailingLockingClauseAt is ever reached; its\nexisting ''offset|fetch'' tail guard for `FOR UPDATE OFFSET 5` is untouched.\n\nFETCH...ONLY/WITH TIES is always the statement''s own limit, never an\napp-enforced cap — WITH TIES can return more rows than named, so the\nhonest report is ''in-statement'', not a row count the app claims to have\nenforced. A cursor''s `FETCH NEXT FROM <cursor>` never reaches this code:\nthe regex matches it, but RETURNS_ROWS already gates returnsRows to false\nfor a bare cursor-FETCH statement before hasFetchLimit is called.\n\nPins both shapes (asclearuc''s review 5249677764) plus depth, dialect,\ncomment/bind-parameter, OFFSET-pairing, and cursor-FETCH coverage in\nfailure.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): describe FETCH row limits in the README\n\nCorrect the row-limit section to match applyRowLimit''s new FETCH\nhandling: a statement bounded by FETCH { FIRST | NEXT } ... { ONLY |\nWITH TIES } is now recognised the same way LIMIT is, and is sent\nunchanged — locking clause and all, in either order — reading \"limit\nin statement\" rather than \"limit 200\" under any header option\n(200/1000/All). Only an outermost FETCH counts, matching LIMIT''s\nexisting subquery/CTE-body scoping, and a bare OFFSET still isn''t a\nlimit on its own. Also notes FETCH''s other meaning (advancing a\ncursor, `FETCH NEXT FROM mycursor`), which never reaches this rewrite,\nand that MySQL has no FETCH FIRST clause at all.\n\nRemoves the now-inaccurate \"still gets a LIMIT appended after it\"\nclaim from the row-limit section and the Limits bullet — introduced\nin 7e4a8611e, which fixed the insertion order but left the doc\ndescribing the old, broken behaviour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sql-ui): report no limit when an unmasked # precedes the statement''s own limit clause\n\nCodeRabbit review 5253101335 (thread r4051099532) on PR #2282: when\n`dialect === ''unknown''` — where the app lands when the dialect probe\nfails, and a MySQL server is a live possibility behind that failure —\n`keywordSites` does not mask `#`, so a same-line `# fetch first 10 rows\nonly` (or `# limit 5`) was read as the statement''s own outer limit\nclause and reported `state: ''in-statement''`. On MySQL that text is an\nordinary comment and the SELECT runs with no bound at all, so the meta\nline claimed a limit that does not exist on the server that actually\nruns the statement.\n\nGive the FETCH/LIMIT check an index instead of a boolean\n(`inStatementLimitAt`, replacing `hasFetchLimit`) so it can be handed to\nthe existing `hashCommentPrecedesClause` guard — the same ambiguity\nruling the trailing-locking-clause case already uses. Both spellings\n(`LIMIT` and `FETCH { FIRST | NEXT } ... ONLY`) now defer to it on any\ndialect but MySQL, including `unknown`, and report `state: ''none''`\ninstead. The guard returns before the append path, so the SQL sent is\nbyte-identical before and after this change in every case — only the\nmeta line changes. `mysql` is unchanged: `#` is masked there, so the\nguard short-circuits and the limit is still appended as before.\n\nHousekeeping: `inStatementLimitAt` subsumes `hasFetchLimit`, and\n`hasTopLevelKeyword` is no longer used in this file (classify.ts still\nuses it), so both are dropped from batch.ts.\n\nTests: flipped the existing fp-10 `#`-parity assertion (LIMIT/FETCH now\nagree with the locking-clause ruling instead of disagreeing with it),\nadded the LIMIT spelling''s twin (previously asserted only in a\ncomment), and pinned the non-regressions — mysql still appends, a `#`\non an earlier line still reads in-statement, and a `#` inside a string\nliteral still reads in-statement. Full run: 674 pass, 0 fail (671\nexisting + 3 net new).\n\nDocs: widened the README''s `#`-ambiguity paragraph and the \"## Limits\"\nbullet to cover the statement''s own limit clause, not just a trailing\nlocking clause.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(sql-ui): pin the PostgreSQL # operator cost of the unmasked-# limit rule\n\nAddresses the two devil''s-advocate findings (DA-2, DA-3) from the\nCodeRabbit round on PR #2282.\n\nDA-3: add a test pinning the one shape where a valid, genuinely bounded\nPostgreSQL statement''s reported state actually changes under the new\nsame-line `#` guard: `SELECT a # b FROM t LIMIT 5` on postgres. On\nPostgreSQL `#` is the bitwise-XOR operator, so the statement is valid\nand the LIMIT is real and bounding — but the guard cannot tell an\noperator from a MySQL comment without masking `#` for PostgreSQL too,\nso it still reports `none` instead of `limit in statement`. This is\nthe same cost the locking-clause guard already pays on this dialect.\nVerified the current output before writing the assertion:\napplyRowLimit(''SELECT a # b FROM t LIMIT 5'', ''200'', ''postgres'') ->\n{ sql: ''SELECT a # b FROM t LIMIT 5'', limit: null, state: ''none'' }.\n\nDA-2: extend the README''s existing PostgreSQL `#`-as-operator sentence\n(the one ending \"write the statement on two lines to have it bounded\")\nwith a clause noting that, on a statement that carries its own LIMIT\nor FETCH clause, the same `#` ambiguity also costs the `limit in\nstatement` reading, even though the statement is genuinely bounded.\nRest of the paragraph is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(sql-ui): correct the Node note above the test-discovery scan\n\nCodeRabbit (thread r4051116284 on apps/sql-ui/scripts/tasks.js:73) flagged\nthat the comment claimed CI resolves `node-version: 20` to a current 20.x\nfor the build job that runs `builder test`. That job (.github/workflows/\n_build.yaml) has no actions/setup-node step and no node-version key at\nall — it runs on the runner image''s preinstalled Node. `node-version: 20`\nis pinned only in ci.yml''s shell-contract job, which does not run\n`builder test`.\n\nReworded the comment to say the build job pins no Node version and the\nfloor is therefore not guaranteed by CI either way, while keeping the\nrest of the comment (and the PR #2282 / r4008843631 pointer) unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nihalnihalani <24360630+nihalnihalani@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsVl0CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAldAQAGQUhclm78tFBxlj4g3WKubC\nIbqb906iltB9qUEt5U9+e8ialRu8tPI0xeZ+DzCplR5G5FI/P723SZOiD/aGzqhl\nquoniwx6XHOF/t7va80e1U7Yg5y+E6jgsTmDWEEzvRULkROdjPedaOzuhrmCUGF9\n7Qaf7zoKGtLmSWTftVrlcHfuWqLWLOLzdafb24qCpuY7jl9Jxs+A7/ssqnZBBg+G\nqxQjiI5RQ2RyoCp8z02eTrwspvQTScKtivSLfKjMtCt+9hll7XmB834b5vii6Q22\nXLGcKo3DstO3JKKZnocy9EJIvE+0pdiG4DIuwGsNtQ7vFGeO2jQRmXiNX9R87h5K\n6OCJWrTfsHmRww3QTX/TAweERtNeMiObM2I9z2wI38gpNE3XdrXHMct2T0+C2FUa\nsO2PT8Wpqg5s1zTcU9r4kEJDH0PflOMIubZlf1YsEazSPlaXv9AQDSJtKI8D1qbF\nP1j8oj9010w6/iRPd81Pf7HwDsYpmsfBFEmWCFaZIsry2ayKN03JgBpqijjzjcLd\nMixSr6/OsRJxFmYXVD0zCskuV4mZP8kdz5jaVDhzcJ8ng6ibe3uge5/bYjTF3IAu\nELYOpRNQvrIe/6Re6cKs0kOYhXpn8x6pS0p1CTJT7EZfhj5f/xcjsSJIhTs21QI1\nc1lHv+uhpR2IIOv3433s\n=R5Cc\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09