c867","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/85271aa880605cbbc2ee36009736fafb5febc867"}],"sha":"6a99fb3c00249869c337671a384786f1fc52d588","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/6a99fb3c00249869c337671a384786f1fc52d588"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/130109357?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dylan-savage","id":130109357,"login":"dylan-savage","node_id":"U_kgDOB8FPrQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/85271aa880605cbbc2ee36009736fafb5febc867/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T15:39:28Z","email":"130109357+dylan-savage@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dylan Savage"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T15:39:28Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(ui): key Microsoft login scopes by node protocol so the broker requests Graph scopes (#2373)\n\nLoginWithMicrosoftButton looked up SERVICE_TIER_SCOPES[formContext.provider],\nbut formContext.provider is the node protocol name (tool_excel, tool_word,\ntool_onedrive, tool_outlook_mail, tool_outlook_calendar) while the map was\nkeyed excel/word/onedrive/outlook_mail/outlook_calendar. Every lookup missed,\nso no scope param was sent, the broker requested identity only, and the node\nrejected the token (e.g. \"Missing: Files.ReadWrite\").\n\nRekey the map by protocol name (scope values unchanged; they mirror\ncore/microsoft_access.py) and hoist it to an exported module constant. Add a\ntest that reads every tool_microsoft_365 services.*.json and asserts the map\nhas non-empty scopes for each protocol and each access tier.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b26242834548b24eb824a273afd252ea2b9b3482","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/b26242834548b24eb824a273afd252ea2b9b3482"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/85271aa880605cbbc2ee36009736fafb5febc867","verification":{"payload":"tree b26242834548b24eb824a273afd252ea2b9b3482\nparent 11a18b5a180520dc12d4f456f4ff536f2d309613\nauthor Dylan Savage <130109357+dylan-savage@users.noreply.github.com> 1790005168 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790005168 -0700\n\nfix(ui): key Microsoft login scopes by node protocol so the broker requests Graph scopes (#2373)\n\nLoginWithMicrosoftButton looked up SERVICE_TIER_SCOPES[formContext.provider],\nbut formContext.provider is the node protocol name (tool_excel, tool_word,\ntool_onedrive, tool_outlook_mail, tool_outlook_calendar) while the map was\nkeyed excel/word/onedrive/outlook_mail/outlook_calendar. Every lookup missed,\nso no scope param was sent, the broker requested identity only, and the node\nrejected the token (e.g. \"Missing: Files.ReadWrite\").\n\nRekey the map by protocol name (scope values unchanged; they mirror\ncore/microsoft_access.py) and hoist it to an exported module constant. Add a\ntest that reads every tool_microsoft_365 services.*.json and asserts the map\nhas non-empty scopes for each protocol and each access tier.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsU+wCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAh+wQAAX4NnRe5e5aYbtt7xpH/FYn\n450JZ2HAzhJh7YmTQX9Mvik1wcsEWouo+FqAdj0c+g3P6EKgbK240j2uFew58ti8\nLRhJBxbxNtyBJaU2gOx0X73Mf/In9d9j7I2NvpIwNP0smgUIl10Sa7Fp2jaVh7pe\n3Eszoe0VHNtHhmTPQp+9M/KczT5HEZxLtVOt8RyytBWoZteHInwaJCbP/5mKExRR\nDZsHWTvK6hy7sCUchZHPu87Frq302iEJWDrl5WLCto9G9sW0oi5Sm7eu/jgDQ1bW\ntCkOZUmxfV0HYCx9jfCQFy7o3FlsQvhm9Gne/17SwNBScyfGKALBAC5pMh0lbDUj\nwy24uimJvgP1y8x97LIvNIxWQljUKi095dVXX0HjZEHjaxtC8kqhuFIOb1zRhAcq\noqicCSqaD7jzSzrNfkzIFSpT4gyiWM8BOn0f8EPZXDSi0yxkNEbBgV9l8AY4yMLa\nSsHhmqSdME1paRsxXzucUFPzvtSSi4l6qmv9cm2030prQd9UmWuBXL8GM1VdljMD\nb6O4d6YOEyMkXWB1Hh7zPMnEgAo7CWzjFwH/d6BQHDPkgRyuM7gAoibGFNzcTWbp\noMQa8xbk/HM3AUxOz3qYzFKToSr4bIAlJiCdDiCw0UQrhW5/4sHYDvKYp+xr0GyX\nGFCs+OrPU9Jex8B9Yfta\n=eqJK\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-21T15:39:28Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/85271aa880605cbbc2ee36009736fafb5febc867","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDg1MjcxYWE4ODA2MDVjYmJjMmVlMzYwMDk3MzZmYWZiNWZlYmM4Njc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/11a18b5a180520dc12d4f456f4ff536f2d309613","sha":"11a18b5a180520dc12d4f456f4ff536f2d309613","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/11a18b5a180520dc12d4f456f4ff536f2d309613"}],"sha":"85271aa880605cbbc2ee36009736fafb5febc867","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/85271aa880605cbbc2ee36009736fafb5febc867"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/258292005?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1","id":258292005,"login":"madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1","node_id":"U_kgDOD2U5JQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/madhumitha-chandrasekaran-1","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/11a18b5a180520dc12d4f456f4ff536f2d309613/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T15:19:52Z","email":"madhumitha317@berkeley.edu","name":"Madhumitha Chandrasekaran"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T15:19:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(graph_falkordb): fail loudly on a missing graph instead of silently falling back (#2292)\n\nA read-only node whose \"Default Graph\" doesn''t exist on the server was\nsilently resolving to whatever preconfig.default declares (agent), rather\nthan the graph the user actually intended. FalkorDB accepts a query against\na missing graph and only fails on the first result, with \"ERR Invalid graph\noperation on empty key\" -- an error that gives no hint the graph itself was\nthe problem (#2155).\n\n- _open_driver now raises a clear error naming the missing graph and the\n  ones that do exist, at pipeline start rather than on first query.\n- _probe_connection warns the same way at save time.\n- Both skip the check when allow_writes or allow_execute is on, since\n  FalkorDB creates a graph lazily on its first write -- a write-capable\n  node may legitimately target one that doesn''t exist yet.\n- The canvas tile was empty, so the active graph -- or that it had silently\n  fallen back to one -- was invisible without opening the inspector. It now\n  shows \"Graph: <name>\".\n\nThe original report described the fields as unexposed/unpersisted; they\nalready were (confirmed by a prior investigation on the issue). The real\ndefect was the silent, unvalidated fallback, which is what this fixes.\n\nCo-authored-by: madhumithachandrasekaran31 <madhumitha2110549@ssn.edu.in>","tree":{"sha":"ae0f4940ab280afce78fa8346a49316e3c5f1980","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/ae0f4940ab280afce78fa8346a49316e3c5f1980"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/11a18b5a180520dc12d4f456f4ff536f2d309613","verification":{"payload":"tree ae0f4940ab280afce78fa8346a49316e3c5f1980\nparent 88f0966732dfab0ea313e9f79e0e665ee8cbef6a\nauthor Madhumitha Chandrasekaran <madhumitha317@berkeley.edu> 1790003992 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790003992 +0200\n\nfix(graph_falkordb): fail loudly on a missing graph instead of silently falling back (#2292)\n\nA read-only node whose \"Default Graph\" doesn''t exist on the server was\nsilently resolving to whatever preconfig.default declares (agent), rather\nthan the graph the user actually intended. FalkorDB accepts a query against\na missing graph and only fails on the first result, with \"ERR Invalid graph\noperation on empty key\" -- an error that gives no hint the graph itself was\nthe problem (#2155).\n\n- _open_driver now raises a clear error naming the missing graph and the\n  ones that do exist, at pipeline start rather than on first query.\n- _probe_connection warns the same way at save time.\n- Both skip the check when allow_writes or allow_execute is on, since\n  FalkorDB creates a graph lazily on its first write -- a write-capable\n  node may legitimately target one that doesn''t exist yet.\n- The canvas tile was empty, so the active graph -- or that it had silently\n  fallen back to one -- was invisible without opening the inspector. It now\n  shows \"Graph: <name>\".\n\nThe original report described the fields as unexposed/unpersisted; they\nalready were (confirmed by a prior investigation on the issue). The real\ndefect was the silent, unvalidated fallback, which is what this fixes.\n\nCo-authored-by: madhumithachandrasekaran31 <madhumitha2110549@ssn.edu.in>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsUsYCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAlaYQAKWPVXQ6QTlYLm3AyPmrUOQd\nLGhVyWu1+EnBUsX5j6fttVgUafWW8j2PY8q4OyRXPNL+Ca7ijs7Ug4jBm0wNzBLM\n9fp06fyy99wBpOjUUB5L1AtCp6aYkAenJ9CvAmyU/2khIIPakME07SyB9QuYwa0h\ngaXZqyfAp6wiVs5aCvpJD/yTy7iZv4RLTI2hvCq1DxiNI2i910OOrqmCtkd9JDT0\nsFF5/7pNevm9oOQfW5Qx6PaEIWp/uFtnxUd6WxX1VzDzDh9bWWTgsnAfhEKf07bB\nc6PByZW0+XiInlTLqe72B3dRpoxEWl/t0ErU20PHM11M4Q6yeOmOZy+yshAczXHW\nLO2ke+yvms03dmtRnmOgjVr8+r90mve96tRoQ6jiUjpHq2UIUBXXGIS1s67LryRM\noZiR8gMqhz2+fSuhwJSHivuTdkh4t1MKucXLMcT+HlNyhGP59azzZs61fzZxM1kn\noeHxV6nwHO1cCXhMGWsp+R3QvQcVfRZSt34dBRRNURCdmowbydZWnTzHdtmiGKLf\nzZ86J22ZumET2F2VgzY1tI53bkzpqT3Cb3kWHqlPx2bTS0OXQZS1Y/TngJqSO6xf\nNAbWb2/Jr7svR9JoAMCX0Or+AnvjKXvsYrk7SNor9qi/klwSSLQ82QWImzhkc7wC\nae1IkK5hi95olYfpWZz7\n=a9EW\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-21T15:19:52Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/11a18b5a180520dc12d4f456f4ff536f2d309613","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDExYTE4YjVhMTgwNTIwZGMxMmQ0ZjQ1NmY0ZmY1MzZmMmQzMDk2MTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/88f0966732dfab0ea313e9f79e0e665ee8cbef6a","sha":"88f0966732dfab0ea313e9f79e0e665ee8cbef6a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/88f0966732dfab0ea313e9f79e0e665ee8cbef6a"}],"sha":"11a18b5a180520dc12d4f456f4ff536f2d309613","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/11a18b5a180520dc12d4f456f4ff536f2d309613"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24360630?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/nihalnihalani","id":24360630,"login":"nihalnihalani","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzYwNjMw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/88f0966732dfab0ea313e9f79e0e665ee8cbef6a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T14:57:58Z","email":"nihal.nihalani@gmail.com","name":"NIHAL NIHALANI"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T14:57:58Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(sdk,ci): ''rocketride diff'' — semantic .pipe diff (layout-noise-free) + PR-comment Action (#1607)\n\n* feat(sdk): rocketride.pipediff — semantic diff engine for .pipe files (#1606)\n\nA raw `git diff` of a `.pipe` file is dominated by canvas coordinate\nchurn: every node carries a `ui` block whose `position.x`/`position.y`\nmove whenever the node is nudged, plus a top-level `viewport` that\nshifts on pan or zoom. Those lines say nothing about behavior, yet they\nbury the changes that do — a swapped LLM provider, a re-tuned chunk\nsize, a rewired retrieval step.\n\n`rocketride.pipediff` compares two parsed `.pipe` documents instead of\ntheir text. Components are matched by `id`; the result is grouped into\nnodes (added / removed / re-provisioned), edges (reconstructed from each\ncomponent''s `input[]` data lanes and `control[]` orchestration lanes),\nand config (deep-diffed into dotted paths such as\n`config.default.strlen`). Layout is collapsed to a single \"changed\" flag\nunless the caller asks for it, and JSON values are compared with JSON\ntyping so `1` and `true` are never conflated.\n\nThe package is import-only and does no I/O beyond reading the files it\nis given: `gitref` shells out to `git show <ref>:<file>` for the\nref-vs-worktree case and maps every failure mode onto an explicit error,\nand `reporters` renders the same diff as human text, stable sorted JSON,\nor Markdown with `|` and backticks escaped so untrusted values cannot\nbreak a PR comment.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(cli): add `rocketride diff` subcommand (#1606)\n\nWires `rocketride.pipediff` onto the CLI as `rocketride diff`, the\nreview half of the \"pipelines as code\" loop: `validate` says a `.pipe`\nis well-formed, `diff` says what changed in it.\n\nThe subcommand is deliberately the odd one out in `setup_parser`: it is\nlocal only — it never contacts an engine or the network — so it does not\ntake the shared `--uri`/`--apikey`/`--token` connection arguments every\nother command gets from `_add_connection_args`, and its `--json` is a\nplain format flag rather than the shared `--json [FILE]`. It compares\ntwo files positionally, or a single working-tree file against a git ref\nvia `--git REF`. `--markdown` renders a PR-comment-friendly report,\n`--include-layout` opts the canvas churn back in, and `--exit-zero`\nturns a gating run into an informational one.\n\nExit codes are the CI contract: 0 for no semantic changes (or any\nsuccessful run under `--exit-zero`), 1 when semantic changes are found,\nand 2 for a usage error, an unreadable or unparseable file, or a bad git\nref.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(ci): add pipe-diff composite action — semantic `.pipe` diff as a sticky PR comment (#1606)\n\nBecause `rocketride diff` is local and exits non-zero on change, it\ndrops straight into a pull-request check. This composite action does the\nassembly: it installs the CLI, resolves the PR base commit (fetching it\nitself, so the default `fetch-depth: 1` checkout is fine), finds every\nchanged `.pipe` file, diffs each against the base, and maintains one\nsticky comment so reviewers see \"OpenAI → Anthropic, chunk size\n512 → 1024, retrieval rewired through a reranker\" instead of a wall of\ncoordinate noise.\n\nEvery run also writes the full report to the job summary, which is what\nkeeps the action useful on a fork pull request: GitHub hands those jobs\na read-only `GITHUB_TOKEN` regardless of `permissions:`, so the comment\nAPI returns 403 — the action warns instead of failing and the diff stays\nvisible in the summary. The summary is written on the base-fetch failure\npath too, rather than leaving a run with no output at all.\n\n`install-from` exists because `rocketride diff` ships in a release after\n1.3.0 and PyPI does not serve it yet; it points pip at a checkout or a\npinned commit instead. Markdown assembled from file names and diff\noutput is escaped, and the action distinguishes \"no `.pipe` files\nchanged\" from \"could not determine what changed\" rather than reporting\nthe reassuring answer blind.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: document `rocketride diff` in the CLI reference and examples (#1606)\n\nDocuments the new subcommand where the restructured docs tree puts CLI\ncontent — `docs/public/product/connect/cli.mdx` — as a `### diff`\nsection alongside `### validate`, the other file-oriented, CI-friendly\ncommand. The section covers the flags, the exit-code contract, what does\nand does not count as a semantic change, all three output modes, and the\n`pipe-diff` action recipe including the fork-PR token caveat and the\npre-release `install-from` workaround.\n\nTwo surrounding statements needed amending rather than just appending:\nthe Python-vs-TypeScript note now records that `diff` is the one\nPython-only command, and the \"Connecting\" preamble now excludes `diff`\nfrom the commands that take connection options, because it takes none.\n\n`examples/pipe-diff-example.md` puts a raw `git diff` next to\n`rocketride diff` on the same edit to `rag-pipeline.pipe`; the examples\nindex gains a Guides entry pointing at it.\n\nNote for reviewers of the earlier revision: this content previously\nlived in `docs/README-python-client.md`,\n`packages/client-python/docs/index.md`, and\n`packages/docs/content-static/cli.mdx`. All three paths were removed by\nthe documentation revamp, so the content is re-homed here instead of\nresurrecting them, and the example''s cross-link now points at the\npublished `/connect/cli#diff` anchor.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(client-python): ship rocketride_common when installing from the source tree (#1607)\n\n`pip install ./packages/client-python` from a clean checkout produced a\n`rocketride` binary that could not start:\n\n    File \".../rocketride/cli/utils/env.py\", line 29, in <module>\n        from rocketride_common.env import (\n    ModuleNotFoundError: No module named ''rocketride_common''\n\n`cli/__init__.py` eagerly imports `.main`, `main.py` imports `.utils.env`\nat module scope, and that imports `rocketride_common` — so every\nsubcommand, `diff` included, dies at import time.\n\n`rocketride_common` is a source library shared with the TypeScript client\nrather than a published distribution, so it cannot be added to\n`dependencies`. It lives at `packages/client-common/python/src` and the\nbuilder copies it into the staging tree (`client-python:wheel-source`)\nbefore the released wheel is built — which is why the published wheel has\nalways been fine and only the source tree is broken. The source tree is\nalso exactly what the new pipe-diff action''s `install-from` input points\nat, in both documented forms (`./packages/client-python` and\n`git+https://…#subdirectory=packages/client-python`, where pip clones the\nwhole repository), so the documented pre-release recipe could never work.\n\nAdding the sibling source root to `packages.find.where` makes setuptools\nfind the shared package in the source-tree layout. setuptools skips a\n`where` entry whose directory does not exist, so the builder''s staged\nlayout — where `rocketride_common` is already inside `src/` and there is\nno sibling directory — is untouched. Verified end to end: from a clean\n`git archive` tree `rocketride diff --help` now exits 0, and rebuilding\nthe staged layout through `python -m build` still yields an sdist and a\nwheel carrying both `rocketride` and `rocketride_common`, byte-identical\nin top-level contents to before.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): diff .pipe files against the merge base, not the base branch tip (#1607)\n\nThe action took `github.event.pull_request.base.sha` -- the tip of the\nbase branch at event time -- and used it directly for both\n`git diff \"${BASE_SHA}\" HEAD` and `rocketride diff --git \"${BASE_SHA}\"`.\nThat tip keeps moving as other pull requests merge, so every commit\nmerged into the base since this branch forked was reported as a change\nmade by THIS pull request, and reported backwards: those commits sit on\nthe left-hand side of the diff, so a node someone else added shows up as\na node this PR deleted.\n\nReproduced in a scratch repository -- fork at C0, this branch adds C1\ntouching `a.pipe`, the base then gains C2 adding `b.pipe`:\n\n    git diff --name-status $C2 HEAD     ->  M a.pipe / D b.pipe   (wrong)\n    git diff --name-status $(git merge-base $C2 HEAD) HEAD\n                                        ->  M a.pipe             (right)\n\nThe action now resolves `git merge-base \"${BASE_SHA}\" HEAD` once and\nfeeds that to the changed-file enumeration, the deletion enumeration and\nevery per-file `rocketride diff --git`. This is the comparison GitHub''s\nown \"Files changed\" tab shows, and the same one the action''s README\nalready recommended in its manual recipe.\n\nFetch depth had to change with it. The base commit was fetched with\n`--depth=1`, which leaves it with no ancestry for `merge-base` to walk,\nso that flag is gone. A default `fetch-depth: 1` checkout also leaves\nHEAD with no ancestry, so when the first `merge-base` attempt comes back\nempty the action deepens (`--unshallow`, falling back to `--deepen`) and\nretries; a workflow that already checks out with `fetch-depth: 0` never\npays for that. Verified against a real depth-1 clone: the first attempt\nreturns nothing, the deepen recovers, and the merge base resolves to the\nfork point.\n\nIf the merge base still cannot be computed the step now fails with a\nreport and an annotation pointing at `fetch-depth: 0`, rather than\nfalling back to the base tip -- silently diffing the wrong thing is the\ndefect being fixed.\n\nThe action README and the CLI reference are updated to describe the\nmerge-base comparison and the optional `fetch-depth: 0`.\n\nVerified: action.yml parses as YAML, `bash -n` and shellcheck pass on all\nfour `run:` blocks.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(client-python): assert the Markdown table escaping without cmarkgfm (#1607)\n\n`test_gfm_renders_escaped_cells_back_to_the_original_value` was guarded by\n`@unittest.skipIf(cmarkgfm is None)` and `cmarkgfm` was in no dependency\ngroup, so the one test that decides whether `_md_cell`''s backslash\nhandling is right never ran — not locally, not in CI.\n\nDeclaring the dependency is not enough on its own: CI does not `pip\ninstall` this package''s extras at all. `builder test` runs\n`client-python:run-pytest`, which invokes pytest through the bundled\nengine binary in its own isolated environment (packages/client-python/\nscripts/tasks.js -> scripts/lib/pytest.js), so an entry under\n`[project.optional-dependencies] test` would still leave the test\nskipped on every CI run.\n\nSo the coverage now comes from an assertion that needs no C extension.\n`_split_gfm_row` applies cmark-gfm''s own table-row rule directly: a `|`\nsplits the row only when no backslash precedes it, and a `|` preceded by\na run of n backslashes is escaped and loses exactly one of them. The new\n`test_escaped_cells_survive_gfm_row_splitting` runs that over the same\nvalue corpus — which both tests now share — and asserts three columns and\nan exact round-trip to the original value.\n\nThat rule was derived from cmark-gfm rather than from the spec prose, and\nthen checked against it: comparing `_split_gfm_row` with `cmarkgfm` over\nseveral thousand random values built from the punctuation that shows up\nin pipeline config produced zero disagreements. Mutation-checked too —\nmaking `_md_cell` double the backslash instead of adding one fails six of\nthe eight corpus values.\n\nThe cmarkgfm test is kept as an oracle rather than deleted: it is the\nstronger check when the library is present, it now shares the corpus, and\n`cmarkgfm` is added to the `test` extra so `pip install .[test]` enables\nit instead of leaving the guard permanently dead. Its skip message says\nwhich check still covers the rules.\n\nVerified: 131 passed with cmarkgfm installed; 130 passed and 1 skipped\n(only the oracle) without it. Ruff check and format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci: run the pipe-diff action on this repo and pin-check composite actions (#1607)\n\nTwo gaps, both of which let the new action ship unexercised.\n\nNothing referenced the action. No workflow under `.github/workflows/`\nmentioned pipe-diff, so the install step, the base resolution, the\n`rocketride diff` invocation and the comment step had never run as a\nGitHub Action — the first consumer to adopt it would have been the one to\nfind out. `.github/workflows/pipe-diff.yml` runs it on `pull_request`\nagainst the repository''s own 45 `.pipe` files, and the paths filter also\nfires on changes to the action, the diff engine and the CLI subcommand,\nso a change to any of them is exercised before it merges even when no\npipeline changed.\n\nIt is `pull_request`, not `pull_request_target`, for the reason the\naction''s own README gives: a `pull_request_target` job checks out the\nbase branch (nothing to diff) and holds a writable token, and this\nworkflow installs the CLI from the checkout. On the `pull_request` event\na fork''s code runs with a read-only token and no secrets, and the report\nlands in the job summary — the documented fork behaviour. `install-from`\npoints at the checkout because PyPI does not serve `rocketride diff`\nyet; the comment says to switch to `cli-version` once it does.\n\nSecond, `scripts/verify-action-pins.mjs` only enumerated\n`.github/workflows/*.y(a)ml`, so the SHAs pinned inside composite actions\nwere outside the pin guard entirely — both this PR''s\n`.github/actions/pipe-diff/action.yml` and the pre-existing\n`.github/actions/validate-pipes/action.yml`. A composite action could\ncarry a mismatched or unpinned `uses:` and CI would pass. The verifier\nnow also enumerates `.github/actions/<name>/action.{yml,yaml}`, treating\na missing directory as an empty list.\n\nVerified: the existing verifier unit tests still pass (7/7); the live\ncheck goes from 26 files to 29 and reports \"Verified 87 action pins\nacross 29 workflow and composite-action files\" with no errors; and\ndrifting the pipe-diff action''s github-script comment from v7 to v6 is\nnow caught with the correct file and line annotation, which it was not\nbefore.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(client-python): run the cmarkgfm escaping oracle in CI\n\n`test_gfm_renders_escaped_cells_back_to_the_original_value` is the test that\ndecides how `_md_cell` escapes a pipe, but `cmarkgfm` was only in the `test`\nextra and CI never installs that extra — it runs pytest on the bundled engine\ninterpreter against the source checkout. So on every CI run the decisive test\nskipped, and the gate was `test_escaped_cells_survive_gfm_row_splitting`,\nwhich resolves the same corpus with our own model of cmark-gfm''s cell\nscanner. Code and test then shared one assumption: if the model were wrong,\nboth would be wrong together and nothing would go red. The values are\nuntrusted `.pipe` content rendered into a PR comment, so a cell that splits\nits row is exactly the failure the escaping exists to prevent.\n\nAdd `cmarkgfm` to `packages/client-python/tests/requirements.txt`, which\n`client-python:setup-test-deps` installs into the engine interpreter through\n`depends()` — the mechanism #1612 added for the otel-bridge tests, so the\nwheel resolves under the same merged constraints as everything else and both\npackages stay on one route. The floor stays `>=2024.1.14`: 2024.11.20\npublishes no macOS wheels, and a tighter pin would make the macOS leg build\ncmark-gfm from source. It resolves to 2025.10.22, which has wheels for every\nCI leg.\n\nThe `pyproject.toml` comment that explained why the extra was not enough now\nnames the route CI actually takes. The library-free check keeps its own\ndocstring but no longer claims CI never installs the renderer.\n\nVerified with the engine interpreter, the same way CI will:\n  depends(tests/requirements.txt)  ->  cmarkgfm==2025.10.22 (wheel)\n  before: 1 passed, 1 skipped (\"cmarkgfm is not installed\")\n  after:  2 passed, 16 subtests passed\nThe oracle agrees with the scanner model on the whole corpus, so `_md_cell`''s\nrule is now checked against the renderer rather than against our model of it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(client-python): document rocketride diff and the pipediff API in the python docs tree\n\nThe Python-SDK half of this PR''s documentation lived in\ndocs/README-python-client.md. The documentation revamp (#2139, 92a1b7e0)\ndeleted that file wholesale as part of moving the client docs into\ndocs/public/{python,typescript}/, and this branch''s merge of develop took the\ndeletion without relocating the pipe-diff content. The result was a regression\nagainst the state this PR was reviewed in: `rocketride diff` and the importable\n`rocketride.pipediff` package had no page anywhere under the new docs tree, and\nnothing in the Python client''s own docs folder mentioned them.\n\nAdd docs/public/python/pipe-diff.md as a Python-only site page (sidebar_position\n14, the slot after otel-bridge at 13) carrying the material that used to live in\nthe deleted README: install/quickstart, the flag table and exit codes, what\ncounts as a change, the human/JSON/Markdown output modes, the CI recipe that\npoints at the bundled composite action, and a reference of every name exported\nfrom `rocketride.pipediff` (engine, data model, reporters) plus troubleshooting\nand limitations. The three sample outputs are the real renderers'' output,\nreproduced byte for byte.\n\nWire it into the docs spine the same way otel-bridge is wired:\n\n- scripts/validate-client-docs.py: declare `pipe-diff` in PY_ONLY_PAGES. This is\n  required — an undeclared page in one client folder and not the other fails the\n  site-page-set parity check.\n- docs/development/clients/readme-schema.md: the schema doc enumerates the\n  declared single-language extras that the validator constant mirrors, so it\n  would otherwise name otel-bridge as Python''s only extra.\n- docs/public/product/clients/index.md: the spine paragraph that lists what the\n  Python package additionally ships would otherwise claim the OTel bridge is the\n  only such surface.\n- docs/public/python/index.mdx: a side card next to the OTel bridge card.\n- docs/public/product/connect/cli.mdx: a pointer from the `diff` section to the\n  in-depth guide. The CLI reference keeps its own flag table; the guide is the\n  one that also documents the importable API.\n\nNo behaviour change.\n\nVerification: `python3 scripts/validate-client-docs.py` ok (6 README sections,\n13 shared site pages, symbol parity); `node scripts/build.js docs:test\ndocs:build docs:check` exit 0 (250 pages staged, docs exports in sync);\n`ruff check` / `ruff format --check` clean on the touched .py file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(client-python): say the cmark-gfm oracle runs in CI, not that it is optional\n\nasclearuc asked for the cmark-gfm oracle to actually execute in CI rather than\nskip, so the backslash-escaping question is settled by the renderer instead of\nby our model of it. That was done by adding `cmarkgfm` to\npackages/client-python/tests/requirements.txt, which the\n`client-python:setup-test-deps` step installs into the engine interpreter the\nsuite runs on. The prose around the test was never updated and still described\nthe renderer as an optional local nicety:\n\n- `_md_cell`''s docstring said the round-trip was \"cross-checked against\n  ``cmarkgfm`` when that optional renderer is installed\";\n- the `skipIf` reason pointed only at `pip install .[test]`, so a reader of a\n  skip line had no way to tell whether CI had skipped it too;\n- the library-free sibling test read as the primary check rather than the\n  fallback.\n\nRewrite all four strings (the guarded import comment, both test docstrings, and\nthe skip reason) to state where the renderer comes from in CI and what a skip\ntherefore means: a local interpreter without it, never the CI leg. The\nlibrary-free `_split_gfm_row` check is named as the fallback over the same\ncorpus; the renderer test is named as the one that decides the rule.\n\nComments only -- no behaviour change, and the skip condition itself is\nunchanged.\n\nVerification: `./dist/server/engine -m pytest\npackages/client-python/tests/test_pipediff_reporters.py -q` -> \"32 passed in\n0.08s\" (exit 0), with\ntest_gfm_renders_escaped_cells_back_to_the_original_value running rather than\nskipping; `ruff check` / `ruff format --check` clean on both files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(client-python): pin the top-level keys diff_pipes reads and ignores\n\nAuthor-side finding, not a reviewer ask. `diff_pipes` reads exactly three\ntop-level keys of a `.pipe`: `components`, `version` and `viewport`. Every other\ntop-level key the schema defines -- `project_id`, `isLocked`, and anything a\nlater schema adds -- is ignored on purpose, because it is editor/session\nmetadata rather than pipeline behaviour. That rule is load-bearing for the exit\ncode (it is why opening a pipeline in the editor and saving it does not fail a\nCI gate) and it was implicit: nothing asserted it, and nothing stated it. A\nlater contributor widening the read set by one key would turn every\n`project_id` rewrite into a gate failure, with no test to catch it.\n\nPin it:\n\n- tests/test_pipediff_engine.py: `test_unknown_top_level_keys_are_ignored`\n  changes `project_id`, `isLocked`, a `name`, and an unknown future key, and\n  asserts an empty diff under both `include_layout=False` and `True`;\n  `test_version_is_compared_but_viewport_only_under_include_layout` pins the\n  other half -- `version` always counts, `viewport` is enumerated and counts\n  only under the flag.\n- engine.py: one paragraph in the `diff_pipes` docstring stating the read set\n  and naming the test.\n- docs/public/product/connect/cli.mdx and docs/public/python/pipe-diff.md: one\n  bullet each in \"What counts as a change\", so the contract is stated where a\n  user decides whether `diff` is safe to gate on.\n\nNo behaviour change -- the tests characterize what the code already does, so\nthey pass on the pre-change engine. Proof that they bite: with a temporary\nmutation adding `project_id` to the compared set,\ntest_unknown_top_level_keys_are_ignored fails with \"assert [NodeChange(i...\nd_changes=[])] == [] / Left contains one more item: NodeChange(id=''__project__'',\nkind=''config'', ...)\" -- \"1 failed, 61 deselected in 0.04s\" (log\nimpl-1607-toplevel-pin-mutation.log). The mutation was reverted before this\ncommit.\n\nVerification: the four pipediff/CLI modules through the bundled engine ->\n\"133 passed in 0.35s\" (exit 0, was 131 before the two new tests); `ruff check` /\n`ruff format --check` clean on both .py files; `node scripts/build.js docs:test\ndocs:build docs:check` exit 0; `python3 scripts/validate-client-docs.py` ok.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(pipediff): pin note and two accuracy clauses on the Python guide\n\nThe CI snippet on the Python guide carried no pin caveat, unlike its two\nsiblings in cli.mdx and examples/pipe-diff-example.md, so a reader landing\nhere was not told that `@develop` is a placeholder. Add the one sentence.\n\nTwo accuracy clauses in the same page: the `--git` row now says a file that\nis absent in the ref also reports a version change from `null` (the CLI\nsubstitutes an empty pipeline), and the `resolve_git_ref` row names the\nremaining `PipeDiffError` case — contents at the ref that are not a valid\npipeline.\n\n\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nihalnihalani <24360630+nihalnihalani@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"c3c8b53de0af80283591307b8a26fd6db1e71a07","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/c3c8b53de0af80283591307b8a26fd6db1e71a07"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/88f0966732dfab0ea313e9f79e0e665ee8cbef6a","verification":{"payload":"tree c3c8b53de0af80283591307b8a26fd6db1e71a07\nparent 38d68be2df0866b0539395baf2596bba55973ac9\nauthor NIHAL NIHALANI <nihal.nihalani@gmail.com> 1790002678 +0530\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790002678 +0300\n\nfeat(sdk,ci): ''rocketride diff'' — semantic .pipe diff (layout-noise-free) + PR-comment Action (#1607)\n\n* feat(sdk): rocketride.pipediff — semantic diff engine for .pipe files (#1606)\n\nA raw `git diff` of a `.pipe` file is dominated by canvas coordinate\nchurn: every node carries a `ui` block whose `position.x`/`position.y`\nmove whenever the node is nudged, plus a top-level `viewport` that\nshifts on pan or zoom. Those lines say nothing about behavior, yet they\nbury the changes that do — a swapped LLM provider, a re-tuned chunk\nsize, a rewired retrieval step.\n\n`rocketride.pipediff` compares two parsed `.pipe` documents instead of\ntheir text. Components are matched by `id`; the result is grouped into\nnodes (added / removed / re-provisioned), edges (reconstructed from each\ncomponent''s `input[]` data lanes and `control[]` orchestration lanes),\nand config (deep-diffed into dotted paths such as\n`config.default.strlen`). Layout is collapsed to a single \"changed\" flag\nunless the caller asks for it, and JSON values are compared with JSON\ntyping so `1` and `true` are never conflated.\n\nThe package is import-only and does no I/O beyond reading the files it\nis given: `gitref` shells out to `git show <ref>:<file>` for the\nref-vs-worktree case and maps every failure mode onto an explicit error,\nand `reporters` renders the same diff as human text, stable sorted JSON,\nor Markdown with `|` and backticks escaped so untrusted values cannot\nbreak a PR comment.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(cli): add `rocketride diff` subcommand (#1606)\n\nWires `rocketride.pipediff` onto the CLI as `rocketride diff`, the\nreview half of the \"pipelines as code\" loop: `validate` says a `.pipe`\nis well-formed, `diff` says what changed in it.\n\nThe subcommand is deliberately the odd one out in `setup_parser`: it is\nlocal only — it never contacts an engine or the network — so it does not\ntake the shared `--uri`/`--apikey`/`--token` connection arguments every\nother command gets from `_add_connection_args`, and its `--json` is a\nplain format flag rather than the shared `--json [FILE]`. It compares\ntwo files positionally, or a single working-tree file against a git ref\nvia `--git REF`. `--markdown` renders a PR-comment-friendly report,\n`--include-layout` opts the canvas churn back in, and `--exit-zero`\nturns a gating run into an informational one.\n\nExit codes are the CI contract: 0 for no semantic changes (or any\nsuccessful run under `--exit-zero`), 1 when semantic changes are found,\nand 2 for a usage error, an unreadable or unparseable file, or a bad git\nref.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(ci): add pipe-diff composite action — semantic `.pipe` diff as a sticky PR comment (#1606)\n\nBecause `rocketride diff` is local and exits non-zero on change, it\ndrops straight into a pull-request check. This composite action does the\nassembly: it installs the CLI, resolves the PR base commit (fetching it\nitself, so the default `fetch-depth: 1` checkout is fine), finds every\nchanged `.pipe` file, diffs each against the base, and maintains one\nsticky comment so reviewers see \"OpenAI → Anthropic, chunk size\n512 → 1024, retrieval rewired through a reranker\" instead of a wall of\ncoordinate noise.\n\nEvery run also writes the full report to the job summary, which is what\nkeeps the action useful on a fork pull request: GitHub hands those jobs\na read-only `GITHUB_TOKEN` regardless of `permissions:`, so the comment\nAPI returns 403 — the action warns instead of failing and the diff stays\nvisible in the summary. The summary is written on the base-fetch failure\npath too, rather than leaving a run with no output at all.\n\n`install-from` exists because `rocketride diff` ships in a release after\n1.3.0 and PyPI does not serve it yet; it points pip at a checkout or a\npinned commit instead. Markdown assembled from file names and diff\noutput is escaped, and the action distinguishes \"no `.pipe` files\nchanged\" from \"could not determine what changed\" rather than reporting\nthe reassuring answer blind.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: document `rocketride diff` in the CLI reference and examples (#1606)\n\nDocuments the new subcommand where the restructured docs tree puts CLI\ncontent — `docs/public/product/connect/cli.mdx` — as a `### diff`\nsection alongside `### validate`, the other file-oriented, CI-friendly\ncommand. The section covers the flags, the exit-code contract, what does\nand does not count as a semantic change, all three output modes, and the\n`pipe-diff` action recipe including the fork-PR token caveat and the\npre-release `install-from` workaround.\n\nTwo surrounding statements needed amending rather than just appending:\nthe Python-vs-TypeScript note now records that `diff` is the one\nPython-only command, and the \"Connecting\" preamble now excludes `diff`\nfrom the commands that take connection options, because it takes none.\n\n`examples/pipe-diff-example.md` puts a raw `git diff` next to\n`rocketride diff` on the same edit to `rag-pipeline.pipe`; the examples\nindex gains a Guides entry pointing at it.\n\nNote for reviewers of the earlier revision: this content previously\nlived in `docs/README-python-client.md`,\n`packages/client-python/docs/index.md`, and\n`packages/docs/content-static/cli.mdx`. All three paths were removed by\nthe documentation revamp, so the content is re-homed here instead of\nresurrecting them, and the example''s cross-link now points at the\npublished `/connect/cli#diff` anchor.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(client-python): ship rocketride_common when installing from the source tree (#1607)\n\n`pip install ./packages/client-python` from a clean checkout produced a\n`rocketride` binary that could not start:\n\n    File \".../rocketride/cli/utils/env.py\", line 29, in <module>\n        from rocketride_common.env import (\n    ModuleNotFoundError: No module named ''rocketride_common''\n\n`cli/__init__.py` eagerly imports `.main`, `main.py` imports `.utils.env`\nat module scope, and that imports `rocketride_common` — so every\nsubcommand, `diff` included, dies at import time.\n\n`rocketride_common` is a source library shared with the TypeScript client\nrather than a published distribution, so it cannot be added to\n`dependencies`. It lives at `packages/client-common/python/src` and the\nbuilder copies it into the staging tree (`client-python:wheel-source`)\nbefore the released wheel is built — which is why the published wheel has\nalways been fine and only the source tree is broken. The source tree is\nalso exactly what the new pipe-diff action''s `install-from` input points\nat, in both documented forms (`./packages/client-python` and\n`git+https://…#subdirectory=packages/client-python`, where pip clones the\nwhole repository), so the documented pre-release recipe could never work.\n\nAdding the sibling source root to `packages.find.where` makes setuptools\nfind the shared package in the source-tree layout. setuptools skips a\n`where` entry whose directory does not exist, so the builder''s staged\nlayout — where `rocketride_common` is already inside `src/` and there is\nno sibling directory — is untouched. Verified end to end: from a clean\n`git archive` tree `rocketride diff --help` now exits 0, and rebuilding\nthe staged layout through `python -m build` still yields an sdist and a\nwheel carrying both `rocketride` and `rocketride_common`, byte-identical\nin top-level contents to before.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): diff .pipe files against the merge base, not the base branch tip (#1607)\n\nThe action took `github.event.pull_request.base.sha` -- the tip of the\nbase branch at event time -- and used it directly for both\n`git diff \"${BASE_SHA}\" HEAD` and `rocketride diff --git \"${BASE_SHA}\"`.\nThat tip keeps moving as other pull requests merge, so every commit\nmerged into the base since this branch forked was reported as a change\nmade by THIS pull request, and reported backwards: those commits sit on\nthe left-hand side of the diff, so a node someone else added shows up as\na node this PR deleted.\n\nReproduced in a scratch repository -- fork at C0, this branch adds C1\ntouching `a.pipe`, the base then gains C2 adding `b.pipe`:\n\n    git diff --name-status $C2 HEAD     ->  M a.pipe / D b.pipe   (wrong)\n    git diff --name-status $(git merge-base $C2 HEAD) HEAD\n                                        ->  M a.pipe             (right)\n\nThe action now resolves `git merge-base \"${BASE_SHA}\" HEAD` once and\nfeeds that to the changed-file enumeration, the deletion enumeration and\nevery per-file `rocketride diff --git`. This is the comparison GitHub''s\nown \"Files changed\" tab shows, and the same one the action''s README\nalready recommended in its manual recipe.\n\nFetch depth had to change with it. The base commit was fetched with\n`--depth=1`, which leaves it with no ancestry for `merge-base` to walk,\nso that flag is gone. A default `fetch-depth: 1` checkout also leaves\nHEAD with no ancestry, so when the first `merge-base` attempt comes back\nempty the action deepens (`--unshallow`, falling back to `--deepen`) and\nretries; a workflow that already checks out with `fetch-depth: 0` never\npays for that. Verified against a real depth-1 clone: the first attempt\nreturns nothing, the deepen recovers, and the merge base resolves to the\nfork point.\n\nIf the merge base still cannot be computed the step now fails with a\nreport and an annotation pointing at `fetch-depth: 0`, rather than\nfalling back to the base tip -- silently diffing the wrong thing is the\ndefect being fixed.\n\nThe action README and the CLI reference are updated to describe the\nmerge-base comparison and the optional `fetch-depth: 0`.\n\nVerified: action.yml parses as YAML, `bash -n` and shellcheck pass on all\nfour `run:` blocks.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(client-python): assert the Markdown table escaping without cmarkgfm (#1607)\n\n`test_gfm_renders_escaped_cells_back_to_the_original_value` was guarded by\n`@unittest.skipIf(cmarkgfm is None)` and `cmarkgfm` was in no dependency\ngroup, so the one test that decides whether `_md_cell`''s backslash\nhandling is right never ran — not locally, not in CI.\n\nDeclaring the dependency is not enough on its own: CI does not `pip\ninstall` this package''s extras at all. `builder test` runs\n`client-python:run-pytest`, which invokes pytest through the bundled\nengine binary in its own isolated environment (packages/client-python/\nscripts/tasks.js -> scripts/lib/pytest.js), so an entry under\n`[project.optional-dependencies] test` would still leave the test\nskipped on every CI run.\n\nSo the coverage now comes from an assertion that needs no C extension.\n`_split_gfm_row` applies cmark-gfm''s own table-row rule directly: a `|`\nsplits the row only when no backslash precedes it, and a `|` preceded by\na run of n backslashes is escaped and loses exactly one of them. The new\n`test_escaped_cells_survive_gfm_row_splitting` runs that over the same\nvalue corpus — which both tests now share — and asserts three columns and\nan exact round-trip to the original value.\n\nThat rule was derived from cmark-gfm rather than from the spec prose, and\nthen checked against it: comparing `_split_gfm_row` with `cmarkgfm` over\nseveral thousand random values built from the punctuation that shows up\nin pipeline config produced zero disagreements. Mutation-checked too —\nmaking `_md_cell` double the backslash instead of adding one fails six of\nthe eight corpus values.\n\nThe cmarkgfm test is kept as an oracle rather than deleted: it is the\nstronger check when the library is present, it now shares the corpus, and\n`cmarkgfm` is added to the `test` extra so `pip install .[test]` enables\nit instead of leaving the guard permanently dead. Its skip message says\nwhich check still covers the rules.\n\nVerified: 131 passed with cmarkgfm installed; 130 passed and 1 skipped\n(only the oracle) without it. Ruff check and format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci: run the pipe-diff action on this repo and pin-check composite actions (#1607)\n\nTwo gaps, both of which let the new action ship unexercised.\n\nNothing referenced the action. No workflow under `.github/workflows/`\nmentioned pipe-diff, so the install step, the base resolution, the\n`rocketride diff` invocation and the comment step had never run as a\nGitHub Action — the first consumer to adopt it would have been the one to\nfind out. `.github/workflows/pipe-diff.yml` runs it on `pull_request`\nagainst the repository''s own 45 `.pipe` files, and the paths filter also\nfires on changes to the action, the diff engine and the CLI subcommand,\nso a change to any of them is exercised before it merges even when no\npipeline changed.\n\nIt is `pull_request`, not `pull_request_target`, for the reason the\naction''s own README gives: a `pull_request_target` job checks out the\nbase branch (nothing to diff) and holds a writable token, and this\nworkflow installs the CLI from the checkout. On the `pull_request` event\na fork''s code runs with a read-only token and no secrets, and the report\nlands in the job summary — the documented fork behaviour. `install-from`\npoints at the checkout because PyPI does not serve `rocketride diff`\nyet; the comment says to switch to `cli-version` once it does.\n\nSecond, `scripts/verify-action-pins.mjs` only enumerated\n`.github/workflows/*.y(a)ml`, so the SHAs pinned inside composite actions\nwere outside the pin guard entirely — both this PR''s\n`.github/actions/pipe-diff/action.yml` and the pre-existing\n`.github/actions/validate-pipes/action.yml`. A composite action could\ncarry a mismatched or unpinned `uses:` and CI would pass. The verifier\nnow also enumerates `.github/actions/<name>/action.{yml,yaml}`, treating\na missing directory as an empty list.\n\nVerified: the existing verifier unit tests still pass (7/7); the live\ncheck goes from 26 files to 29 and reports \"Verified 87 action pins\nacross 29 workflow and composite-action files\" with no errors; and\ndrifting the pipe-diff action''s github-script comment from v7 to v6 is\nnow caught with the correct file and line annotation, which it was not\nbefore.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(client-python): run the cmarkgfm escaping oracle in CI\n\n`test_gfm_renders_escaped_cells_back_to_the_original_value` is the test that\ndecides how `_md_cell` escapes a pipe, but `cmarkgfm` was only in the `test`\nextra and CI never installs that extra — it runs pytest on the bundled engine\ninterpreter against the source checkout. So on every CI run the decisive test\nskipped, and the gate was `test_escaped_cells_survive_gfm_row_splitting`,\nwhich resolves the same corpus with our own model of cmark-gfm''s cell\nscanner. Code and test then shared one assumption: if the model were wrong,\nboth would be wrong together and nothing would go red. The values are\nuntrusted `.pipe` content rendered into a PR comment, so a cell that splits\nits row is exactly the failure the escaping exists to prevent.\n\nAdd `cmarkgfm` to `packages/client-python/tests/requirements.txt`, which\n`client-python:setup-test-deps` installs into the engine interpreter through\n`depends()` — the mechanism #1612 added for the otel-bridge tests, so the\nwheel resolves under the same merged constraints as everything else and both\npackages stay on one route. The floor stays `>=2024.1.14`: 2024.11.20\npublishes no macOS wheels, and a tighter pin would make the macOS leg build\ncmark-gfm from source. It resolves to 2025.10.22, which has wheels for every\nCI leg.\n\nThe `pyproject.toml` comment that explained why the extra was not enough now\nnames the route CI actually takes. The library-free check keeps its own\ndocstring but no longer claims CI never installs the renderer.\n\nVerified with the engine interpreter, the same way CI will:\n  depends(tests/requirements.txt)  ->  cmarkgfm==2025.10.22 (wheel)\n  before: 1 passed, 1 skipped (\"cmarkgfm is not installed\")\n  after:  2 passed, 16 subtests passed\nThe oracle agrees with the scanner model on the whole corpus, so `_md_cell`''s\nrule is now checked against the renderer rather than against our model of it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(client-python): document rocketride diff and the pipediff API in the python docs tree\n\nThe Python-SDK half of this PR''s documentation lived in\ndocs/README-python-client.md. The documentation revamp (#2139, 92a1b7e0)\ndeleted that file wholesale as part of moving the client docs into\ndocs/public/{python,typescript}/, and this branch''s merge of develop took the\ndeletion without relocating the pipe-diff content. The result was a regression\nagainst the state this PR was reviewed in: `rocketride diff` and the importable\n`rocketride.pipediff` package had no page anywhere under the new docs tree, and\nnothing in the Python client''s own docs folder mentioned them.\n\nAdd docs/public/python/pipe-diff.md as a Python-only site page (sidebar_position\n14, the slot after otel-bridge at 13) carrying the material that used to live in\nthe deleted README: install/quickstart, the flag table and exit codes, what\ncounts as a change, the human/JSON/Markdown output modes, the CI recipe that\npoints at the bundled composite action, and a reference of every name exported\nfrom `rocketride.pipediff` (engine, data model, reporters) plus troubleshooting\nand limitations. The three sample outputs are the real renderers'' output,\nreproduced byte for byte.\n\nWire it into the docs spine the same way otel-bridge is wired:\n\n- scripts/validate-client-docs.py: declare `pipe-diff` in PY_ONLY_PAGES. This is\n  required — an undeclared page in one client folder and not the other fails the\n  site-page-set parity check.\n- docs/development/clients/readme-schema.md: the schema doc enumerates the\n  declared single-language extras that the validator constant mirrors, so it\n  would otherwise name otel-bridge as Python''s only extra.\n- docs/public/product/clients/index.md: the spine paragraph that lists what the\n  Python package additionally ships would otherwise claim the OTel bridge is the\n  only such surface.\n- docs/public/python/index.mdx: a side card next to the OTel bridge card.\n- docs/public/product/connect/cli.mdx: a pointer from the `diff` section to the\n  in-depth guide. The CLI reference keeps its own flag table; the guide is the\n  one that also documents the importable API.\n\nNo behaviour change.\n\nVerification: `python3 scripts/validate-client-docs.py` ok (6 README sections,\n13 shared site pages, symbol parity); `node scripts/build.js docs:test\ndocs:build docs:check` exit 0 (250 pages staged, docs exports in sync);\n`ruff check` / `ruff format --check` clean on the touched .py file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(client-python): say the cmark-gfm oracle runs in CI, not that it is optional\n\nasclearuc asked for the cmark-gfm oracle to actually execute in CI rather than\nskip, so the backslash-escaping question is settled by the renderer instead of\nby our model of it. That was done by adding `cmarkgfm` to\npackages/client-python/tests/requirements.txt, which the\n`client-python:setup-test-deps` step installs into the engine interpreter the\nsuite runs on. The prose around the test was never updated and still described\nthe renderer as an optional local nicety:\n\n- `_md_cell`''s docstring said the round-trip was \"cross-checked against\n  ``cmarkgfm`` when that optional renderer is installed\";\n- the `skipIf` reason pointed only at `pip install .[test]`, so a reader of a\n  skip line had no way to tell whether CI had skipped it too;\n- the library-free sibling test read as the primary check rather than the\n  fallback.\n\nRewrite all four strings (the guarded import comment, both test docstrings, and\nthe skip reason) to state where the renderer comes from in CI and what a skip\ntherefore means: a local interpreter without it, never the CI leg. The\nlibrary-free `_split_gfm_row` check is named as the fallback over the same\ncorpus; the renderer test is named as the one that decides the rule.\n\nComments only -- no behaviour change, and the skip condition itself is\nunchanged.\n\nVerification: `./dist/server/engine -m pytest\npackages/client-python/tests/test_pipediff_reporters.py -q` -> \"32 passed in\n0.08s\" (exit 0), with\ntest_gfm_renders_escaped_cells_back_to_the_original_value running rather than\nskipping; `ruff check` / `ruff format --check` clean on both files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(client-python): pin the top-level keys diff_pipes reads and ignores\n\nAuthor-side finding, not a reviewer ask. `diff_pipes` reads exactly three\ntop-level keys of a `.pipe`: `components`, `version` and `viewport`. Every other\ntop-level key the schema defines -- `project_id`, `isLocked`, and anything a\nlater schema adds -- is ignored on purpose, because it is editor/session\nmetadata rather than pipeline behaviour. That rule is load-bearing for the exit\ncode (it is why opening a pipeline in the editor and saving it does not fail a\nCI gate) and it was implicit: nothing asserted it, and nothing stated it. A\nlater contributor widening the read set by one key would turn every\n`project_id` rewrite into a gate failure, with no test to catch it.\n\nPin it:\n\n- tests/test_pipediff_engine.py: `test_unknown_top_level_keys_are_ignored`\n  changes `project_id`, `isLocked`, a `name`, and an unknown future key, and\n  asserts an empty diff under both `include_layout=False` and `True`;\n  `test_version_is_compared_but_viewport_only_under_include_layout` pins the\n  other half -- `version` always counts, `viewport` is enumerated and counts\n  only under the flag.\n- engine.py: one paragraph in the `diff_pipes` docstring stating the read set\n  and naming the test.\n- docs/public/product/connect/cli.mdx and docs/public/python/pipe-diff.md: one\n  bullet each in \"What counts as a change\", so the contract is stated where a\n  user decides whether `diff` is safe to gate on.\n\nNo behaviour change -- the tests characterize what the code already does, so\nthey pass on the pre-change engine. Proof that they bite: with a temporary\nmutation adding `project_id` to the compared set,\ntest_unknown_top_level_keys_are_ignored fails with \"assert [NodeChange(i...\nd_changes=[])] == [] / Left contains one more item: NodeChange(id=''__project__'',\nkind=''config'', ...)\" -- \"1 failed, 61 deselected in 0.04s\" (log\nimpl-1607-toplevel-pin-mutation.log). The mutation was reverted before this\ncommit.\n\nVerification: the four pipediff/CLI modules through the bundled engine ->\n\"133 passed in 0.35s\" (exit 0, was 131 before the two new tests); `ruff check` /\n`ruff format --check` clean on both .py files; `node scripts/build.js docs:test\ndocs:build docs:check` exit 0; `python3 scripts/validate-client-docs.py` ok.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(pipediff): pin note and two accuracy clauses on the Python guide\n\nThe CI snippet on the Python guide carried no pin caveat, unlike its two\nsiblings in cli.mdx and examples/pipe-diff-example.md, so a reader landing\nhere was not told that `@develop` is a placeholder. Add the one sentence.\n\nTwo accuracy clauses in the same page: the `--git` row now says a file that\nis absent in the ref also reports a version change from `null` (the CLI\nsubstitutes an empty pipeline), and the `resolve_git_ref` row names the\nremaining `PipeDiffError` case — contents at the ref that are not a valid\npipeline.\n\n\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nihalnihalani <24360630+nihalnihalani@users.noreply.github.com>","re