wMDRkZjZkZTZmZDlmN2Q3ZmY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/e23e66ea3d0152a99299af9983c96f0d0996b51f","sha":"e23e66ea3d0152a99299af9983c96f0d0996b51f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/e23e66ea3d0152a99299af9983c96f0d0996b51f"}],"sha":"23393411e70fbb3bc937a1004df6de6fd9f7d7ff","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/23393411e70fbb3bc937a1004df6de6fd9f7d7ff"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24360630?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/nihalnihalani","id":24360630,"login":"nihalnihalani","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzYwNjMw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nihalnihalani","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/e23e66ea3d0152a99299af9983c96f0d0996b51f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T19:33:16Z","email":"nihal.nihalani@gmail.com","name":"NIHAL NIHALANI"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T19:33:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(ai,nodes): surface database error text from execute and add refresh_schema tool (#2283)\n\n* fix(ai,nodes): surface database error text from execute and add refresh_schema tool\n\nTwo gaps in the shared database node base, both scoped to stay out of the\nway of the pull requests already open against this file.\n\n_executeRawQuery caught every SQLAlchemyError, logged it, and returned\nNone, so execute() answered with the same ''check server logs for details''\nstring whether the statement had a typo, named a missing table, or was\nrefused for permissions. Its except clause now raises with the driver''s own\nmessage through IGlobal._format_db_error. Only that clause changes:\nexecute()''s `if result is None` branch becomes unreachable but is left in\nplace, with a comment saying so, because an open PR is rewriting that\nregion. Session-bound execute already re-raised the real exception, so the\ntwo paths finally agree, and the max_execute_rows rollback is untouched.\n\nIGlobal.db_schema is assigned exactly once, in beginGlobal, so get_schema\nserves a task-start snapshot for the life of the task and a table created\nthrough execute stays invisible until the pipeline restarts. The new\nrefresh_schema tool re-reflects and replaces it, returning the get_schema\nshape plus a refreshed_at UTC timestamp so a caller can show how current\nthe schema it holds is. Reflection and publication run under a module-level\nlock: concurrent callers would otherwise repeat the same full table walk\nand race to publish the result. The tool is additive and lives entirely in\ndb_instance_base.py, since another open PR is adding to db_global_base.py.\nIt also fixes the natural-language path, which describes that same dict to\nthe LLM. get_schema''s nested table formatter is lifted to a shared private\nmethod so both tools render one shape.\n\nTests are a new module rather than an addition to test_db_base.py, which\ntwo open PRs are both appending to. Eleven cases over an in-memory SQLite\nengine cover the driver text reaching the caller, the gate still refusing\nfirst, re-reflection seeing created and dropped tables, the timestamp\nformat, and four concurrent refreshes all returning.\n\nTool tables in the four node READMEs that inherit this surface list\nrefresh_schema and say in one sentence what get_schema''s snapshot does not\nshow. services.json declares no tool list, so nothing to mirror there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n\n* test(ai): give the execute-session IGlobal stub a real _format_db_error\n\nexecute()''s stateless failure path now formats the driver error through\nIGlobal, but this module builds IGlobal as a SimpleNamespace carrying only\nthe four fields the transaction tests needed. Nothing failed, because no\ntest here drove a bad statement through that path — the next one to try\nwould have died with AttributeError instead of the RuntimeError it was\nasserting.\n\nThe stub borrows the real DatabaseGlobalBase implementation rather than a\nlambda, so the message a test sees is the message a caller gets. A test for\nthe stateless failure path comes with it, so the fixture stays honest:\nremoving the one line makes it fail with AttributeError.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n\n* refactor(ai): shrink the refresh_schema diff in db_instance_base\n\nNo behaviour change; this only narrows what the previous commit touched in\na file three open pull requests are editing.\n\nget_schema''s body is back to exactly what develop has, docstring aside: the\nnested _format_table closure returns, and the _format_table static method\nand _schemaPayload helper it had been lifted into are gone. Sharing them\nwas tidier, but it rewrote a method none of the open PRs needs to change,\nand a few duplicated formatting lines cost less than that. That hunk is now\nfive added docstring lines instead of a 31-line rewrite.\n\nrefresh_schema moves to the end of the tool section, below dialect, so the\nwhole tool arrives as one appended block past the execute body PR #1908\nrewrites rather than as an insertion ahead of it. It formats its own tables\ninline, and the lock is now _REFLECT_LOCK.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n\n* docs(nodes): complete the agent tool contract in the db READMEs\n\nTwo review findings on the READMEs this PR touched, both in the section\nthe node README schema calls the agent-facing contract and holds to the\nhighest accuracy bar.\n\ndb_postgres listed four tool functions in the summary and in \"As a tool\",\nthen a separate table of three transaction tools — seven of the nine\n@tool_function methods an agent actually sees on DatabaseInstanceBase.\n`execute` appeared only as prose inside the transaction paragraph and\n`dialect` only as the QuestionType.DIALECT lane behaviour, so an agent\nauthor reading the inventory would not know either exists as a tool. The\ncount is now nine in both places, `dialect` joins the open table, and\n`execute` joins what was the Transactions table, renamed \"Raw SQL and\ntransactions\" because a statement without a session_id is neither. That\ntable''s gate sentence also said requests are \"silently dropped\" when\nallow_execute is off; the four tools raise (db_instance_base.py:301,\n339, 371), and it is the questions lane that logs and drops — the two\nbehaviours are now stated apart. The `execute` row records the driver\nmessage and the rollback-on-overflow that this PR''s own change produced,\nrather than implying a truncated result set.\n\nrocketride_sql named refresh_schema in its tool table but left its\ncontract out of the prose beneath, where that README already documents\nevery other non-obvious argument and return shape. It takes no arguments\n(input_schema declares no properties) and returns {database, tables}\nexactly as get_schema does, plus a refreshed_at UTC ISO-8601 timestamp\nfrom datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat().\n\nDocs only; no behaviour change. Both nodes still pass\nscripts/validate-node-readme.py, and so does the whole corpus under\n./builder docs:validate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): keep SQL statements and bind parameters out of execute error text\n\n`_format_db_error` only produced a clean `Error <code>: <msg>` when the\ndriver put an integer first in `orig.args` — the pymysql and\nclickhouse-driver shape. psycopg2 and sqlite3 put a string there, so both\nfell through to `str(exc)`, which for a SQLAlchemy StatementError is the\nfull repr: the executed statement in `[SQL: ...]` and the values bound\ninto it in `[parameters: ...]`.\n\nThat string is no longer log-only. `_executeRawQuery` re-raises it as a\nRuntimeError that the `execute` tool returns to its caller, and #2282\nwires a user-facing Refresh/Run UI onto exactly that path, so a failed\nINSERT would hand the caller back the row it tried to write.\n\nUnwrap each driver shape explicitly and keep only the primary message:\nthe numeric-code branch as before; psycopg2''s `diag.message_primary`\n(its `str()` also carries a `LINE n:` echo of the statement, which\npsycopg2 has already interpolated the bind values into); and `args[0]`\nfor sqlite3 and the generic DBAPI shape. Every branch then runs through\n`_strip_statement_detail`, which truncates at the first `[SQL:`,\n`[parameters:`, `LINE n:`, `DETAIL:`/`HINT:`/`CONTEXT:` or sqlalche.me\nmarker, so a driver shape not enumerated here still cannot leak the\ntail. DETAIL is dropped along with the rest because PostgreSQL uses it\nto restate the offending key values.\n\nThe helper is module-level rather than a method so `_format_db_error`\nkeeps working when tests bind it onto a stub IGlobal.\n\nTests cover the real sqlite3 driver end-to-end through `execute`, plus\nthe psycopg2 (with and without `diag`), sqlite3 and pymysql shapes and a\nbare StatementError repr.\n`test_format_db_error_falls_back_to_str_when_args_not_int_first` pinned\nthe old leaking fallback, so it is replaced by an assertion that the\ndriver message — not `str(exc)` — is what comes back.\n\n`_strip_statement_detail`''s degenerate case gets the same treatment. A\nmessage that is nothing but detail markers left no primary sentence, and\nthe old fallback returned the first line — which on that input is the\n`[SQL: ...]` echo the function exists to remove. It now returns a\nneutral constant, so there is no input on which the helper hands back the\nstatement.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): refresh the answers-lane column map in refresh_schema\n\n`refresh_schema` replaced `IGlobal.db_schema`, the database-wide\nreflection the natural-language path describes to the LLM, and stopped\nthere. `IGlobal.schema` — the configured table''s column map that\n`_insertData` iterates to build every answers-lane INSERT — is a second\nstart-up snapshot, and it was left untouched, rebuilt only lazily when\nfalsy.\n\nSo the node was current on one path and stale on the other: after\nrunning `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN` and calling the refresh tool, the\nLLM path saw the new column while the insert lane kept writing the\nstart-up column set and silently dropped it. The four db node READMEs\ndocument both the insert lane and \"re-reads the schema from the\ndatabase\", so the documented contract overstated the code.\n\nInvalidate the map inside the same reflection lock. Emptying it re-arms\nthe lazy rebuild already at the top of `_insertData`, which reflects\nthrough the same `_getTableSchema` call `beginGlobal` uses, so the next\ninsert sees what a freshly started node would. Emptying rather than\nre-reflecting here keeps the call cheap for a node with no answers lane\nwired, and makes the write a single atomic rebind: a concurrent\n`_insertData` either reads the old map or finds it falsy and rebuilds,\nnever a half-built dict.\n\nInvalidating the map is only safe once `_insertData` stops binding a\ngenerated primary key, so that is fixed in the same commit.\n`_createTableFromData` curates `IGlobal.schema` down to the data columns\nfor an auto-created table, because the `id` it prepends is generated by\nthe database; a map built by reflection carries `id` instead. Since\n`_insertData` binds NULL for any schema column the rows do not supply,\nswapping the curated map for a reflected one would have started binding\n`id=None` on every later insert — tolerated by SQLite''s rowid alias, a\nnot-null violation against the `id SERIAL NOT NULL` Postgres renders for\nthe same column. `_insertData` now drops primary-key columns that no row\nin the batch supplies, decided once per batch so every mapping handed to\nexecutemany keeps an identical key set. The two maps therefore produce\nthe same INSERT and refreshing cannot change answers-lane behaviour\nmid-task. It also fixes the same NULL bind on the path that always\nreflected: a table that already existed when `beginGlobal` ran.\n\nTests cover the refreshed insert picking up a new column, the\npre-existing stale behaviour it fixes, a dropped configured table leaving\na falsy map, and — by asserting on the compiled INSERT rather than on\nwhat SQLite tolerates — that an auto-created table''s insert still carries\nthe data columns only after a refresh, that a reflected primary key the\nrows do not supply is omitted, that one they do supply is still bound,\nand that a primary-key-only table falls back to binding it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): describe the raw-SQL gate the db nodes actually have\n\nThe `Raw SQL and transactions` section said `allow_execute` is \"the same\ngate as `QuestionType.EXECUTE`\" and contrasted the tools with \"the\n`questions` lane, which logs and drops the request\". No SQL database\nnode implements that branch: `DatabaseInstanceBase.writeQuestions` does\nnot look at `question.type` at all, and `QuestionType` appears in\ndb_instance_base.py only at the import and in one unrelated\n`Question(type=QuestionType.QUESTION, ...)` construction. The\ndispatch-on-type behaviour belongs to the graph node\n(`graph_instance_base.py` handles `DIALECT` and `EXECUTE`), and the\nwording was carried across without being checked against this file.\n\nCorrect it wherever it appears in prose:\n\n- db_postgres: the \"Two special question types are handled on the\n  questions lane\" bullets are removed — the lane has one behaviour,\n  translate-then-execute, and `dialect` / `execute` are the tool\n  functions that reach those behaviours. The gate sentence now says\n  what `allow_execute` gates (the four raw-SQL tools) and that it does\n  not change the questions lane, which never runs raw SQL.\n- rocketride_sql: same two claims, in the config and Notes sections.\n\nAlso make the refresh_schema wording match the code now that the tool\ninvalidates the answers-lane column map as well as the database-wide\nschema, so \"re-reads the schema from the database\" is true end to end\nrather than only for the LLM path.\n\nThe same claim lived in the generated config tables, whose source of\ntruth is the `allow_execute` field description in each node''s\nservices*.json. Those five descriptions now name the four tool functions\nthe flag actually gates, and the generated regions are regenerated from\nthem, so db_postgres/README.md no longer contradicts itself between its\nprose and its parameter table. Only the SQL nodes are touched: the graph\nnodes do dispatch on `QuestionType.EXECUTE`, so their copy is correct as\nit stands.\n\nTwo smaller instances of the same transposition: the new db_postgres\nsentence named `db_neo4j`, a retired path that redirects to\n`graph_neo4j`, and db_clickhouse/IInstance.py said its dialect string is\n\"surfaced via QuestionType.DIALECT\" when only the `dialect` tool reaches\nit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): apply the same execute error contract to the session path\n\nA raw statement run with a session_id went through\nTransactionRegistry.execute, which has no try/except, and the existing\n`except Exception` arm in execute() re-raised the SQLAlchemy exception\nunchanged. str() of that exception ends in `[SQL: ...]` and\n`[parameters: ...]`, so the same tool behind the same allow_execute gate\nreturned the statement and its bound values to the caller purely because\na session id was passed, while the sessionless half stripped them.\n\nAdd a `SQLAlchemyError` arm above the existing `except Exception` (the\nmax-rows RuntimeError is not a SQLAlchemyError, so its wording and its\nrollback are untouched): log the full exception, release the session, and\nraise `RuntimeError(''SQL execution failed: '' + _format_db_error(e))`.\nThe fix lives in execute() rather than tx_registry because the registry\nhas no IGlobal and is shared with other callers.\n\nSession release moves into _releaseFailedSession, shared by both arms.\nA rollback that itself fails is logged and swallowed: previously an\nexception raised inside the cleanup replaced the error the caller was\nbeing told about (a monkeypatched rollback surfaced as ''boom'').\n\nAlso drop the dead `result is None` branch in execute() and correct\n_executeRawQuery''s annotation to `-> dict`: the method has returned the\nshaped dict or raised since it started carrying the driver''s message, so\nthe branch could only ever have produced the opaque ''check server logs\nfor details'' string this work exists to replace, and the annotation\ncontradicted the comment above the branch. execute() is its only caller.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(ai): share one table formatter between get_schema and refresh_schema\n\nrefresh_schema rebuilt get_schema''s nested _format_table line for line,\nwith `result` renamed to `entry`. Both tools promise callers the same\nper-table shape -- their tool descriptions say so, and an LLM picks\nbetween them on that sentence -- but the equality test compared the two\ncopies to each other, so it would have stayed green while a later change\nadded a key (an index list, a column comment, a nullable flag) to one\ncopy only.\n\nLift the formatter to a module-level _format_table and call it from\nboth; get_schema''s body loses the nested definition, refresh_schema\nloses the loop. The wire format is byte-identical, so the existing shape\ntests pass untouched. The docstring paragraph calling the duplication\ndeliberate goes with it.\n\ntest_refresh_schema_matches_get_schema_shape now reflects a table with\nboth a primary key and a foreign key, so the two optional entries are\nactually exercised on both sides, and _format_table gains a direct unit\ntest for the optional-key rules an equality check cannot see.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): make the lazy schema rebuild safe for concurrent inserts\n\nrefresh_schema empties IGlobal.schema to re-arm the rebuild at the top of\n_insertData, which is what makes this reachable at runtime: a second\ninsert can now arrive while the first is reflecting.\n\n_getTableSchema published `self.schema = {}` and then grew it column by\ncolumn, and _insertData checked, rebuilt and then took a reference to\nthat same dict without a lock. A concurrent insert could therefore see\nthe empty map (falling into the \"no schema cached\" branch, which binds\nthe item''s raw keys and skips the generated-key handling), or a truthy\nbut half-built one (silently dropping columns the caller had supplied),\nor a dict changing size while the per-row loop iterated it. A reflection\nthat failed midway left the half-built map published for the next insert\nto believe.\n\n_getTableSchema now builds into a local dict and publishes it with one\nassignment, so a failure leaves the previous map untouched. _insertData\nholds _REFLECT_LOCK across the falsy check, the rebuild and a snapshot\ncopy, and builds the batch from the snapshot; the lock is released before\nthe Table reflection and before the INSERT, neither of which reads the\nmap. The comment above refresh_schema''s invalidation no longer claims\nthe single rebind makes the rebuild atomic.\n\nTests hold a rebuild open inside the real column walk rather than\nsimulating the window: without the lock the second insert stored NULL in\na column it had supplied, an overlapping refresh left a one-column map,\nand a failed reflection published a partial one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): decide generated primary keys per row, not per batch\n\nThe answers lane worked out which primary-key columns to leave unbound\nonce per batch, from the union of every row''s keys. One row carrying\n`id` therefore put `id` into every mapping, and the rows that omitted it\nbound NULL into a key the database was supposed to generate. SQLite\ntolerates that for a rowid alias; Postgres rejects the whole batch for an\n`id SERIAL NOT NULL` column. The sole-primary-key table had the same\nproblem by construction: the `omit_columns = set()` fallback bound NULL\nbecause omitting everything would have left an empty mapping.\n\nWhich primary keys the database generates is now read from the reflected\nTable -- `autoincrement_column` (SQLAlchemy''s own resolution, covering a\nreflected `autoincrement=True` and the `''auto''` rule that recognises\nSQLite''s rowid alias), plus any primary-key column with a server_default,\nan Identity, or an explicit autoincrement -- rather than assumed of every\nprimary key. Per row: a generated key the row omits is left out of that\nrow''s mapping, a supplied value is bound, and an explicitly supplied None\nis bound as NULL, so the three cases stay distinguishable. A primary key\nthe database does NOT generate and the row does not carry is a ValueError\nnaming the table, the columns and the row position, raised before the\ntransaction opens.\n\nRows are then executed as contiguous runs sharing a key set, all inside\none engine.begin(): contiguous rather than gathered, because generated\nids follow insertion order and regrouping would hand back ids in an order\nthe caller''s rows never had, and one transaction because a batch must\nstay all-or-nothing as a single executemany was. A run whose mapping is\nempty gets a values-less `insert(table)` per row, which SQLAlchemy\nrenders as `DEFAULT VALUES` on PostgreSQL and SQLite and `() VALUES ()`\non MySQL.\n\ntest_insert_binds_the_primary_key_when_it_is_the_only_column pinned the\nold fallback and is replaced by the default-values test, plus a\ncompile-only check of all three dialect renderings (no live PostgreSQL or\nMySQL in this suite).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ai): pin the caller-facing error policy for Postgres and MySQL\n\nCodeRabbit''s security finding asks for a generic message or a stable\ncategory instead of the driver''s own words. That is not adopted: the\nmaintainer''s review endorses the primary-message contract precisely\nbecause \"no such column: foo\" is what lets a caller fix their statement.\nWhat was missing was the policy written down and held in place by tests.\n\n_format_db_error''s docstring now states the residual it accepts: a\ndriver''s primary sentence may quote a value from the statement being\nreported (Postgres `invalid input syntax for type integer: \"abc\"`, MySQL\n`Duplicate entry ''x'' for key ''users.email''`). That value was submitted by\nthe caller now reading the message, who also holds allow_execute; values\nbelonging to other rows appear only in DETAIL / HINT / CONTEXT, which are\nstripped. Blanking quoted tokens generically is rejected because MySQL\nquotes identifiers with single quotes too, so the redaction would delete\n\"which column\" from the message that most needs it.\n\nThe tests build real DBAPIError wrappers via DBAPIError.instance, so\nstr(exc) genuinely carries the `[SQL: ...]` / `[parameters: ...]` tail and\nthe assertions bite: a Postgres duplicate-key whose DETAIL restates the\nemail, the same without .diag so the LINE echo is exercised, the MySQL\nduplicate entry that documents the accepted residual, and the MySQL\nunknown-column that documents why redaction is refused. One more drives\nboth shapes through BOTH execute paths and asserts the two messages are\nidentical -- against the pre-fix source the session-bound half raised the\nraw DBAPIError with the bound ''ada@example.com'' attached.\n\nMock-based by necessity: no live PostgreSQL or MySQL in this suite, so\nthe driver exception shapes are reproduced rather than provoked. No\nbehaviour change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): correct the lane, cache and error prose in the four SQL READMEs\n\nAll four checked against the node code and services.json rather than\nagainst each other.\n\ndb_clickhouse declares only a `questions` lane (services.json\n`lanes: {questions: [...]}`), so the sentence about schema refresh\nreaching the `answers` insert lane described a lane the node does not\nhave; it is removed. db_mysql, db_postgres and rocketride_sql do declare\n`answers`, so theirs stays — reworded, because refresh_schema\ninvalidates that column map rather than rebuilding it, and the rebuild\nhappens on the next insert.\n\n\"Return the schema reflected when the node started\" was wrong in all\nfour after refresh_schema shipped: get_schema serves whatever snapshot\nthe node currently holds, which is the start-up reflection until a\nrefresh replaces it.\n\ndb_postgres said every question on the `questions` lane is translated to\nSQL and executed. When the LLM reports the question is not a database\nquery (`isValid: false`), writeQuestions emits its prose answer and\nnothing runs against the database, so the lane is described as the\nnatural-language path with only valid generated SQL executed.\n\nEach README now states the execute error contract the code enforces: a\nfailure raises `SQL execution failed:` plus the driver''s primary\nmessage, identically with and without a session_id, with the statement\nand bound parameters kept to the server log — and with the residual\nnamed, since a driver''s primary sentence can quote a value the caller\nitself submitted.\n\nscripts/validate-node-readme.py passes for all four nodes; no GENERATED\nblock touched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): publish the auto-created table''s column map in one assignment\n\n_createTableFromData is the second writer of IGlobal.schema and still had\nthe publish-then-fill shape B4 removed from _getTableSchema: it created\nthe table, assigned `self.schema = {}`, then added one key per column,\nall outside _REFLECT_LOCK. By the time that loop runs the table exists,\nso a second first-batch can already be inside _insertData; it takes the\nlock, finds a truthy one-column map, snapshots it and silently drops\nevery other column its row carried. Reproduced by pausing the real fill\nloop: the second row landed as {''label'': ''b'', ''size'': None,\n''colour'': None} for input that supplied all three.\n\nThe map is now built in a local dict and published in a single\nassignment. The lock is NOT widened over table creation, so the\npre-existing double-create race is untouched and still out of scope.\n\nAlso seeds a stale map into a direct _getTableSchema failure test: the\nexisting rebuild-failure test starts from {}, which cannot tell \"previous\nmap left untouched\" from \"map reset to {}\". Against the pre-B4 source\nthat new test fails with the stale entry replaced by a partial one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): cut the clickhouse stack trace and position echo from execute errors\n\nThe formatter''s docstring claimed \"pymysql / clickhouse-driver put\n(errno, message) in .orig.args\". Only pymysql does. clickhouse-driver''s\nDBAPI layer raises OperationalError(ServerException), so .orig.args holds\none exception object, every branch falls through to str(orig), and what\nreached the caller was the driver''s full string form: \"Code: N.\\n\nDB::Exception: <message>. Stack trace:\\n\\n0. DB::Exception::Exception(...)\n@ 0x...\" — server internals the caller cannot act on — plus, for a syntax\nerror, the failing statement fragment quoted after \"failed at position N\n(''...'')\", which is the same class of statement echo as psycopg2''s\n\"LINE n:\".\n\n_DB_ERROR_DETAIL gains \"Stack trace:\" and \"failed at position\" as\nmarkers, so both are cut by the stripper that already handles the other\ndrivers rather than by a new branch. Neither phrase can misfire on the\nshapes the other drivers produce.\n\nThe docstring now states the real clickhouse-driver shape instead of\nclaiming the pymysql one. Two constructed tests cover it, labelled in\ntheir docstrings as reproduced from the driver''s documented behaviour:\nclickhouse-driver is not installed in this engine, so the shape cannot be\nprovoked here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ai): state the real execute error contract and schema-cache wording\n\nThree corrections to text, no behaviour change.\n\nThe error-contract paragraph overclaimed. It said values belonging to\nother rows appear only in DETAIL / HINT / CONTEXT, which are stripped.\nBoth halves of that are wrong: a real SQLite run returns\n`SQL execution failed: near \"''hunter2''\": syntax error` — the statement\nfragment the parser stopped on is in the primary sentence — and\nPostgreSQL''s `invalid input syntax for type integer: \"<value>\"` names a\nrow value an INSERT ... SELECT or a CAST merely touched, which can come\nfrom another table. pymysql and psycopg2 interpolate binds client-side,\nso such a fragment can carry a bound value too. The docstring now lists\nwhat is removed (the SQLAlchemy tail, LINE n:, DETAIL/HINT/CONTEXT/\nQUERY/STATEMENT, the ClickHouse stack trace and position echo) and what\nis passed through, and rests the policy on the argument that actually\nholds: the recipient has allow_execute and can already read the same data\nwith a SELECT, so the error channel does not widen read access. Two tests\n(one per execute path) pin the syntax-error shape on a real SQLite run.\n\nget_schema''s docstring said the schema is \"reflected once in beginGlobal\"\nand refresh_schema''s tool description — the sentence an LLM reads when\nchoosing between the two tools — said get_schema serves the snapshot\nreflected when the node started. Both became false the moment\nrefresh_schema shipped, and both contradicted the READMEs corrected for\nexactly that in c258211d.\n\n_generated_primary_keys gains a paragraph recording the SQLite-only\nresiduals of SQLAlchemy''s ''auto'' rule: INT/BIGINT/INTEGER-DESC primary\nkeys are treated as generated although SQLite does not alias them to the\nrowid, and an INTEGER PRIMARY KEY that is also a foreign key is treated\nas required although it is an alias. PostgreSQL and MySQL reflection set\nautoincrement explicitly, so production nodes resolve exactly.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): correct the error-contract and remaining schema wording\n\nTwo corrections, both to sentences this PR itself introduced or left\nbehind.\n\nThe error sentence in all four READMEs promised that \"the statement text\nand the bound parameter values are never part of it\". That is not what\nthe code does: a real run returns `SQL execution failed: near\n\"''hunter2''\": syntax error`, and PostgreSQL''s `invalid input syntax for\ntype integer: \"<value>\"` names a value an INSERT ... SELECT or a CAST\nread from another table. Each README now says what is actually removed\n(SQLAlchemy''s `[SQL:]`/`[parameters:]` echo, and per engine: PostgreSQL''s\nLINE n:/DETAIL/HINT/CONTEXT, ClickHouse''s server stack trace and its\n`failed at position` echo) and that the primary sentence is passed\nthrough as the database wrote it, so it can name a value the statement\ncarried or touched — resting the policy on the argument that holds, that\nreaching this tool requires Allow direct query execution and such a\ncaller can read the same data with a SELECT. The full text stays in the\nserver log. db_postgres moves that contract out of the already-long\n`execute` table cell into prose below the table, as the other three do.\n\nThe intro sentences in db_clickhouse and db_mysql still said\n\"startup-reflected schema\", the wording corrected everywhere else in\nc258211d; both now describe the cached snapshot and name refresh_schema.\n\nscripts/validate-node-readme.py passes for all four; no GENERATED block\ntouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): leave every database-owned column to the database on the insert lane\n\nTwo ways an explicit NULL still reached a column the database was meant\nto fill, both silent because writeAnswers only logs, and one comment that\nclaimed neither could happen.\n\n_generated_primary_keys skipped primary-key columns only, so any other\nnewly reflected column was bound NULL — and an explicit NULL overrides a\nserver default. The sequence the refresh tool invites: answers is\nauto-created as (id, q, a) with the curated map {q, a}; an agent runs\nALTER TABLE answers ADD COLUMN created_at ... NOT NULL DEFAULT now(); the\ntool description tells it to call refresh_schema, so the next batch\nrebuilds the map by reflection, binds created_at = NULL, and the batch\nthat worked a moment earlier is dropped with one log line. Reproduced on\nSQLite: NOT NULL constraint failed: answers.created_at, parameters\n(''how'', ''like this'', None). _insertData now also reads\ngenerated_defaults — every column with a server_default or an identity —\noff the reflected table and omits those the row does not supply.\n\nAn explicit null on a generated key was bound as NULL. On the execute\ntool that is right: a person wrote the statement. On the insert lane the\ncaller is an upstream node, and an LLM node or a JSON mapper emits every\nschema key, writing null for the ones it has no value for — the same\nintent as omitting it. Postgres rejects that NULL for a SERIAL column;\nMySQL and SQLite generate a value, so the failure is dialect-dependent as\nwell as silent. A supplied null on a generated primary key is now treated\nas omitted; everywhere else it is still bound as given.\n\nThe comments at refresh_schema''s invalidation and above the binding loop\nsaid the invalidation cannot change answers-lane behaviour. This PR''s own\ntest shows it can, and that half is the point of the tool. They now state\nthe guarantee that actually holds: the columns present only in the\nreflected map are omitted rather than bound NULL, so the database still\nfills in what it owns.\n\nAlso drops the unreachable \"no schema cached\" branch: after the locked\nblock IGlobal.schema is populated or the method has raised, so the\nsnapshot is always truthy. The empty-map half of the lock rationale went\nwith it; the half-built-map half, which the concurrency test proves,\nstays.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): format driver errors the dialect did not wrap in a SQLAlchemy exception\n\nThe error contract this PR documents was not delivered on the ClickHouse\nnode at all. db_clickhouse connects with `clickhouse+native://`, which is\nclickhouse-sqlalchemy''s own connector; its Cursor.execute raises\n`clickhouse_sqlalchemy.exceptions.DatabaseException`, a plain Exception\nsubclass carrying the driver''s ServerException in `.orig`. SQLAlchemy\ndoes not wrap a non-DBAPI exception, so `except SQLAlchemyError` never\nfired on EITHER execute path, `_format_db_error` never ran, and the\ncaller got `Orig exception: Code: 62.\\nSyntax error: failed at position\n21 (''hunter2'') ... Stack trace:\\n\\n0. DB::Exception::Exception(...)` —\nraw, on both paths. The markers added in 27a04f68 were verified against\nclickhouse-driver''s own DBAPI layer, which this node does not use, so\nthey were unreachable for the node they were added for.\n\nBoth paths now handle a non-SQLAlchemy exception that carries a driver\n`.orig`: log it and raise the same `SQL execution failed: <formatted>`\nRuntimeError, releasing the session on the session path as before. Duck\n-typed on `.orig` rather than importing clickhouse modules into the\nshared base — a driver error carrying its original is precisely the shape\nbeing handled, and the base must not depend on one node''s driver.\nAnything without `.orig` is re-raised untouched, so the max_execute_rows\nRuntimeError keeps its wording and rollback and a programming error still\nsurfaces as itself.\n\n_format_db_error also reads `.code` and `.message` off the original when\nthey are an int and a string: clickhouse-driver''s ServerException puts\nthe code there rather than in args, so the numeric branch missed it and\nthe caller lost the code. ClickHouse now reads like pymysql —\n`Error 60: Table default.widgets doesn''t exist` — and the message still\nruns through the stripper, so the stack trace and the `failed at\nposition` fragment inside it are cut.\n\nThe two existing clickhouse formatter tests are relabelled: they cover\nclickhouse_driver.dbapi''s shape, which this node never produces. New\ntests cover the connector''s real shape at the formatter and drive it\nthrough both execute paths, asserting no stack trace, no statement\nfragment, and a released session; two more assert that an exception\nwithout `.orig` is not swallowed. All labelled as constructed shapes —\nclickhouse-sqlalchemy is not installed in this engine.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): reject a malformed params argument instead of misreporting it\n\n`to_sqlalchemy_text` turns `$n` into a bind by indexing `params[n - 1]`,\nso a JSON object for `params` raised `KeyError: 0` and a short list an\n`IndexError`, both from inside the rewriter. The sessionless path leaked\nthose bare; worse, on the session path the `KeyError` was caught by the\narm that maps a missing session id, so `execute` reported \"unknown or\nexpired transaction session: <sid>\" for a session that was still open and\nstill usable — the caller was told to abandon a live transaction because\nthey passed the wrong JSON type.\n\n`execute` now validates before dispatching anything: `params` must be\nabsent or an array, and every `$n` the statement references must have a\nvalue, with the offending placeholder named. tx_registry.py is untouched\n— the check lives in `execute` and imports the placeholder pattern from\nthe module that owns it so it cannot drift from the rewriter it guards.\n\nAn empty list keeps its current meaning of \"no binds\", the same as\nomitting `params`, because a statement carrying a literal `$n` inside a\nstring body relies on the rewriter leaving it alone.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): document the insert-lane null rule and the clickhouse error layer\n\nThe three nodes that declare an `answers` lane now state, beside the\nexisting insert prose, what the code does with a value the row does not\ncarry: a column with a generated key or a DEFAULT is left out of the\nstatement so the database supplies it, rather than bound as an explicit\nNULL that would override the default; a generated primary key supplied as\n`null` counts as not carried, because the sender on this lane is an\nupstream node that may emit every schema key, while a `null` anywhere\nelse is inserted as given. db_postgres also records the rejection of a\nprimary key it cannot generate. db_clickhouse declares no `answers` lane,\nso none of this applies there.\n\ndb_clickhouse''s error sentence now describes the layer that actually\nraises: `clickhouse+native://` does not produce a DBAPI error, so the\nnode unwraps the driver error it carries, and the message is prefixed\nwith the server''s own `Error <code>:`.\n\nThe comment above refresh_schema''s invalidation also drops the last\nsentence that was still too strong. It said the database fills in what it\nowns either way, which holds only for a column the row omits. For a\nsupplied primary key the two maps genuinely differ: on an auto-created\ntable the curated map has no `id`, so a row carrying one has it silently\ndropped and the database generates a different value, and after a refresh\nthe reflected map binds it and the row''s own value is kept. The comment\nnow says that, and notes that curating the key into the auto-created map\nwould be a separate decision.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ai,nodes): tighten the refresh comment and two README sentences\n\nComments and prose only; no behaviour change.\n\nThe refresh_schema comment said _insertData \"leaves out any primary-key\nor server-default column the rows do NOT supply, so a column present only\nin the reflected map is omitted rather than bound NULL\". Two clauses\noverreached: a primary key the database cannot generate is rejected with\na ValueError when the rows omit it, not left out; and a plain nullable\ncolumn with no default that appears only in the reflected map IS bound\nNULL. The guarantee named in the sentence before it still holds -- the\nstored value is the NULL the database would have used anyway -- so the\nsentence now says that instead of describing a mechanism the code does\nnot have.\n\nrocketride_sql''s insert-lane rule had been spliced onto the end of the\n\"Table name and database description\" paragraph, running the two subjects\ntogether; it now ends a paragraph of its own. db_clickhouse''s \"unwraps\nits exception by the driver error it carries\" reads as intended:\n\"unwraps it to the driver error it carries\".\n\nTwo directories: 194 passed. Validator passes for both edited nodes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): regenerate the DB node parameter tables\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, nit 5) flagged that the\nROCKETRIDE:GENERATED:PARAMS regions of the four DB node READMEs had been\nhand-edited, which AGENTS.md:87-88 forbids, and that\nrocketride_sql/README.md carried an EMPTY region even though this PR\nchanged its services.json.\n\nDevelop has since filled that region: e035c26c3 (#2285) regenerated it\nfrom the pre-PR wording, so merging develop reinstated\n\"Permit QuestionType.EXECUTE callers to run raw SQL...\" — the exact claim\nthis PR corrected at the source (services.json:125 now says \"Permit the\nexecute, begin, commit, and rollback tool functions...\").\n\nRegenerated with the repo generator rather than by hand: a scratch\n`git archive HEAD | tar -x` copy with a fresh `git init` (so the\nbranch-name gate at nodes/scripts/gen-node-tables.mjs:211 passes and the\nsource links resolve to the upstream fallbacks\nrocketride-org/rocketride-server @ develop, not this fork''s origin), then\n`node nodes/scripts/gen-node-tables.mjs db_mysql db_postgres\ndb_clickhouse rocketride_sql`. Only rocketride_sql/README.md changed;\nthe generator reproduced the other three regions byte-for-byte, which\nalso settles dylan''s hand-edit concern for them — they already match\ngenerator output.\n\nVerified: python3 scripts/validate-node-readme.py PASS on all four nodes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): anchor the PostgreSQL block markers and stop stripping diag.message_primary\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 4) showed that _DB_ERROR_DETAIL\nmatched DETAIL:/HINT:/CONTEXT:/QUERY:/STATEMENT: as bare substrings with\n.search(), so the stripper truncated messages that merely contain one of\nthose words, and that every branch — psycopg2''s already-clean\ndiag.message_primary included — ran through it. All three of his shapes\nreproduced on the PR head:\n\n  RAISE EXCEPTION ''Order rejected. HINT: contact billing''\n      -> ''Order rejected.''\n  pymysql (1644, ''Invalid QUERY: missing tenant filter'')\n      -> ''Error 1644: Invalid''\n  a message whose first token is ''HINT:''\n      -> ''Database error''\n\nThe five PostgreSQL blocks are separate LINES of the server message, so\nthey are now matched only after a newline (\\n[ \\t]*), while the tails a\nlibrary appends — [SQL:, [parameters:, [cached since, [generated in,\n(Background on this error, LINE n:, Stack trace:, failed at position —\nstay unanchored, because those genuinely appear mid-string and anchoring\nthem would reopen the leak this PR exists to close.\n\nThe anchor is \\n rather than (?m)^ on purpose: ^ also matches at position\n0, which is what turned a message beginning with ''HINT:'' into\nall-detail and degraded it to the fallback constant.\n\n_format_db_error now returns diag.message_primary without the stripper.\npsycopg2 splits DETAIL/HINT/CONTEXT/QUERY/STATEMENT and the LINE n: echo\nonto their own diag fields, so that string carries no tail to cut and a\nmarker firing in it could only be truncating the application''s own\nwording.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_base.py): the three shapes above\nplus a message whose inline HINT: is followed by a real DETAIL: line\n(only the line goes), and test_format_db_error_still_cuts_a_real_postgres_detail_block,\nwhich pins that the anchoring does not let a genuine DETAIL block\nthrough on the no-diag path. Against the pre-fix code the four new\nassertions failed: \"4 failed, 1 passed, 65 deselected in 0.52s\".\n\nNo live PostgreSQL or MySQL is available here, so each case is a\nconstructed exception shape reproducing what the driver hands SQLAlchemy;\nevery new test''s docstring says so.\n\nVerified: 243 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): cut ClickHouse''s in-message statement echoes\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 3) pointed out that _DB_ERROR_DETAIL\nknew only ClickHouse''s `Stack trace:` and `failed at position` tails, and\nthat ClickHouse does not always append its statement echo — code 47\n(UNKNOWN_IDENTIFIER) splices the whole query into the MIDDLE of the\nsentence. Reproduced on the PR head by running the module''s own regex:\n\n  Code: 47. DB::Exception: Missing columns: ''secret'' while processing\n  query: ''SELECT secret FROM users WHERE token = ''''hunter2'''''', required\n  columns: ''secret''. Stack trace:…\n\nwas cut only at `Stack trace:`, so the statement — with the bound value\nalready inlined — reached the caller. The analyzer''s `in scope <sql>`\nform leaked the same way.\n\nThree markers added: `while processing query:`, `, required columns:`,\nand `in scope `. The first two are distinctive enough as bare\nsubstrings; `in scope ` carries a lookahead for an uppercase statement\nkeyword, because ClickHouse formats the echoed query back out of its AST\n(so the phrase is always followed by SELECT/INSERT/… there) while in\nprose — \"not in scope for this trigger\" — it is not. That is the same\nlesson as the DETAIL/HINT anchoring in the previous commit: a marker that\ncan fire inside an application''s own sentence will.\n\nThis dialect is the one where the echo genuinely carries user data:\nclickhouse-driver sends no server-side parameters by default, so it\ninterpolates binds client-side and the repeated statement contains the\ncaller''s literal rather than a placeholder. The docstring is reconciled\naccordingly — its opening line no longer claims \"never the statement or\nits parameters\" flatly but points at the REMOVED / PASSED THROUGH\nsection, the ClickHouse bullet now names the two in-message echoes and\nthe client-side interpolation, and the \"every branch runs through the\nstripper\" sentence now excepts the diag.message_primary branch the\nprevious commit took off it.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_base.py): the code-47 shape on\nboth connectors — clickhouse_driver.dbapi''s OperationalError(\nServerException) and clickhouse-sqlalchemy''s DatabaseException, which is\nwhat the db_clickhouse node''s `clickhouse+native://` URL actually raises\n— plus a lone `, required columns:` tail and a guard that prose\ncontaining \"in scope\" is not truncated. Against the pre-fix code:\n\"3 failed, 1 passed, 70 deselected in 0.57s\" (the fourth is a guard and\npassed before too).\n\nNo live ClickHouse server is available here and neither clickhouse-driver\nnor clickhouse-sqlalchemy is installed, so both connector shapes are\nconstructed from the message forms the server documents; each test\ndocstring says so.\n\nVerified: 247 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): format _validateQuery''s EXPLAIN error before returning it\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, nit 1) noted that _validateQuery''s\n`except Exception as e: return False, str(e)` made the PR description''s\nsecurity claim false. The EXPLAIN failure does not stop at the retry\nloop: _buildSQLQuery carries it into `result[''error'']`, get_sql returns\nthat as its `error` field, and get_data returns get_sql''s dict\nunchanged. Neither tool consults IGlobal.allow_execute — the gate exists\nonly in execute() — so `str()` of a SQLAlchemy StatementError, tail and\nall, reached callers who were never granted raw SQL. On the pre-fix code\nthe string was:\n\n  (sqlite3.OperationalError) no such table: no_such_table\n  [SQL: EXPLAIN SELECT secret FROM no_such_table WHERE token = ''hunter2'']\n  (Background on this error at: https://sqlalche.me/e/20/e3q8)\n\nNow `self._format_db_error(e)`, the same formatter execute() uses, and\nthe full exception is logged with warning() first so the statement stays\nin the server log where the retry loop''s own log line used to keep it.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_execute_errors.py): the helper\ndirectly, then both ungated lanes — get_sql and get_data with\nallow_execute switched OFF and a stubbed _buildSQLQueryOnce that keeps\nproposing a query against a missing table — asserting the driver''s\nsentence survives while ''[SQL:'', ''[parameters:'' and the bound literal do\nnot. Plus the success half, `(True, '''')`, which is unchanged. Against\nthe pre-fix code: \"3 failed, 1 passed, 40 deselected in 0.55s\".\n\nVerified: 251 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): read an explicit null on any database-owned column as omitted\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 7) pointed out the asymmetry the\nprevious round left behind: the OMITTED branch skips a generated key AND\nanything carrying a server default or identity, while the EXPLICIT-null\nbranch only skipped generated keys. So `{''created_at'': None}` against\n`created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()` fell through to\n`prepare_value(None)` and bound NULL. Reproduced on SQLite, which raises\nthe same class of error:\n\n  sqlite3.IntegrityError: NOT NULL constraint failed: notes.created_at\n  [SQL: INSERT INTO notes (label, created_at) VALUES (?, ?)]\n  [parameters: (''a'', None)]\n\nand on the answers lane writeAnswers only logs, so the batch vanished\nwith one line — the exact failure mode the generated-key rule was added\nto prevent.\n\nThe condition now reads `lowered in generated_pk_columns or lowered in\ngenerated_defaults`, i.e. the same column set as the omitted branch.\nSplitting the rule by nullability or by key-ness would make one emitted\nrow land differently depending on a column attribute its sender — an LLM\nnode or a JSON mapper that emits every schema key — cannot see.\n\nThe three insert-lane README paragraphs said \"a null on any other column\nis inserted as NULL as given\"; they now say a null on any column the\ndatabase fills in counts as not carried, and a null on a column with\nnothing behind it is inserted as given. Hand-written prose only; the\nGENERATED:PARAMS regions are untouched (validate-node-readme passes on\nall four DB nodes).\n\nThis is a behaviour change for a pipeline that deliberately writes NULL\ninto a column that happens to carry a DEFAULT — it now gets the default\ninstead. The `execute` tool is a different contract and is unaffected.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_execute_errors.py): the NOT NULL\nDEFAULT case dylan named, the nullable-default case, a column with\nnothing behind it still binding NULL, and one showing the run-splitting\nthis collapses — a batch alternating an explicit null and an omission is\nnow a single executemany instead of one run per row. The narrow-rule pin\ntest_insert_still_binds_an_explicit_null_on_a_non_key_column is removed:\nit asserted the behaviour this item reverses, and the two new cases cover\nboth halves of what replaced it. Against the pre-fix code:\n\"3 failed, 3 passed, 42 deselected in 0.78s\".\n\nVerified: 254 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean, validate-node-readme PASS on db_postgres, db_mysql,\ndb_clickhouse and rocketride_sql.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): guard refresh_schema''s reflection and keep both caches on failure\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 5) pointed out that\n`self.IGlobal.db_schema = self.IGlobal._getDatabaseSchema()` ran\nunguarded inside the locked block. _getDatabaseSchema has no per-table\ntry/except — one `inspector.get_columns(table)` the database refuses (a\nrevoked grant, a lock timeout, a table dropped mid-walk) propagates out\nof the whole walk — so refresh_schema was the one tool in this file\nwhose failure still reached the caller as the SQLAlchemy repr, tail\nincluded, and this tool has no allow_execute gate in front of it.\n\nThe reflection now runs in a try/except: the full exception goes to the\nlog with error(), and the caller gets\n`RuntimeError(''Schema refresh failed: '' + _format_db_error(e))` with\n`from None`, matching execute()''s shape.\n\nPublication is all-or-nothing, and the docstring now says so and why.\nThe result is assigned only after the walk returns, so a failure leaves\ndb_schema AND IGlobal.schema exactly as they were — the node keeps\nserving the schema it has instead of publishing a half-walked database,\nand IGlobal.schema is not emptied, which would otherwise re-arm the\ninsert lane''s lazy rebuild against a database that just refused to be\nreflected. Skipping the offending table and continuing (what\n_getTableSchema does for a single table) is rejected explicitly: the LLM\npath cannot tell a table that vanished from one that could not be read,\nand would write queries as if it were gone.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_execute_errors.py): a patched\ninspector whose get_columns raises a wrapped driver error for one table\n— injected, since no PostgreSQL is available here to revoke a grant on,\nand injected after get_table_names so the walk fails part-way as a real\none would. Three cases: the message carries the driver''s sentence and\nneither ''[SQL:'' nor the reflection statement; both caches are byte-equal\nto their pre-refresh values and a table created in between does not\nappear; and _REFLECT_LOCK — process-wide, so a leak would wedge every\nnode in the engine — is free afterwards and the next refresh succeeds.\nAgainst the pre-fix code: \"3 failed, 1 passed, 46 deselected in 0.65s\".\n\nVerified: 257 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): format an ODBC (sqlstate, message) pair instead of returning the code\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, nit 6) noticed that the generic\n`args[0]` branch returns the SQLSTATE alone for a driver that reports\n`(''42S02'', ''Base table or view not found'')` — pyodbc and the other\nODBC-backed drivers put a five-character SQLSTATE where pymysql puts an\ninteger errno, so the integer branch misses them and the caller gets the\nhalf of the pair they cannot act on. The str(exc) fallback test this PR\nremoved used to cover the shape incidentally.\n\nA branch before the generic one formats it as `Error <sqlstate>:\n<message>`, matched strictly against `\\A[0-9A-Z]{5}\\Z` and requiring a\nnon-empty string second entry, so a driver that merely happens to put\ntwo strings in `args` keeps the old behaviour. The result still runs\nthrough the stripper.\n\ndylan notes correctly that no node in this repo uses such a driver today\n(there is no pyodbc dialect under nodes/src/nodes), so this is hardening\nfor a shape we do not ship rather than a live bug; the docstring bullet\nsays as much.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_base.py): the pair formatted, the\nstripper still cutting a STATEMENT block inside it, a two-string `args`\nthat is not a SQLSTATE left alone, and the single-argument sqlite3 shape\nunchanged. Constructed shapes — no ODBC driver is installed here. Against\nthe pre-fix code: \"2 failed, 2 passed, 74 deselected in 0.46s\" (the two\nguard cases passed before, which is the point of them).\n\nVerified: 261 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): give each database node its own reflection lock\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 8): `_REFLECT_LOCK` was module-level\nand held across `refresh_schema`''s full-database inspector walk, which runs\non an engine with no statement timeout. `_insertData` took the same lock\nunconditionally on every batch, just to evaluate `if not IGlobal.schema`. So\none node refreshing a wide database stalled the answers lane of every other\nDB node in the same engine process -- cross-node blocking the pre-PR code\nnever had, since two nodes share no cache and have nothing to race on.\n\nThe lock is now per-IGlobal, created in `beginGlobal` (`reflect_lock`,\ndeclared `None` on the class so a global that never ran `beginGlobal` fails\nloudly instead of silently sharing class state). Both writers rebind\n`schema` / `db_schema` wholesale and neither reads the other node''s, so the\nstate the lock guards is exactly one node''s.\n\n`_insertData` still takes the lock on every batch, and the comment now says\nwhy that is the deliberate choice: an uncontended acquire is nanoseconds\nagainst an insert about to do network I/O, while a check-then-lock fast path\nneeds the map read once into a local and snapshotted from THAT -- read twice,\na `refresh_schema` landing between the two hands the batch an empty map --\nwhich is a second path no test pins. What made the unconditional acquire\nexpensive was its process-wide scope, and that is what changed.\n\nAlso item nit 4 (DS-N4), first half: `refresh_schema` rendered the tool\npayload inside the lock. It is pure formatting, so it now runs after the\nrelease, over the local `refreshed` map rather than `IGlobal.db_schema` --\na refresh that overtook this one has already published its own map, and this\ncall still owes its caller the schema it reflected. The second half of that\nnit (rebuilding `IGlobal.schema` from `db_schema[table][''columns'']` instead\nof emptying it) is left alone: it changes the map''s value shape and the\ntable-is-gone case that `test_refresh_schema_empties_the_column_map_when_the_table_is_gone`\npins, so it is not a nit-sized change.\n\nTest: `test_a_refresh_does_not_block_another_nodes_insert` builds two\nglobals over two separate SQLite files, pauses one node''s refresh inside the\ncolumn walk, and asserts the other node''s insert completes while it is held.\nAgainst the pre-fix code it fails with \"the second node''s insert waited for\nthe first node''s refresh\" (1 failed, 50 deselected in 5.63s). The five\nexisting deterministic concurrency tests are unchanged in substance; the two\nthat assert the lock is not stranded now acquire the node''s own\n`reflect_lock`, and the fixture builds one per global the way `beginGlobal`\ndoes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ai): pin the post-refresh supplied-id flip on an auto-created table\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 9): the comment in `refresh_schema`\nconcedes that on an auto-created table the curated column map has no `id`, so\na row carrying one has it dropped, while after a refresh the reflected map\nbinds it -- and asks for that flip to be either removed or pinned by a test.\nNothing exercised it: `test_insert_after_refresh_does_not_bind_the_generated_primary_key`\nonly inserts rows that OMIT `id`.\n\n`test_a_supplied_id_is_dropped_before_a_refresh_and_bound_after` auto-creates\nthrough the real path, asserts the compiled column list of an insert that\nsupplies `id` before the refresh ({q, a}) and after it ({id, q, a}), and\nchecks the stored rows. Its docstring names the consequence the SQLite\nfixtures cannot show: PostgreSQL advances a SERIAL sequence only for rows\nthat take the DEFAULT, so a bound `id` of 501 leaves the sequence at 3 and\nthe next generated row collides on the primary key, which `writeAnswers`\nlogs and drops.\n\nNo behaviour change -- this is characterisation only. Curating `id` into\n`_createTableFromData`''s map so both maps agree is the alternative dylan\noffers; it is a product decision about what an auto-created table promises\nand is not taken here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): name the trigger-populated primary key as a known limitation\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 6): `_insertData` rejects a row that\nomits a primary key with no reflected `server_default` / identity /\nautoincrement. A `CHAR(36)` key filled by a `BEFORE INSERT` trigger -- the\nstandard MySQL UUID idiom before 8.0.13, same shape on PostgreSQL -- reflects\nexactly that way, so the batch is refused before the trigger can run, and\n`writeAnswers` turns that `ValueError` into one log line and drops every row.\nHe asks for this to be documented at minimum.\n\nThis is the documentation half. The three insert-lane sections (db_postgres,\ndb_mysql, rocketride_sql; db_clickhouse has no answers lane) now name the\ntrigger case, say that the answers lane logs and drops the batch rather than\nreporting it, and give the workaround: a real column default\n(`DEFAULT (uuid())` on MySQL 8.0.13+, `DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()` on\nPostgreSQL) reflects and is honoured. db_mysql''s paragraph also gained the\nrejection sentence itself, which only the other two READMEs carried. All\nedits are hand-written prose; no GENERATED:PARAMS region is touched.\n\n`_generated_primary_keys`''s docstring gains the same paragraph, stated as the\nfalse positive it is: reflection describes columns, not triggers.\n\nThe stronger option dylan offers -- drop the pre-flight rejection, bind\nnothing, let the driver''s error come back through `_format_db_error` -- is\nnot taken here. It costs the guarantee that a refused batch leaves nothing\nbehind, which is what makes the current rejection safe, so it is put to him\nas a question on the thread rather than decided here.\n\nVerified: validate-node-readme.py PASS for all four DB nodes; 263 passed in\npackages/ai/tests/database + packages/ai/tests/ai/common/database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(ai): make _getTableSchema the only publisher of IGlobal.schema\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, nit 3), two parts.\n\n`_getTableSchema` already assigns `self.schema` and then returns the same\ncolumns as pairs; `beginGlobal` and `_insertData` rebuilt the map from those\npairs and assigned it again, comment-less. The rebuild was not a no-op only\nin that it replaced each reflected column comment with '''' -- and this map is\nread exclusively by `_insertData`, by key. Both rebuilds are gone; the two\ncall sites now test the return value and say why there is nothing to assign.\n`beginGlobal` keeps its one real assignment, `self.schema = {}` for a table\nthat does not exist yet, which is the case `_getTableSchema` leaves untouched.\nBehaviour for every reader is unchanged: same keys, same order, in the same\nsingle atomic rebind.\n\nThe comment above the `dict()` snapshot claimed it guarded an in-place\nmutation. No publisher mutates in place any more -- both build a map and\nrebind it, which is what they were fixed for earlier in this PR -- so the\ncomment now says what the snapshot actually buys: binding the map to a local\nis what stops a `refresh_schema` mid-batch swapping the map the per-row loop\nis walking, and copying it is what keeps that true if a publisher ever fills\none in place again.\n\nNo behaviour change. 263 passed in packages/ai/tests/database +\npackages/ai/tests/ai/common/database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): cut a parenthesised ClickHouse in-scope echo as well\n\nThe `in scope ` marker''s lookahead required the uppercase statement\nkeyword to follow the phrase immediately, so it fired on the analyzer''s\n`in scope SELECT ...` echo but not on `in scope (SELECT ...)`.\nClickHouse formats a scope that is itself a subquery or a CTE body\nthrough ASTSubquery, which parenthesises it, and clickhouse-driver\ninterpolates bind values client-side -- so that form carries the\ncaller''s literal straight through the stripper.\n\nThe lookahead now allows one optional `(` before the keyword. Added\ntest_format_db_error_clickhouse_cuts_a_parenthesised_subquery_scope,\nwhich fails against the previous regex with the whole subquery (and its\nbound literal) still in the message. No live ClickHouse server or driver\nis available here, so the shape is constructed from the documented\nmessage form, as the neighbouring cases are; the test docstring says so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): name the table a refresh lost instead of echoing a bare noun\n\n`refresh_schema`''s guard formats every reflection failure through\n`_format_db_error`, which reads the driver''s own sentence. The one case\ndylan-savage''s item 5 names that has no such sentence is a table dropped\nbetween `get_table_names` and its `get_columns`: SQLAlchemy raises\n`NoSuchTableError`, built from the table name alone and carrying no\n`.orig`, so the generic branch returned `args[0]` and the caller saw\n\"Schema refresh failed: ghost\" -- a bare noun with no verb.\n\nA `NoSuchTableError` arm before the generic one now says what happened\n(\"table \\\"ghost\\\" disappeared while it was being reflected\") and logs the\nsame sentence server-side. The all-or-nothing publication is unchanged:\nthe arm raises before either cache is assigned.\n\nAdded test_refresh_schema_names_a_table_dropped_mid_walk, which fails\nagainst the previous code with the bare table name. The drop is injected\nthrough the inspector rather than raced against a live server, and the\ndocstring says so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): name beginGlobal when a global reaches the lock without one\n\n`reflect_lock` defaults to None on DatabaseGlobalBase so that a global\nbuilt without `beginGlobal` cannot silently share one lock across every\nnode in the process. The failure it produced was loud but anonymous:\n`with None:` raises \"''NoneType'' object does not support the context\nmanager protocol\", naming neither the attribute nor the call that\ncreates it, and on the answers lane `writeAnswers` reduces any exception\nto a single log line -- the quiet failure this PR removes elsewhere.\n\n`_insertData` and `refresh_schema` now take the lock through\n`_reflectLock()`, which raises RuntimeError(''reflect_lock is created in\nbeginGlobal; this global never ran it''). No production path reaches it:\nno DB node overrides `beginGlobal`. Added\ntest_a_global_that_never_ran_begin_global_names_the_missing_lock, which\nfails against the previous code with the bare TypeError.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ai): correct the explicit-null sentence in _generated_primary_keys\n\nThe docstring still described the pre-item-7 rule -- \"anywhere else a\nsupplied null is bound as given\" -- which caf5bc6e4 made false when it\nwidened the explicit-null branch to every server-default and identity\ncolumn, and the trigger-limitation paragraph added in fd5e29eb7 had been\nstitched onto that sentence, leaving a 130-character line.\n\nThe paragraph is now its own, and states the live rule: the generated\nkeys this returns plus the server-default / identity columns _insertData\nreads off the reflected table are one set, and an explicit null on any of\nthem means \"no value\". Documentation only; no behaviour change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic…","tree":{"sha":"efeefe8ca012f08299af2ac9b7bae692cd374db0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/efeefe8ca012f08299af2ac9b7bae692cd374db0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/e23e66ea3d0152a99299af9983c96f0d0996b51f","verification":{"payload":"tree efeefe8ca012f08299af2ac9b7bae692cd374db0\nparent d4dcd070b5510e4691b7b96f14cb6b7f12247ddf\nauthor NIHAL NIHALANI <nihal.nihalani@gmail.com> 1789759996 +0530\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789759996 +0530\n\nfix(ai,nodes): surface database error text from execute and add refresh_schema tool (#2283)\n\n* fix(ai,nodes): surface database error text from execute and add refresh_schema tool\n\nTwo gaps in the shared database node base, both scoped to stay out of the\nway of the pull requests already open against this file.\n\n_executeRawQuery caught every SQLAlchemyError, logged it, and returned\nNone, so execute() answered with the same ''check server logs for details''\nstring whether the statement had a typo, named a missing table, or was\nrefused for permissions. Its except clause now raises with the driver''s own\nmessage through IGlobal._format_db_error. Only that clause changes:\nexecute()''s `if result is None` branch becomes unreachable but is left in\nplace, with a comment saying so, because an open PR is rewriting that\nregion. Session-bound execute already re-raised the real exception, so the\ntwo paths finally agree, and the max_execute_rows rollback is untouched.\n\nIGlobal.db_schema is assigned exactly once, in beginGlobal, so get_schema\nserves a task-start snapshot for the life of the task and a table created\nthrough execute stays invisible until the pipeline restarts. The new\nrefresh_schema tool re-reflects and replaces it, returning the get_schema\nshape plus a refreshed_at UTC timestamp so a caller can show how current\nthe schema it holds is. Reflection and publication run under a module-level\nlock: concurrent callers would otherwise repeat the same full table walk\nand race to publish the result. The tool is additive and lives entirely in\ndb_instance_base.py, since another open PR is adding to db_global_base.py.\nIt also fixes the natural-language path, which describes that same dict to\nthe LLM. get_schema''s nested table formatter is lifted to a shared private\nmethod so both tools render one shape.\n\nTests are a new module rather than an addition to test_db_base.py, which\ntwo open PRs are both appending to. Eleven cases over an in-memory SQLite\nengine cover the driver text reaching the caller, the gate still refusing\nfirst, re-reflection seeing created and dropped tables, the timestamp\nformat, and four concurrent refreshes all returning.\n\nTool tables in the four node READMEs that inherit this surface list\nrefresh_schema and say in one sentence what get_schema''s snapshot does not\nshow. services.json declares no tool list, so nothing to mirror there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n\n* test(ai): give the execute-session IGlobal stub a real _format_db_error\n\nexecute()''s stateless failure path now formats the driver error through\nIGlobal, but this module builds IGlobal as a SimpleNamespace carrying only\nthe four fields the transaction tests needed. Nothing failed, because no\ntest here drove a bad statement through that path — the next one to try\nwould have died with AttributeError instead of the RuntimeError it was\nasserting.\n\nThe stub borrows the real DatabaseGlobalBase implementation rather than a\nlambda, so the message a test sees is the message a caller gets. A test for\nthe stateless failure path comes with it, so the fixture stays honest:\nremoving the one line makes it fail with AttributeError.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n\n* refactor(ai): shrink the refresh_schema diff in db_instance_base\n\nNo behaviour change; this only narrows what the previous commit touched in\na file three open pull requests are editing.\n\nget_schema''s body is back to exactly what develop has, docstring aside: the\nnested _format_table closure returns, and the _format_table static method\nand _schemaPayload helper it had been lifted into are gone. Sharing them\nwas tidier, but it rewrote a method none of the open PRs needs to change,\nand a few duplicated formatting lines cost less than that. That hunk is now\nfive added docstring lines instead of a 31-line rewrite.\n\nrefresh_schema moves to the end of the tool section, below dialect, so the\nwhole tool arrives as one appended block past the execute body PR #1908\nrewrites rather than as an insertion ahead of it. It formats its own tables\ninline, and the lock is now _REFLECT_LOCK.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T4fLnswQF6yKQ6x7eWzSdd\n\n* docs(nodes): complete the agent tool contract in the db READMEs\n\nTwo review findings on the READMEs this PR touched, both in the section\nthe node README schema calls the agent-facing contract and holds to the\nhighest accuracy bar.\n\ndb_postgres listed four tool functions in the summary and in \"As a tool\",\nthen a separate table of three transaction tools — seven of the nine\n@tool_function methods an agent actually sees on DatabaseInstanceBase.\n`execute` appeared only as prose inside the transaction paragraph and\n`dialect` only as the QuestionType.DIALECT lane behaviour, so an agent\nauthor reading the inventory would not know either exists as a tool. The\ncount is now nine in both places, `dialect` joins the open table, and\n`execute` joins what was the Transactions table, renamed \"Raw SQL and\ntransactions\" because a statement without a session_id is neither. That\ntable''s gate sentence also said requests are \"silently dropped\" when\nallow_execute is off; the four tools raise (db_instance_base.py:301,\n339, 371), and it is the questions lane that logs and drops — the two\nbehaviours are now stated apart. The `execute` row records the driver\nmessage and the rollback-on-overflow that this PR''s own change produced,\nrather than implying a truncated result set.\n\nrocketride_sql named refresh_schema in its tool table but left its\ncontract out of the prose beneath, where that README already documents\nevery other non-obvious argument and return shape. It takes no arguments\n(input_schema declares no properties) and returns {database, tables}\nexactly as get_schema does, plus a refreshed_at UTC ISO-8601 timestamp\nfrom datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat().\n\nDocs only; no behaviour change. Both nodes still pass\nscripts/validate-node-readme.py, and so does the whole corpus under\n./builder docs:validate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): keep SQL statements and bind parameters out of execute error text\n\n`_format_db_error` only produced a clean `Error <code>: <msg>` when the\ndriver put an integer first in `orig.args` — the pymysql and\nclickhouse-driver shape. psycopg2 and sqlite3 put a string there, so both\nfell through to `str(exc)`, which for a SQLAlchemy StatementError is the\nfull repr: the executed statement in `[SQL: ...]` and the values bound\ninto it in `[parameters: ...]`.\n\nThat string is no longer log-only. `_executeRawQuery` re-raises it as a\nRuntimeError that the `execute` tool returns to its caller, and #2282\nwires a user-facing Refresh/Run UI onto exactly that path, so a failed\nINSERT would hand the caller back the row it tried to write.\n\nUnwrap each driver shape explicitly and keep only the primary message:\nthe numeric-code branch as before; psycopg2''s `diag.message_primary`\n(its `str()` also carries a `LINE n:` echo of the statement, which\npsycopg2 has already interpolated the bind values into); and `args[0]`\nfor sqlite3 and the generic DBAPI shape. Every branch then runs through\n`_strip_statement_detail`, which truncates at the first `[SQL:`,\n`[parameters:`, `LINE n:`, `DETAIL:`/`HINT:`/`CONTEXT:` or sqlalche.me\nmarker, so a driver shape not enumerated here still cannot leak the\ntail. DETAIL is dropped along with the rest because PostgreSQL uses it\nto restate the offending key values.\n\nThe helper is module-level rather than a method so `_format_db_error`\nkeeps working when tests bind it onto a stub IGlobal.\n\nTests cover the real sqlite3 driver end-to-end through `execute`, plus\nthe psycopg2 (with and without `diag`), sqlite3 and pymysql shapes and a\nbare StatementError repr.\n`test_format_db_error_falls_back_to_str_when_args_not_int_first` pinned\nthe old leaking fallback, so it is replaced by an assertion that the\ndriver message — not `str(exc)` — is what comes back.\n\n`_strip_statement_detail`''s degenerate case gets the same treatment. A\nmessage that is nothing but detail markers left no primary sentence, and\nthe old fallback returned the first line — which on that input is the\n`[SQL: ...]` echo the function exists to remove. It now returns a\nneutral constant, so there is no input on which the helper hands back the\nstatement.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): refresh the answers-lane column map in refresh_schema\n\n`refresh_schema` replaced `IGlobal.db_schema`, the database-wide\nreflection the natural-language path describes to the LLM, and stopped\nthere. `IGlobal.schema` — the configured table''s column map that\n`_insertData` iterates to build every answers-lane INSERT — is a second\nstart-up snapshot, and it was left untouched, rebuilt only lazily when\nfalsy.\n\nSo the node was current on one path and stale on the other: after\nrunning `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN` and calling the refresh tool, the\nLLM path saw the new column while the insert lane kept writing the\nstart-up column set and silently dropped it. The four db node READMEs\ndocument both the insert lane and \"re-reads the schema from the\ndatabase\", so the documented contract overstated the code.\n\nInvalidate the map inside the same reflection lock. Emptying it re-arms\nthe lazy rebuild already at the top of `_insertData`, which reflects\nthrough the same `_getTableSchema` call `beginGlobal` uses, so the next\ninsert sees what a freshly started node would. Emptying rather than\nre-reflecting here keeps the call cheap for a node with no answers lane\nwired, and makes the write a single atomic rebind: a concurrent\n`_insertData` either reads the old map or finds it falsy and rebuilds,\nnever a half-built dict.\n\nInvalidating the map is only safe once `_insertData` stops binding a\ngenerated primary key, so that is fixed in the same commit.\n`_createTableFromData` curates `IGlobal.schema` down to the data columns\nfor an auto-created table, because the `id` it prepends is generated by\nthe database; a map built by reflection carries `id` instead. Since\n`_insertData` binds NULL for any schema column the rows do not supply,\nswapping the curated map for a reflected one would have started binding\n`id=None` on every later insert — tolerated by SQLite''s rowid alias, a\nnot-null violation against the `id SERIAL NOT NULL` Postgres renders for\nthe same column. `_insertData` now drops primary-key columns that no row\nin the batch supplies, decided once per batch so every mapping handed to\nexecutemany keeps an identical key set. The two maps therefore produce\nthe same INSERT and refreshing cannot change answers-lane behaviour\nmid-task. It also fixes the same NULL bind on the path that always\nreflected: a table that already existed when `beginGlobal` ran.\n\nTests cover the refreshed insert picking up a new column, the\npre-existing stale behaviour it fixes, a dropped configured table leaving\na falsy map, and — by asserting on the compiled INSERT rather than on\nwhat SQLite tolerates — that an auto-created table''s insert still carries\nthe data columns only after a refresh, that a reflected primary key the\nrows do not supply is omitted, that one they do supply is still bound,\nand that a primary-key-only table falls back to binding it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): describe the raw-SQL gate the db nodes actually have\n\nThe `Raw SQL and transactions` section said `allow_execute` is \"the same\ngate as `QuestionType.EXECUTE`\" and contrasted the tools with \"the\n`questions` lane, which logs and drops the request\". No SQL database\nnode implements that branch: `DatabaseInstanceBase.writeQuestions` does\nnot look at `question.type` at all, and `QuestionType` appears in\ndb_instance_base.py only at the import and in one unrelated\n`Question(type=QuestionType.QUESTION, ...)` construction. The\ndispatch-on-type behaviour belongs to the graph node\n(`graph_instance_base.py` handles `DIALECT` and `EXECUTE`), and the\nwording was carried across without being checked against this file.\n\nCorrect it wherever it appears in prose:\n\n- db_postgres: the \"Two special question types are handled on the\n  questions lane\" bullets are removed — the lane has one behaviour,\n  translate-then-execute, and `dialect` / `execute` are the tool\n  functions that reach those behaviours. The gate sentence now says\n  what `allow_execute` gates (the four raw-SQL tools) and that it does\n  not change the questions lane, which never runs raw SQL.\n- rocketride_sql: same two claims, in the config and Notes sections.\n\nAlso make the refresh_schema wording match the code now that the tool\ninvalidates the answers-lane column map as well as the database-wide\nschema, so \"re-reads the schema from the database\" is true end to end\nrather than only for the LLM path.\n\nThe same claim lived in the generated config tables, whose source of\ntruth is the `allow_execute` field description in each node''s\nservices*.json. Those five descriptions now name the four tool functions\nthe flag actually gates, and the generated regions are regenerated from\nthem, so db_postgres/README.md no longer contradicts itself between its\nprose and its parameter table. Only the SQL nodes are touched: the graph\nnodes do dispatch on `QuestionType.EXECUTE`, so their copy is correct as\nit stands.\n\nTwo smaller instances of the same transposition: the new db_postgres\nsentence named `db_neo4j`, a retired path that redirects to\n`graph_neo4j`, and db_clickhouse/IInstance.py said its dialect string is\n\"surfaced via QuestionType.DIALECT\" when only the `dialect` tool reaches\nit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): apply the same execute error contract to the session path\n\nA raw statement run with a session_id went through\nTransactionRegistry.execute, which has no try/except, and the existing\n`except Exception` arm in execute() re-raised the SQLAlchemy exception\nunchanged. str() of that exception ends in `[SQL: ...]` and\n`[parameters: ...]`, so the same tool behind the same allow_execute gate\nreturned the statement and its bound values to the caller purely because\na session id was passed, while the sessionless half stripped them.\n\nAdd a `SQLAlchemyError` arm above the existing `except Exception` (the\nmax-rows RuntimeError is not a SQLAlchemyError, so its wording and its\nrollback are untouched): log the full exception, release the session, and\nraise `RuntimeError(''SQL execution failed: '' + _format_db_error(e))`.\nThe fix lives in execute() rather than tx_registry because the registry\nhas no IGlobal and is shared with other callers.\n\nSession release moves into _releaseFailedSession, shared by both arms.\nA rollback that itself fails is logged and swallowed: previously an\nexception raised inside the cleanup replaced the error the caller was\nbeing told about (a monkeypatched rollback surfaced as ''boom'').\n\nAlso drop the dead `result is None` branch in execute() and correct\n_executeRawQuery''s annotation to `-> dict`: the method has returned the\nshaped dict or raised since it started carrying the driver''s message, so\nthe branch could only ever have produced the opaque ''check server logs\nfor details'' string this work exists to replace, and the annotation\ncontradicted the comment above the branch. execute() is its only caller.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(ai): share one table formatter between get_schema and refresh_schema\n\nrefresh_schema rebuilt get_schema''s nested _format_table line for line,\nwith `result` renamed to `entry`. Both tools promise callers the same\nper-table shape -- their tool descriptions say so, and an LLM picks\nbetween them on that sentence -- but the equality test compared the two\ncopies to each other, so it would have stayed green while a later change\nadded a key (an index list, a column comment, a nullable flag) to one\ncopy only.\n\nLift the formatter to a module-level _format_table and call it from\nboth; get_schema''s body loses the nested definition, refresh_schema\nloses the loop. The wire format is byte-identical, so the existing shape\ntests pass untouched. The docstring paragraph calling the duplication\ndeliberate goes with it.\n\ntest_refresh_schema_matches_get_schema_shape now reflects a table with\nboth a primary key and a foreign key, so the two optional entries are\nactually exercised on both sides, and _format_table gains a direct unit\ntest for the optional-key rules an equality check cannot see.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): make the lazy schema rebuild safe for concurrent inserts\n\nrefresh_schema empties IGlobal.schema to re-arm the rebuild at the top of\n_insertData, which is what makes this reachable at runtime: a second\ninsert can now arrive while the first is reflecting.\n\n_getTableSchema published `self.schema = {}` and then grew it column by\ncolumn, and _insertData checked, rebuilt and then took a reference to\nthat same dict without a lock. A concurrent insert could therefore see\nthe empty map (falling into the \"no schema cached\" branch, which binds\nthe item''s raw keys and skips the generated-key handling), or a truthy\nbut half-built one (silently dropping columns the caller had supplied),\nor a dict changing size while the per-row loop iterated it. A reflection\nthat failed midway left the half-built map published for the next insert\nto believe.\n\n_getTableSchema now builds into a local dict and publishes it with one\nassignment, so a failure leaves the previous map untouched. _insertData\nholds _REFLECT_LOCK across the falsy check, the rebuild and a snapshot\ncopy, and builds the batch from the snapshot; the lock is released before\nthe Table reflection and before the INSERT, neither of which reads the\nmap. The comment above refresh_schema''s invalidation no longer claims\nthe single rebind makes the rebuild atomic.\n\nTests hold a rebuild open inside the real column walk rather than\nsimulating the window: without the lock the second insert stored NULL in\na column it had supplied, an overlapping refresh left a one-column map,\nand a failed reflection published a partial one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): decide generated primary keys per row, not per batch\n\nThe answers lane worked out which primary-key columns to leave unbound\nonce per batch, from the union of every row''s keys. One row carrying\n`id` therefore put `id` into every mapping, and the rows that omitted it\nbound NULL into a key the database was supposed to generate. SQLite\ntolerates that for a rowid alias; Postgres rejects the whole batch for an\n`id SERIAL NOT NULL` column. The sole-primary-key table had the same\nproblem by construction: the `omit_columns = set()` fallback bound NULL\nbecause omitting everything would have left an empty mapping.\n\nWhich primary keys the database generates is now read from the reflected\nTable -- `autoincrement_column` (SQLAlchemy''s own resolution, covering a\nreflected `autoincrement=True` and the `''auto''` rule that recognises\nSQLite''s rowid alias), plus any primary-key column with a server_default,\nan Identity, or an explicit autoincrement -- rather than assumed of every\nprimary key. Per row: a generated key the row omits is left out of that\nrow''s mapping, a supplied value is bound, and an explicitly supplied None\nis bound as NULL, so the three cases stay distinguishable. A primary key\nthe database does NOT generate and the row does not carry is a ValueError\nnaming the table, the columns and the row position, raised before the\ntransaction opens.\n\nRows are then executed as contiguous runs sharing a key set, all inside\none engine.begin(): contiguous rather than gathered, because generated\nids follow insertion order and regrouping would hand back ids in an order\nthe caller''s rows never had, and one transaction because a batch must\nstay all-or-nothing as a single executemany was. A run whose mapping is\nempty gets a values-less `insert(table)` per row, which SQLAlchemy\nrenders as `DEFAULT VALUES` on PostgreSQL and SQLite and `() VALUES ()`\non MySQL.\n\ntest_insert_binds_the_primary_key_when_it_is_the_only_column pinned the\nold fallback and is replaced by the default-values test, plus a\ncompile-only check of all three dialect renderings (no live PostgreSQL or\nMySQL in this suite).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ai): pin the caller-facing error policy for Postgres and MySQL\n\nCodeRabbit''s security finding asks for a generic message or a stable\ncategory instead of the driver''s own words. That is not adopted: the\nmaintainer''s review endorses the primary-message contract precisely\nbecause \"no such column: foo\" is what lets a caller fix their statement.\nWhat was missing was the policy written down and held in place by tests.\n\n_format_db_error''s docstring now states the residual it accepts: a\ndriver''s primary sentence may quote a value from the statement being\nreported (Postgres `invalid input syntax for type integer: \"abc\"`, MySQL\n`Duplicate entry ''x'' for key ''users.email''`). That value was submitted by\nthe caller now reading the message, who also holds allow_execute; values\nbelonging to other rows appear only in DETAIL / HINT / CONTEXT, which are\nstripped. Blanking quoted tokens generically is rejected because MySQL\nquotes identifiers with single quotes too, so the redaction would delete\n\"which column\" from the message that most needs it.\n\nThe tests build real DBAPIError wrappers via DBAPIError.instance, so\nstr(exc) genuinely carries the `[SQL: ...]` / `[parameters: ...]` tail and\nthe assertions bite: a Postgres duplicate-key whose DETAIL restates the\nemail, the same without .diag so the LINE echo is exercised, the MySQL\nduplicate entry that documents the accepted residual, and the MySQL\nunknown-column that documents why redaction is refused. One more drives\nboth shapes through BOTH execute paths and asserts the two messages are\nidentical -- against the pre-fix source the session-bound half raised the\nraw DBAPIError with the bound ''ada@example.com'' attached.\n\nMock-based by necessity: no live PostgreSQL or MySQL in this suite, so\nthe driver exception shapes are reproduced rather than provoked. No\nbehaviour change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): correct the lane, cache and error prose in the four SQL READMEs\n\nAll four checked against the node code and services.json rather than\nagainst each other.\n\ndb_clickhouse declares only a `questions` lane (services.json\n`lanes: {questions: [...]}`), so the sentence about schema refresh\nreaching the `answers` insert lane described a lane the node does not\nhave; it is removed. db_mysql, db_postgres and rocketride_sql do declare\n`answers`, so theirs stays — reworded, because refresh_schema\ninvalidates that column map rather than rebuilding it, and the rebuild\nhappens on the next insert.\n\n\"Return the schema reflected when the node started\" was wrong in all\nfour after refresh_schema shipped: get_schema serves whatever snapshot\nthe node currently holds, which is the start-up reflection until a\nrefresh replaces it.\n\ndb_postgres said every question on the `questions` lane is translated to\nSQL and executed. When the LLM reports the question is not a database\nquery (`isValid: false`), writeQuestions emits its prose answer and\nnothing runs against the database, so the lane is described as the\nnatural-language path with only valid generated SQL executed.\n\nEach README now states the execute error contract the code enforces: a\nfailure raises `SQL execution failed:` plus the driver''s primary\nmessage, identically with and without a session_id, with the statement\nand bound parameters kept to the server log — and with the residual\nnamed, since a driver''s primary sentence can quote a value the caller\nitself submitted.\n\nscripts/validate-node-readme.py passes for all four nodes; no GENERATED\nblock touched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): publish the auto-created table''s column map in one assignment\n\n_createTableFromData is the second writer of IGlobal.schema and still had\nthe publish-then-fill shape B4 removed from _getTableSchema: it created\nthe table, assigned `self.schema = {}`, then added one key per column,\nall outside _REFLECT_LOCK. By the time that loop runs the table exists,\nso a second first-batch can already be inside _insertData; it takes the\nlock, finds a truthy one-column map, snapshots it and silently drops\nevery other column its row carried. Reproduced by pausing the real fill\nloop: the second row landed as {''label'': ''b'', ''size'': None,\n''colour'': None} for input that supplied all three.\n\nThe map is now built in a local dict and published in a single\nassignment. The lock is NOT widened over table creation, so the\npre-existing double-create race is untouched and still out of scope.\n\nAlso seeds a stale map into a direct _getTableSchema failure test: the\nexisting rebuild-failure test starts from {}, which cannot tell \"previous\nmap left untouched\" from \"map reset to {}\". Against the pre-B4 source\nthat new test fails with the stale entry replaced by a partial one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): cut the clickhouse stack trace and position echo from execute errors\n\nThe formatter''s docstring claimed \"pymysql / clickhouse-driver put\n(errno, message) in .orig.args\". Only pymysql does. clickhouse-driver''s\nDBAPI layer raises OperationalError(ServerException), so .orig.args holds\none exception object, every branch falls through to str(orig), and what\nreached the caller was the driver''s full string form: \"Code: N.\\n\nDB::Exception: <message>. Stack trace:\\n\\n0. DB::Exception::Exception(...)\n@ 0x...\" — server internals the caller cannot act on — plus, for a syntax\nerror, the failing statement fragment quoted after \"failed at position N\n(''...'')\", which is the same class of statement echo as psycopg2''s\n\"LINE n:\".\n\n_DB_ERROR_DETAIL gains \"Stack trace:\" and \"failed at position\" as\nmarkers, so both are cut by the stripper that already handles the other\ndrivers rather than by a new branch. Neither phrase can misfire on the\nshapes the other drivers produce.\n\nThe docstring now states the real clickhouse-driver shape instead of\nclaiming the pymysql one. Two constructed tests cover it, labelled in\ntheir docstrings as reproduced from the driver''s documented behaviour:\nclickhouse-driver is not installed in this engine, so the shape cannot be\nprovoked here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ai): state the real execute error contract and schema-cache wording\n\nThree corrections to text, no behaviour change.\n\nThe error-contract paragraph overclaimed. It said values belonging to\nother rows appear only in DETAIL / HINT / CONTEXT, which are stripped.\nBoth halves of that are wrong: a real SQLite run returns\n`SQL execution failed: near \"''hunter2''\": syntax error` — the statement\nfragment the parser stopped on is in the primary sentence — and\nPostgreSQL''s `invalid input syntax for type integer: \"<value>\"` names a\nrow value an INSERT ... SELECT or a CAST merely touched, which can come\nfrom another table. pymysql and psycopg2 interpolate binds client-side,\nso such a fragment can carry a bound value too. The docstring now lists\nwhat is removed (the SQLAlchemy tail, LINE n:, DETAIL/HINT/CONTEXT/\nQUERY/STATEMENT, the ClickHouse stack trace and position echo) and what\nis passed through, and rests the policy on the argument that actually\nholds: the recipient has allow_execute and can already read the same data\nwith a SELECT, so the error channel does not widen read access. Two tests\n(one per execute path) pin the syntax-error shape on a real SQLite run.\n\nget_schema''s docstring said the schema is \"reflected once in beginGlobal\"\nand refresh_schema''s tool description — the sentence an LLM reads when\nchoosing between the two tools — said get_schema serves the snapshot\nreflected when the node started. Both became false the moment\nrefresh_schema shipped, and both contradicted the READMEs corrected for\nexactly that in c258211d.\n\n_generated_primary_keys gains a paragraph recording the SQLite-only\nresiduals of SQLAlchemy''s ''auto'' rule: INT/BIGINT/INTEGER-DESC primary\nkeys are treated as generated although SQLite does not alias them to the\nrowid, and an INTEGER PRIMARY KEY that is also a foreign key is treated\nas required although it is an alias. PostgreSQL and MySQL reflection set\nautoincrement explicitly, so production nodes resolve exactly.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): correct the error-contract and remaining schema wording\n\nTwo corrections, both to sentences this PR itself introduced or left\nbehind.\n\nThe error sentence in all four READMEs promised that \"the statement text\nand the bound parameter values are never part of it\". That is not what\nthe code does: a real run returns `SQL execution failed: near\n\"''hunter2''\": syntax error`, and PostgreSQL''s `invalid input syntax for\ntype integer: \"<value>\"` names a value an INSERT ... SELECT or a CAST\nread from another table. Each README now says what is actually removed\n(SQLAlchemy''s `[SQL:]`/`[parameters:]` echo, and per engine: PostgreSQL''s\nLINE n:/DETAIL/HINT/CONTEXT, ClickHouse''s server stack trace and its\n`failed at position` echo) and that the primary sentence is passed\nthrough as the database wrote it, so it can name a value the statement\ncarried or touched — resting the policy on the argument that holds, that\nreaching this tool requires Allow direct query execution and such a\ncaller can read the same data with a SELECT. The full text stays in the\nserver log. db_postgres moves that contract out of the already-long\n`execute` table cell into prose below the table, as the other three do.\n\nThe intro sentences in db_clickhouse and db_mysql still said\n\"startup-reflected schema\", the wording corrected everywhere else in\nc258211d; both now describe the cached snapshot and name refresh_schema.\n\nscripts/validate-node-readme.py passes for all four; no GENERATED block\ntouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): leave every database-owned column to the database on the insert lane\n\nTwo ways an explicit NULL still reached a column the database was meant\nto fill, both silent because writeAnswers only logs, and one comment that\nclaimed neither could happen.\n\n_generated_primary_keys skipped primary-key columns only, so any other\nnewly reflected column was bound NULL — and an explicit NULL overrides a\nserver default. The sequence the refresh tool invites: answers is\nauto-created as (id, q, a) with the curated map {q, a}; an agent runs\nALTER TABLE answers ADD COLUMN created_at ... NOT NULL DEFAULT now(); the\ntool description tells it to call refresh_schema, so the next batch\nrebuilds the map by reflection, binds created_at = NULL, and the batch\nthat worked a moment earlier is dropped with one log line. Reproduced on\nSQLite: NOT NULL constraint failed: answers.created_at, parameters\n(''how'', ''like this'', None). _insertData now also reads\ngenerated_defaults — every column with a server_default or an identity —\noff the reflected table and omits those the row does not supply.\n\nAn explicit null on a generated key was bound as NULL. On the execute\ntool that is right: a person wrote the statement. On the insert lane the\ncaller is an upstream node, and an LLM node or a JSON mapper emits every\nschema key, writing null for the ones it has no value for — the same\nintent as omitting it. Postgres rejects that NULL for a SERIAL column;\nMySQL and SQLite generate a value, so the failure is dialect-dependent as\nwell as silent. A supplied null on a generated primary key is now treated\nas omitted; everywhere else it is still bound as given.\n\nThe comments at refresh_schema''s invalidation and above the binding loop\nsaid the invalidation cannot change answers-lane behaviour. This PR''s own\ntest shows it can, and that half is the point of the tool. They now state\nthe guarantee that actually holds: the columns present only in the\nreflected map are omitted rather than bound NULL, so the database still\nfills in what it owns.\n\nAlso drops the unreachable \"no schema cached\" branch: after the locked\nblock IGlobal.schema is populated or the method has raised, so the\nsnapshot is always truthy. The empty-map half of the lock rationale went\nwith it; the half-built-map half, which the concurrency test proves,\nstays.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): format driver errors the dialect did not wrap in a SQLAlchemy exception\n\nThe error contract this PR documents was not delivered on the ClickHouse\nnode at all. db_clickhouse connects with `clickhouse+native://`, which is\nclickhouse-sqlalchemy''s own connector; its Cursor.execute raises\n`clickhouse_sqlalchemy.exceptions.DatabaseException`, a plain Exception\nsubclass carrying the driver''s ServerException in `.orig`. SQLAlchemy\ndoes not wrap a non-DBAPI exception, so `except SQLAlchemyError` never\nfired on EITHER execute path, `_format_db_error` never ran, and the\ncaller got `Orig exception: Code: 62.\\nSyntax error: failed at position\n21 (''hunter2'') ... Stack trace:\\n\\n0. DB::Exception::Exception(...)` —\nraw, on both paths. The markers added in 27a04f68 were verified against\nclickhouse-driver''s own DBAPI layer, which this node does not use, so\nthey were unreachable for the node they were added for.\n\nBoth paths now handle a non-SQLAlchemy exception that carries a driver\n`.orig`: log it and raise the same `SQL execution failed: <formatted>`\nRuntimeError, releasing the session on the session path as before. Duck\n-typed on `.orig` rather than importing clickhouse modules into the\nshared base — a driver error carrying its original is precisely the shape\nbeing handled, and the base must not depend on one node''s driver.\nAnything without `.orig` is re-raised untouched, so the max_execute_rows\nRuntimeError keeps its wording and rollback and a programming error still\nsurfaces as itself.\n\n_format_db_error also reads `.code` and `.message` off the original when\nthey are an int and a string: clickhouse-driver''s ServerException puts\nthe code there rather than in args, so the numeric branch missed it and\nthe caller lost the code. ClickHouse now reads like pymysql —\n`Error 60: Table default.widgets doesn''t exist` — and the message still\nruns through the stripper, so the stack trace and the `failed at\nposition` fragment inside it are cut.\n\nThe two existing clickhouse formatter tests are relabelled: they cover\nclickhouse_driver.dbapi''s shape, which this node never produces. New\ntests cover the connector''s real shape at the formatter and drive it\nthrough both execute paths, asserting no stack trace, no statement\nfragment, and a released session; two more assert that an exception\nwithout `.orig` is not swallowed. All labelled as constructed shapes —\nclickhouse-sqlalchemy is not installed in this engine.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): reject a malformed params argument instead of misreporting it\n\n`to_sqlalchemy_text` turns `$n` into a bind by indexing `params[n - 1]`,\nso a JSON object for `params` raised `KeyError: 0` and a short list an\n`IndexError`, both from inside the rewriter. The sessionless path leaked\nthose bare; worse, on the session path the `KeyError` was caught by the\narm that maps a missing session id, so `execute` reported \"unknown or\nexpired transaction session: <sid>\" for a session that was still open and\nstill usable — the caller was told to abandon a live transaction because\nthey passed the wrong JSON type.\n\n`execute` now validates before dispatching anything: `params` must be\nabsent or an array, and every `$n` the statement references must have a\nvalue, with the offending placeholder named. tx_registry.py is untouched\n— the check lives in `execute` and imports the placeholder pattern from\nthe module that owns it so it cannot drift from the rewriter it guards.\n\nAn empty list keeps its current meaning of \"no binds\", the same as\nomitting `params`, because a statement carrying a literal `$n` inside a\nstring body relies on the rewriter leaving it alone.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): document the insert-lane null rule and the clickhouse error layer\n\nThe three nodes that declare an `answers` lane now state, beside the\nexisting insert prose, what the code does with a value the row does not\ncarry: a column with a generated key or a DEFAULT is left out of the\nstatement so the database supplies it, rather than bound as an explicit\nNULL that would override the default; a generated primary key supplied as\n`null` counts as not carried, because the sender on this lane is an\nupstream node that may emit every schema key, while a `null` anywhere\nelse is inserted as given. db_postgres also records the rejection of a\nprimary key it cannot generate. db_clickhouse declares no `answers` lane,\nso none of this applies there.\n\ndb_clickhouse''s error sentence now describes the layer that actually\nraises: `clickhouse+native://` does not produce a DBAPI error, so the\nnode unwraps the driver error it carries, and the message is prefixed\nwith the server''s own `Error <code>:`.\n\nThe comment above refresh_schema''s invalidation also drops the last\nsentence that was still too strong. It said the database fills in what it\nowns either way, which holds only for a column the row omits. For a\nsupplied primary key the two maps genuinely differ: on an auto-created\ntable the curated map has no `id`, so a row carrying one has it silently\ndropped and the database generates a different value, and after a refresh\nthe reflected map binds it and the row''s own value is kept. The comment\nnow says that, and notes that curating the key into the auto-created map\nwould be a separate decision.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ai,nodes): tighten the refresh comment and two README sentences\n\nComments and prose only; no behaviour change.\n\nThe refresh_schema comment said _insertData \"leaves out any primary-key\nor server-default column the rows do NOT supply, so a column present only\nin the reflected map is omitted rather than bound NULL\". Two clauses\noverreached: a primary key the database cannot generate is rejected with\na ValueError when the rows omit it, not left out; and a plain nullable\ncolumn with no default that appears only in the reflected map IS bound\nNULL. The guarantee named in the sentence before it still holds -- the\nstored value is the NULL the database would have used anyway -- so the\nsentence now says that instead of describing a mechanism the code does\nnot have.\n\nrocketride_sql''s insert-lane rule had been spliced onto the end of the\n\"Table name and database description\" paragraph, running the two subjects\ntogether; it now ends a paragraph of its own. db_clickhouse''s \"unwraps\nits exception by the driver error it carries\" reads as intended:\n\"unwraps it to the driver error it carries\".\n\nTwo directories: 194 passed. Validator passes for both edited nodes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): regenerate the DB node parameter tables\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, nit 5) flagged that the\nROCKETRIDE:GENERATED:PARAMS regions of the four DB node READMEs had been\nhand-edited, which AGENTS.md:87-88 forbids, and that\nrocketride_sql/README.md carried an EMPTY region even though this PR\nchanged its services.json.\n\nDevelop has since filled that region: e035c26c3 (#2285) regenerated it\nfrom the pre-PR wording, so merging develop reinstated\n\"Permit QuestionType.EXECUTE callers to run raw SQL...\" — the exact claim\nthis PR corrected at the source (services.json:125 now says \"Permit the\nexecute, begin, commit, and rollback tool functions...\").\n\nRegenerated with the repo generator rather than by hand: a scratch\n`git archive HEAD | tar -x` copy with a fresh `git init` (so the\nbranch-name gate at nodes/scripts/gen-node-tables.mjs:211 passes and the\nsource links resolve to the upstream fallbacks\nrocketride-org/rocketride-server @ develop, not this fork''s origin), then\n`node nodes/scripts/gen-node-tables.mjs db_mysql db_postgres\ndb_clickhouse rocketride_sql`. Only rocketride_sql/README.md changed;\nthe generator reproduced the other three regions byte-for-byte, which\nalso settles dylan''s hand-edit concern for them — they already match\ngenerator output.\n\nVerified: python3 scripts/validate-node-readme.py PASS on all four nodes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): anchor the PostgreSQL block markers and stop stripping diag.message_primary\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 4) showed that _DB_ERROR_DETAIL\nmatched DETAIL:/HINT:/CONTEXT:/QUERY:/STATEMENT: as bare substrings with\n.search(), so the stripper truncated messages that merely contain one of\nthose words, and that every branch — psycopg2''s already-clean\ndiag.message_primary included — ran through it. All three of his shapes\nreproduced on the PR head:\n\n  RAISE EXCEPTION ''Order rejected. HINT: contact billing''\n      -> ''Order rejected.''\n  pymysql (1644, ''Invalid QUERY: missing tenant filter'')\n      -> ''Error 1644: Invalid''\n  a message whose first token is ''HINT:''\n      -> ''Database error''\n\nThe five PostgreSQL blocks are separate LINES of the server message, so\nthey are now matched only after a newline (\\n[ \\t]*), while the tails a\nlibrary appends — [SQL:, [parameters:, [cached since, [generated in,\n(Background on this error, LINE n:, Stack trace:, failed at position —\nstay unanchored, because those genuinely appear mid-string and anchoring\nthem would reopen the leak this PR exists to close.\n\nThe anchor is \\n rather than (?m)^ on purpose: ^ also matches at position\n0, which is what turned a message beginning with ''HINT:'' into\nall-detail and degraded it to the fallback constant.\n\n_format_db_error now returns diag.message_primary without the stripper.\npsycopg2 splits DETAIL/HINT/CONTEXT/QUERY/STATEMENT and the LINE n: echo\nonto their own diag fields, so that string carries no tail to cut and a\nmarker firing in it could only be truncating the application''s own\nwording.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_base.py): the three shapes above\nplus a message whose inline HINT: is followed by a real DETAIL: line\n(only the line goes), and test_format_db_error_still_cuts_a_real_postgres_detail_block,\nwhich pins that the anchoring does not let a genuine DETAIL block\nthrough on the no-diag path. Against the pre-fix code the four new\nassertions failed: \"4 failed, 1 passed, 65 deselected in 0.52s\".\n\nNo live PostgreSQL or MySQL is available here, so each case is a\nconstructed exception shape reproducing what the driver hands SQLAlchemy;\nevery new test''s docstring says so.\n\nVerified: 243 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): cut ClickHouse''s in-message statement echoes\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 3) pointed out that _DB_ERROR_DETAIL\nknew only ClickHouse''s `Stack trace:` and `failed at position` tails, and\nthat ClickHouse does not always append its statement echo — code 47\n(UNKNOWN_IDENTIFIER) splices the whole query into the MIDDLE of the\nsentence. Reproduced on the PR head by running the module''s own regex:\n\n  Code: 47. DB::Exception: Missing columns: ''secret'' while processing\n  query: ''SELECT secret FROM users WHERE token = ''''hunter2'''''', required\n  columns: ''secret''. Stack trace:…\n\nwas cut only at `Stack trace:`, so the statement — with the bound value\nalready inlined — reached the caller. The analyzer''s `in scope <sql>`\nform leaked the same way.\n\nThree markers added: `while processing query:`, `, required columns:`,\nand `in scope `. The first two are distinctive enough as bare\nsubstrings; `in scope ` carries a lookahead for an uppercase statement\nkeyword, because ClickHouse formats the echoed query back out of its AST\n(so the phrase is always followed by SELECT/INSERT/… there) while in\nprose — \"not in scope for this trigger\" — it is not. That is the same\nlesson as the DETAIL/HINT anchoring in the previous commit: a marker that\ncan fire inside an application''s own sentence will.\n\nThis dialect is the one where the echo genuinely carries user data:\nclickhouse-driver sends no server-side parameters by default, so it\ninterpolates binds client-side and the repeated statement contains the\ncaller''s literal rather than a placeholder. The docstring is reconciled\naccordingly — its opening line no longer claims \"never the statement or\nits parameters\" flatly but points at the REMOVED / PASSED THROUGH\nsection, the ClickHouse bullet now names the two in-message echoes and\nthe client-side interpolation, and the \"every branch runs through the\nstripper\" sentence now excepts the diag.message_primary branch the\nprevious commit took off it.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_base.py): the code-47 shape on\nboth connectors — clickhouse_driver.dbapi''s OperationalError(\nServerException) and clickhouse-sqlalchemy''s DatabaseException, which is\nwhat the db_clickhouse node''s `clickhouse+native://` URL actually raises\n— plus a lone `, required columns:` tail and a guard that prose\ncontaining \"in scope\" is not truncated. Against the pre-fix code:\n\"3 failed, 1 passed, 70 deselected in 0.57s\" (the fourth is a guard and\npassed before too).\n\nNo live ClickHouse server is available here and neither clickhouse-driver\nnor clickhouse-sqlalchemy is installed, so both connector shapes are\nconstructed from the message forms the server documents; each test\ndocstring says so.\n\nVerified: 247 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): format _validateQuery''s EXPLAIN error before returning it\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, nit 1) noted that _validateQuery''s\n`except Exception as e: return False, str(e)` made the PR description''s\nsecurity claim false. The EXPLAIN failure does not stop at the retry\nloop: _buildSQLQuery carries it into `result[''error'']`, get_sql returns\nthat as its `error` field, and get_data returns get_sql''s dict\nunchanged. Neither tool consults IGlobal.allow_execute — the gate exists\nonly in execute() — so `str()` of a SQLAlchemy StatementError, tail and\nall, reached callers who were never granted raw SQL. On the pre-fix code\nthe string was:\n\n  (sqlite3.OperationalError) no such table: no_such_table\n  [SQL: EXPLAIN SELECT secret FROM no_such_table WHERE token = ''hunter2'']\n  (Background on this error at: https://sqlalche.me/e/20/e3q8)\n\nNow `self._format_db_error(e)`, the same formatter execute() uses, and\nthe full exception is logged with warning() first so the statement stays\nin the server log where the retry loop''s own log line used to keep it.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_execute_errors.py): the helper\ndirectly, then both ungated lanes — get_sql and get_data with\nallow_execute switched OFF and a stubbed _buildSQLQueryOnce that keeps\nproposing a query against a missing table — asserting the driver''s\nsentence survives while ''[SQL:'', ''[parameters:'' and the bound literal do\nnot. Plus the success half, `(True, '''')`, which is unchanged. Against\nthe pre-fix code: \"3 failed, 1 passed, 40 deselected in 0.55s\".\n\nVerified: 251 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): read an explicit null on any database-owned column as omitted\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 7) pointed out the asymmetry the\nprevious round left behind: the OMITTED branch skips a generated key AND\nanything carrying a server default or identity, while the EXPLICIT-null\nbranch only skipped generated keys. So `{''created_at'': None}` against\n`created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()` fell through to\n`prepare_value(None)` and bound NULL. Reproduced on SQLite, which raises\nthe same class of error:\n\n  sqlite3.IntegrityError: NOT NULL constraint failed: notes.created_at\n  [SQL: INSERT INTO notes (label, created_at) VALUES (?, ?)]\n  [parameters: (''a'', None)]\n\nand on the answers lane writeAnswers only logs, so the batch vanished\nwith one line — the exact failure mode the generated-key rule was added\nto prevent.\n\nThe condition now reads `lowered in generated_pk_columns or lowered in\ngenerated_defaults`, i.e. the same column set as the omitted branch.\nSplitting the rule by nullability or by key-ness would make one emitted\nrow land differently depending on a column attribute its sender — an LLM\nnode or a JSON mapper that emits every schema key — cannot see.\n\nThe three insert-lane README paragraphs said \"a null on any other column\nis inserted as NULL as given\"; they now say a null on any column the\ndatabase fills in counts as not carried, and a null on a column with\nnothing behind it is inserted as given. Hand-written prose only; the\nGENERATED:PARAMS regions are untouched (validate-node-readme passes on\nall four DB nodes).\n\nThis is a behaviour change for a pipeline that deliberately writes NULL\ninto a column that happens to carry a DEFAULT — it now gets the default\ninstead. The `execute` tool is a different contract and is unaffected.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_execute_errors.py): the NOT NULL\nDEFAULT case dylan named, the nullable-default case, a column with\nnothing behind it still binding NULL, and one showing the run-splitting\nthis collapses — a batch alternating an explicit null and an omission is\nnow a single executemany instead of one run per row. The narrow-rule pin\ntest_insert_still_binds_an_explicit_null_on_a_non_key_column is removed:\nit asserted the behaviour this item reverses, and the two new cases cover\nboth halves of what replaced it. Against the pre-fix code:\n\"3 failed, 3 passed, 42 deselected in 0.78s\".\n\nVerified: 254 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean, validate-node-readme PASS on db_postgres, db_mysql,\ndb_clickhouse and rocketride_sql.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): guard refresh_schema''s reflection and keep both caches on failure\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 5) pointed out that\n`self.IGlobal.db_schema = self.IGlobal._getDatabaseSchema()` ran\nunguarded inside the locked block. _getDatabaseSchema has no per-table\ntry/except — one `inspector.get_columns(table)` the database refuses (a\nrevoked grant, a lock timeout, a table dropped mid-walk) propagates out\nof the whole walk — so refresh_schema was the one tool in this file\nwhose failure still reached the caller as the SQLAlchemy repr, tail\nincluded, and this tool has no allow_execute gate in front of it.\n\nThe reflection now runs in a try/except: the full exception goes to the\nlog with error(), and the caller gets\n`RuntimeError(''Schema refresh failed: '' + _format_db_error(e))` with\n`from None`, matching execute()''s shape.\n\nPublication is all-or-nothing, and the docstring now says so and why.\nThe result is assigned only after the walk returns, so a failure leaves\ndb_schema AND IGlobal.schema exactly as they were — the node keeps\nserving the schema it has instead of publishing a half-walked database,\nand IGlobal.schema is not emptied, which would otherwise re-arm the\ninsert lane''s lazy rebuild against a database that just refused to be\nreflected. Skipping the offending table and continuing (what\n_getTableSchema does for a single table) is rejected explicitly: the LLM\npath cannot tell a table that vanished from one that could not be read,\nand would write queries as if it were gone.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_execute_errors.py): a patched\ninspector whose get_columns raises a wrapped driver error for one table\n— injected, since no PostgreSQL is available here to revoke a grant on,\nand injected after get_table_names so the walk fails part-way as a real\none would. Three cases: the message carries the driver''s sentence and\nneither ''[SQL:'' nor the reflection statement; both caches are byte-equal\nto their pre-refresh values and a table created in between does not\nappear; and _REFLECT_LOCK — process-wide, so a leak would wedge every\nnode in the engine — is free afterwards and the next refresh succeeds.\nAgainst the pre-fix code: \"3 failed, 1 passed, 46 deselected in 0.65s\".\n\nVerified: 257 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): format an ODBC (sqlstate, message) pair instead of returning the code\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, nit 6) noticed that the generic\n`args[0]` branch returns the SQLSTATE alone for a driver that reports\n`(''42S02'', ''Base table or view not found'')` — pyodbc and the other\nODBC-backed drivers put a five-character SQLSTATE where pymysql puts an\ninteger errno, so the integer branch misses them and the caller gets the\nhalf of the pair they cannot act on. The str(exc) fallback test this PR\nremoved used to cover the shape incidentally.\n\nA branch before the generic one formats it as `Error <sqlstate>:\n<message>`, matched strictly against `\\A[0-9A-Z]{5}\\Z` and requiring a\nnon-empty string second entry, so a driver that merely happens to put\ntwo strings in `args` keeps the old behaviour. The result still runs\nthrough the stripper.\n\ndylan notes correctly that no node in this repo uses such a driver today\n(there is no pyodbc dialect under nodes/src/nodes), so this is hardening\nfor a shape we do not ship rather than a live bug; the docstring bullet\nsays as much.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_base.py): the pair formatted, the\nstripper still cutting a STATEMENT block inside it, a two-string `args`\nthat is not a SQLSTATE left alone, and the single-argument sqlite3 shape\nunchanged. Constructed shapes — no ODBC driver is installed here. Against\nthe pre-fix code: \"2 failed, 2 passed, 74 deselected in 0.46s\" (the two\nguard cases passed before, which is the point of them).\n\nVerified: 261 passed (tests/database + tests/ai/common/database), ruff\ncheck + format clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): give each database node its own reflection lock\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 8): `_REFLECT_LOCK` was module-level\nand held across `refresh_schema`''s full-database inspector walk, which runs\non an engine with no statement timeout. `_insertData` took the same lock\nunconditionally on every batch, just to evaluate `if not IGlobal.schema`. So\none node refreshing a wide database stalled the answers lane of every other\nDB node in the same engine process -- cross-node blocking the pre-PR code\nnever had, since two nodes share no cache and have nothing to race on.\n\nThe lock is now per-IGlobal, created in `beginGlobal` (`reflect_lock`,\ndeclared `None` on the class so a global that never ran `beginGlobal` fails\nloudly instead of silently sharing class state). Both writers rebind\n`schema` / `db_schema` wholesale and neither reads the other node''s, so the\nstate the lock guards is exactly one node''s.\n\n`_insertData` still takes the lock on every batch, and the comment now says\nwhy that is the deliberate choice: an uncontended acquire is nanoseconds\nagainst an insert about to do network I/O, while a check-then-lock fast path\nneeds the map read once into a local and snapshotted from THAT -- read twice,\na `refresh_schema` landing between the two hands the batch an empty map --\nwhich is a second path no test pins. What made the unconditional acquire\nexpensive was its process-wide scope, and that is what changed.\n\nAlso item nit 4 (DS-N4), first half: `refresh_schema` rendered the tool\npayload inside the lock. It is pure formatting, so it now runs after the\nrelease, over the local `refreshed` map rather than `IGlobal.db_schema` --\na refresh that overtook this one has already published its own map, and this\ncall still owes its caller the schema it reflected. The second half of that\nnit (rebuilding `IGlobal.schema` from `db_schema[table][''columns'']` instead\nof emptying it) is left alone: it changes the map''s value shape and the\ntable-is-gone case that `test_refresh_schema_empties_the_column_map_when_the_table_is_gone`\npins, so it is not a nit-sized change.\n\nTest: `test_a_refresh_does_not_block_another_nodes_insert` builds two\nglobals over two separate SQLite files, pauses one node''s refresh inside the\ncolumn walk, and asserts the other node''s insert completes while it is held.\nAgainst the pre-fix code it fails with \"the second node''s insert waited for\nthe first node''s refresh\" (1 failed, 50 deselected in 5.63s). The five\nexisting deterministic concurrency tests are unchanged in substance; the two\nthat assert the lock is not stranded now acquire the node''s own\n`reflect_lock`, and the fixture builds one per global the way `beginGlobal`\ndoes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(ai): pin the post-refresh supplied-id flip on an auto-created table\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 9): the comment in `refresh_schema`\nconcedes that on an auto-created table the curated column map has no `id`, so\na row carrying one has it dropped, while after a refresh the reflected map\nbinds it -- and asks for that flip to be either removed or pinned by a test.\nNothing exercised it: `test_insert_after_refresh_does_not_bind_the_generated_primary_key`\nonly inserts rows that OMIT `id`.\n\n`test_a_supplied_id_is_dropped_before_a_refresh_and_bound_after` auto-creates\nthrough the real path, asserts the compiled column list of an insert that\nsupplies `id` before the refresh ({q, a}) and after it ({id, q, a}), and\nchecks the stored rows. Its docstring names the consequence the SQLite\nfixtures cannot show: PostgreSQL advances a SERIAL sequence only for rows\nthat take the DEFAULT, so a bound `id` of 501 leaves the sequence at 3 and\nthe next generated row collides on the primary key, which `writeAnswers`\nlogs and drops.\n\nNo behaviour change -- this is characterisation only. Curating `id` into\n`_createTableFromData`''s map so both maps agree is the alternative dylan\noffers; it is a product decision about what an auto-created table promises\nand is not taken here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): name the trigger-populated primary key as a known limitation\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, item 6): `_insertData` rejects a row that\nomits a primary key with no reflected `server_default` / identity /\nautoincrement. A `CHAR(36)` key filled by a `BEFORE INSERT` trigger -- the\nstandard MySQL UUID idiom before 8.0.13, same shape on PostgreSQL -- reflects\nexactly that way, so the batch is refused before the trigger can run, and\n`writeAnswers` turns that `ValueError` into one log line and drops every row.\nHe asks for this to be documented at minimum.\n\nThis is the documentation half. The three insert-lane sections (db_postgres,\ndb_mysql, rocketride_sql; db_clickhouse has no answers lane) now name the\ntrigger case, say that the answers lane logs and drops the batch rather than\nreporting it, and give the workaround: a real column default\n(`DEFAULT (uuid())` on MySQL 8.0.13+, `DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()` on\nPostgreSQL) reflects and is honoured. db_mysql''s paragraph also gained the\nrejection sentence itself, which only the other two READMEs carried. All\nedits are hand-written prose; no GENERATED:PARAMS region is touched.\n\n`_generated_primary_keys`''s docstring gains the same paragraph, stated as the\nfalse positive it is: reflection describes columns, not triggers.\n\nThe stronger option dylan offers -- drop the pre-flight rejection, bind\nnothing, let the driver''s error come back through `_format_db_error` -- is\nnot taken here. It costs the guarantee that a refused batch leaves nothing\nbehind, which is what makes the current rejection safe, so it is put to him\nas a question on the thread rather than decided here.\n\nVerified: validate-node-readme.py PASS for all four DB nodes; 263 passed in\npackages/ai/tests/database + packages/ai/tests/ai/common/database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(ai): make _getTableSchema the only publisher of IGlobal.schema\n\ndylan-savage (review 5215826786, nit 3), two parts.\n\n`_getTableSchema` already assigns `self.schema` and then returns the same\ncolumns as pairs; `beginGlobal` and `_insertData` rebuilt the map from those\npairs and assigned it again, comment-less. The rebuild was not a no-op only\nin that it replaced each reflected column comment with '''' -- and this map is\nread exclusively by `_insertData`, by key. Both rebuilds are gone; the two\ncall sites now test the return value and say why there is nothing to assign.\n`beginGlobal` keeps its one real assignment, `self.schema = {}` for a table\nthat does not exist yet, which is the case `_getTableSchema` leaves untouched.\nBehaviour for every reader is unchanged: same keys, same order, in the same\nsingle atomic rebind.\n\nThe comment above the `dict()` snapshot claimed it guarded an in-place\nmutation. No publisher mutates in place any more -- both build a map and\nrebind it, which is what they were fixed for earlier in this PR -- so the\ncomment now says what the snapshot actually buys: binding the map to a local\nis what stops a `refresh_schema` mid-batch swapping the map the per-row loop\nis walking, and copying it is what keeps that true if a publisher ever fills\none in place again.\n\nNo behaviour change. 263 passed in packages/ai/tests/database +\npackages/ai/tests/ai/common/database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): cut a parenthesised ClickHouse in-scope echo as well\n\nThe `in scope ` marker''s lookahead required the uppercase statement\nkeyword to follow the phrase immediately, so it fired on the analyzer''s\n`in scope SELECT ...` echo but not on `in scope (SELECT ...)`.\nClickHouse formats a scope that is itself a subquery or a CTE body\nthrough ASTSubquery, which parenthesises it, and clickhouse-driver\ninterpolates bind values client-side -- so that form carries the\ncaller''s literal straight through the stripper.\n\nThe lookahead now allows one optional `(` before the keyword. Added\ntest_format_db_error_clickhouse_cuts_a_parenthesised_subquery_scope,\nwhich fails against the previous regex with the whole subquery (and its\nbound literal) still in the message. No live ClickHouse server or driver\nis available here, so the shape is constructed from the documented\nmessage form, as the neighbouring cases are; the test docstring says so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): name the table a refresh lost instead of echoing a bare noun\n\n`refresh_schema`''s guard formats every reflection failure through\n`_format_db_error`, which reads the driver''s own sentence. The one case\ndylan-savage''s item 5 names that has no such sentence is a table dropped\nbetween `get_table_names` and its `get_columns`: SQLAlchemy raises\n`NoSuchTableError`, built from the table name alone and carrying no\n`.orig`, so the generic branch returned `args[0]` and the caller saw\n\"Schema refresh failed: ghost\" -- a bare noun with no verb.\n\nA `NoSuchTableError` arm before the generic one now says what happened\n(\"table \\\"ghost\\\" disappeared while it was being reflected\") and logs the\nsame sentence server-side. The all-or-nothing publication is unchanged:\nthe arm raises before either cache is assigned.\n\nAdded test_refresh_schema_names_a_table_dropped_mid_walk, which fails\nagainst the previous code with the bare table name. The drop is injected\nthrough the inspector rather than raced against a live server, and the\ndocstring says so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): name beginGlobal when a global reaches the lock without one\n\n`reflect_lock` defaults to None on DatabaseGlobalBase so that a global\nbuilt without `beginGlobal` cannot silently share one lock across every\nnode in the process. The failure it produced was loud but anonymous:\n`with None:` raises \"''NoneType'' object does not support the context\nmanager protocol\", naming neither the attribute nor the call that\ncreates it, and on the answers lane `writeAnswers` reduces any exception\nto a single log line -- the quiet failure this PR removes elsewhere.\n\n`_insertData` and `refresh_schema` now take the lock through\n`_reflectLock()`, which raises RuntimeError(''reflect_lock is created in\nbeginGlobal; this global never ran it''). No production path reaches it:\nno DB node overrides `beginGlobal`. Added\ntest_a_global_that_never_ran_begin_global_names_the_missing_lock, which\nfails against the previous code with the bare TypeError.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ai): correct the explicit-null sentence in _generated_primary_keys\n\nThe docstring still described the pre-item-7 rule -- \"anywhere else a\nsupplied null is bound as given\" -- which caf5bc6e4 made false when it\nwidened the explicit-null branch to every server-default and identity\ncolumn, and the trigger-limitation paragraph added in fd5e29eb7 had been\nstitched onto that sentence, leaving a 130-character line.\n\nThe paragraph is now its own, and states the live rule: the generated\nkeys this returns plus the server-default / identity columns _insertData\nreads off the reflected table are one set, and an explicit null on any of\nthem means \"no value\". Documentation only; no behaviour change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(nodes): state refresh_schema''s failure contract in the DB READMEs\n\nce2058618 gave the refresh_schema tool a public failure contract -- a\nreflection the database refuses raises `Schema refresh failed:` and\nleaves both cached schemas untouched, rather than publishing a\nhalf-walked database -- and a3b1a54e2 named the table for the\ndropped-mid-walk case. None of the four node READMEs said so, which\nAGENTS.md''s documentation rule requires of a contract change.\n\nOne sentence per refresh_schema paragraph in db_postgres, db_mysql,\ndb_clickhouse and rocketride_sql. Hand-written prose only: no\nROCKETRIDE:GENERATED:PARAMS region is touched. db_clickhouse''s sentence\nsays \"the cached schema\" rather than \"both cached schemas\" because that\nnode has no answers lane and its README never introduces the second\ncache. validate-node-readme.py PASSes on all four.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): cut ClickHouse''s sentence-initial In scope echo too\n\ncd4992aaf and 526f247da matched `in scope ` case-sensitively, which\ncovers only the two or three analyzer messages per release that splice\nthe phrase into the middle of a sentence (\"Unknown expression identifier\n''{}'' in scope {}\"). The analyzer''s common form ends the sentence first\nand starts a new one -- \"There are no table sources. In scope {}\",\n\"Compound identifier ''{}'' cannot be resolved as {}. In scope {}\",\n\"Multiple expressions with the same alias {}. In scope {}\" -- and that\ncapitalised form appears in dozens of messages per release across 24.3\nthrough 26.8. Both substitute the same `formatASTForErrorMessage`\noutput, so both hand back the statement with the caller''s bind values\nalready inlined by clickhouse-driver; matching only the lowercase form\nleft the majority of the analyzer''s messages echoing the query.\n\nThe marker becomes `[Ii]n scope ` with the same uppercase-keyword\nlookahead, so prose is guarded exactly as before: \"not in scope for this\ntrigger\", \"In scope for this policy\", \"in scope select 1\" and \"in scope\nSELECTION\" all pass through untouched. The residual is unchanged in\nkind and now applies to either case -- an application''s own text with an\nuppercase SQL keyword right after the phrase (\"in scope WITH respect\nto\") is still cut.\n\nAdds test_format_db_error_clickhouse_cuts_a_sentence_initial_in_scope_echo\non the released `There are no table sources. In scope {}` shape through\nthe connector db_clickhouse uses; it fails against the previous regex\nwith the whole statement and the literal present. The prose guard test\nis unchanged and still passes.\n\nAlso rewraps the refresh_schema docstring paragraph a3b1a54e2 left\nragged; no wording change there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ai): state the session-after-error recovery policy in the execute tool\n\ndylan-savage, review item 2: \"whichever policy wins should be stated in the\nerror message\". The policy that won at the develop merge is #1908''s — a failed\nstatement leaves the session open and the client recovers — but it cannot go\nin the error message: asclearuc''s contract is that the two execute paths\nreport a driver failure with identical bytes, and\n`test_both_execute_paths_report_a_driver_failure_identically` pins it. A\nsession-only suffix would break that invariant.\n\nOption (b), offered in the PR thread and taken here: state the policy where\ncallers actually read it, and pin it with a test so it cannot drift.\n\n- `execute`''s tool description now carries it: a failed statement inside a\n  `session_id` rolls nothing back and leaves the session open; recover with\n  `rollback`, or `rollback to savepoint <name>`, before sending more work on\n  that session; on PostgreSQL the transaction stays aborted until then and\n  `commit` is refused. Every agent reads this string before calling the tool.\n- `rocketride_sql` and `db_clickhouse` READMEs gain the same paragraph in\n  their `execute`/session prose; `db_postgres` (:97) and `db_mysql` (:95-102)\n  already state it and are unchanged.\n- The comment on the session error arm now says why the message is silent and\n  where the policy lives instead.\n\nThe behaviour and every error string are unchanged; the error text stays\nbyte-identical on both paths.\n\nTest: `test_the_execute_tool_description_states_the_session_recovery_policy`\nreads `execute.__tool_meta__[''description'']` and asserts the three clauses.\nFails before this commit (the description was two sentences long).\n267 -> 268 passed across tests/database + tests/ai/common/database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ai): let the database refuse a primary key the node cannot see\n\nBEHAVIOUR CHANGE, and it reverses an earlier round of this PR.\n\ndylan-savage, review item 6: `_insertData` raised a ValueError for a row that\nomitted a primary key with no reflected server_default / identity /\nautoincrement. Reflection describes columns, not triggers, so the standard\npre-8.0.13 MySQL UUID idiom -- a CHAR(36) key filled by a BEFORE INSERT\ntrigger, same shape on PostgreSQL -- reflected exactly like a key nobody\nsupplies. The row was refused before the transaction opened, so the trigger\nnever ran, and on the answers lane `writeAnswers` reduces any exception to one\nlog line: the whole batch disappeared silently. He offered documenting the\nlimitation or letting the database enforce it; the stronger option is taken.\n\nThe node no longer decides. An omitted primary key it cannot see a generator\nfor is left OUT of the INSERT (never bound NULL -- an explicit NULL suppresses\na trigger value and a server default alike), so a trigger or a default the\nreflection missed can fill it. Where nothing does, the database refuses the\nrow, and:\n\n- its own message comes back through `_format_db_error`, as\n  `Insert into \"<table>\" failed: <driver sentence>`, with SQLAlchemy''s\n  `[SQL: ...]` / `[parameters: ...]` echo stripped the same way `execute`\n  strips it. A failure that is not a database failure is re-raised untouched.\n- nothing of the batch is written. Every run shares one `engine.begin()`, so\n  the rollback covers the rows that had already gone out. (A table\n  auto-created earlier in the same call is DDL and does survive.)\n- on the `answers` lane `writeAnswers` still only logs it -- unchanged, and\n  now the log carries the driver''s reason instead of the node''s guess.\n\nSQLAlchemy emits a SAWarning for a statement that leaves a primary key\nunbound. It is correct and is not silenced; it lands in the server log.\n\nDocs: `_generated_primary_keys` docstring, the loop comment and the\ninsert-lane paragraphs of db_postgres, db_mysql and rocketride_sql READMEs\nlose the \"rejected\" wording and describe the new behaviour and the trigger\ncase. No services.json changed, so no GENERATED:PARAMS region moved.\n\nTests (tests/ai/common/database/test_db_execute_errors.py): the three that\npinned the rejection are replaced --\n`test_insert_omits_a_text_primary_key_a_trigger_might_fill` (the trigger shape:\nTEXT PK, no default, column omitted and left to the database),\n`test_insert_leaves_a_sole_primary_key_the_database_does_not_generate_to_the_database`,\n`test_insert_omits_a_composite_primary_key_column_the_row_does_not_carry` --\nplus `test_insert_surfaces_the_drivers_refusal_of_an_ungenerated_primary_key`\n(NOT NULL PK: the driver sentence surfaces formatted, no row survives) and\n`test_insert_still_lets_an_integer_primary_key_generate_itself`. The four new\nbehaviours fail before this commit with the old ValueError.\n268 -> 270 passed across tests/database + tests/ai/common/database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(ai): one _isDbFailure predicate for the driver-error except arms\n\ndylan-savage, review 5215826786 nit 2: \"The driver-error -> RuntimeError\nconversion and the .orig duck-test are copy-pasted into four except arms and\nalready drift in log wording. One _isDbFailure(e) predicate with a single\nexcept Exception arm per site is behaviourally identical.\"\n\nThe log wording does NOT drift within a site — both arms of `execute`''s\nsession block logged `Error executing raw SQL in session {session_id}` and\nboth arms of `_executeRawQuery` logged `Error executing raw SQL query`; the\ndifference is BETWEEN the two sites and is deliberate. The duplication is\nreal, and this takes the refactor.\n\n`_isDbFailure(exc)` is a module-level predicate next to the other helpers:\nTrue for a SQLAlchemy exception or for anything carrying `.orig` (the\n`clickhouse+native://` connector''s unwrapped `DatabaseException`), False for\neverything else. Each site now has ONE `except Exception as e:` arm that\nre-raises when the predicate says no and formats when it says yes. Three\nsites, not two: `_insertData`''s arm asks the same question since the previous\ncommit, so it uses the same predicate.\n\nBehaviour-preserving by construction and by the existing tests:\n\n- the `max_execute_rows` RuntimeError is not a SQLAlchemyError and has no\n  `.orig`, so it is still re-raised with its pinned wording and its rollback;\n- the two log prefixes are unchanged;\n- `except KeyError -> ValueError(''unknown or expired transaction session'')`\n  stays ahead of the collapsed arm;\n- the error strings are identical, so\n  `test_both_execute_paths_report_a_driver_failure_identically` is untouched.\n\nThe proof is that every existing test passes unchanged. Added only\n`test_is_db_failure_answers_for_every_shape_the_except_arms_used_to_test`,\nwhich pins the four cases the old arms distinguished; it fails before this\ncommit with ImportError.\n270 -> 271 passed across tests/database + tests/ai/common/database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* perf(ai): rebuild the insert-lane column map from the refresh''s own walk\n\ndylan-savage, review 5215826786 nit 4 (second half): \"refresh_schema ... empties\nIGlobal.schema after _getDatabaseSchema already reflected the configured table;\nrebuilding from db_schema[table][''columns''] avoids the extra round trips on the\nnext insert.\" (The first half -- rendering the payload outside the lock -- was\nalready done in an earlier commit on this branch.)\n\n`refresh_schema` now publishes\n`{name: (type, '''') for name, type in refreshed[table][''columns'']}` instead of\n`{}`. That is exactly the value shape `_getTableSchema` publishes and\n`_insertData` consumes, with the comment slot empty because\n`_getDatabaseSchema` does not collect comments and `_insertData` only ever\nreads this map by key -- so the key set, which is all that reaches the INSERT,\nis identical to what the lazy rebuild produced. The next answers-lane batch no\nlonger re-reflects a table the walk just read.\n\nThe table-is-gone case is unchanged: a configured table the walk did not find\n(dropped, renamed, or never configured) still publishes `{}`, which re-arms the\nlazy rebuild for a table that comes back.\n`test_refresh_schema_empties_the_column_map_when_the_table_is_gone` is\nuntouched and green. The rebind stays inside `reflect_lock`, and the payload\nrendering stays outside it.\n\nTests: `test_refresh_schema_invalidates_the_insert_lane_column_map` becomes\n`test_refresh_schema_rebuilds_the_insert_lane_column_map` and asserts the\nrebuilt map equals what `_getTableSchema` would publish; new\n`test_an_insert_after_a_refresh_does_not_reflect_the_table_again` patches\n`_getTableSchema` and asserts it is never called. Both fail before this commit.\nProse in the refresh_schema docstring, the `_insertData` comments and the\ndb_postgres / db_mysql / rocketride_sql READMEs now says \"rebuilds\" rather\nthan \"invalidates\".\n271 -> 272 passed across tests/database + tests/ai/common/database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(ai): correct the omit-not-bind rationale and name the MySQL strict-mode residual\n\nProse only; no behaviour, test logic or generated region touched.\n\nThe justification for leaving an ungenerated primary key out of the INSERT\nclaimed an explicit NULL \"suppresses both a trigger''s NEW-value default and a\nserver default\". For the idiom this change targets that is wrong: a BEFORE ROW\ntrigger fires before the NOT NULL check on PostgreSQL and on MySQL 5.7+, sees\nthe bound NULL and fills it. What an explicit NULL does override is a column\nDEFAULT, and where no trigger exists it is a NOT NULL violation -- which is\nstill sufficient reason to omit, so the decision stands and only the stated\nreason changes. Corrected in the `_insertData` pk_names arm and in the\ndb_mysql and rocketride_sql READMEs; the db_postgres README and the\n`_generated_primary_keys` docstring never made the trigger claim.\n\nThree further wording fixes from the same pass:\n\n- The `_generated_primary_keys` docstring still said a row omitting\n  `id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES parent(id)` \"is rejected\". Since an\n  unreflected key is simply left out of the INSERT, SQLite still generates the\n  rowid; the only cost is the SAWarning.\n- The db_mysql README''s \"MySQL refuses the row itself\" now names the condition:\n  strict mode (STRICT_TRANS_TABLES, the default since 5.7). Under a non-strict\n  sql_mode MySQL stores the implicit default instead, so an omitted CHAR key\n  lands as an empty string and the next such row collides with a duplicate-key\n  error. The matching test docstring gains the same qualifier.\n- The SAWarning is emitted through Python''s `warnings` module -- stderr under\n  the default filter, once per distinct message per process -- and never\n  reaches the tool response; rocketlib routes no warnings through its logger.\n  \"It lands in the server log\" and the READMEs'' \"SQLAlchemy logs a warning\"\n  said otherwise.\n\nFinally, the `_insertData` lazy-rebuild comment argued against \"a second\npublisher for no gain\" while `refresh_schema` now publishes the same\ncomment-less shape from its own walk. One reconciling clause: that is fine for\na map read by key; what this block avoids is a THIRD publisher, on every batch.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nihalnihalani <24360630+nihalnihalani@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqrZH8CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA6qwQACHb9+QCEDE99VfPS1btXfls\nq7xLgOANw/6iqBfEzzI01CaxF3tamy8zhxGPPkcRYlSteuWlbVqBGPOQozoEXZnv\nyK3ZO7yBLCKelqbN4pqLnrQrSQJ1bBTfNmYBc3/p/2FCpPbWdJzFhoQXQ20O5mPK\nSFcVACouli6ssKHy/c8aXBsCfVLvgYYa7TJze2cjaPiSuWZUL68Uuo6y+I4+qscX\nu4mo5mrKIR5CzgxO3VfL1h4C3vtbDK7VJWdm9GzYIhkLoPEYB8DMOMZZKudmjIxj\nXbT2oowCsXRdF8HlrGnR3jPmWfFvSmLOfz+xnhbd7B5ChhCVBivRYxiTIDoNjdPc\nGbWefA/vcSz3wCxZea7fb+2vSQCQjDYPJF/VMKLHcBncGpk4OzqYjxAdA0r3sSPe\npPCEhUiY6V7XgPA0Vdth1Q4U3LDwXLxepWTH6eKjOz0XXjcbFh2fhwRSOZ8VPf6y\nbU0v1OsX6HFGxoTrp/Qm3LOzJbHTwDlch2M5aQU9pURfP15iFhdjtYP72QwFDwcL\nZtdezTnbjzqv/B1zFDxpH4GrzqF4gRMFH1233yojgT6OdZxbm3hePUGUc7FX0lG2\nvCv+lMPD34aIANLJZktwMABMwYNLposBDt9XRWGppfrje/TUratFHGuESQQzGGa3\n8aU/pojbjNJrw/ZCT3oC\n=GZDi\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-18T19:33:17Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","