ithub.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/6b2dd34cb28d23fcea48045331a20bcba81bbcf9","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDZiMmRkMzRjYjI4ZDIzZmNlYTQ4MDQ1MzMxYTIwYmNiYTgxYmJjZjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/11800744d875f08958019aa8179303e16f8919ae","sha":"11800744d875f08958019aa8179303e16f8919ae","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/11800744d875f08958019aa8179303e16f8919ae"}],"sha":"6b2dd34cb28d23fcea48045331a20bcba81bbcf9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/6b2dd34cb28d23fcea48045331a20bcba81bbcf9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/392807?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dsapandora","id":392807,"login":"dsapandora","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM5MjgwNw==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dsapandora","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/11800744d875f08958019aa8179303e16f8919ae/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T01:22:28Z","email":"ariel@lazyracoon.tech","name":"Ariel Vernaza"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T01:22:28Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(ai): accept the single-component validation payload the node panel sends (#2346)\n\n* fix(ai): accept the single-component validation payload the node panel sends\n\nThe node config panel validates one component on save and sends\n`{version, component}` — the `IComponentValidatePayload` shape the shell\ncontract declares this endpoint accepts (shell/src/types/project.ts:308). The\nengine validates pipelines and requires `components`, so every save answered\n\"''pipeline.components'' must be an array\", whatever the node.\n\nThe component now travels as a one-item `components` list, built before the\nsource is inferred so `resolve_implied_source` sees it. A config that already\ncarries `components` is untouched.\n\nReachable since #1573: until then `rrext_validate` failed on every call with\n\"''pipeline'' is missing or invalid\", so this payload never reached the\nvalidator. The panel has sent this shape since #1843 (7 Aug).\n\nRemoving the four lines fails the two new tests, and the engine itself answers\nthe reported message:\n\n    validatePipeline({''pipeline'': {''version'': 1, ''component'': c}})    -> \"''pipeline.components'' must be an array\"\n    validatePipeline({''pipeline'': {''version'': 1, ''components'': [c]}}) -> 0 errors\n\n* docs(clients,ai): document the single-component validate payload\n\nThe endpoint now accepts {version, component} as well as a full pipeline, which\nis a public-contract change: both SDK references say so, and on_rrext_validate''s\nArgs: block does too — the inline comment explained it, but the docstring is\nwhat a reader hits first.\n\nAlso states that a payload carrying both keys keeps ''components'' and drops the\nstray ''component'', so the next reader does not have to derive that it is\ndeliberate.","tree":{"sha":"1b0b7ca40338c384331052f6da7082b49be61b5d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/1b0b7ca40338c384331052f6da7082b49be61b5d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/11800744d875f08958019aa8179303e16f8919ae","verification":{"payload":"tree 1b0b7ca40338c384331052f6da7082b49be61b5d\nparent 0d12d30a4ddf54c1540a4710cc7a77ca12500a7a\nauthor Ariel Vernaza <ariel@lazyracoon.tech> 1789694548 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789694548 +0000\n\nfix(ai): accept the single-component validation payload the node panel sends (#2346)\n\n* fix(ai): accept the single-component validation payload the node panel sends\n\nThe node config panel validates one component on save and sends\n`{version, component}` — the `IComponentValidatePayload` shape the shell\ncontract declares this endpoint accepts (shell/src/types/project.ts:308). The\nengine validates pipelines and requires `components`, so every save answered\n\"''pipeline.components'' must be an array\", whatever the node.\n\nThe component now travels as a one-item `components` list, built before the\nsource is inferred so `resolve_implied_source` sees it. A config that already\ncarries `components` is untouched.\n\nReachable since #1573: until then `rrext_validate` failed on every call with\n\"''pipeline'' is missing or invalid\", so this payload never reached the\nvalidator. The panel has sent this shape since #1843 (7 Aug).\n\nRemoving the four lines fails the two new tests, and the engine itself answers\nthe reported message:\n\n    validatePipeline({''pipeline'': {''version'': 1, ''component'': c}})    -> \"''pipeline.components'' must be an array\"\n    validatePipeline({''pipeline'': {''version'': 1, ''components'': [c]}}) -> 0 errors\n\n* docs(clients,ai): document the single-component validate payload\n\nThe endpoint now accepts {version, component} as well as a full pipeline, which\nis a public-contract change: both SDK references say so, and on_rrext_validate''s\nArgs: block does too — the inline comment explained it, but the docstring is\nwhat a reader hits first.\n\nAlso states that a payload carrying both keys keeps ''components'' and drops the\nstray ''component'', so the next reader does not have to derive that it is\ndeliberate.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqrJJUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvV4QAFGMctXBJ8ycs7lQlM/rSHc8\nTzA/86/iLZHCAdRvgqQLgaXoZG/bAkLpERWWUb1AYZqL8Fq1uxmU5KuriXELlB/W\nq9gFlhDx9uiKgzijOi1MsCak49YWxk1RBknOXhkBtxB6UW9lJFEeCHzPz6unQbHu\niUQaaan/zkXTIIUQe8WRQwZY6gBSryJ0Pku6cExq6ouyyjnVGETmpHFxLVCWOgIG\niNlLNHMo1Nu2Qwv92sCq5kOJzvzCtPPGwS7l9RTAfY9CcTnGYT+yW9o9GdTBSgJn\nFEIe/yPjGYQ8GZhH2v2IpKIMcqCmTGQy6Ri61ahRgPQoNwVROLKeHTHkwQmK5TQx\nzaw31B/LS8mh+R1b93Mm1+OVbhsInD932br3lIvwlUJwmkhwfWMsbmY50/EDvKHU\niVe+LKQSkpgqdJiQmUpynN+cG2xeMggedKNTskz6o1BBVUmcpWzAoSb3qZCJTF8K\nrOmTGOwgxN8i4kphQ3mP2hC85/qUCv0/03WR7ts1JdO2kigAvuIUVf0e4yz2NiB1\n0WiOMkh021FDUUjMbkTBXIK5paNVqqtgGlsUdGPnBvWPGhktinrORk8HaVMvMIxh\n2qOzzxWZUbYBYhL697F1ceYdR+ANO8NKB5DwbXnwezk+FOFbc5414l6YEeAP+wU+\nJQCjhbyU0LulAn/3xXgE\n=7a+M\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-18T01:22:29Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/11800744d875f08958019aa8179303e16f8919ae","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDExODAwNzQ0ZDg3NWYwODk1ODAxOWFhODE3OTMwM2UxNmY4OTE5YWU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/0d12d30a4ddf54c1540a4710cc7a77ca12500a7a","sha":"0d12d30a4ddf54c1540a4710cc7a77ca12500a7a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/0d12d30a4ddf54c1540a4710cc7a77ca12500a7a"}],"sha":"11800744d875f08958019aa8179303e16f8919ae","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/11800744d875f08958019aa8179303e16f8919ae"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/130109357?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/dylan-savage","id":130109357,"login":"dylan-savage","node_id":"U_kgDOB8FPrQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dylan-savage","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/0d12d30a4ddf54c1540a4710cc7a77ca12500a7a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T00:25:16Z","email":"130109357+dylan-savage@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Dylan Savage"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T00:25:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(mcp): make the HTTP MCP server testable end to end (auth, routing, engine URI, tools) (#2315)\n\n* fix(mcp): refuse query-string credentials on /mcp and never fall back to the shared client for authenticated callers\n\nAuthMiddleware falls back to `?auth=` when no Authorization header is sent,\nbut auth.authorize() only read the header. `POST /mcp/?auth=<key>` therefore\npassed the middleware, skipped every /mcp check (tk_/pk_/cd_ refusal, JWT\nsignature, issuer, audience) and ran tools on the server''s shared engine\nclient.\n\n- authorize() now refuses any request carrying an `auth` query parameter\n  (parsed as Starlette parses query_params), per the MCP authorization spec.\n- Defence in depth: a request the middleware authenticated\n  (scope[''state''][''account'']) that carries no header credential is refused\n  instead of served by the shared client. The credential-less dev bypass\n  (public route, no account) is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): reserve /mcp for the MCP API — move the shell page to /mcp-server and route bare /mcp to the handler\n\nThe shell registered a public GET page at /mcp. The MCP endpoint is a\nStarlette Mount, which only matches /mcp/..., so the page won the bare\npath: POST /mcp answered 405 with no WWW-Authenticate challenge (spec\nclients POST to the advertised resource .../mcp and never saw the OAuth\nchallenge), and AuthMiddleware treated every /mcp request as public.\nadd_route''s duplicate check never saw the clash because the Mount goes\nstraight onto the router.\n\n- shell: the MCP marketing page moves to /mcp-server (still unlisted).\n- mcp: an exact, method-agnostic Route serves bare /mcp alongside the\n  Mount, so /mcp and /mcp/ reach handle_mcp identically (no 405/307).\n- mcp: initModule refuses to start if any route already claims /mcp or\n  /mcp/*, or a public pattern matches the endpoint.\n- mcp: MCP_DEV_NO_AUTH now opens exactly /mcp and /mcp/ (loopback only).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(mcp): default the engine URI to the public MCP origin, or the local address on loopback binds\n\nROCKETRIDE_URI had no default, so on staging/prod every MCP tool call\nraised \"Missing engine URI\". The URI is now resolved once in initModule\nand injected into the config every engine client is built from, and it\nalso drives the widget CSP origin and the upload/dropper links:\n\n1. explicit: config rocketride_uri / env ROCKETRIDE_URI, used as-is\n2. loopback default: ws://127.0.0.1:<port> (ws://[::1]:<port>) so a\n   laptop engine never silently calls RocketRide''s cloud\n3. public default: the MCP_RESOURCE_IDENTIFIER origin, https->wss /\n   http->ws (default wss://api.rocketride.ai)\n\nOne startup log line names the resolved URI (userinfo/query stripped)\nand the rule that chose it. ROCKETRIDE_AUTH/APIKEY handling is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): only offer send_files on loopback-bound engines — it reads paths on the engine host\n\nThe SDK''s send_files checks and opens each caller-supplied path on the\nmachine running the MCP module. On a deployed engine that is our server, so\nany authenticated caller could probe file existence (\"File not found\") and\nupload server files into their own pipeline.\n\ninitModule now decides local_engine = is_loopback_bind(bind_host) once and\npasses it to build_mcp_server -> ToolRegistry(local_engine=...). Tools\nregistered local_engine_only (send_files) are neither listed nor dispatched\non a deployed engine: a call by name gets an Unavailable refusal that ignores\nthe client and arguments. The registry defaults to the deployed surface.\nLoopback binds are unchanged.\n\nAlso corrects the send_files description (engine-host paths, not\nstore-relative), drops the stale \"or filepath\" from run_pipeline, and\nupdates the MCP docs and agent skill contract.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): send no SDK env from deployed engines so caller secrets keep precedence\n\nWsEngineClient built RocketRideClient(uri, auth) with no env, so the SDK\nloaded os.environ plus ./.env and use() forwarded every ROCKETRIDE_* var as\nthe run''s env. The engine applies caller env last, over org/team/user\nsecrets, so on a deployed engine the server''s own ROCKETRIDE_* vars\noverrode a customer''s same-named secrets for MCP-started runs.\n\ninitModule injects local_engine (the same loopback decision that gates\nsend_files) into config alongside the resolved URI; make_engine_client\nbuilds the client with env={} unless it is set. Loopback binds keep the SDK\ndefault. Explicit uri/auth are unaffected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): rewrite deploy tools against the current SDK deploy API\n\nThe MCP deploy tools (#1880) were written against a Python SDK deploy API\nthat deploy-2 (#1764, 0dfdc8b44) had already removed. In production every\ncall failed: deploy_add passed a `schedule` kwarg add() no longer takes,\ndeploy_status/deploy_update called methods that no longer exist,\ndeploy_remove omitted the now-required team_id, and deploy_list counted\nthe keys of the {rows,total,page,pageSize} envelope. The suite stayed\ngreen because FakeEngine and the seam tests mirrored the old API.\n\nThe seam (WsEngineClient) is now one keyword-argument passthrough per\nDeployApi method, and the tools are thin wrappers in the\nteams-as-environments model:\n\n- deploy_add -> add (pipeline, comment, deploy_to)\n- deploy_list -> list (team_id, paging, search, filters, sort); returns\n  deployments + count/total/page/pageSize\n- deploy_status -> get (project_id, team_id)\n- deploy_versions -> versions (project_id, paging)\n- deploy_to_team -> deploy (project_id, version, team_id)\n- deploy_set_schedule -> set_schedule (project_id, source_id, schedule,\n  team_id, ttl)\n- deploy_enable / deploy_disable / deploy_remove -> enable / disable /\n  remove (project_id, team_id)\n\ndeploy_update is gone; its operations are deploy_add + deploy_to_team and\ndeploy_set_schedule. The rocketride://pipelines resource returns the\nunwrapped envelope. Tool count: 33 (32 on a deployed engine).\n\nDrift guard: test_engine.py calls every deploy_* seam method against a\nrecording fake and binds each recorded call to the real\nrocketride.deploy.DeployApi signature (method must exist, arguments must\nbind, every seam argument must arrive under the same name). It fails on\nthe old seam code. A second test pins the EngineClient protocol and the\nconftest FakeEngineClient to the seam''s deploy signatures.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(task_http): don''t re-send engine env as caller env from task_Execute on deployed engines\n\ntask_Execute built RocketRideClient(uri, auth) with no env, so the SDK\nsnapshotted os.environ plus ./.env and use() forwarded every ROCKETRIDE_*\nvar as the run''s caller env. The engine applies caller env last, over the\norg/team/user secrets from get_merged_env, so on a deployed engine the\nserver''s own ROCKETRIDE_* vars overrode a user''s same-named secrets for\nruns started via POST /task.\n\nApply the rule MCP already uses (76708650a): the client is built with\nenv={} unless the server''s configured bind host is loopback\n(ai.modules.mcp.auth.is_loopback_bind, falling back to the same\nCONST_DEFAULT_WEB_HOST WebServer binds with). Loopback binds keep the SDK\ndefault so a local developer''s .env still applies. The MCP import is\ndeferred so loading task_http doesn''t pull in the MCP package.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): close the credential-less auth hole and harden the MCP boundary (#2315)\n\nAddresses the four review findings on PR #2315.\n\n1. Log the resolved bind mode at startup. `is_loopback_bind` judges the\n   CONFIGURED host and `CONST_DEFAULT_WEB_HOST` is ''localhost'', so a\n   deployment that never sets `host` silently runs as a local engine.\n   `is_loopback_bind` is unchanged (as the reviewer asked); instead the first\n   lines of the log now state the mode, the host it was judged from, and what\n   that grants (dev-bypass eligibility, `send_files`, caller-env forwarding),\n   with a WARNING when the bind is loopback only by fallback.\n\n2. Refuse credential-less MCP requests. A public route pattern matching an\n   MCP descendant (`/mcp/{path}`) makes AuthMiddleware skip the request while\n   the Mount still forwards it to `handle_mcp`, where `authorize()` used to\n   return None and run tools on the shared, server-identity engine client.\n   Both ends are fixed: `authorize()` now refuses a request with no credential\n   unless the loopback dev bypass is genuinely active, and the startup guard\n   probes representative descendant paths through `is_public_route`, not just\n   `/mcp` and `/mcp/`. The bypass rule itself lives in one place --\n   `auth.dev_bypass_active` -- used by both `initModule` and `authorize`.\n\n3. Require encrypted engine transport on a non-loopback bind. `handle_mcp`\n   hands the caller''s credential to `WsEngineClient`, which sends it in the\n   first DAP `auth` message, so a `ws://`/`http://` engine URI (explicit or\n   derived from the MCP resource identifier) now fails boot with a message\n   naming the value and the variable it came from. Loopback binds keep\n   cleartext; the https staging/prod defaults are unaffected.\n\n4. Reconcile MCP_TOOL_CONTRACT.md with the registrations. The tables listed\n   ~23 of the 33 registered tools: `resolve_config` and `scaffold_node` were\n   undocumented and grouped capability rows hid individual tools. Every tool\n   now has its own row derived from its real schema and result, and the stale\n   `origin/develop @ eb67ddea` pin is replaced with what is accurate now.\n\nTest-first: 27 new tests fail against the previous implementation. Three\nexisting tests encoded behaviour findings 2 and 3 deliberately change and were\nupdated; the WebServer-backed route tests now clear ROCKETRIDE_URI /\nMCP_RESOURCE_IDENTIFIER after construction so a developer''s dist/server/.env\nno longer decides what they resolve.\n\n717 passed, 0 skipped (was 683).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): key the startup guards on patterns and targets, not probes and binds (#2315)\n\nThree follow-ups to the review findings on PR #2315.\n\n1. The startup guard now inspects the registered public PATTERN STRINGS, not\n   just probe literals. Public entries are compiled with `compile_path`, so a\n   converter-typed pattern (`/mcp/{id:int}`, `/mcp/{x:uuid}`) narrows the regex\n   to values no fixed probe satisfies -- the guard missed it while\n   AuthMiddleware still skipped auth for every `/mcp/<digits>` the Mount\n   forwards. `_registered_public_patterns` reads `_public_paths` (real\n   WebServer) and `public` (test double), the same pair the dev bypass already\n   writes to, and degrades to nothing when neither is present.\n   `_claims_mcp_pattern` normalises (whitespace, trailing slashes) and refuses\n   only a whole-segment match, so `/mcp`, `/mcp/` and `/mcp/{...}` trip while\n   `/mcpx` and `/mcp-server` do not. Message shape and per-claimant naming are\n   unchanged.\n\n2. The cleartext refusal now keys on the engine URI''s TARGET HOST rather than\n   the bind. A credential sent to `ws://127.0.0.1:5565` never reaches a wire\n   anyone can tap, and the bind-keyed rule was a deployment hazard:\n   `WebServer.__init__` loads `dist/server/.env`, `.env.template` ships\n   `ROCKETRIDE_URI=http://localhost:5565`, and `docker/Dockerfile.engine`\n   copies `dist/server/` into the image, so a pod carrying that file would have\n   failed to boot. `auth.is_loopback_bind` judges a configured bind string\n   against exact literals and does not fit a URI netloc (userinfo, port, IPv6\n   brackets, the whole 127.0.0.0/8 range), so a small `_targets_loopback_host`\n   helper was added next to the rule instead of bending it; it still reuses\n   `auth.LOOPBACK_HOSTS` for the by-name allowlist. Non-loopback targets fail\n   boot as before, with the same redaction.\n\n3. `MCP_TOOL_CONTRACT.md`''s `scaffold_node` row described only\n   `local_nodes/<name>/...`, so a contract-following client omitted the\n   `local_nodes/__init__.py` package marker `_scaffold_node` also returns and\n   the engine could not import `local_nodes.<name>`. The row now names the\n   marker and says to write every returned path; `docs/public/mcp/http/tools.md`\n   carried the same omission and is corrected too. The engine-module doc now\n   states the target-keyed transport rule.\n\nTest-first for 1 and 2: 46 new tests fail against the previous implementation.\nNo existing test encoded the old bind-keyed rule (every refusal case already\nused a non-loopback target), so none needed updating.\n\n763 passed, 0 skipped (was 717).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): refuse a cleartext engine URI on every bind, not just a public one (#2315)\n\nCloses the gap the previous commit left in `_resolve_engine_uri`. That commit\nmoved the cleartext refusal onto the engine''s TARGET host\n(`_targets_loopback_host`), but the explicit branch still guarded the call with\nthe BIND:\n\n    if explicit:\n        if not loopback:\n            _refuse_cleartext_engine_uri(...)\n\nso a loopback-bound MCP server with `ROCKETRIDE_URI=ws://engine.remote:5565`\nskipped the check entirely and sent the CALLER''s credential over the network in\nthe clear -- `handle_mcp` binds it to the per-request engine client and\n`WsEngineClient` puts it in the first DAP `auth` frame. The bind says nothing\nabout whether the wire is exposed; only the target does.\n\n`_refuse_cleartext_engine_uri` now runs for every explicit URI, on any bind,\nwith the same `rocketride_uri` / `ROCKETRIDE_URI` source naming and the same\nredaction. Loopback TARGETS stay allowed inside the helper, so the shipped\n`dist/server/.env` (`ROCKETRIDE_URI=http://localhost:5565`) still boots. The\nlocal `loopback` variable is gone: `auth.is_loopback_bind` is now read inline\nwhere it genuinely selects the DEFAULT URI, so no bind-keyed condition is left\nanywhere near the encryption rule. The `loopback default` branch resolves\n`ws://127.0.0.1:<port>`, a loopback target, and the `public default` branch\nalready called the helper.\n\nDocs and docstrings that stated the old bind-keyed rule now say the check keys\non the target host: `_resolve_engine_uri`, `initModule`,\n`_refuse_cleartext_engine_uri`, and `docs/development/engine/mcp-module.md`.\n`docs/public/mcp/http/self-hosting.md` never stated the rule at all even though\nit fails an operator''s boot, so its guard list now carries it.\n\nTest-first: 8 new cases fail against the previous implementation (explicit\n`ws://` and `http://` remote targets on `127.0.0.1` / `localhost` / `::1`\nbinds, from env and from config, redaction included), plus explicit `wss://`\naccepted on any bind and the loopback-target cases extended over loopback\nbinds. Two existing tests used an explicit remote `ws://` URI incidentally --\none for dropper-link origins, one for `engine_origin` threading -- and now use\n`wss://`; neither was asserting the transport rule.\n\n794 passed, 0 skipped (was 763).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"fca37e3d42b517b6a02d76688c67939a65db6e72","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/fca37e3d42b517b6a02d76688c67939a65db6e72"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/0d12d30a4ddf54c1540a4710cc7a77ca12500a7a","verification":{"payload":"tree fca37e3d42b517b6a02d76688c67939a65db6e72\nparent 4d1ec4ed73be03090ce506b8da7654a0af0ea183\nauthor Dylan Savage <130109357+dylan-savage@users.noreply.github.com> 1789691116 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789691116 +0000\n\nfix(mcp): make the HTTP MCP server testable end to end (auth, routing, engine URI, tools) (#2315)\n\n* fix(mcp): refuse query-string credentials on /mcp and never fall back to the shared client for authenticated callers\n\nAuthMiddleware falls back to `?auth=` when no Authorization header is sent,\nbut auth.authorize() only read the header. `POST /mcp/?auth=<key>` therefore\npassed the middleware, skipped every /mcp check (tk_/pk_/cd_ refusal, JWT\nsignature, issuer, audience) and ran tools on the server''s shared engine\nclient.\n\n- authorize() now refuses any request carrying an `auth` query parameter\n  (parsed as Starlette parses query_params), per the MCP authorization spec.\n- Defence in depth: a request the middleware authenticated\n  (scope[''state''][''account'']) that carries no header credential is refused\n  instead of served by the shared client. The credential-less dev bypass\n  (public route, no account) is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): reserve /mcp for the MCP API — move the shell page to /mcp-server and route bare /mcp to the handler\n\nThe shell registered a public GET page at /mcp. The MCP endpoint is a\nStarlette Mount, which only matches /mcp/..., so the page won the bare\npath: POST /mcp answered 405 with no WWW-Authenticate challenge (spec\nclients POST to the advertised resource .../mcp and never saw the OAuth\nchallenge), and AuthMiddleware treated every /mcp request as public.\nadd_route''s duplicate check never saw the clash because the Mount goes\nstraight onto the router.\n\n- shell: the MCP marketing page moves to /mcp-server (still unlisted).\n- mcp: an exact, method-agnostic Route serves bare /mcp alongside the\n  Mount, so /mcp and /mcp/ reach handle_mcp identically (no 405/307).\n- mcp: initModule refuses to start if any route already claims /mcp or\n  /mcp/*, or a public pattern matches the endpoint.\n- mcp: MCP_DEV_NO_AUTH now opens exactly /mcp and /mcp/ (loopback only).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(mcp): default the engine URI to the public MCP origin, or the local address on loopback binds\n\nROCKETRIDE_URI had no default, so on staging/prod every MCP tool call\nraised \"Missing engine URI\". The URI is now resolved once in initModule\nand injected into the config every engine client is built from, and it\nalso drives the widget CSP origin and the upload/dropper links:\n\n1. explicit: config rocketride_uri / env ROCKETRIDE_URI, used as-is\n2. loopback default: ws://127.0.0.1:<port> (ws://[::1]:<port>) so a\n   laptop engine never silently calls RocketRide''s cloud\n3. public default: the MCP_RESOURCE_IDENTIFIER origin, https->wss /\n   http->ws (default wss://api.rocketride.ai)\n\nOne startup log line names the resolved URI (userinfo/query stripped)\nand the rule that chose it. ROCKETRIDE_AUTH/APIKEY handling is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): only offer send_files on loopback-bound engines — it reads paths on the engine host\n\nThe SDK''s send_files checks and opens each caller-supplied path on the\nmachine running the MCP module. On a deployed engine that is our server, so\nany authenticated caller could probe file existence (\"File not found\") and\nupload server files into their own pipeline.\n\ninitModule now decides local_engine = is_loopback_bind(bind_host) once and\npasses it to build_mcp_server -> ToolRegistry(local_engine=...). Tools\nregistered local_engine_only (send_files) are neither listed nor dispatched\non a deployed engine: a call by name gets an Unavailable refusal that ignores\nthe client and arguments. The registry defaults to the deployed surface.\nLoopback binds are unchanged.\n\nAlso corrects the send_files description (engine-host paths, not\nstore-relative), drops the stale \"or filepath\" from run_pipeline, and\nupdates the MCP docs and agent skill contract.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): send no SDK env from deployed engines so caller secrets keep precedence\n\nWsEngineClient built RocketRideClient(uri, auth) with no env, so the SDK\nloaded os.environ plus ./.env and use() forwarded every ROCKETRIDE_* var as\nthe run''s env. The engine applies caller env last, over org/team/user\nsecrets, so on a deployed engine the server''s own ROCKETRIDE_* vars\noverrode a customer''s same-named secrets for MCP-started runs.\n\ninitModule injects local_engine (the same loopback decision that gates\nsend_files) into config alongside the resolved URI; make_engine_client\nbuilds the client with env={} unless it is set. Loopback binds keep the SDK\ndefault. Explicit uri/auth are unaffected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): rewrite deploy tools against the current SDK deploy API\n\nThe MCP deploy tools (#1880) were written against a Python SDK deploy API\nthat deploy-2 (#1764, 0dfdc8b44) had already removed. In production every\ncall failed: deploy_add passed a `schedule` kwarg add() no longer takes,\ndeploy_status/deploy_update called methods that no longer exist,\ndeploy_remove omitted the now-required team_id, and deploy_list counted\nthe keys of the {rows,total,page,pageSize} envelope. The suite stayed\ngreen because FakeEngine and the seam tests mirrored the old API.\n\nThe seam (WsEngineClient) is now one keyword-argument passthrough per\nDeployApi method, and the tools are thin wrappers in the\nteams-as-environments model:\n\n- deploy_add -> add (pipeline, comment, deploy_to)\n- deploy_list -> list (team_id, paging, search, filters, sort); returns\n  deployments + count/total/page/pageSize\n- deploy_status -> get (project_id, team_id)\n- deploy_versions -> versions (project_id, paging)\n- deploy_to_team -> deploy (project_id, version, team_id)\n- deploy_set_schedule -> set_schedule (project_id, source_id, schedule,\n  team_id, ttl)\n- deploy_enable / deploy_disable / deploy_remove -> enable / disable /\n  remove (project_id, team_id)\n\ndeploy_update is gone; its operations are deploy_add + deploy_to_team and\ndeploy_set_schedule. The rocketride://pipelines resource returns the\nunwrapped envelope. Tool count: 33 (32 on a deployed engine).\n\nDrift guard: test_engine.py calls every deploy_* seam method against a\nrecording fake and binds each recorded call to the real\nrocketride.deploy.DeployApi signature (method must exist, arguments must\nbind, every seam argument must arrive under the same name). It fails on\nthe old seam code. A second test pins the EngineClient protocol and the\nconftest FakeEngineClient to the seam''s deploy signatures.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(task_http): don''t re-send engine env as caller env from task_Execute on deployed engines\n\ntask_Execute built RocketRideClient(uri, auth) with no env, so the SDK\nsnapshotted os.environ plus ./.env and use() forwarded every ROCKETRIDE_*\nvar as the run''s caller env. The engine applies caller env last, over the\norg/team/user secrets from get_merged_env, so on a deployed engine the\nserver''s own ROCKETRIDE_* vars overrode a user''s same-named secrets for\nruns started via POST /task.\n\nApply the rule MCP already uses (76708650a): the client is built with\nenv={} unless the server''s configured bind host is loopback\n(ai.modules.mcp.auth.is_loopback_bind, falling back to the same\nCONST_DEFAULT_WEB_HOST WebServer binds with). Loopback binds keep the SDK\ndefault so a local developer''s .env still applies. The MCP import is\ndeferred so loading task_http doesn''t pull in the MCP package.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): close the credential-less auth hole and harden the MCP boundary (#2315)\n\nAddresses the four review findings on PR #2315.\n\n1. Log the resolved bind mode at startup. `is_loopback_bind` judges the\n   CONFIGURED host and `CONST_DEFAULT_WEB_HOST` is ''localhost'', so a\n   deployment that never sets `host` silently runs as a local engine.\n   `is_loopback_bind` is unchanged (as the reviewer asked); instead the first\n   lines of the log now state the mode, the host it was judged from, and what\n   that grants (dev-bypass eligibility, `send_files`, caller-env forwarding),\n   with a WARNING when the bind is loopback only by fallback.\n\n2. Refuse credential-less MCP requests. A public route pattern matching an\n   MCP descendant (`/mcp/{path}`) makes AuthMiddleware skip the request while\n   the Mount still forwards it to `handle_mcp`, where `authorize()` used to\n   return None and run tools on the shared, server-identity engine client.\n   Both ends are fixed: `authorize()` now refuses a request with no credential\n   unless the loopback dev bypass is genuinely active, and the startup guard\n   probes representative descendant paths through `is_public_route`, not just\n   `/mcp` and `/mcp/`. The bypass rule itself lives in one place --\n   `auth.dev_bypass_active` -- used by both `initModule` and `authorize`.\n\n3. Require encrypted engine transport on a non-loopback bind. `handle_mcp`\n   hands the caller''s credential to `WsEngineClient`, which sends it in the\n   first DAP `auth` message, so a `ws://`/`http://` engine URI (explicit or\n   derived from the MCP resource identifier) now fails boot with a message\n   naming the value and the variable it came from. Loopback binds keep\n   cleartext; the https staging/prod defaults are unaffected.\n\n4. Reconcile MCP_TOOL_CONTRACT.md with the registrations. The tables listed\n   ~23 of the 33 registered tools: `resolve_config` and `scaffold_node` were\n   undocumented and grouped capability rows hid individual tools. Every tool\n   now has its own row derived from its real schema and result, and the stale\n   `origin/develop @ eb67ddea` pin is replaced with what is accurate now.\n\nTest-first: 27 new tests fail against the previous implementation. Three\nexisting tests encoded behaviour findings 2 and 3 deliberately change and were\nupdated; the WebServer-backed route tests now clear ROCKETRIDE_URI /\nMCP_RESOURCE_IDENTIFIER after construction so a developer''s dist/server/.env\nno longer decides what they resolve.\n\n717 passed, 0 skipped (was 683).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): key the startup guards on patterns and targets, not probes and binds (#2315)\n\nThree follow-ups to the review findings on PR #2315.\n\n1. The startup guard now inspects the registered public PATTERN STRINGS, not\n   just probe literals. Public entries are compiled with `compile_path`, so a\n   converter-typed pattern (`/mcp/{id:int}`, `/mcp/{x:uuid}`) narrows the regex\n   to values no fixed probe satisfies -- the guard missed it while\n   AuthMiddleware still skipped auth for every `/mcp/<digits>` the Mount\n   forwards. `_registered_public_patterns` reads `_public_paths` (real\n   WebServer) and `public` (test double), the same pair the dev bypass already\n   writes to, and degrades to nothing when neither is present.\n   `_claims_mcp_pattern` normalises (whitespace, trailing slashes) and refuses\n   only a whole-segment match, so `/mcp`, `/mcp/` and `/mcp/{...}` trip while\n   `/mcpx` and `/mcp-server` do not. Message shape and per-claimant naming are\n   unchanged.\n\n2. The cleartext refusal now keys on the engine URI''s TARGET HOST rather than\n   the bind. A credential sent to `ws://127.0.0.1:5565` never reaches a wire\n   anyone can tap, and the bind-keyed rule was a deployment hazard:\n   `WebServer.__init__` loads `dist/server/.env`, `.env.template` ships\n   `ROCKETRIDE_URI=http://localhost:5565`, and `docker/Dockerfile.engine`\n   copies `dist/server/` into the image, so a pod carrying that file would have\n   failed to boot. `auth.is_loopback_bind` judges a configured bind string\n   against exact literals and does not fit a URI netloc (userinfo, port, IPv6\n   brackets, the whole 127.0.0.0/8 range), so a small `_targets_loopback_host`\n   helper was added next to the rule instead of bending it; it still reuses\n   `auth.LOOPBACK_HOSTS` for the by-name allowlist. Non-loopback targets fail\n   boot as before, with the same redaction.\n\n3. `MCP_TOOL_CONTRACT.md`''s `scaffold_node` row described only\n   `local_nodes/<name>/...`, so a contract-following client omitted the\n   `local_nodes/__init__.py` package marker `_scaffold_node` also returns and\n   the engine could not import `local_nodes.<name>`. The row now names the\n   marker and says to write every returned path; `docs/public/mcp/http/tools.md`\n   carried the same omission and is corrected too. The engine-module doc now\n   states the target-keyed transport rule.\n\nTest-first for 1 and 2: 46 new tests fail against the previous implementation.\nNo existing test encoded the old bind-keyed rule (every refusal case already\nused a non-loopback target), so none needed updating.\n\n763 passed, 0 skipped (was 717).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(mcp): refuse a cleartext engine URI on every bind, not just a public one (#2315)\n\nCloses the gap the previous commit left in `_resolve_engine_uri`. That commit\nmoved the cleartext refusal onto the engine''s TARGET host\n(`_targets_loopback_host`), but the explicit branch still guarded the call with\nthe BIND:\n\n    if explicit:\n        if not loopback:\n            _refuse_cleartext_engine_uri(...)\n\nso a loopback-bound MCP server with `ROCKETRIDE_URI=ws://engine.remote:5565`\nskipped the check entirely and sent the CALLER''s credential over the network in\nthe clear -- `handle_mcp` binds it to the per-request engine client and\n`WsEngineClient` puts it in the first DAP `auth` frame. The bind says nothing\nabout whether the wire is exposed; only the target does.\n\n`_refuse_cleartext_engine_uri` now runs for every explicit URI, on any bind,\nwith the same `rocketride_uri` / `ROCKETRIDE_URI` source naming and the same\nredaction. Loopback TARGETS stay allowed inside the helper, so the shipped\n`dist/server/.env` (`ROCKETRIDE_URI=http://localhost:5565`) still boots. The\nlocal `loopback` variable is gone: `auth.is_loopback_bind` is now read inline\nwhere it genuinely selects the DEFAULT URI, so no bind-keyed condition is left\nanywhere near the encryption rule. The `loopback default` branch resolves\n`ws://127.0.0.1:<port>`, a loopback target, and the `public default` branch\nalready called the helper.\n\nDocs and docstrings that stated the old bind-keyed rule now say the check keys\non the target host: `_resolve_engine_uri`, `initModule`,\n`_refuse_cleartext_engine_uri`, and `docs/development/engine/mcp-module.md`.\n`docs/public/mcp/http/self-hosting.md` never stated the rule at all even though\nit fails an operator''s boot, so its guard list now carries it.\n\nTest-first: 8 new cases fail against the previous implementation (explicit\n`ws://` and `http://` remote targets on `127.0.0.1` / `localhost` / `::1`\nbinds, from env and from config, redaction included), plus explicit `wss://`\naccepted on any bind and the loopback-target cases extended over loopback\nbinds. Two existing tests used an explicit remote `ws://` URI incidentally --\none for dropper-link origins, one for `engine_origin` threading -- and now use\n`wss://`; neither was asserting the transport rule.\n\n794 passed, 0 skipped (was 763).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqrITsCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAQnsQAKI6pVYDDYZiQ61jPgOda4d+\nOka/0pu6CNMfGPPJtq7hw2aL5kNVhdACkbziInnSABS9pD/WcHHHcox/9KsnhSJk\nTlHnvEx9B9rah0KDb+aKCApRO6GAO+knw4D+kdEoe26TpVYE10Ei6QXj5I70BFaQ\nlpppkV2Udgwb3zcUzNwYV2wbFAbEjowEOymojPF7GoxBI2JRVk+1ecU9+E0xMGdV\n1lRadFBt1Uw6aMHwXC8T9XB5Kez+9UBjakUqWLI3DzofqKEYKtnyb7LyRmWH3qap\nyjb2pB+myBsQpMd875R+9UoSq67fss+OaAHGuA9f8hUG4PnzD3uEXpVFJCJx38nD\n0xWVeDQcHtxvyHMpF+LBNjMUbxZU8yWsuzxcnfk/XPRHMMxPwn4GzJxYdcaKnbHr\nu6MRGffHtrHpwut5UXLDjjI93yifJqKeOQl9ZDfiCuF957q4vi1o3TV9ezbt1HIZ\n9kH8aZL53T2hwB7T84ZiMqs72rv65Z1UcH/CkmRonKOCWZW7XN0tnJKfkFn7/2SU\n0209XVewcFyQ1bbm2XskuVmGBoKp8lyWhYZMa7Smdz0q7E6ktLKj61UhsZKrs2fv\n3lEY34gC1OsU2k+08QXeilnZCLLr+XdC0KVmWtvZ1Q7tTGKP0TSaT+AfKZMbjTfZ\nOBGbbudrsKHGsv71uQLa\n=vjLj\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-18T00:25:17Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/0d12d30a4ddf54c1540a4710cc7a77ca12500a7a","node_id":"C_kwDOROCxS9oAKDBkMTJkMzBhNGRkZjU0YzE1NDBhNDcxMGNjN2E3N2NhMTI1MDBhN2E","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commit/4d1ec4ed73be03090ce506b8da7654a0af0ea183","sha":"4d1ec4ed73be03090ce506b8da7654a0af0ea183","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/4d1ec4ed73be03090ce506b8da7654a0af0ea183"}],"sha":"0d12d30a4ddf54c1540a4710cc7a77ca12500a7a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/0d12d30a4ddf54c1540a4710cc7a77ca12500a7a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/29624014?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/asclearuc","id":29624014,"login":"asclearuc","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI5NjI0MDE0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/asclearuc","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/commits/4d1ec4ed73be03090ce506b8da7654a0af0ea183/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-17T19:52:19Z","email":"asclearuc@gmail.com","name":"Alexandru Sclearuc"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-17T19:52:19Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(profiler): per-thread breakdown and a call tree that keeps real work (#2299)\n\n* feat(ai): break the profiler report down by thread\n\nstop() now also captures the session per yappi context, next to the flat\ncapture and before clear_stats(). threads() lists them busiest first with\ntheir counts, and report_tree() takes an optional thread id; without one it\nbehaves as before. DAP: new rrext_cprofile_threads, plus an optional thread\nargument on rrext_cprofile_report_tree, in the engine subprocess and the\ntask-server proxy.\n\nTwo yappi traps shape the capture:\n- get_func_stats(ctx_id=...) tests the id for truthiness, so thread 0 would\n  silently get every thread''s functions. The filter goes in as a dict.\n- The default context name is the thread''s class, so workers read ''Thread''\n  and engine workers ''_DummyThread''. start() installs a callback reading\n  Thread.name from threading._active; current_thread() would deadlock when\n  the profiled call is _active_limbo_lock.acquire() (yappi #48). yappi keeps\n  the first name it gets, so the thread ident is reported as well.\n\nCost on a parse -> preprocessor pipeline over 12 and 36 documents: the\nper-thread copy holds 2.1-3.5 MB against 0.5 MB flat (13-25 threads), and\nthe capture under _lock grows from ~117 ms to ~250-275 ms.\n\n* perf(ai): cache _relativize_path\n\nstop() calls it under _lock for every entry and callee of each capture:\nabout 5,900 calls per stop on a parse pipeline over 12 documents, since the\nper-thread capture repeats the flat one''s paths, over a few hundred\ndistinct paths. The result depends only on the path, so cache it.\n\nOn the same pipeline the capture under _lock drops from ~250-275 ms to\n~160-170 ms, against ~117 ms before the per-thread capture existed. Memory\ndrops only ~10%: every entry of a path now shares one string, but the\nper-entry dicts are most of the size.\n\n* fix(ai): stop the call tree from pruning away real work\n\nreport_tree() dropped every node whose own time fell under min_pct, and with\nit the whole subtree. Two kinds of node read light while real work hangs\nbelow them: frames still running at stop() (thread entry points, executor\nworker loops) read ~0 because yappi books time on return, and the event\nloop step that resumes a coroutine is short while the coroutine is booked\nits whole lifetime. On a parse pipeline this emptied the merged tree, the\nevent-loop thread and every executor thread started mid-session; threads\nthat predated the session survived only because their zero-time frames sat\nat depths 0 and 1, which are never pruned.\n\nA node is now pruned only when nothing reachable below it reaches the\nthreshold either. The threshold was also inflated: total_time summed ttot,\ncounting nested calls again (a 5 s session summed to 412 s). It is now the\ntime the threads ran, as yappi measures it per thread - the selected one,\nor all summed - falling back to the sum of self times without thread data.\n\nSame session after the fix: project code shows in every view at the\ndefault threshold, and trees stay at 4-168 nodes, built in under 60 ms.\n\n* feat(client): list profiled threads and fetch one thread''s tree\n\nPython: cprofile_threads() and a thread parameter on cprofile_report_tree().\nTypeScript: cprofileThreads() and a trailing thread parameter on\ncprofileReportTree(), with CProfileThreadInfo / CProfileThreadsResponse.\nThread 0 is a real id, so both clients test for absence, not truthiness.\n\nThe shell re-exports the SDK''s types, so the two new ones are new shell-api\nnames and shell:check fails until they are frozen: this freezes shell-api\nv4. Its delta over v3 is exactly these types and the two method signatures.\n\nThe contract test runs data through a task''s pipeline and checks that no\nthread carries yappi''s class-based names, that one thread''s tree totals that\nthread''s calls, and that an unknown id is refused, which proves the\nargument reached the engine.\n\ntest-ui''s API sweep and the SDK references list the new method; the Python\nreference also claimed include_system defaults to False, which it never did.\n\n* feat(profiler-ui): pick a thread to visualise\n\nA Thread dropdown heads the viz controls: all threads (the old view) or one,\nlabelled with name, thread id and time, since names repeat. The views get\nthe same tree shape either way. The list is refetched with the report, a\nselection missing from the new list falls back to all threads, and a server\nwithout rrext_cprofile_threads just hides the dropdown. Start clears the\nlist, since ids are per session.\n\nBumps the app to 1.3.0: an OSS server reseeds a built-in app only when its\napps.json version changes, so an upgraded server would keep serving the\nold profiler otherwise.\n\n* fix(profiler-ui): prune the charts by subtree weight\n\nThe client-side cutoff compared a child''s own cumtime with its parent''s,\nso a zero-time frame the server now keeps was dropped again at the root,\ntaking the work under it along. It now compares the largest cumtime in\neach subtree, and runs before the depth limit so that weight covers the\nwhole subtree.\n\n* fix(profiler-ui): clear the two type errors tsc reports\n\ninitConnectionStore took the shell''s settings as Record<string, string>,\nbut they are Record<string, SettingValue>, and a port setting may well be\na number; the seeded host and port are now coerced to strings. The\nsunburst filtered on x0/x1 before casting to HierarchyRectangularNode,\nwhich the flame graph already does first. Neither changes behaviour: the\napp build does not type-check, which is how both went unnoticed.\n\n* fix: CodeRabbit","tree":{"sha":"592433c92703f5f005a16da9100646bebb0aed75","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/trees/592433c92703f5f005a16da9100646bebb0aed75"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rocketride-org/rocketride-server/git/commits/4d1ec4ed73be03090ce506b8da7654a0af0ea183","verification":{"payload":"tree 592433c92703f5f005a16da9100646bebb0aed75\nparent 7fb23a224abc94c2c768fb421c5ffda4862b1505\nauthor Alexandru Sclearuc <asclearuc@gmail.com> 1789674739 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789674739 +0000\n\nfeat(profiler): per-thread breakdown and a call tree that keeps real work (#2299)\n\n* feat(ai): break the profiler report down by thread\n\nstop() now also captures the session per yappi context, next to the flat\ncapture and before clear_stats(). threads() lists them busiest first with\ntheir counts, and report_tree() takes an optional thread id; without one it\nbehaves as before. DAP: new rrext_cprofile_threads, plus an optional thread\nargument on rrext_cprofile_report_tree, in the engine subprocess and the\ntask-server proxy.\n\nTwo yappi traps shape the capture:\n- get_func_stats(ctx_id=...) tests the id for truthiness, so thread 0 would\n  silently get every thread''s functions. The filter goes in as a dict.\n- The default context name is the thread''s class, so workers read ''Thread''\n  and engine workers ''_DummyThread''. start() installs a callback reading\n  Thread.name from threading._active; current_thread() would deadlock when\n  the profiled call is _active_limbo_lock.acquire() (yappi #48). yappi keeps\n  the first name it gets, so the thread ident is reported as well.\n\nCost on a parse -> preprocessor pipeline over 12 and 36 documents: the\nper-thread copy holds 2.1-3.5 MB against 0.5 MB flat (13-25 threads), and\nthe capture under _lock grows from ~117 ms to ~250-275 ms.\n\n* perf(ai): cache _relativize_path\n\nstop() calls it under _lock for every entry and callee of each capture:\nabout 5,900 calls per stop on a parse pipeline over 12 documents, since the\nper-thread capture repeats the flat one''s paths, over a few hundred\ndistinct paths. The result depends only on the path, so cache it.\n\nOn the same pipeline the capture under _lock drops from ~250-275 ms to\n~160-170 ms, against ~117 ms before the per-thread capture existed. Memory\ndrops only ~10%: every entry of a path now shares one string, but the\nper-entry dicts are most of the size.\n\n* fix(ai): stop the call tree from pruning away real work\n\nreport_tree() dropped every node whose own time fell under min_pct, and with\nit the whole subtree. Two kinds of node read light while real work hangs\nbelow them: frames still running at stop() (thread entry points, executor\nworker loops) read ~0 because yappi books time on return, and the event\nloop step that resumes a coroutine is short while the coroutine is booked\nits whole lifetime. On a parse pipeline this emptied the merged tree, the\nevent-loop thread and every executor thread started mid-session; threads\nthat predated the session survived only because their zero-time frames sat\nat depths 0 and 1, which are never pruned.\n\nA node is now pruned only when nothing reachable below it reaches the\nthreshold either. The threshold was also inflated: total_time summed ttot,\ncounting nested calls again (a 5 s session summed to 412 s). It is now the\ntime the threads ran, as yappi measures it per thread - the selected one,\nor all summed - falling back to the sum of self times without thread data.\n\nSame session after the fix: project code shows in every view at the\ndefault threshold, and trees stay at 4-168 nodes, built in under 60 ms.\n\n* feat(client): list profiled threads and fetch one thread''s tree\n\nPython: cprofile_threads() and a thread parameter on cprofile_report_tree().\nTypeScript: cprofileThreads() and a trailing thread parameter on\ncprofileReportTree(), with CProfileThreadInfo / CProfileThreadsResponse.\nThread 0 is a real id, so both clients test for absence, not truthiness.\n\nThe shell re-exports the SDK''s types, so the two new ones are new shell-api\nnames and shell:check fails until they are frozen: this freezes shell-api\nv4. Its delta over v3 is exactly these types and the two method signatures.\n\nThe contract test runs data through a task''s pipeline and checks that no\nthread carries yappi''s class-based names, that one thread''s tree totals that\nthread''s calls, and that an unknown id is refused, which proves the\nargument reached the engine.\n\ntest-ui''s API sweep and the SDK references list the new method; the Python\nreference also claimed include_system defaults to False, which it never did.\n\n* feat(profiler-ui): pick a thread to visualise\n\nA Thread dropdown heads the viz controls: all threads (the old view) or one,\nlabelled with name, thread id and time, since names repeat. The views get\nthe same tree shape either way. The list is refetched with the report, a\nselection missing from the new list falls back to all threads, and a server\nwithout rrext_cprofile_threads just hides the dropdown. Start clears the\nlist, since ids are per session.\n\nBumps the app to 1.3.0: an OSS server reseeds a built-in app only when its\napps.json version changes, so an upgraded server would keep serving the\nold profiler otherwise.\n\n* fix(profiler-ui): prune the charts by subtree weight\n\nThe client-side cutoff compared a child''s own cumtime with its parent''s,\nso a zero-time frame the server now keeps was dropped again at the root,\ntaking the work under it along. It now compares the largest cumtime in\neach subtree, and runs before the depth limit so that weight covers the\nwhole subtree.\n\n* fix(profiler-ui): clear the two type errors tsc reports\n\ninitConnectionStore took the shell''s settings as Record<string, string>,\nbut they are Record<string, SettingValue>, and a port setting may well be\na number; the seeded host and port are now coerced to strings. The\nsunburst filtered on x0/x1 before casting to HierarchyRectangularNode,\nwhich the flame graph already does first. Neither changes behaviour: the\napp build does not type-check, which is how both went unnoticed.\n\n* fix: CodeRabbit","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqrETzCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAf6gQAIFIlHswygUT4pjo8qGIKYVe\nD5H2Jr6B9l1r1nTteJot+soxw2X660WwQZzJNEmoYVf2BQelFOpIQ1/jxjQsSkrp\n/Y4ts9R3T80fnwLsKrvJrQ43xZFwiwg85Et+3oHS8IIjQxO301IltsAjX3hHGZwr\n4ZDnnxSG0Uf3Bg/wmwkeYWUhvZGURAp2nWGvpr9wwpJt35CUoQsuM/nlMONEUBbJ\n49QsLFRcKHY2o6r8R3h3wN0tpsCJRpx0mMTOEAhi9mFMhbH+wqiHUCjiTiUg4JkW\nhw9/MYSJnaIBVlRPwPhBsTuKoharA9QUabi8xG1rocw2Wdm/DI7prD8JTuH7aw0M\nr6zCJeOydrvDHD9pC01ae5dWFcxW4TgZZ6R5nMqtMlWvX2ZwcFo6Wci3lNVd7jbq\nUqecR8lRkF6NK44IvbISX/fKrrjo44fn3GZP3MVtdCMHQHEaUaILeTZPAW/7+/o4\ngLlQ//YZAq+ECiBJoi4591GPRZvrHHaf11XJ1WD9QlPzxVrfuc2uGFpYjFaUQC5/\nMlDzfrTao4w+Ro7BYK/PmyJDLfqrAV6vyRi632n0MwQ7RY8ao1slC99atGv4Gve6\nGTQ+QrzlTPy0nRzR0YI5otb9357Ntkdrc7tySMN6L2